window.QUIZ_DATA = {"exams":[{"examNumber":1,"sdls":[{"sdlNumber":1,"title":"SDL 1 — Embryology of the Foregut","questions":[{"id":"sdl01_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":1,"stem":"In a study of the embryonic gut tube, an investigator maps which adult structures receive their blood supply from the artery of the midgut. Which of the following structures belongs to this territory?","choices":{"A":"Gastric fundus","B":"Sigmoid colon","C":"Pancreatic tail","D":"Terminal ileum","E":"Upper rectum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The midgut begins just caudal to the liver bud and ends at the junction of the right two-thirds and left one-third of the transverse colon; it forms the distal duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal transverse colon, all supplied by the superior mesenteric artery. The terminal ileum therefore lies in the midgut territory. The gastric fundus and the pancreatic tail are foregut derivatives supplied by the celiac artery (the pancreatic tail through the splenic artery). The sigmoid colon and upper rectum are hindgut derivatives supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, four divisions of the gut tube); Langman Ch. 15 (celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric supply of the gut).","boardPrep":"Step 1: foregut = celiac, midgut = SMA, hindgut = IMA; the midgut runs from the lower duodenum to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: map visceral pain and ischemia to the gut region and its artery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 5 — arterial supply of the midgut-derived small intestine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, the duodenum receives arterial branches from two separate aortic trunks. Which feature of its development explains this?","choices":{"A":"Dual midgut and hindgut origin","B":"Rotation around the SMA axis","C":"Dual foregut and midgut origin","D":"Secondary retroperitoneal fixation","E":"Origin from the hepatic diverticulum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The duodenum forms from the terminal part of the foregut and the cephalic part of the midgut. Because the foregut is supplied by the celiac artery and the midgut by the superior mesenteric artery, the duodenum receives branches of both: the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery from the celiac system and the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery from the SMA. The hindgut contributes nothing to the duodenum. Rotation around the SMA describes the midgut loop, not the source of duodenal arteries. Secondary retroperitoneal fixation by Toldt fascia changes position, not blood supply. The hepatic diverticulum gives rise to the liver and biliary tree. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, dual blood supply of the duodenum); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the foregut-midgut junction lies at the ampulla of Vater, where the bile and pancreatic ducts enter (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: pancreaticoduodenal arcades link the celiac and SMA territories as a collateral route.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 5 — arterial supply of the midgut-derived small intestine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":1,"stem":"An embryology student traces the boundary between the foregut-derived and midgut-derived parts of the duodenum. Which landmark marks this boundary?","choices":{"A":"Pyloric sphincter","B":"Duodenojejunal flexure","C":"Minor duodenal papilla","D":"Superior duodenal flexure","E":"Major duodenal papilla"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The junction between the foregut and midgut lies just distal to the origin of the liver bud, which in the adult corresponds to the entrance of the common bile duct at the major duodenal papilla. Duodenum proximal to the papilla is foregut and celiac supplied; duodenum distal to it is midgut and SMA supplied. The pyloric sphincter and the superior duodenal flexure lie within the foregut portion. The duodenojejunal flexure lies within the midgut. The minor papilla, where the accessory pancreatic duct drains, lies proximal to the major papilla and does not mark the junction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, dual blood supply of the duodenum); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum); First Aid 2025 GI (normal gastrointestinal embryology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: foregut runs from the esophagus to the duodenum at the level of the pancreatic duct and common bile duct insertion (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: obstruction distal to this point produces bilious vomiting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 5 — arterial supply of the midgut-derived small intestine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula most often results from which developmental error?","choices":{"A":"Posterior deviation of the septum","B":"Failure of the lung bud to form","C":"Failed fusion of the pleuroperitoneal folds","D":"Arrested lengthening of the esophagus","E":"Abnormal migration of neural crest cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Langman attributes esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula to spontaneous posterior deviation of the tracheoesophageal septum or to a mechanical force pushing the dorsal foregut wall anteriorly, so the septum partitions the foregut incompletely. Failure of the lung bud to form causes pulmonary agenesis rather than a fistula. Failed fusion of the pleuroperitoneal folds causes congenital diaphragmatic hernia. Arrested lengthening of the esophagus pulls the stomach into the hiatus as a congenital hiatal hernia. Abnormal neural crest migration produces aganglionic segments such as Hirschsprung disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, pathogenesis of esophageal atresia and TEF); Langman Ch. 15 (esophageal abnormalities).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the most common variant is esophageal atresia with a distal TEF, which presents with polyhydramnios and choking at the first feed; associated with VACTERL (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: an NG tube that will not pass into the stomach confirms the atresia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — muscle type transitions and innervation along the esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, which variant of esophageal atresia or tracheoesophageal fistula is the most common?","choices":{"A":"Isolated atresia without a fistula","B":"Proximal atresia with distal fistula","C":"H-type fistula without atresia","D":"Upper pouch fistula with distal atresia","E":"Separate fistulas at both segments"},"correct":"B","explanation":"About 90% of cases are a blind proximal esophageal pouch with the distal esophagus joined to the trachea just above the bifurcation. Isolated atresia without a fistula and the H-type fistula without atresia each account for about 4%. A proximal fistula with a blind distal segment and a double fistula each account for about 1%. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, anatomic variations and frequency); Langman Ch. 15 (Fig. 15.7, variants in order of frequency). First Aid 2025 GI gives 85% for this variant; Langman and the lecture give 90%.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EA with distal TEF is the most common type; air entering the stomach through the fistula produces a prominent gastric bubble, whereas pure EA gives a gasless abdomen (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: aspiration of gastric acid through the distal fistula causes chemical pneumonitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — muscle type transitions and innervation along the esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, why is esophageal atresia associated with polyhydramnios?","choices":{"A":"Fetal kidneys produce excess urine","B":"Fluid leaks from the fistula into the lungs","C":"Swallowed fluid does not reach the intestine","D":"Amniotic membranes secrete more fluid","E":"Placental transfer of fluid to the mother falls"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The fetus normally swallows amniotic fluid, which is absorbed in the intestine. With esophageal atresia the fluid does not pass the blind pouch into the stomach and gut, so it accumulates in the amniotic sac. Polyhydramnios here is a problem of fetal clearance, not of fetal urine production, which is unchanged. Fluid entering the airway through a fistula is not a route of amniotic clearance. The membranes and placenta are not the site of the defect. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, clinical features); Langman Ch. 15 (atresia prevents passage of amniotic fluid into the intestinal tract).","boardPrep":"Step 1: polyhydramnios points to an inability to swallow or absorb amniotic fluid, as in esophageal or duodenal atresia; oligohydramnios points to absent fetal urine (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: polyhydramnios on prenatal ultrasound should prompt evaluation for upper GI atresia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — muscle type transitions and innervation along the esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, after the stomach rotates 90 degrees clockwise around its longitudinal axis, the left vagus nerve innervates which part of the stomach?","choices":{"A":"Posterior wall","B":"Lesser curvature","C":"Greater curvature","D":"Anterior wall","E":"Pyloric antrum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The 90-degree clockwise rotation turns the original left side of the stomach to face anteriorly and the right side to face posteriorly. The left vagus nerve, which ran along the left side, therefore comes to innervate the anterior wall, and the right vagus nerve innervates the posterior wall. The lesser and greater curvatures form from the slower-growing anterior and faster-growing posterior walls, not from vagal territory. The pyloric antrum moves upward and to the right during rotation around the anteroposterior axis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, rotation around the longitudinal axis); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stomach rotates 90 degrees clockwise; left vagus becomes anterior and right vagus becomes posterior (First Aid 2025 GI; Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: the anterior vagal trunk lies on the front of the distal esophagus during vagotomy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — distribution of vagal parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, the greater curvature of the stomach forms because one of the original walls grows faster than the opposite wall during rotation. Which wall is this?","choices":{"A":"Original anterior wall","B":"Original left lateral wall","C":"Original right lateral wall","D":"Original cranial wall","E":"Original posterior wall"},"correct":"E","explanation":"During rotation the original posterior wall of the stomach grows faster than the anterior wall, forming the greater curvature; the slower-growing anterior wall forms the lesser curvature. The original left and right walls become the anterior and posterior surfaces after the 90-degree clockwise rotation and carry the left and right vagal trunks. The original cranial end becomes the cardia and shifts during rotation around the anteroposterior axis; it does not form a curvature. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, differential wall growth and curvatures); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dorsal mesogastrium attaches to the greater curvature and becomes the greater omentum; ventral mesogastrium attaches to the lesser curvature and forms the lesser omentum (Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: these attachments define the peritoneal ligaments seen in gastric surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — distribution of vagal parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, the spleen primordium appears during the fifth week of development between the two leaves of which structure?","choices":{"A":"Ventral mesogastrium","B":"Dorsal mesogastrium","C":"Lesser omentum","D":"Dorsal mesoduodenum","E":"Septum transversum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The spleen primordium arises as a mesodermal proliferation between the leaves of the dorsal mesogastrium. Gastric rotation swings it to the left, and the dorsal mesogastrium between spleen and stomach becomes the gastrosplenic ligament, while the part between spleen and left kidney becomes the splenorenal ligament. The ventral mesogastrium gives rise to the lesser omentum and falciform ligament and contains the developing liver. The dorsal mesoduodenum receives the dorsal pancreatic bud. The septum transversum is the mesoderm into which the liver bud grows. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, development and position of the spleen); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach mesenteries and spleen).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the spleen arises in the dorsal mesogastrium and is mesodermal, yet it has a foregut blood supply through the splenic branch of the celiac trunk (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: the gastrosplenic and splenorenal ligaments are divided during splenectomy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — distribution of vagal parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"Congenital duodenal atresia most often results from which process?","choices":{"A":"Vascular accident with resorption","B":"Abnormal rotation of the ventral bud","C":"Failed recanalization of the lumen","D":"Posterior deviation of the septum","E":"Hypertrophy of circular muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The duodenal lumen is obliterated by epithelial proliferation and then recanalized by vacuolization; when recanalization fails, the lumen stays occluded, producing atresia, and when it is incomplete it produces stenosis. Vascular accidents with resorption of bowel cause jejunal and ileal atresia. Abnormal rotation of the ventral pancreatic bud creates an annular pancreas that encircles the duodenum. Posterior deviation of the tracheoesophageal septum causes esophageal atresia. Hypertrophy of circular muscle at the pylorus causes hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, failure of recanalization); Langman Ch. 15 (upper duodenal atresias probably from lack of recanalization; Langman attributes atresia from the distal duodenum caudally to vascular accidents, while the lecture applies failed recanalization to the duodenum); First Aid 2025 GI (intestinal atresia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal atresia = failure to recanalize from the solid cord stage, double bubble on radiograph, associated with Down syndrome; jejunal and ileal atresia = vascular disruption (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: check infants with trisomy 21 for duodenal atresia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 4 — obstruction from failed recanalization versus vascular atresia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"During normal development of the duodenum, epithelial proliferation briefly converts the lumen into a solid cord. According to Langman, which event follows this solid stage?","choices":{"A":"Vacuole formation and coalescence","B":"Rotation around the SMA axis","C":"Fusion of the pancreatic buds","D":"Herniation into the umbilical cord","E":"Outgrowth of the hepatic diverticulum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"After the solid stage, vacuoles form within the epithelial cord through apoptosis and cleft formation and then coalesce into a continuous lumen, restoring a patent tube. Rotation around the SMA axis and physiologic herniation into the umbilical cord are events of the midgut loop. Fusion of the dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds follows rotation of the duodenum but does not reopen its lumen. The hepatic diverticulum grows out from the foregut before this stage and forms the liver and biliary tree. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, normal duodenal recanalization); Langman Ch. 15 (solid stage and cavity formation, Fig. 15.18).","boardPrep":"Step 1: failure of this vacuolization gives duodenal atresia or stenosis (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: the same failure in the esophagus produces esophageal stenosis, usually in the lower third.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 4 — obstruction from failed recanalization versus vascular atresia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"In an infant with congenital duodenal obstruction, the vomitus is bile stained. Where is the obstruction most likely located?","choices":{"A":"Proximal to the major papilla","B":"Distal to the major papilla","C":"At the pyloric sphincter","D":"At the gastroesophageal junction","E":"Within the gastric antrum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bile enters the duodenum at the major papilla, so obstruction distal to it produces bilious vomiting; the lecture notes that about 80% to 85% of duodenal obstructions lie distal to the papilla. Obstruction proximal to the papilla, at the pylorus, at the gastroesophageal junction, or in the antrum keeps bile out of the vomitus, which is nonbilious, as in hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, clinical and radiographic presentation); Langman Ch. 15 (entrance of the bile duct at the major papilla); First Aid 2025 GI (intestinal atresia presents with bilious vomiting).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilious emesis in a neonate signals obstruction distal to the ampulla of Vater, such as duodenal atresia or malrotation with volvulus (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: bilious vomiting in a newborn is a surgical emergency until proven otherwise.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 4 — obstruction from failed recanalization versus vascular atresia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, hypertrophic pyloric stenosis results mainly from enlargement of which tissue layer of the pylorus?","choices":{"A":"Circular muscularis externa","B":"Pyloric mucosal epithelium","C":"Submucosal connective tissue","D":"Antral muscularis mucosae","E":"Visceral peritoneal serosa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pyloric stenosis occurs when the circular musculature of the pylorus, and to a lesser degree the longitudinal musculature, hypertrophies and narrows the pyloric lumen. The lecture adds that the circular layer of the muscularis propria often thickens by 50% to 100%. The mucosa and submucosa may become edematous and inflamed, but they are secondary changes. The muscularis mucosae is a thin layer within the mucosa that does not form the obstructing mass. The serosa is a covering layer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, underlying developmental abnormality); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach abnormalities); Robbins Ch. 17 (hyperplasia of the pyloric muscularis propria).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the pyloric sphincter muscle produce the palpable olive; treat with pyloromyotomy (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: the myotomy divides the hypertrophied muscle down to the submucosa and spares the mucosa.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — pyloric resistance during gastric mixing and emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"An investigator examines pyloric muscle from infants with hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and finds few neurons expressing the enzyme that produces the main relaxing transmitter of the sphincter. Which transmitter is deficient?","choices":{"A":"Acetylcholine","B":"Substance P","C":"Serotonin","D":"Nitric oxide","E":"Norepinephrine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pyloric relaxation is mediated by nonadrenergic, noncholinergic neurons that release nitric oxide made by neuronal nitric oxide synthase. In hypertrophic pyloric stenosis, neuronal nitric oxide synthase is markedly deficient in the pyloric muscle plexus, so the sphincter fails to relax, and persistent contraction drives work hypertrophy and narrowing. Acetylcholine and substance P are excitatory transmitters that contract GI smooth muscle. Serotonin acts mainly as a sensory and secretory mediator in the enteric nervous system. Norepinephrine from sympathetic fibers relaxes gut wall muscle but contracts sphincters. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, nitric oxide synthase deficiency). Among the staged sources this mechanism is stated by the lecture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loss of NO-mediated relaxation also underlies achalasia, where NO and VIP inhibitory neurons of the myenteric plexus degenerate at the lower esophageal sphincter (First Aid 2025 GI achalasia). COMLEX: think of failed relaxation when a sphincter region thickens in an infant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — pyloric resistance during gastric mixing and emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, postnatal exposure to which drug substantially increases the risk of hypertrophic pyloric stenosis?","choices":{"A":"Amoxicillin","B":"Gentamicin","C":"Ceftriaxone","D":"Metronidazole","E":"Erythromycin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Langman notes that treatment with erythromycin in the newborn period substantially increases the risk of pyloric stenosis, suggesting that the defect can develop after birth; Robbins and the lecture extend the association to azithromycin, either given directly or through breast milk in the first 2 weeks of life. Amoxicillin, gentamicin, ceftriaxone, and metronidazole are commonly used in neonates but are not macrolides and are not linked to pyloric stenosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, environmental and medication exposure); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach abnormalities); Robbins Ch. 17 (pyloric stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is more common in firstborn males and is associated with macrolide exposure (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: weigh this risk when choosing pertussis prophylaxis for a young infant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — pyloric resistance during gastric mixing and emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has a bleeding posterior ulcer of the duodenal bulb, and the gastroduodenal artery is surgically ligated. Afterward the duodenum distal to the major papilla remains well perfused. This part of the duodenum is perfused mainly by branches of which artery?","choices":{"A":"Superior mesenteric artery","B":"Common hepatic artery","C":"Left gastric artery","D":"Right gastroepiploic artery","E":"Inferior mesenteric artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The duodenum distal to the major papilla is midgut and is supplied by the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery, a branch of the superior mesenteric artery, so it stays perfused after the gastroduodenal artery is tied. The common hepatic artery gives rise to the gastroduodenal artery and supplies the foregut part of the duodenum through the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery. The left gastric artery supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach. The right gastroepiploic artery is a branch of the ligated gastroduodenal artery. The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the hindgut. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, dual blood supply of the duodenum); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum supplied by both celiac and superior mesenteric arteries).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac trunk branches are the common hepatic, splenic, and left gastric arteries, and the pancreaticoduodenal arcades join the celiac and SMA territories (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: a posterior duodenal ulcer can erode the gastroduodenal artery, and the arcades protect the duodenum when it is ligated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — the arterial supply of midgut-derived small intestine comes from the SMA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman develops left-sided abdominal pain and bloody stools after a prolonged episode of hypotension. Colonoscopy shows ischemic mucosa from the distal third of the transverse colon through the sigmoid colon, with a normal cecum and ascending colon. The ischemic bowel is derived from which embryonic gut region?","choices":{"A":"Midgut","B":"Foregut","C":"Pharyngeal gut","D":"Hindgut","E":"Cloaca"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The hindgut extends from the distal one-third of the transverse colon to the cloacal membrane and forms the distal transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon, rectum, and upper anal canal, all supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery; hypoperfusion of that territory explains the pattern. The midgut forms the cecum, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon, which are normal. The foregut forms the esophagus through the proximal duodenum. The pharyngeal gut forms the pharynx. The cloaca is the terminal dilation of the hindgut that the urorectal septum divides into the rectum and urogenital sinus; it does not form the colon. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, four divisions of the gut tube); Langman Ch. 15 (gut divisions and arteries).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hindgut = IMA, with parasympathetic supply from the pelvic splanchnic nerves rather than the vagus (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: the splenic flexure is a watershed between SMA and IMA territories.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — pelvic nerves rather than the vagus supply parasympathetic fibers to the distal colon","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has endoscopic removal of an adenoma in the descending duodenum just proximal to the major duodenal papilla. Several hours later he has bleeding from the main artery supplying the resected segment. Which artery is most likely bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","B":"Right gastroepiploic artery","C":"Short gastric branch of the splenic artery","D":"Ileocolic branch of the SMA","E":"Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Duodenum proximal to the major papilla is foregut and is supplied by the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery, which arises from the gastroduodenal artery of the celiac system. The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery is an SMA branch that supplies the midgut part distal to the papilla. The right gastroepiploic artery supplies the greater curvature of the stomach. Short gastric branches of the splenic artery supply the gastric fundus. The ileocolic artery supplies the terminal ileum, cecum, and appendix. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, dual blood supply of the duodenum); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum).","boardPrep":"Step 1: posterior duodenal ulcers erode the gastroduodenal artery, the parent of the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: localize duodenal bleeding by its position relative to the ampulla.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — arterial supply of the duodenum and small intestine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy born after a pregnancy complicated by polyhydramnios drools, chokes, and becomes cyanotic during his first feed. A nasogastric tube coils in the upper chest, and a chest radiograph shows a gas-filled stomach. Which additional finding is most likely in this infant?","choices":{"A":"Hypertrophied pyloric muscle","B":"Annular pancreatic tissue","C":"Vertebral segmentation defects","D":"Aganglionic distal colon","E":"Bilateral congenital cataracts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A blind upper esophageal pouch that stops the nasogastric tube, together with air in the stomach, indicates esophageal atresia with a distal tracheoesophageal fistula. Thirty to fifty percent of these infants have other anomalies, most notably the VACTERL association, which includes vertebral defects. Hypertrophied pyloric muscle presents weeks later with projectile nonbilious vomiting. Annular pancreas is associated with trisomy 21 and duodenal obstruction. Aganglionic colon is Hirschsprung disease, which presents with delayed meconium passage. Congenital cataracts point to infections such as rubella. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, clinical features and associated anomalies); Langman Ch. 15 (esophageal abnormalities); First Aid 2025 GI (tracheoesophageal anomalies).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VACTERL = vertebral, anal atresia, cardiac, tracheoesophageal fistula, renal, limb defects; failure to pass an NG tube is the bedside test for EA (First Aid 2025 GI; Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: screen with echocardiography and renal ultrasound once EA is found.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — annular pancreas is another foregut anomaly with polyhydramnios and neonatal vomiting","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-month-old girl has had three episodes of pneumonia and coughs and gags during most feeds. A contrast study shows a continuous, patent esophagus with contrast entering the trachea through a short oblique tract in the lower neck. Which developmental error best explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"Failed lengthening of the esophagus during descent","B":"Incomplete recanalization of the esophageal lumen","C":"Loss of ganglion cells in the myenteric plexus","D":"Defective fusion of the pleuroperitoneal folds","E":"Incomplete fusion of tracheoesophageal ridges"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A patent esophagus with a fistula to the trachea is the H-type fistula. It results from incomplete partitioning by the tracheoesophageal septum, which forms when the paired tracheoesophageal ridges grow toward each other and fuse; where fusion is incomplete, a communication persists. Failed lengthening of the esophagus causes congenital hiatal hernia. Incomplete recanalization narrows the lumen as esophageal stenosis without a fistula. Loss of myenteric ganglion cells causes motility disorders such as achalasia. Defective pleuroperitoneal fold fusion causes diaphragmatic hernia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, normal formation of the septum and H-type TEF); Langman Ch. 15 (esophagus and esophageal abnormalities).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the H-type fistula resembles the letter H and causes recurrent aspiration pneumonia (First Aid 2025 GI; Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: suspect it in an infant with recurrent pneumonia and choking with feeds despite a passable NG tube.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — esophageal stenosis and webs arise from structural narrowing rather than a fistula","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman at 32 weeks of gestation has a fundal height larger than expected. Ultrasound shows an amniotic fluid index of 32 cm (reference range: 5–25 cm) and a fetal stomach that is not visualized on repeated scans. Which finding is most likely at birth?","choices":{"A":"Failure to pass a nasogastric tube","B":"Bilious vomiting after the first feed","C":"Olive-shaped epigastric mass","D":"Delayed passage of meconium","E":"Bowel loops outside the abdomen"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Polyhydramnios with an absent fetal stomach bubble means swallowed amniotic fluid is not reaching the stomach, which indicates esophageal atresia; at birth a nasogastric tube will stop in the blind upper pouch. Bilious vomiting with polyhydramnios fits duodenal atresia distal to the papilla, but that lesion produces a distended stomach and duodenum on ultrasound. The olive-shaped mass of pyloric stenosis appears weeks after birth. Delayed meconium passage suggests Hirschsprung disease or meconium ileus. Bowel loops outside the abdomen fit gastroschisis or omphalocele, which are seen directly on ultrasound. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, in utero polyhydramnios and postnatal presentation); Langman Ch. 15 (atresia and polyhydramnios); First Aid 2025 GI (tracheoesophageal anomalies).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pure EA gives a gasless abdomen, whereas EA with distal TEF gives a prominent gastric bubble (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: a small or absent fetal stomach with polyhydramnios is the prenatal clue to EA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — omphalocele and gastroschisis show bowel outside the abdomen on prenatal ultrasound","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man with refractory peptic ulcer disease undergoes truncal vagotomy. The surgeon divides the vagal trunk that lies on the anterior surface of the distal esophagus. The fibers in this trunk come mainly from which nerve?","choices":{"A":"Right vagus nerve","B":"Left phrenic nerve","C":"Left vagus nerve","D":"Greater splanchnic nerve","E":"Left recurrent laryngeal nerve"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Before rotation the left and right vagus nerves run along the left and right sides of the stomach. The 90-degree clockwise rotation carries the left vagus anteriorly, where it becomes the anterior vagal trunk innervating the anterior gastric wall. The right vagus becomes the posterior trunk. The phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm. The greater splanchnic nerve carries sympathetic fibers to the celiac ganglion. The left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the aortic arch and supplies the larynx. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, vagal innervation changes); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach rotation and vagus nerves).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after the 90-degree clockwise rotation the left vagus lies anterior and the right vagus posterior (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: vagotomy reduces acid secretion but can delay gastric emptying.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — the vagus distributes parasympathetic fibers to the stomach and proximal GI tract","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man recovering from acute pancreatitis develops a fluid collection that fills the space directly behind the stomach. This space formed as a result of which effect of gastric rotation?","choices":{"A":"Leftward pull on the ventral mesogastrium","B":"Leftward pull on the dorsal mesogastrium","C":"Rightward swing of the duodenal loop","D":"Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut","E":"Fusion of the transverse mesocolon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The space behind the stomach is the omental bursa, or lesser peritoneal sac. As the stomach rotates around its longitudinal axis, it pulls the dorsal mesogastrium to the left and opens this space behind it. The ventral mesogastrium, which becomes the lesser omentum, is pulled to the right rather than the left. The rightward swing of the C-shaped duodenum moves the duodenum and pancreatic head against the posterior wall. Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut loop around the SMA positions the intestines. Fusion of the greater omentum with the transverse mesocolon occurs later and closes off the lower part of the bursa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, formation of the omental bursa); Langman Ch. 15 (omental bursa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lesser sac communicates with the greater sac through the epiploic foramen of Winslow (First Aid 2025 GI anatomy). COMLEX: pancreatic pseudocysts and posterior gastric ulcer perforations collect in the lesser sac.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — pancreatic pseudocysts are nonneoplastic collections that follow pancreatitis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman undergoes laparoscopic fundoplication for reflux. Afterward her gastric emptying is delayed, and repeat surgery shows injury to the vagal trunk on the posterior surface of the distal esophagus. The fibers in this trunk come mainly from which nerve?","choices":{"A":"Left vagus nerve","B":"Right phrenic nerve","C":"Lesser splanchnic nerve","D":"Right vagus nerve","E":"Right recurrent laryngeal nerve"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The 90-degree clockwise rotation of the stomach carries the right vagus nerve posteriorly, where it forms the posterior vagal trunk and innervates the posterior gastric wall; the left vagus becomes the anterior trunk. Loss of vagal input reduces antral motility and delays emptying. The right phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm. The lesser splanchnic nerve carries sympathetic fibers. The right recurrent laryngeal nerve loops under the subclavian artery and supplies the larynx. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, vagal innervation changes); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach rotation and vagus nerves).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the posterior vagal trunk comes from the right vagus (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: vagal injury during fundoplication is a cause of postoperative gastroparesis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — vagal input supports the peristalsis that mixes and empties the stomach","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl with upslanting palpebral fissures and a single palmar crease vomits bile after every feed. An abdominal radiograph shows a dilated stomach and proximal duodenum with no gas beyond them. At surgery the pancreas is normal, and a thin membrane occludes the bowel just distal to the major papilla. Which developmental process failed?","choices":{"A":"Recanalization of the duodenal lumen","B":"Perfusion of the jejunal mesentery","C":"Rotation of the ventral pancreatic bud","D":"Rotation of the midgut loop","E":"Relaxation of the pyloric sphincter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The double bubble with no distal gas, bilious vomiting, and features of Down syndrome indicate duodenal atresia; an occluding membrane distal to the papilla is the result of failed vacuolization and recanalization after the solid stage. Loss of mesenteric perfusion causes jejunal atresia, with a triple bubble, and ileal atresia, with multiple dilated loops. Abnormal rotation of the ventral pancreatic bud creates an annular pancreas, excluded by the normal pancreas at surgery. Malrotation of the midgut causes obstruction by bands or volvulus rather than an intraluminal membrane. Failed pyloric relaxation causes hypertrophic pyloric stenosis with nonbilious vomiting weeks after birth. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, failed recanalization with an occluding membrane, the 80–85% of obstructions distal to the papilla, and associated conditions); First Aid 2025 GI (intestinal atresia); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (triple bubble in jejunal atresia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal atresia = double bubble, polyhydramnios, bilious vomiting, Down syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI; Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: look for associated cardiac defects in infants with trisomy 21.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — annular pancreas is the other congenital cause of a double bubble","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-day-old boy has repeated nonbilious vomiting after feeds. An abdominal radiograph shows a distended stomach and duodenal bulb with a small amount of gas in the distal bowel, and a contrast study shows a narrowed duodenal segment with a perforated web. Where is this lesion most likely located?","choices":{"A":"Distal to the major papilla","B":"At the duodenojejunal flexure","C":"In the terminal ileum","D":"At the gastroesophageal junction","E":"Proximal to the major papilla"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A perforated web with some distal gas indicates duodenal stenosis from incomplete recanalization, and nonbilious vomiting places the narrowing proximal to the major papilla, where bile has not yet entered the lumen. A lesion distal to the papilla or at the duodenojejunal flexure would produce bilious vomiting. Terminal ileal obstruction causes bilious vomiting with diffuse distension rather than a dilated duodenal bulb. Obstruction at the gastroesophageal junction prevents feeds from reaching the stomach, so the stomach and duodenum would not distend. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, duodenal stenosis and bilious versus nonbilious vomiting); Langman Ch. 15 (atresias and stenoses of the duodenum).","boardPrep":"Step 1: complete atresia gives no distal gas; stenosis or a perforated web lets some gas pass (lecture; First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: bile in the vomitus localizes the obstruction relative to the ampulla.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — the bile duct enters at the major papilla as the duodenum and ventral bud rotate","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman at 24 weeks of gestation has an ultrasound showing polyhydramnios and two fluid-filled structures in the fetal upper abdomen that connect through the pylorus. No other fluid-filled bowel is seen. The fetus is most likely to have which additional condition?","choices":{"A":"Trisomy 18","B":"Trisomy 21","C":"Trisomy 13","D":"Turner syndrome","E":"Klinefelter syndrome"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A fluid-filled stomach and proximal duodenum connected through the pylorus is the prenatal double bubble of duodenal atresia, and polyhydramnios reflects swallowed fluid that does not reach the distal bowel. Duodenal atresia is strongly associated with Down syndrome (trisomy 21), present in up to 30% of cases. Trisomy 18 and Turner syndrome carry other congenital anomalies but are not the classic association. Trisomy 13 is associated with midline defects such as holoprosencephaly. Klinefelter syndrome has no characteristic GI atresia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, associated conditions); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenal atresia); First Aid 2025 GI (intestinal atresia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Down syndrome GI associations include duodenal atresia, annular pancreas, and Hirschsprung disease (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: a prenatal double bubble prompts karyotype counseling.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — recanalization failure versus vascular compromise as causes of intestinal atresia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-week-old firstborn boy has had projectile, nonbilious vomiting after every feed for 5 days and is eager to feed again right after vomiting. A firm, mobile mass is palpable in the epigastrium. Laboratory studies show a serum chloride of 88 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), potassium of 3.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and bicarbonate of 33 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). The palpable mass consists mainly of which tissue?","choices":{"A":"Edematous submucosal connective tissue","B":"Hyperplastic antral mucosal glands","C":"Hypertrophied circular smooth muscle","D":"Enlarged submucosal lymphoid follicles","E":"Heterotopic pancreatic tissue"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A hungry, firstborn male infant with projectile nonbilious vomiting, an epigastric olive, and hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis has hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. The olive is the hypertrophied and hyperplastic circular smooth muscle of the pyloric muscularis propria. Mucosal and submucosal edema are secondary findings and do not form the mass. Antral gland hyperplasia and lymphoid follicles are not part of the lesion; lymphoid hyperplasia is a lead point for intussusception. Heterotopic pancreatic tissue can form a small gastric nodule but does not cause this presentation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, developmental abnormality and clinical presentation); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach abnormalities); Robbins Ch. 17 (pyloric stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophic pyloric stenosis = olive mass, visible peristalsis, projectile nonbilious vomiting at about 3–6 weeks (lecture; First Aid gives 2–6 weeks), hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: correct the fluid and electrolyte deficit before pyloromyotomy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — loss of parietal cell HCl and the alkaline tide explain the metabolic alkalosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old girl whose mother has pertussis was given a 5-day course of a prophylactic antibiotic starting at 6 days of age. She now has projectile, nonbilious vomiting after feeds and an olive-shaped epigastric mass. The prophylactic drug most likely acts by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Binding the 30S ribosomal subunit","B":"Binding the 50S ribosomal subunit","C":"Inhibiting cell wall transpeptidase","D":"Inhibiting DNA gyrase","E":"Inhibiting dihydropteroate synthase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pertussis prophylaxis in a young infant uses a macrolide, and macrolide exposure in the first 2 weeks of life is associated with hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. Macrolides such as erythromycin and azithromycin bind the 50S ribosomal subunit and block translocation. Binding the 30S subunit is the mechanism of aminoglycosides and tetracyclines. Inhibition of cell wall transpeptidases is the mechanism of beta-lactams. Inhibition of DNA gyrase is the mechanism of fluoroquinolones. Inhibition of dihydropteroate synthase is the mechanism of sulfonamides. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, macrolide exposure in the first 2 weeks); Langman Ch. 15 (erythromycin in the newborn period); First Aid 2025 GI (macrolide exposure); Katzung Ch. 44 (macrolides bind the 50S ribosomal subunit, Fig. 44–1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is associated with macrolide exposure (First Aid 2025 GI); erythromycin also acts as a motilin agonist and prokinetic. COMLEX: counsel families about vomiting after neonatal macrolide therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — duodenal feedback and pyloric resistance control gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-week-old girl has forceful vomiting immediately after feeds and remains hungry afterward. Ultrasound shows a thickened, elongated pyloric channel, and her vomitus contains milk but no bile. Which of the following best explains the absence of bile in her vomitus?","choices":{"A":"Absent bile secretion before 3 months of age","B":"Reflux blocked by the sphincter of Oddi","C":"Bile reabsorbed in the gastric antrum","D":"Obstruction proximal to the major papilla","E":"Bile diverted through the cystic duct"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pylorus lies proximal to the major duodenal papilla, where bile enters the gut, so a pyloric obstruction keeps bile out of the stomach and the vomitus is nonbilious. Infants secrete bile from birth, so age does not explain it. The sphincter of Oddi regulates bile entry into the duodenum but does not prevent bile from reaching the stomach when the obstruction lies distal to the papilla. The gastric antrum does not reabsorb bile; bile salts are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum. Diversion through the cystic duct stores bile in the gallbladder but does not eliminate it from the duodenum. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, forceful projectile nonbilious vomiting); Robbins Ch. 17 (pyloric stenosis); First Aid 2025 GI (hypertrophic pyloric stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nonbilious projectile vomiting points to pyloric stenosis; bilious vomiting points to obstruction distal to the ampulla (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: an ultrasound of the pylorus confirms the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — bile enters the duodenum through the sphincter of Oddi under CCK control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":3,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation has sudden severe periumbilical pain. CT shows an embolus in the artery that leaves the aorta just below the celiac trunk, with ischemia from the jejunum to the proximal transverse colon. The ischemic bowel comes from which embryonic gut region?","choices":{"A":"Foregut","B":"Hindgut","C":"Midgut","D":"Cloaca","E":"Pharyngeal gut"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The artery just below the celiac trunk is the superior mesenteric artery, the artery of the midgut, which forms the distal duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The foregut is supplied by the celiac artery. The hindgut, from the distal third of the transverse colon onward, is supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery. The cloaca is the terminal hindgut that forms the rectum and urogenital sinus. The pharyngeal gut forms the pharynx. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, four divisions of the gut tube); Langman Ch. 15 (gut divisions and arteries).","boardPrep":"Step 1: foregut = celiac, midgut = SMA, hindgut = IMA (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: acute mesenteric ischemia gives pain out of proportion to the examination.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — arterial supply of the small intestine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":3,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy has a congenital tracheal anomaly. His airway primordium first appeared at about 4 weeks of gestation as an outgrowth of which region of the gut tube?","choices":{"A":"Ventral foregut wall","B":"Dorsal foregut wall","C":"Ventral midgut wall","D":"Dorsal pharyngeal wall","E":"Lateral hindgut wall"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At about 4 weeks, the respiratory diverticulum (lung bud) appears on the ventral wall of the foregut at its border with the pharyngeal gut; the ventral part of the partitioned foregut forms the trachea and lung buds, and the dorsal part forms the esophagus. The dorsal foregut forms the esophagus rather than the airway. The midgut, the dorsal pharyngeal wall, and the hindgut do not give rise to the respiratory primordium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, normal formation of the tracheoesophageal septum); Langman Ch. 15 (respiratory diverticulum).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the respiratory diverticulum forms at about 4 weeks of gestation (lecture SDL 1). COMLEX: the foregut also gives rise to the stomach and proximal duodenum (lecture SDL 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelium of the esophagus and airway","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old man has a splenectomy after a motor vehicle collision. While dividing the peritoneal fold that runs from the spleen to the left kidney, the surgeon protects a structure that lies within it. Which structure does this ligament contain?","choices":{"A":"Short gastric arteries","B":"Left gastric artery","C":"Left adrenal gland","D":"Tail of the pancreas","E":"Left gonadal vein"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The part of the dorsal mesogastrium between the spleen and the left kidney becomes the splenorenal ligament, and the tail of the pancreas, which grows from the dorsal mesoduodenum into the dorsal mesogastrium, lies in it along with the splenic artery and vein. The short gastric arteries run in the gastrosplenic ligament. The left gastric artery runs along the lesser curvature in the lesser omentum. The left adrenal gland and left gonadal vein are retroperitoneal structures behind the peritoneum, not within the ligament. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, development and position of the spleen and pancreas); First Aid 2025 GI (ligaments: splenorenal ligament contains the splenic vessels and tail of pancreas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the splenorenal ligament contains the splenic artery and vein and the tail of the pancreas, and the gastrosplenic ligament contains the short gastric and left gastroepiploic vessels (First Aid 2025 GI ligaments). COMLEX: the pancreatic tail can be injured during splenectomy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — pancreatic buds in the dorsal and ventral mesentery","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":3,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman brings her 3-month-old son for follow-up after repair of a duodenal stenosis caused by a perforated mucosal web. He has no features of a chromosomal syndrome and is now feeding well. She asks when in development the lesion began. The surgeon explains that it arose from a stage when proliferating epithelium temporarily filled the duodenal lumen. During which period of gestation did that stage occur?","choices":{"A":"Weeks 5 to 6","B":"Weeks 2 to 3","C":"Weeks 8 to 10","D":"Weeks 12 to 14","E":"Weeks 20 to 24"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A perforated duodenal web causes stenosis through incomplete recanalization of the solid epithelial cord. During weeks 5 to 6, rapid proliferation of the endodermal epithelium fills the duodenal lumen and forms the solid stage; vacuolization and coalescence of vacuoles then restore the lumen. Weeks 2 to 3 precede folding of the embryo into a gut tube, so there is no duodenal lumen to fill. By weeks 8 to 10 the lumen is normally fully restored, which marks the end of recanalization rather than the solid stage. Weeks 12 to 14 and weeks 20 to 24 come after the lumen has already reopened. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, solid stage at weeks 5 to 6 and lumen restoration by weeks 8 to 10, lines 189–203, and duodenal stenosis from a perforated web, lines 210–212); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (duodenal atresia, failed recanalization).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists VACTERL syndrome and trisomy 21 as associations of duodenal atresia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, duodenal atresia). COMLEX: First Aid notes that intestinal atresia presents with bilious vomiting and abdominal distension within the first 1–2 days of life (First Aid 2025 GI, intestinal atresia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — recanalization failure versus vascular atresia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-week-old boy whose father had a pyloromyotomy as an infant has had forceful nonbilious vomiting right after feeds for 4 days and cries to be fed again afterward. He appears mildly dehydrated. Laboratory studies show a serum chloride of 91 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L) and bicarbonate of 31 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Inspection of his abdomen just before an episode of emesis is most likely to show which finding?","choices":{"A":"Waves moving right to left across the lower abdomen","B":"Waves moving left to right across the epigastrium","C":"A sausage shaped mass in the right upper quadrant","D":"Diffuse distension of the lower abdomen","E":"Bluish periumbilical discoloration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Forceful nonbilious vomiting right after feeds in a hungry young infant with a family history of the disorder and hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis indicates hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. As the hypertrophied antrum tries to drive chyme past the narrowed pylorus, prominent left-to-right peristaltic waves can be seen crossing the epigastrium just before emesis. Waves running right to left over the lower abdomen do not follow gastric peristalsis toward the pylorus. A sausage shaped right upper quadrant mass suggests intussusception, which causes colicky pain and bloody stools rather than projectile vomiting in a hungry infant. Diffuse lower abdominal distension points to a distal bowel obstruction, whereas pyloric obstruction leaves the lower abdomen flat. Bluish periumbilical discoloration reflects retroperitoneal hemorrhage, as in hemorrhagic pancreatitis, not gastric outlet obstruction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, family history, hungry vomiter, visible gastric peristalsis, and metabolic alkalosis, lines 268–293); First Aid 2025 GI (hypertrophic pyloric stenosis); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (peristaltic wave in epigastrium).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists bottle feeding, nicotine exposure, erythromycin, and azithromycin as risk factors for pyloric stenosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hypertrophic pyloric stenosis). COMLEX: First Aid notes that ultrasound shows a thickened and lengthened pylorus (First Aid 2025 GI, hypertrophic pyloric stenosis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — pyloric resistance in gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has left-sided abdominal pain and bloody stool after a period of hypotension during cardiac surgery. Colonoscopy shows ischemia centered on the splenic flexure. Why is this site vulnerable?","choices":{"A":"Celiac and SMA boundary","B":"Portal and caval anastomosis","C":"Hindgut and cloaca junction","D":"Single IMA end artery","E":"SMA and IMA watershed"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The splenic flexure lies near the boundary between the midgut territory of the superior mesenteric artery and the hindgut territory of the inferior mesenteric artery, a watershed area that is vulnerable to low flow. The celiac and SMA territories meet at the duodenum. A portal and caval anastomosis is a venous connection in the esophagus, rectum, or umbilical region and does not explain arterial low flow ischemia. The hindgut and cloaca junction is in the pelvis. A single end artery does not describe the flexure, whose supply comes from the edges of two territories, which is what makes it a watershed. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, midgut and hindgut boundary at the distal third of the transverse colon); First Aid 2025 GI (colonic ischemia at watershed areas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ischemic colitis affects watershed areas such as the splenic flexure (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: pain followed by bloody diarrhea after hypotension suggests colonic ischemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — colonic ischemia","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":2,"title":"SDL 2 — Histology of the Foregut part 1","questions":[{"id":"sdl02_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the muscularis mucosae forms the boundary between which two parts of the alimentary canal wall?","choices":{"A":"Epithelium and lamina propria","B":"Inner and outer muscle layers","C":"Submucosa and serosa","D":"Mucosa and submucosa","E":"Lamina propria and serosa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The muscularis mucosae is the deepest part of the mucosa, a thin band of smooth muscle that separates the mucosa from the submucosa beneath it. The epithelium and lamina propria are both above it, inside the mucosa. The inner circular and outer longitudinal layers belong to the muscularis externa and are separated by the myenteric plexus. The serosa is the outermost coat and does not contact the muscularis mucosae; the submucosa and muscularis externa lie between them. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, mucosa); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (the muscularis mucosae forms the boundary between mucosa and submucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gut wall layers from inside out are mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia (MSMS); erosions stay in the mucosa, while ulcers extend into the submucosa or deeper (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: depth of a lesion relative to the muscularis mucosae separates an erosion from an ulcer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — layers of the GI wall and location of the enteric plexuses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the thoracic esophagus is fixed to surrounding mediastinal structures for most of its length. Which coat forms its outermost layer?","choices":{"A":"Serosa","B":"Mesothelium","C":"Mesentery","D":"Submucosa","E":"Adventitia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Where the gut tube is bound to adjacent structures, its outer coat is adventitia, connective tissue that blends with the surrounding connective tissue; the thoracic esophagus is the classic example. Serosa, a simple squamous mesothelium on loose connective tissue, covers only the short abdominal segment after the esophagus enters the peritoneal cavity. Mesothelium is the epithelial part of a serosa, so it is absent where there is no serosa. A mesentery suspends intraperitoneal bowel and does not cover the thoracic esophagus. The submucosa lies inside the muscularis externa, not outside it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, serosa or adventitia); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal outer layer is adventitia; the abdominal segment is covered by serosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serosa covers intraperitoneal segments and adventitia covers retroperitoneal or fixed segments (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: the thoracic esophagus lacks a serosal covering.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — layers of the GI wall and location of the enteric plexuses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":1,"stem":"Within the muscularis externa of the small intestine, contraction of which component shortens the gut tube during peristalsis?","choices":{"A":"Inner circular layer","B":"Muscularis mucosae","C":"Outer longitudinal layer","D":"Inner oblique layer","E":"Lamina propria myofibroblasts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The muscularis externa has an inner circular and an outer longitudinal layer of smooth muscle. Contraction of the outer longitudinal layer shortens the tube, while contraction of the inner circular layer constricts the lumen; together they drive peristalsis and segmentation. The muscularis mucosae lies in the mucosa and makes local folds without moving the whole segment. An inner oblique layer is a regional feature of the stomach, not the small intestine. Myofibroblasts in the lamina propria do not form a muscle layer that shortens the bowel. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, muscularis externa); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (muscularis externa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus between the two layers of the muscularis externa controls motility (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: the stomach adds an inner oblique layer and the colon condenses its longitudinal layer into teniae coli.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — layers of the GI wall and location of the enteric plexuses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, which epithelium lines the human esophagus and resists abrasion by swallowed food?","choices":{"A":"Keratinized stratified squamous","B":"Nonkeratinized stratified squamous","C":"Simple columnar with goblet cells","D":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar","E":"Stratified columnar with microvilli"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, whose multiple cell layers resist abrasion from rough food boluses while staying moist. Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium is not the normal human esophageal lining. Simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells is the intestinal pattern and appears in the esophagus only as Barrett metaplasia. Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines the airway. Stratified columnar epithelium with microvilli is not a lining of the alimentary canal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, protection); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal mucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophagus has nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium; Barrett esophagus replaces it with intestinal columnar epithelium containing goblet cells (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology and Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: chronic acid reflux drives this metaplasia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":1,"stem":"The surface mucous cells of the stomach secrete a thick gel that protects the epithelium from luminal acid. This gel is rich in which ions?","choices":{"A":"Hydrogen and chloride","B":"Ammonium and phosphate","C":"Calcium and magnesium","D":"Bicarbonate and K+","E":"Sodium and sulfate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Surface mucous cells secrete a viscous, visible mucus layer rich in bicarbonate and K+. The bicarbonate trapped in the gel neutralizes acid at the epithelial surface, and the gel slows pepsin, which protects the mucosa from autodigestion. Hydrogen and chloride ions are the components of the hydrochloric acid secreted by parietal cells, the injury the gel guards against. Ammonium is generated by H. pylori urease, not by surface mucous cells. Phosphate, calcium, magnesium, and sulfate are not the defining ions of this barrier. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, surface mucous cells and alkaline mucus layer); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (surface mucous cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: prostaglandins maintain gastric mucus and bicarbonate secretion; NSAIDs reduce them and predispose to ulcers (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: misoprostol, a prostaglandin analog, restores this protection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, which crypt-base cell type of the small intestine secretes lysozyme and alpha-defensins?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cells","B":"Enteroendocrine cells","C":"Paneth cells","D":"Enterocytes","E":"Tuft cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Paneth cells at the base of the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete lysozyme and alpha-defensins, antimicrobial products of innate immunity. Goblet cells secrete mucin for lubrication. Enteroendocrine cells release peptide hormones such as CCK, secretin, GIP, and motilin. Enterocytes absorb nutrients through their microvillous brush border. Tuft cells are chemosensory cells and do not secrete these antimicrobial proteins. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, secretion in the small intestine); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (Paneth cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: crypts of Lieberkühn contain stem cells and Paneth cells that secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: Paneth cells sit at the crypt base, below the stem cells that renew the epithelium.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, where in the wall of the alimentary canal is the myenteric plexus located?","choices":{"A":"Between the muscle layers","B":"Within the submucosa","C":"Within the lamina propria","D":"Beneath the epithelium","E":"Within the adventitia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The myenteric (Auerbach) plexus lies in the thin connective tissue between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa, where it innervates both layers. The submucosa holds the submucosal (Meissner) plexus. The lamina propria and the region beneath the epithelium carry nerve fibers but not the myenteric ganglia. The adventitia is the outer connective tissue coat outside the muscularis externa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, anatomic location); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (myenteric plexus between the outer and inner muscle layers).","boardPrep":"Step 1: submucosa contains the Meissner plexus; muscularis externa contains the Auerbach plexus (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: Hirschsprung disease shows absent ganglion cells in both plexuses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility and secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":1,"stem":"In the enteric nervous system, which plexus mainly regulates local secretion, absorption, and blood flow in each gut segment?","choices":{"A":"Myenteric plexus","B":"Celiac plexus","C":"Hypogastric plexus","D":"Esophageal vagal plexus","E":"Submucosal plexus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The submucosal (Meissner) plexus innervates mucosal and submucosal glands, local blood vessels, and the muscularis mucosae, controlling secretion, absorption, blood flow, and mucosal folding in each segment. The myenteric (Auerbach) plexus controls motility of the muscularis externa along the length of the gut. The celiac and hypogastric plexuses are extrinsic autonomic plexuses outside the gut wall. The esophageal vagal plexus carries extrinsic vagal fibers on the esophagus surface. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, main functional role); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (submucosal plexus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meissner plexus regulates secretion and blood flow; Auerbach plexus regulates motility (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: both plexuses receive extrinsic vagal and sympathetic modulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility and secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":1,"stem":"Inhibitory neurons of the myenteric plexus relax the lower esophageal sphincter to let a bolus enter the stomach. Which transmitter do these neurons release?","choices":{"A":"Acetylcholine","B":"Nitric oxide","C":"Substance P","D":"Norepinephrine","E":"Serotonin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Inhibitory peptidergic neurons of the myenteric plexus release nitric oxide, together with VIP or ATP, to relax GI sphincters such as the lower esophageal, pyloric, and ileocecal sphincters. Acetylcholine and substance P are excitatory transmitters that contract smooth muscle. Norepinephrine comes from extrinsic sympathetic postganglionic fibers and inhibits motility rather than mediating enteric sphincter relaxation. Serotonin acts mainly as a sensory and paracrine signal in the mucosa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, sphincter control).","boardPrep":"Step 1: achalasia is caused by loss of NO and VIP inhibitory neurons in the myenteric plexus, so the LES fails to relax (First Aid 2025 GI achalasia). COMLEX: bird beak narrowing on barium swallow.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility and secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the muscularis externa of the upper third of the esophagus consists of which tissue?","choices":{"A":"Striated muscle","B":"Smooth muscle","C":"Mixed striated and smooth","D":"Cardiac muscle","E":"Myoepithelial cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The upper third of the esophageal muscularis externa is striated muscle, a continuation of the pharyngeal musculature. Smooth muscle forms the distal third. Mixed and interwoven striated and smooth bundles form the middle third. Cardiac muscle is not part of the gut wall. Myoepithelial cells surround the secretory units of some glands and do not form the muscularis externa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, muscle type transitions); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (upper one-third is striated muscle).","boardPrep":"Step 1: upper 1/3 of the esophagus is striated muscle, middle 1/3 mixed, and lower 1/3 smooth muscle (lecture; First Aid 2025 GI groups the middle and lower 2/3 as smooth muscle with some overlap at the transition). COMLEX: upper esophageal dysphagia suggests a skeletal muscle or neuromuscular cause.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — neural and muscular coordination of swallowing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":1,"stem":"The striated muscle of the proximal esophagus is supplied by somatic motor neurons that travel in the vagus nerve. From which brainstem nucleus do these neurons arise?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal motor nucleus","B":"Hypoglossal nucleus","C":"Nucleus tractus solitarius","D":"Nucleus ambiguus","E":"Facial motor nucleus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Somatic motor neurons to the striated esophageal muscle arise in the nucleus ambiguus and end directly on motor endplates. The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus sends preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the smooth muscle of the distal esophagus. The hypoglossal nucleus supplies tongue muscles. The nucleus tractus solitarius receives visceral afferent input. The facial motor nucleus supplies muscles of facial expression. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, innervation of striated musculature).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatic motor neurons of the nucleus ambiguus travel in the vagus to the striated muscle of the upper esophagus, whereas the dorsal motor nucleus supplies the smooth muscle (Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 2, section 4; Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: a brainstem lesion involving the nucleus ambiguus can cause dysphagia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — neural and muscular coordination of swallowing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":1,"stem":"Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus reach the smooth muscle of the distal esophagus. Where do these fibers synapse?","choices":{"A":"Celiac ganglion neurons","B":"Motor endplates on muscle","C":"Myenteric plexus neurons","D":"Stellate ganglion neurons","E":"Submucosal gland cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vagal preganglionic fibers from the dorsal motor nucleus synapse on postganglionic enteric neurons whose cell bodies lie in the myenteric plexus of the esophageal wall; these neurons then drive contraction and peristalsis. The celiac and stellate ganglia are sympathetic ganglia. Motor endplates are the targets of somatic fibers from the nucleus ambiguus on striated muscle, not of preganglionic parasympathetic fibers. Submucosal gland cells are effectors, not the site of the ganglionic synapse. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, parasympathetic control of smooth musculature).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasympathetic preganglionic fibers are long and synapse in ganglia in or near the target organ, within the myenteric and submucosal plexuses (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vagal input enhances peristalsis through the enteric neurons.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — neural and muscular coordination of swallowing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the esophageal glands proper are located in which layer of the esophageal wall?","choices":{"A":"Lamina propria","B":"Adventitia","C":"Epithelium","D":"Muscularis mucosae","E":"Submucosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Esophageal glands proper are small compound tubuloalveolar glands in the submucosa, and their ducts cross the muscularis mucosae and mucosa to open into the lumen. The lamina propria holds the esophageal cardiac glands, not the glands proper. The adventitia is the outer connective tissue coat. The epithelium is crossed only by the ducts. The muscularis mucosae is a smooth muscle layer that the ducts pass through. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal glands proper); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal glands proper lie in the submucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: submucosal glands are found in the esophagus and in the duodenum as Brunner glands (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: identify gland layer to name the gland type on a slide.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — reflux injury and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the esophageal cardiac glands near the gastroesophageal junction secrete which product?","choices":{"A":"Neutral mucus","B":"Acidic mucus","C":"Serous fluid","D":"Bicarbonate fluid","E":"Pepsinogen"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Esophageal cardiac glands secrete neutral mucus that protects the distal esophagus from regurgitated gastric contents. The esophageal glands proper secrete a slightly acidic mucus, which makes acidic mucus the wrong gland. Serous fluid comes from serous glands such as the parotid. Bicarbonate fluid is the product of duodenal Brunner glands. Pepsinogen is secreted by gastric chief cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal cardiac glands); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal cardiac glands produce neutral mucus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: when reflux overwhelms distal protection, GERD can lead to Barrett metaplasia (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: heartburn reflects acid and pepsin injury of the distal mucosa.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — reflux injury and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the esophageal glands proper secrete a slightly acidic, viscous mucus. What is the main function of this secretion?","choices":{"A":"Neutralizing refluxed acid","B":"Lubricating the lumen","C":"Digesting dietary starch","D":"Absorbing swallowed water","E":"Secreting immunoglobulin A"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The slightly acidic mucus of the esophageal glands proper lubricates the luminal surface so boluses pass smoothly and the squamous lining is protected from mechanical injury. Neutralizing refluxed acid is the role of the neutral mucus from esophageal cardiac glands near the stomach. Starch digestion begins with salivary amylase, not esophageal mucus. The esophagus does not absorb swallowed water. IgA is secreted by plasma cells and transported across epithelium, not produced by these glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, secretion type and function); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (mucus of glands proper lubricates the luminal wall).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal mucosa is protective, not absorptive (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: impaired lubrication contributes to the sensation of food sticking.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — reflux injury and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman who takes daily naproxen for arthritis has epigastric pain. Endoscopic biopsy of a gastric lesion shows that the tissue loss has crossed the muscularis mucosae and ends in dense irregular connective tissue containing large vessels. Which layer forms the base of this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Epithelium","B":"Lamina propria","C":"Adventitia","D":"Mesothelium","E":"Submucosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dense irregular connective tissue with large blood and lymphatic vessels just deep to the muscularis mucosae is the submucosa. A defect that reaches it is an ulcer rather than an erosion. The epithelium and lamina propria lie above the muscularis mucosae, so a lesion ending there would be an erosion. The adventitia and the mesothelium of the serosa lie outside the muscularis externa, much deeper than this lesion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, submucosa); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (submucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: erosions are confined to the mucosa; ulcers extend into the submucosa or muscularis (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: NSAIDs cause gastric ulcers by reducing prostaglandins.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man has squamous cell carcinoma of the mid-thoracic esophagus. CT shows the tumor extending directly into the surrounding mediastinal fat. Absence of which layer in this segment of the esophageal wall helps explain this spread?","choices":{"A":"Serosa","B":"Mucosa","C":"Submucosa","D":"Adventitia","E":"Epithelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The thoracic esophagus has no serosa; its outer coat is adventitia, which blends into the mediastinal connective tissue, so there is no mesothelial boundary between the tumor and the mediastinum. Serosa covers only the short abdominal segment. The mucosa, submucosa, and epithelium are present in the thoracic esophagus, and the tumor arises from its epithelium. Adventitia is present, not absent, and it is the coat the tumor crosses. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, adventitia of the thoracic esophagus); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal adventitia); First Aid 2025 GI (esophageal cancer: lack of serosa in the esophageal wall allows rapid extension).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma involves the upper 2/3 of the esophagus and adenocarcinoma the lower 1/3 (First Aid 2025 GI esophageal cancer). COMLEX: smoking and alcohol are leading risks for squamous cell carcinoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 5 — anatomic distribution of esophageal squamous cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman undergoes small-bowel biopsy for bloating. The pathologist points to a thin band of smooth muscle at the base of the crypts that separates the mucosa from the submucosa. Contraction of this band has which effect?","choices":{"A":"Propulsive peristalsis of chyme","B":"Shortening of the bowel segment","C":"Local ridges in the inner lining","D":"Closure of the ileocecal valve","E":"Constriction of the full bowel lumen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The band is the muscularis mucosae. Its local contractions raise folds and ridges of the mucosa that improve contact with chyme for absorption and secretion, independent of whole-organ peristalsis. Propulsive peristalsis, shortening of the segment, and constriction of the whole lumen come from the outer longitudinal and inner circular layers of the muscularis externa. Closure of the ileocecal valve depends on sphincteric circular muscle of the muscularis externa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, muscularis mucosae); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (muscularis mucosae).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the submucosal plexus controls the muscularis mucosae, local secretion, and blood flow (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: mucosal movement aids contact between chyme and the absorptive surface.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman has chronic diarrhea and iron deficiency anemia. Duodenal biopsy shows flattened mucosa with loss of the finger-like projections that contain central lacteals. Loss of these projections impairs which function?","choices":{"A":"Acid neutralization","B":"Pathogen sampling","C":"Peristaltic propulsion","D":"Nutrient absorption","E":"Pepsinogen secretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The finger-like projections are villi, cores of lamina propria with capillaries and a central lacteal covered by enterocytes. With plicae circulares and microvilli, they amplify absorptive surface area, so villous atrophy impairs nutrient absorption. Acid neutralization in the duodenum depends on Brunner gland and pancreatic bicarbonate. Pathogen sampling is the role of Peyer patches and M cells. Peristaltic propulsion depends on the muscularis externa. Pepsinogen is secreted by gastric chief cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, absorption and amplification specializations); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (villi).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytosis, mostly in the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum (First Aid 2025 GI celiac disease). COMLEX: iron deficiency reflects duodenal malabsorption.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — celiac disease morphology and malabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with long-standing heartburn undergoes endoscopy. Biopsy of the distal esophagus shows columnar epithelium with goblet cells replacing the normal lining. The replaced lining was specialized for which function?","choices":{"A":"Absorbing nutrients and water","B":"Secreting acid and pepsinogen","C":"Resisting mechanical abrasion","D":"Sampling luminal antigens","E":"Secreting antimicrobial peptides"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The normal esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, whose layered cells resist mechanical abrasion from food boluses. Here it has been replaced by intestinal-type columnar epithelium, which is Barrett esophagus. Absorbing nutrients and water is the role of intestinal enterocytes. Secreting acid and pepsinogen is the role of gastric parietal and chief cells. Sampling luminal antigens is done by M cells over Peyer patches. Secreting antimicrobial peptides is the role of Paneth cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, protection, and section 5, cardiac glands and Barrett esophagus); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal epithelium).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Barrett esophagus is intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells replacing nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium in the distal esophagus, a precursor of adenocarcinoma (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: chronic GERD is the driving exposure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — GERD and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman who takes high-dose aspirin develops gastric erosions. The drug reduces prostaglandin-driven secretion by the cells that line the gastric surface and pits. Loss of their secretion impairs which protective mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Killing microbes with lysozyme","B":"Neutralizing acid at the epithelium","C":"Resisting abrasion with keratin","D":"Absorbing acid through tight junctions","E":"Trapping antigens in Peyer patches"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Surface mucous cells line the gastric surface and pits and secrete a viscous mucus rich in bicarbonate that neutralizes acid at the epithelium and slows pepsin. Prostaglandins sustain this secretion, so aspirin weakens the barrier. Killing microbes with lysozyme is a Paneth cell function in the intestine. Gastric epithelium is columnar and not keratinized. Tight junctions seal the epithelium against back-diffusion of acid rather than absorbing it. Peyer patches are lymphoid aggregates of the ileum. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, surface mucous cells and alkaline mucus layer); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (surface mucous cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs inhibit COX and reduce prostaglandin-dependent mucus and bicarbonate secretion, causing erosions and ulcers (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: misoprostol is used to prevent NSAID-induced ulcers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — gastric mucosal barrier and its disruption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-day-old boy has not passed meconium and has abdominal distension. Full-thickness rectal biopsy shows no ganglion cells between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers or in the submucosa. Loss of the ganglia between the muscle layers impairs which function?","choices":{"A":"Local secretion by mucosal glands","B":"Regulation of submucosal blood flow","C":"Coordinated propulsive motility","D":"Absorption of nutrients by enterocytes","E":"Antigen sampling by Peyer patches"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ganglia between the circular and longitudinal layers form the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus, which controls tone, rhythmic contraction, and conduction of peristaltic waves and relaxes sphincters. Without it the aganglionic segment stays contracted and does not propel stool. Local secretion and submucosal blood flow are mainly regulated by the submucosal (Meissner) plexus. Absorption by enterocytes is an epithelial function. Antigen sampling is a lymphoid function. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, myenteric plexus function); First Aid 2025 GI (Hirschsprung disease; suction biopsy, which samples mucosa and submucosa, is the usual first test).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hirschsprung disease is absent ganglion cells in both plexuses of the distal colon from failed neural crest migration; diagnosed by rectal suction biopsy; associated with Down syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: empty rectum with explosive stool on rectal exam.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has progressive dysphagia to both solids and liquids. Barium swallow shows a dilated esophagus with a narrow distal segment, and manometry shows a lower sphincter that does not relax with swallowing. Loss of which neuronal products in the myenteric plexus explains the sphincter finding?","choices":{"A":"Acetylcholine and tachykinins","B":"Serotonin and histamine","C":"Norepinephrine and neuropeptide Y","D":"Gastrin and somatostatin","E":"Nitric oxide and VIP"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This is achalasia. Inhibitory neurons of the myenteric plexus release nitric oxide and VIP to relax the lower esophageal sphincter; their loss leaves the sphincter contracted. Acetylcholine and tachykinins are excitatory and their loss would weaken contraction. Serotonin and histamine are mucosal paracrine signals. Norepinephrine and neuropeptide Y are released by extrinsic sympathetic fibers, not by the inhibitory myenteric neurons. Gastrin and somatostatin are gastric endocrine and paracrine products. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, sphincter control); First Aid 2025 GI (achalasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: achalasia shows bird beak narrowing, increased LES pressure, and absent peristalsis; secondary causes include Chagas disease (First Aid 2025 GI achalasia). COMLEX: treatment includes surgery or botulinum toxin injection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — achalasia and patterns of dysphagia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man receives a small-bowel transplant, which severs all extrinsic vagal and sympathetic nerves to the graft. Within days the graft shows coordinated peristalsis and local secretion. Which explains this function?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic enteric plexuses remain","B":"Sympathetic fibers regenerate","C":"Vagal fibers regrow into the graft","D":"Smooth muscle needs no neurons","E":"Hormones replace neural control"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The enteric nervous system is an intrinsic network of neurons and glia in two plexuses inside the gut wall. The myenteric plexus coordinates peristalsis and the submucosal plexus controls local secretion, and both work without extrinsic input, which only modulates them. Severed sympathetic and vagal fibers do not regenerate into a graft within days. Smooth muscle pacing alone does not coordinate peristalsis. Hormones modulate gut function but do not replace neural reflex circuits. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, enteric nervous system and extrinsic modulation, and section 4, intrinsic autonomy); Guyton Ch. 63 (enteric nervous system).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the enteric nervous system can function independently of extrinsic nerves, which modulate it (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: parasympathetic input increases and sympathetic input decreases peristalsis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — extrinsic modulation of the enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman has a brainstem stroke with hoarseness and difficulty initiating swallowing. Manometry shows weak contractions in the upper esophagus with normal peristalsis in the distal esophagus. Which tissue in the upper esophagus explains this selective weakness?","choices":{"A":"Visceral smooth muscle","B":"Abundant myenteric neurons","C":"Serosal covering","D":"Striated skeletal muscle","E":"Cardiac gland mucosa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The upper third of the esophagus is striated skeletal muscle supplied by somatic motor neurons from the nucleus ambiguus, which a brainstem stroke can damage. The distal esophagus is smooth muscle driven by the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus and the myenteric plexus, which can keep running on its own, so distal peristalsis is preserved. Visceral smooth muscle is the distal tissue, the one spared. Myenteric neurons are not selectively abundant in the upper third. The thoracic esophagus has adventitia, not serosa. Cardiac glands are mucosal glands and do not contract. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, muscle type transitions and innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: upper 1/3 striated, middle 1/3 mixed, lower 1/3 smooth muscle (Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 2, section 4; Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: oropharyngeal dysphagia points to striated muscle or its brainstem control.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — neural coordination of swallowing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with Raynaud phenomenon and sclerodactyly has heartburn and dysphagia. Manometry shows absent contractions in the distal esophagus with low lower sphincter pressure and normal proximal contractions. Which tissue is affected?","choices":{"A":"Striated muscle","B":"Pharyngeal constrictors","C":"Diaphragmatic crura","D":"Cricopharyngeus","E":"Smooth muscle"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Scleroderma causes atrophy and fibrosis of esophageal smooth muscle, which makes up the distal esophagus and the lower sphincter, so distal peristalsis and sphincter tone fail. Striated muscle of the proximal third is spared, which fits the normal proximal contractions. The pharyngeal constrictors and cricopharyngeus are striated muscles above or at the upper esophagus. The diaphragmatic crura reinforce the sphincter externally but are skeletal muscle and not the site of disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, muscle type transitions); First Aid 2025 GI (scleroderma esophageal dysmotility).","boardPrep":"Step 1: scleroderma causes esophageal smooth muscle atrophy with decreased LES pressure and distal dysmotility, leading to reflux and dysphagia; part of CREST (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: GERD from scleroderma can cause strictures and Barrett esophagus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — esophageal motility disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has injury to both vagal trunks during a thoracic operation. Afterward, balloon distension of the distal esophagus still triggers peristaltic waves in that segment. What mediates these waves?","choices":{"A":"Nucleus ambiguus output","B":"Myenteric plexus reflexes","C":"Glossopharyngeal afferents","D":"Phrenic nerve reflexes","E":"Sympathetic celiac outflow"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When vagal fibers to the distal esophagus are interrupted, the intrinsic myenteric plexus can still start secondary peristaltic waves in response to local distension. The nucleus ambiguus sends its output through the vagus, which is injured, and it supplies striated muscle. Glossopharyngeal afferents trigger the pharyngeal phase of swallowing, not local esophageal reflexes. The phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm. Sympathetic celiac outflow suppresses esophageal motility. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, intrinsic autonomy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary peristalsis is mediated by the enteric nervous system and begins at the point of distension (Costanzo Ch. 8; Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 6, secondary peristalsis). COMLEX: intrinsic reflexes keep some motility after vagal injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — primary versus secondary esophageal peristalsis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man with occasional heartburn has biopsies taken just above the gastroesophageal junction. The pathologist notes branched tubular mucous glands in the lamina propria that resemble gastric cardiac glands. What is the main function of these glands?","choices":{"A":"Protecting against refluxed acid","B":"Lubricating the upper esophagus","C":"Secreting acid into the lumen","D":"Secreting pepsinogen for digestion","E":"Absorbing fluid from the bolus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"These are esophageal cardiac glands. Their neutral mucus protects the distal esophageal mucosa from regurgitated acidic gastric juice; when reflux overwhelms them, heartburn and GERD follow. Lubricating the upper esophagus is the role of the esophageal glands proper in the submucosa, which are concentrated in the upper half. Acid is secreted by gastric parietal cells and pepsinogen by chief cells, not by these mucous glands. The esophagus does not absorb fluid from the bolus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal cardiac glands); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal cardiac glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GERD complications include erosive esophagitis, strictures, and Barrett esophagus (First Aid 2025 GI GERD). COMLEX: heartburn reflects acid and pepsin contact with distal mucosa.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — reflux injury of the distal esophagus","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman has a section of mid-esophagus examined after surgery. The slide shows small tubuloalveolar mucous glands in the dense connective tissue beneath the muscularis mucosae, with a dilated duct lined by stratified squamous epithelium. Which gland is this?","choices":{"A":"Esophageal cardiac gland","B":"Esophageal gland proper","C":"Duodenal submucosal gland","D":"Gastric cardiac gland","E":"Minor salivary gland"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Small compound tubuloalveolar glands in the submucosa with conspicuous dilated ducts lined by stratified squamous epithelium are esophageal glands proper. Esophageal cardiac glands lie in the lamina propria near the gastroesophageal junction and drain through short columnar-lined ducts. Duodenal submucosal (Brunner) glands lie in the duodenum and secrete alkaline mucus. Gastric cardiac glands lie in the stomach mucosa. Minor salivary glands lie in the oral cavity. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, morphology of esophageal glands proper); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal glands proper, Fig. 17.4).","boardPrep":"Step 1: submucosal glands in the esophagus and duodenum (Brunner) are a histology identifier (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: gland layer and duct lining identify the gland type.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 5 — esophageal versus gastric glands","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has endoscopy for intermittent dysphagia. Small cysts are found in the upper esophagus, and biopsy shows dilated submucosal ducts lined by stratified squamous epithelium and filled with mucus. Which property of the secretion favors these cysts?","choices":{"A":"High bicarbonate content","B":"Enzymatic digestion of ducts","C":"Neutral pH of the mucus","D":"High viscosity of the mucus","E":"Serous watery composition"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ducts belong to esophageal glands proper, which secrete a viscous, slightly acidic mucus. Its viscosity favors transient cysts in the ducts. High bicarbonate content is typical of Brunner gland and pancreatic secretion. These glands make mucus, not enzymes that digest ducts. Neutral mucus is the product of the cardiac glands, not the glands proper. A serous watery secretion would flow freely and is not what these glands make. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal glands proper); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (viscous secretion; transient cysts in the ducts).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal epithelium is nonkeratinized stratified squamous (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: esophageal glands proper are most concentrated in the upper half of the esophagus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — structural causes of dysphagia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":3,"stem":"A 56-year-old man has free air under the diaphragm after an anterior gastric ulcer perforates through the outermost coat of the stomach wall. Which tissue lines that coat?","choices":{"A":"Simple columnar epithelium","B":"Stratified squamous epithelium","C":"Simple squamous mesothelium","D":"Dense irregular connective tissue","E":"Transitional epithelium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The stomach is intraperitoneal, so its outermost coat is a serosa, which consists of a simple squamous mesothelium over a thin layer of connective tissue. Simple columnar epithelium lines the gastric lumen. Stratified squamous epithelium lines the esophagus. Dense irregular connective tissue forms the submucosa and the adventitia of fixed segments. Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, serosa is a simple squamous mesothelium, lines 336–337); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (serosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcers can extend into the submucosa and muscular layers; erosions stay in the mucosa (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: perforation causes peritonitis with free air.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — complications of peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":3,"stem":"An 18-month-old boy develops an ileocolic intussusception a week after a viral illness. The lead point is enlarged lymphoid tissue in the wall of the distal ileum. What is this lymphoid tissue called?","choices":{"A":"Brunner glands","B":"Peyer patches","C":"Paneth cells","D":"Plicae circulares","E":"Crypts of Lieberkühn"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Peyer patches are aggregated lymphatic nodules in the lamina propria and submucosa of the distal ileum, part of the gut-associated lymphatic tissue that samples and neutralizes ingested pathogens. Brunner glands are submucosal bicarbonate-secreting glands of the duodenum. Paneth cells are crypt-base cells that secrete lysozyme and defensins. Plicae circulares are transverse folds of mucosa and submucosa. Crypts of Lieberkühn are intestinal glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, immune barrier); First Aid 2025 GI (Peyer patches).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peyer patches are unencapsulated lymphoid tissue in the lamina propria and submucosa of the ileum, and their B cells make IgA (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: Peyer patch hypertrophy can act as an intussusception lead point in young children (First Aid 2025 GI intussusception).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 5 — GALT and Peyer patches","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":3,"stem":"A 51-year-old man from rural Brazil has progressive dysphagia, and serology is positive for Trypanosoma cruzi. Biopsy shows destruction of the neurons between the muscle layers of the distal esophagus. Which function is lost?","choices":{"A":"LES relaxation","B":"Gland secretion","C":"Mucosal blood flow","D":"Bolus lubrication","E":"Gastric acid output"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chagas disease destroys neurons of the myenteric plexus, including the inhibitory NO and VIP neurons that relax the lower esophageal sphincter, producing secondary achalasia. Gland secretion and mucosal blood flow are mainly controlled by the submucosal plexus. Bolus lubrication comes from the esophageal glands proper. Gastric acid output is controlled at the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, myenteric plexus and sphincter control); First Aid 2025 GI (achalasia and Chagas disease).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary achalasia can result from Chagas disease (First Aid 2025 GI achalasia). COMLEX: barium swallow shows bird beak narrowing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — achalasia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has an esophagectomy, and a section from the upper third shows cross-striated fibers in the muscularis externa. What innervates this muscle?","choices":{"A":"Preganglionic vagal fibers","B":"Sympathetic celiac fibers","C":"Glossopharyngeal fibers","D":"Vagal branchial efferents","E":"Phrenic motor fibers"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Striated muscle in the upper third is supplied by motor neurons of the nucleus ambiguus whose axons travel in the vagus and end on motor endplates; the lecture calls them somatic motor neurons, and standard texts classify them as branchial or special visceral efferents. Preganglionic vagal fibers from the dorsal motor nucleus synapse in the myenteric plexus to control the smooth muscle of the distal esophagus. Sympathetic celiac fibers supply smooth muscle and vessels and suppress motility. Glossopharyngeal fibers serve the pharynx. Phrenic fibers supply the diaphragm. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, innervation of striated musculature); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal muscularis externa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the upper esophagus is striated muscle and the lower esophagus smooth muscle (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: oropharyngeal dysphagia points to striated muscle or its control.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — swallowing coordination","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":3,"stem":"A 69-year-old man has nightly heartburn from acid reflux. The mucous glands in the lamina propria just above the gastroesophageal junction normally limit this injury. Which glands are these?","choices":{"A":"Esophageal glands proper","B":"Gastric fundic oxyntic glands","C":"Duodenal Brunner glands","D":"Minor salivary glands","E":"Esophageal cardiac glands"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Esophageal cardiac glands lie in the lamina propria of the terminal esophagus and secrete neutral mucus that protects against refluxed acid; when reflux overwhelms them, heartburn and GERD develop. Esophageal glands proper lie in the submucosa, are concentrated in the upper half, and lubricate the lumen. Gastric fundic (oxyntic) glands secrete acid and pepsinogen. Duodenal Brunner glands lie in the duodenal submucosa. Minor salivary glands lie in the oral cavity. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal cardiac glands); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal cardiac glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal cardiac glands lie in the lamina propria near the esophagogastric junction (lecture SDL 2). COMLEX: reflux symptoms worsen when lying flat.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — reflux injury","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":3,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy has his gallbladder removed for pigment stones from a hemolytic anemia. Which epithelium lines the mucosa of the resected organ?","choices":{"A":"Simple columnar with microvilli","B":"Simple cuboidal epithelium","C":"Stratified squamous epithelium","D":"Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium","E":"Transitional epithelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Simple columnar epithelium with microvilli lines the small and large intestines and the gallbladder. Simple cuboidal epithelium is not the gallbladder lining. Stratified squamous epithelium lines the oral cavity, esophagus, and lower anal canal. Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium lines the airway. Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, absorption: simple columnar epithelium with microvilli). The airway and urinary linings are not in the lecture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: simple columnar cells of the large intestine mainly resorb water and electrolytes (lecture SDL 2). COMLEX: gallbladder epithelium concentrates bile (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — gallbladder wall histology","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":3,"title":"SDL 3 — Histology of the Foregut part 2","questions":[{"id":"sdl03_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, in which region of the stomach do the gastric pits extend through about half the thickness of the mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Cardiac region","B":"Fundic region","C":"Esophagogastric junction","D":"Pyloric region","E":"Duodenal bulb"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pyloric region has deep gastric pits that occupy about half of the mucosal thickness, opening onto branched, coiled pyloric glands with wide lumina. The cardiac region has relatively shallow pits over tubular mucous glands. The fundic region has shallow pits, about one-fourth to one-third of the mucosal thickness, with long fundic glands below. The esophagogastric junction is lined by squamous epithelium on its esophageal side and lacks gastric pits there. The duodenal bulb has villi and crypts rather than gastric pits. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, pyloric region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pyloric glands empty into deep gastric pits that occupy about half the thickness of the mucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: G cells in the antrum and duodenum secrete gastrin (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: pit depth and gland shape identify the gastric region on a slide.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — gastrin, histamine, and somatostatin regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":1,"stem":"A histology student examines glands that surround the esophageal orifice of the stomach. Which cell type predominates in these glands?","choices":{"A":"Mucous gland cells","B":"Zymogenic chief cells","C":"Parietal cells","D":"Enteroendocrine G cells","E":"Paneth cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Glands around the esophageal orifice are cardiac glands, tubular and somewhat tortuous glands made up mostly of mucus-secreting cells with flattened basal nuclei and pale apical cytoplasm. Chief cells predominate in the base of fundic glands. Parietal cells are most numerous in the neck of fundic glands and appear only occasionally at the cardiac boundary. G cells are concentrated in the pyloric glands. Paneth cells sit at the base of intestinal crypts. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, cardiac region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (cardiac glands are composed of mucus-secreting cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Barrett metaplasia arises in the distal esophagus just above the gastroesophageal junction (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: mucous cardiac glands resemble the esophageal cardiac glands.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — gastrin, histamine, and somatostatin regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, which cell type predominates in the deep base of the fundic glands?","choices":{"A":"Parietal cells","B":"Mucous neck cells","C":"Chief cells","D":"Surface mucous cells","E":"Stem cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chief cells are concentrated in the base of fundic glands. Parietal cells are most numerous in the neck and upper part of the gland. Mucous neck cells are localized to the neck, among the parietal cells. Surface mucous cells line the surface and the gastric pits. Stem cells lie in the isthmus, between the pit and the gland. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, fundic region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (fundic glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chief cells secrete pepsinogen, which is activated to pepsin by acid (First Aid 2025 GI GI secretory products). COMLEX: chief cells are basophilic on H&E.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — gastrin, histamine, and somatostatin regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, which gastric epithelial cell secretes intrinsic factor?","choices":{"A":"Chief cells","B":"Mucous neck cells","C":"Surface mucous cells","D":"ECL cells","E":"Parietal cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor, a 44-kDa glycoprotein that binds vitamin B12 for absorption in the distal ileum. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and gastric lipase. Mucous neck cells secrete a soluble mucus that lubricates the gland lumen. Surface mucous cells secrete the insoluble alkaline mucus coat. ECL cells release histamine that stimulates parietal cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, parietal cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (intrinsic factor is a 44-kDa glycoprotein).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells make gastric acid and intrinsic factor; autoimmune destruction of parietal cells causes pernicious anemia (First Aid 2025 GI GI secretory products). COMLEX: B12 absorption requires intrinsic factor and an intact terminal ileum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion through the H+/K+-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":1,"stem":"In H&E sections of fundic glands, chief cells show strongly basophilic basal cytoplasm. Which cellular feature produces this staining?","choices":{"A":"Abundant mitochondria","B":"Intracellular canaliculi","C":"Apical mucin granules","D":"Abundant rough ER","E":"Tubulovesicular membranes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chief cells have the morphology of protein-secreting cells: a basal nucleus, basal cytoplasm packed with rough endoplasmic reticulum whose ribosomal RNA stains basophilic, and apical zymogen granules. Abundant mitochondria, intracellular canaliculi, and a tubulovesicular membrane system are features of parietal cells and produce their eosinophilic staining. Apical mucin granules belong to surface mucous cells and appear pale on H&E. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, chief cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (chief cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells are eosinophilic (pink) and chief cells are basophilic (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: staining pattern reflects the dominant organelle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion through the H+/K+-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":1,"stem":"Chief cells secrete pepsinogen as an inactive proenzyme. Which condition in the gastric lumen converts it to active pepsin?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin cleavage","B":"Enterokinase action","C":"Acidic pH below 5","D":"Bile salt binding","E":"Alkaline pH above 8"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pepsinogen undergoes autocatalytic cleavage to pepsin on contact with acidic gastric juice at a pH below 5, with an optimum around pH 1.8 to 3.5; pepsin is an endopeptidase that starts protein digestion. Trypsin cleaves pancreatic zymogens in the duodenum, and enterokinase activates trypsinogen to trypsin. Bile salts emulsify fat rather than activate proteases. An alkaline pH inactivates pepsin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, chief cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pepsinogen and pepsin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pepsinogen is converted to pepsin in the presence of H+ (First Aid 2025 GI GI secretory products). COMLEX: acid suppression reduces gastric pepsin activity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion through the H+/K+-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":1,"stem":"In autoimmune atrophic gastritis, what is the principal reason that even crystalline oral vitamin B12 is malabsorbed?","choices":{"A":"Loss of gastric acid","B":"Loss of intrinsic factor","C":"Loss of bile secretion","D":"Loss of ileal receptors","E":"Loss of pancreatic proteases"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Parietal cells are the gastric source of intrinsic factor. When autoimmune destruction removes them, B12 does not form the B12 and intrinsic factor complex needed for receptor-mediated uptake in the distal ileum, which produces pernicious anemia. Crystalline B12 is not bound to food protein, so loss of gastric acid, which with pepsin frees dietary B12, does not explain its malabsorption. Bile secretion is unaffected by gastritis and does not carry B12 to the ileum. The ileal receptors are intact in this disease. Pancreatic proteases are not affected by gastritis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, parietal cell loss and vitamin B12 malabsorption); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pernicious anemia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune gastritis has antibodies to the parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase and to intrinsic factor, affects the body and fundus, and raises the risk of pernicious anemia (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: macrocytic anemia with neurologic signs suggests B12 deficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis and pernicious anemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":1,"stem":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis causes achlorhydria and markedly elevated serum gastrin. What causes the gastrin elevation?","choices":{"A":"Direct antibody stimulation of G cells","B":"Increased vagal drive to G cells","C":"Reduced renal gastrin clearance","D":"Gastrin release by ECL cells","E":"Loss of acid feedback on G cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Luminal acid normally provides negative feedback on antral G cells. With parietal cells destroyed, the antral lumen is no longer acidic, the feedback is lost, and G cells secrete gastrin continuously, which leads to G cell and ECL cell hyperplasia. The autoantibodies target parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase and intrinsic factor, not G cells. Vagal drive is not the mechanism of the elevation. Renal clearance is not reduced in this disease. ECL cells release histamine, not gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, achlorhydria and hypergastrinemia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic atrophic gastritis causes hypochlorhydria and hypergastrinemia, with risk of gastric cancer (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: hypergastrinemia with achlorhydria points away from gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis and pernicious anemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with chronic atrophic gastritis has lost gastric pepsin activity, yet protein digestion and absorption remain adequate. What explains this preserved digestion?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic proteases","B":"Salivary proteases","C":"Gastric lipase activity","D":"Colonic bacterial enzymes","E":"Intestinal pepsin secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancreatic proteases, including trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidases, work at neutral to alkaline pH in the duodenum and hydrolyze dietary protein into absorbable peptides and amino acids, so loss of gastric pepsin is compensated. Saliva contains amylase and lingual lipase rather than significant proteases. Gastric lipase digests fat. Colonic bacteria act after the main site of protein absorption. The intestine does not secrete pepsin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, chief cell dysfunction and protein digestion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: trypsinogen is activated by enterokinase and then activates the other pancreatic zymogens (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic secretions). COMLEX: protein malabsorption suggests pancreatic insufficiency rather than gastric loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis and pernicious anemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":1,"stem":"The unstirred layer beneath the gastric mucus gel stays near pH 7 while the lumen is near pH 1. Which component chiefly creates this alkaline microenvironment?","choices":{"A":"Tight junction sealing of cells","B":"Prostaglandin vasodilation","C":"Epithelial bicarbonate secretion","D":"Pepsin inactivation by the mucus gel","E":"Rapid epithelial restitution"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Surface epithelial cells secrete bicarbonate into the fluid trapped beneath the mucus gel, creating an unstirred alkaline layer with a surface pH near 7 that neutralizes back-diffusing hydrogen ions and inactivates pepsin. Tight junctions prevent back-diffusion into the lamina propria but do not alkalinize the surface. Prostaglandins stimulate mucus and bicarbonate and maintain blood flow, but the alkaline layer itself is made by bicarbonate. Pepsin inactivation by the mucus gel is a result of the alkaline layer, not its cause. Restitution repairs defects. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, epithelial bicarbonate secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PGE2 increases mucus and bicarbonate secretion and decreases acid; NSAIDs remove this protection (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: misoprostol restores prostaglandin protection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori and NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":1,"stem":"Nonselective NSAIDs injure the gastric mucosa mainly by inhibiting cyclooxygenase. Which change follows?","choices":{"A":"Increased gastrin release","B":"Reduced PGE2 synthesis","C":"Urease ammonia production","D":"Blocked H2 receptors","E":"Direct vagal stimulation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cyclooxygenase inhibition suppresses PGE2 synthesis, which decreases bicarbonate and mucus secretion, reduces mucosal blood flow, and impairs restitution. Increased gastrin release follows loss of acid feedback, not COX inhibition. Urease ammonia production is a mechanism of H. pylori. Blocked H2 receptors would reduce acid and protect the mucosa. Direct vagal stimulation of acid occurs with brain injury in Cushing ulcer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, NSAIDs and aspirin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs reduce PGE2, decreasing gastric mucosal protection; Curling ulcer follows burns and Cushing ulcer follows brain injury (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: co-prescribe misoprostol or a PPI for high-risk NSAID users.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori and NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":1,"stem":"Within minutes of a superficial gastric epithelial injury, the defect is sealed without new cell division. Which process accomplishes this?","choices":{"A":"Migration of neck cells","B":"Rapid stem cell mitosis","C":"Fibroblast scar formation","D":"Mucus plug secretion","E":"Platelet clot formation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Restitution seals small epithelial defects as gland neck cells migrate along the basal lamina to restore continuity, without requiring immediate mitosis. Stem cell mitosis renews surface cells over days, not minutes. Fibroblast scarring follows deeper injury such as ulcers. Mucus slows pepsin and abrasion but does not close the epithelial gap. Platelet clotting stops bleeding but does not restore epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, epithelial restitution and continuous cell renewal).","boardPrep":"Step 1: erosions are limited to the mucosa and heal without scarring; ulcers extend deeper (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: rapid restitution limits superficial injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori and NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium of the esophagus changes abruptly at the esophagogastric junction to which epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Stratified columnar","B":"Pseudostratified columnar","C":"Simple cuboidal","D":"Simple columnar","E":"Transitional"},"correct":"D","explanation":"At the esophagogastric junction the stratified squamous lining changes abruptly to the simple columnar epithelium of the stomach, made up of surface mucous cells. Stratified columnar epithelium lines some large ducts. Pseudostratified columnar epithelium lines the airway. Simple cuboidal epithelium lines small ducts and the thyroid follicles. Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, lining epithelium); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophagogastric junction).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Barrett esophagus replaces squamous epithelium with intestinal columnar epithelium and goblet cells (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: the squamocolumnar junction is the endoscopic landmark for metaplasia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — Barrett metaplasia at the esophagogastric junction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":1,"stem":"The muscularis externa of the stomach differs from that of the esophagus by an additional layer. Which layer is this?","choices":{"A":"Outer circular layer","B":"Inner oblique layer","C":"Striated upper layer","D":"Middle longitudinal layer","E":"Submucosal muscle layer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The stomach has three smooth muscle layers: an innermost oblique layer deep to the circular layer, an inner circular layer, and an outer longitudinal layer. The esophagus has two layers, inner circular and outer longitudinal. The outer layer of both organs is longitudinal, not circular. The striated muscle of the upper esophagus is absent from the stomach. There is no middle longitudinal layer or submucosal muscle layer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, muscularis externa); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (stomach muscularis externa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach adds an innermost oblique layer deep to the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers (Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 3, section 5; Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: oblique fibers aid churning of chyme.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — muscle type transitions along the esophagus","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":1,"stem":"Unlike the esophagus, the stomach has no submucosal glands. In which layer do all gastric glands lie?","choices":{"A":"Submucosa","B":"Muscularis mucosae","C":"Adventitia","D":"Muscularis externa","E":"Lamina propria"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gastric glands are mucosal, confined to the lamina propria and opening into the bases of gastric pits; they include cardiac, fundic, and pyloric glands. The submucosa holds esophageal glands proper and duodenal Brunner glands, not gastric glands. The muscularis mucosae lies beneath the gland bases. The adventitia and muscularis externa are outer layers without glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, glands); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (gastric mucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: submucosal glands identify the esophagus and the duodenum (Brunner glands) on a slide (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: gland layer separates esophageal from gastric biopsies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 5 — esophageal glands proper and cardiac glands","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with dyspepsia has an endoscopic biopsy labeled gastric body. The section shows deep pits occupying half of the mucosa, coiled glands with wide lumina lined by mucous cells, and many gastrin-producing cells. Where was the biopsy most likely taken?","choices":{"A":"Gastric fundus","B":"Gastric body","C":"Cardiac region","D":"Pyloric antrum","E":"Duodenal bulb"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Deep pits occupying about half of the mucosa, branched coiled glands with wide lumina, mucous lining cells, and abundant G cells define the pyloric region in the antrum. The fundus and body share fundic glands with shallow pits and many parietal and chief cells. The cardiac region has shallow pits over tubular mucous glands without concentrated G cells. The duodenal bulb has villi and submucosal Brunner glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, pyloric region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pyloric glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyloric glands are lined mainly by mucus secreting cells (lecture SDL 3). COMLEX: H. pylori gastritis affects the antrum first.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — source and release triggers of gastrin","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman with vitiligo has fatigue and macrocytic anemia. Serum antibodies against the parietal cell proton pump are present. Which gastric region is the main target of this destruction?","choices":{"A":"Fundus and body","B":"Antrum and pylorus","C":"Cardia and esophagus","D":"Duodenal bulb","E":"Lesser curvature"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Parietal cells are concentrated in the fundic glands of the fundus and body, so autoimmune gastritis targets these regions. The antrum and pylorus contain pyloric glands with few parietal cells and are spared; H. pylori gastritis begins there instead. The cardia has mucous cardiac glands, and the esophagus has squamous mucosa. The duodenal bulb is intestinal mucosa. The lesser curvature is an anatomic margin, not a gland region. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, fundic region, and section 3, parietal cell loss); First Aid 2025 GI (autoimmune gastritis affects the body and fundus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune gastritis affects the body and fundus; H. pylori affects the antrum first and spreads to the body (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: autoimmune disease clusters, including vitiligo and thyroiditis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis anatomic distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man undergoes antrectomy for a bleeding ulcer. Afterward his serum gastrin falls well below his preoperative value. Removal of which cells explains this change?","choices":{"A":"Fundic ECL cells","B":"Pyloric G cells","C":"Cardiac mucous cells","D":"Fundic chief cells","E":"Duodenal S cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastrin-producing G cells are concentrated in the pyloric glands of the antrum, so antrectomy removes the main source of gastrin. Fundic ECL cells release histamine and remain after antrectomy. Cardiac mucous cells secrete mucus. Fundic chief cells secrete pepsinogen. Duodenal S cells secrete secretin, not gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, pyloric region, and section 2, enteroendocrine cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pyloric glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin is released by G cells in the antrum and duodenum in response to peptides, amino acids, and gastric distension (First Aid 2025 GI GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: antrectomy reduces gastrin-driven acid.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — gastrin regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman has a gastric biopsy. In the neck of the fundic glands are large triangular cells with central nuclei and intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm full of mitochondria and canaliculi. What do these cells secrete into the lumen?","choices":{"A":"Pepsinogen and lipase","B":"Soluble neck mucus","C":"Histamine","D":"Somatostatin","E":"Hydrochloric acid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Large triangular eosinophilic cells with central nuclei, abundant mitochondria, and intracellular canaliculi in the gland neck are parietal cells. Their apical H+/K+-ATPase secretes hydrochloric acid, and they also secrete intrinsic factor. Pepsinogen and lipase come from basophilic chief cells in the gland base. Soluble neck mucus comes from mucous neck cells. Histamine is a paracrine product of ECL cells, and somatostatin of D cells, released toward the lamina propria rather than the lumen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, parietal cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (parietal cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells secrete gastric acid, stimulated by histamine, ACh, and gastrin and inhibited by somatostatin (First Aid 2025 GI GI secretory products). COMLEX: PPIs block the H+/K+-ATPase.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — cellular mechanism of HCl secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old healthy volunteer in a physiology study receives a gastrin infusion, which raises gastric acid output. Pretreatment with an H2 receptor blocker abolishes most of this rise. Gastrin acts mainly through which paracrine product?","choices":{"A":"D cell somatostatin","B":"Chief cell pepsinogen","C":"Mucous neck cell mucus","D":"ECL cell histamine","E":"Parietal cell intrinsic factor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastrin raises acid mainly by stimulating enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells in the fundic glands to release histamine, which acts on parietal cell H2 receptors; that is why H2 blockade abolishes most of the response. D cell somatostatin inhibits acid and gastrin release. Chief cell pepsinogen is a digestive proenzyme, not a paracrine signal. Mucous neck cell mucus lubricates the gland. Parietal cell intrinsic factor binds B12. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, enteroendocrine cells); First Aid 2025 GI (gastrin acts mainly through ECL cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin increases acid secretion primarily through its effects on ECL cells, leading to histamine release, rather than through its direct effect on parietal cells (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: H2 blockers reduce gastrin-driven acid.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — histamine and somatostatin control of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old woman has a gastric biopsy stained with PAS. Tall columnar cells lining the surface and pits have large apical cups that stain intensely. Their secretion contributes which component of gastric defense?","choices":{"A":"Soluble lubricating gland mucus","B":"Acid into the gland lumen","C":"Insoluble alkaline gel coat","D":"Pepsinogen for protein digestion","E":"Intrinsic factor for B12"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Tall columnar cells with PAS-positive apical mucous cups lining the surface and pits are surface mucous cells. They secrete an insoluble visible mucus rich in bicarbonate and potassium that forms a protective alkaline gel. Soluble lubricating mucus in the gland lumen comes from mucous neck cells, which lack prominent apical cups. Acid and intrinsic factor come from parietal cells. Pepsinogen comes from chief cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, surface mucous cells and mucous neck cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (surface mucous cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: prostaglandins increase gastric mucus and bicarbonate secretion (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: the mucus layer limits pepsin contact with the epithelium.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man had a total gastrectomy for cancer 6 years ago and takes no supplements. He now has numbness in both feet and decreased vibration sense, hemoglobin 9.8 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and mean corpuscular volume 112 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). Which explains his findings?","choices":{"A":"Absent gastric pepsin","B":"Ileal bacterial overgrowth","C":"Reduced folate absorption","D":"Pancreatic protease loss","E":"Absent intrinsic factor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Total gastrectomy removes all parietal cells, the gastric source of intrinsic factor. Without it, B12 is not absorbed in the distal ileum, and liver stores are depleted over several years, producing megaloblastic anemia and posterior and lateral column demyelination. Absent pepsin reduces release of B12 from food but is not the main cause, because intrinsic factor is also gone and supplements are needed even in crystalline form. Ileal bacterial overgrowth can follow gastric surgery but is not the main cause of this delayed, stores-depleted pattern. Folate deficiency causes macrocytosis without neurologic signs. Pancreatic proteases are preserved. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, parietal cell loss and vitamin B12 malabsorption).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 absorption requires intrinsic factor; deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia with subacute combined degeneration (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: give parenteral B12 after total gastrectomy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — pernicious anemia from loss of parietal cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman with antibodies to intrinsic factor has paresthesias, ataxia, and loss of position sense in both feet. Her vitamin B12 deficiency has gone untreated. Demyelination in which spinal cord region explains her neurologic signs?","choices":{"A":"Posterior and lateral columns","B":"Anterior horn motor neurons","C":"Anterior spinothalamic tracts","D":"Cerebellar vermis and flocculus","E":"Ventral nerve roots"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Severe B12 deficiency causes demyelination of the posterior and lateral columns of the spinal cord, producing sensory loss, ataxia, and weakness. Loss of position sense reflects the posterior columns, and the lateral columns carry the corticospinal tracts. Anterior horn neurons are lost in motor neuron diseases. Anterior spinothalamic tracts carry crude touch and are not the classic target. The cerebellar vermis and flocculus are brain structures, not spinal cord regions. Ventral roots carry motor fibers and are not the primary site. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, hematologic and neurologic impairment).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration (Bootcamp Gastroenterology), with demyelination of the posterior and lateral columns of the spinal cord (Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 3, section 3). COMLEX: neurologic damage may persist after treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — complications of autoimmune gastritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with long-standing autoimmune atrophic gastritis has a serum gastrin of 1,200 pg/mL (reference range: 0–100 pg/mL). Endoscopy shows small nodules in the gastric body, and biopsy shows proliferating small uniform cells with neuroendocrine granules. Which cell type is most likely proliferating?","choices":{"A":"G cells","B":"ECL cells","C":"D cells","D":"EC cells","E":"Ghrelin cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Loss of acid removes negative feedback on antral G cells, and the resulting hypergastrinemia drives hyperplasia of the gastrin-responsive ECL cells in the body, which can form small nodules. G cells also become hyperplastic, but they lie in the antrum, not in body nodules. D cells secrete somatostatin, which inhibits gastrin and acid, and are not driven by gastrin. EC cells release serotonin and are not the gastrin-responsive population named in the lecture. Ghrelin cells of the fundus modulate appetite and are not the cells that gastrin drives to proliferate. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, enteroendocrine cells, and section 3, achlorhydria and hypergastrinemia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic atrophic gastritis leads to hypochlorhydria and hypergastrinemia (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: hypergastrinemia from achlorhydria is distinguished from gastrinoma by gastric pH.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 5 — gastric neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":2,"stem":"A 37-year-old man with epigastric pain has a positive stool antigen test. Biopsy shows curved rods within the mucus over the surface foveolar cells. Which bacterial mechanism lets the organism survive at this acidic site?","choices":{"A":"Bicarbonate secretion","B":"Proton pump inhibition","C":"Gastrin receptor blockade","D":"Capsule blocking acid entry","E":"Urease generating ammonia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"H. pylori uses urease to convert urea into an ammonia cloud that buffers local acid, the basis of the urea breath test, and uses flagella to move through mucus. Bicarbonate is secreted by the host epithelium, not the bacterium. The organism does not inhibit the proton pump. The organism does not block gastrin receptors. A capsule is not its mechanism of acid survival. Its cytotoxins, such as CagA, then damage tight junctions and the mucus gel. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, Helicobacter pylori infection).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori is the most common cause of chronic gastritis, increases risk of peptic ulcer disease and MALT lymphoma, and affects the antrum first (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: urea breath test and stool antigen confirm infection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with burns over 40% of his body surface area develops upper GI bleeding on day 3. Endoscopy shows multiple superficial gastric erosions. Failure of which barrier component is most responsible?","choices":{"A":"Tight junction proteins","B":"Surface cell turnover","C":"Mucosal blood flow","D":"Mucus gel thickness","E":"Parietal cell number"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Severe burns cause hypovolemia and mucosal ischemia. Reduced blood flow lowers oxygen and bicarbonate delivery and fails to wash away back-diffused hydrogen ions, leading to local acidosis, cell death, and erosions (Curling ulcer). Tight junctions are disrupted by H. pylori toxins and aspirin. Surface cell turnover and mucus thickness fall secondarily but are not the initiating failure. Parietal cell number is unchanged. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, ischemia, shock, alcohol, and stress); First Aid 2025 GI (Curling ulcer).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Curling ulcer follows burns from hypovolemia and mucosal ischemia; Cushing ulcer follows brain injury from vagal stimulation and increased acid (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: give stress ulcer prophylaxis to critically ill patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — acute stress gastropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman takes high-dose aspirin for headaches and develops gastric erosions. Apart from inhibiting cyclooxygenase, aspirin injures the gastric surface by which direct action?","choices":{"A":"Damages hydrophobic membranes","B":"Stimulates gastrin release","C":"Blocks bicarbonate channels","D":"Activates pepsinogen in cells","E":"Raises vagal acid output"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aspirin directly damages the hydrophobic apical cell membranes, allowing acid to leak down its electrochemical gradient into the tissue, in addition to suppressing PGE2. It does not stimulate gastrin release. Its effect on bicarbonate comes through loss of prostaglandins, not channel blockade. Pepsinogen is activated in the lumen, not inside cells. Vagal acid output rises in Cushing ulcer after brain injury. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, NSAIDs and aspirin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs reduce PGE2 and gastric mucosal protection (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: misoprostol prevents NSAID-induced ulcers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — NSAID-associated ulcer prevention","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman has a biopsy labeled distal esophagus. It shows simple columnar surface cells, gastric pits, and glands packed with numerous parietal and chief cells in the lamina propria, with no glands in the submucosa. Where was the tissue taken?","choices":{"A":"Distal esophagus","B":"Gastric cardia","C":"Duodenal bulb","D":"Gastric body","E":"Pyloric antrum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Simple columnar epithelium, gastric pits, and mucosal glands rich in parietal and chief cells, without submucosal glands, define fundic mucosa of the gastric body. The distal esophagus has stratified squamous epithelium and submucosal glands proper. The cardia has mucous glands with few parietal cells. The duodenal bulb has villi and Brunner glands in the submucosa. The pyloric antrum has deep pits over mucous glands with G cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, histologic comparison); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (fundic glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophagus has nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium; stomach has gastric glands with parietal and chief cells (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: a mislabeled specimen is identified by its epithelium and glands.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial types along the GI tract","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old man has leg edema and an albumin of 2.4 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Endoscopy shows giant folds in the body of the stomach resembling brain gyri, and biopsy shows foveolar hyperplasia with loss of parietal cells. Which normal structure is enlarged?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal villi","B":"Gastric rugae","C":"Plicae circulares","D":"Colonic haustra","E":"Esophageal papillae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastric rugae are longitudinal folds of mucosa and submucosa that flatten when the stomach fills; in Ménétrier disease they become hypertrophied, with excess mucus production and protein loss. Intestinal villi are mucosal projections of the small intestine. Plicae circulares are permanent transverse folds of the small intestine. Colonic haustra are sacculations of the colon. Esophageal papillae are connective tissue projections into the esophageal epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, mucosal surface organization); First Aid 2025 GI (Ménétrier disease).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ménétrier disease is gastric mucosal hyperplasia with hypertrophied rugae, excess mucus, protein loss, and parietal cell atrophy with reduced acid (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: protein-losing gastropathy presents with edema.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — hypertrophic gastropathy (Ménétrier disease)","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman undergoes repair of a hiatal hernia. The surgeon notes that the stomach has a smooth glistening outer coat, whereas the thoracic esophagus is wrapped in fibrous connective tissue. What forms the outer coat of the stomach?","choices":{"A":"Dense irregular adventitia","B":"Stratified squamous epithelium","C":"Simple squamous mesothelium","D":"Loose areolar fascia","E":"Parietal pleural lining"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The stomach is covered throughout by serosa, a simple squamous mesothelium over thin connective tissue, which gives a smooth glistening surface. The thoracic esophagus is covered by adventitia, connective tissue that binds it to the mediastinum; only its short abdominal segment of 1 to 2 cm has serosa. Stratified squamous epithelium lines the esophageal lumen. Loose areolar fascia lacks a mesothelial surface. Parietal pleura lines the thoracic cavity. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, outermost coat); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (serosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serosa covers intraperitoneal organs and adventitia covers retroperitoneal ones (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: a sliding hiatal hernia displaces the gastroesophageal junction above the diaphragm.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — serosa versus adventitia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":3,"stem":"A 36-year-old man has an endoscopic biopsy taken just below the gastroesophageal junction. It shows shallow gastric pits over tubular, somewhat tortuous mucous glands with only occasional parietal cells. Which gastric region was sampled?","choices":{"A":"Fundic region","B":"Pyloric region","C":"Cardiac region","D":"Distal esophagus","E":"Duodenal bulb"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Shallow pits over tubular, tortuous glands composed mostly of mucous cells, with occasional parietal cells at the border, define the cardiac region around the esophageal orifice. The fundic region has shallow pits over long glands full of parietal and chief cells. The pyloric region has deep pits that occupy about half of the mucosa. The distal esophagus is lined by stratified squamous epithelium without gastric pits. The duodenal bulb has villi and Brunner glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, cardiac region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (cardiac glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cardia is the junction zone where Barrett metaplasia is assessed (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: biopsy site matters when reporting intestinal metaplasia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 5 — esophageal cardiac glands","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old man takes a drug that irreversibly blocks the apical H+/K+-ATPase of large cells in the neck of the fundic glands. Which feature identifies these cells on H&E staining?","choices":{"A":"Intensely basophilic basal cytoplasm","B":"Clear apical mucous granule cups","C":"Flattened nuclei at the cell base","D":"Apical zymogen granule clusters","E":"Intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The drug targets parietal cells, large triangular cells with central nuclei and intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm packed with mitochondria and canaliculi. Basophilic basal cytoplasm and apical zymogen granules identify chief cells, whose apical cytoplasm is also eosinophilic but less intensely. Clear apical mucous cups identify surface mucous cells. Flattened basal nuclei are typical of mucous gland cells in cardiac glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, parietal cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (parietal cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells are eosinophilic and chief cells are basophilic (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: PPIs heal 85 to 90 percent of erosive esophagitis (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — proton pump inhibitors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has autoimmune gastritis and pernicious anemia. Her B12 absorption returns to normal when oral B12 is given together with intrinsic factor. Where is the B12 and intrinsic factor complex normally absorbed?","choices":{"A":"Distal ileum","B":"Duodenal bulb","C":"Proximal jejunum","D":"Gastric antrum","E":"Ascending colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intrinsic factor from parietal cells binds B12, and the complex is taken up by receptor mediated endocytosis in the distal ileum, so an intact ileum explains why added intrinsic factor corrects absorption. The duodenal bulb and proximal jejunum absorb iron, folate, and most nutrients but not the B12 complex. The gastric antrum does not absorb B12. The ascending colon absorbs water and electrolytes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, B12 and intrinsic factor uptake in the distal ileum, lines 596 and 632); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pernicious anemia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune gastritis targets parietal cells and intrinsic factor and causes pernicious anemia (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: treat with parenteral B12.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":3,"stem":"A 23-year-old woman develops hematemesis after a night of binge drinking. Endoscopy shows superficial gastric erosions. How does ethanol injure the gastric surface?","choices":{"A":"COX enzyme inhibition","B":"Urease ammonia production","C":"Vagal acid stimulation","D":"Direct cell toxicity","E":"Gastrin hypersecretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Alcohol injures the gastric surface by direct chemical cytotoxicity, leading to local mucosal acidosis and cell death. COX inhibition is the mechanism of NSAIDs. Urease ammonia production is how H. pylori buffers acid to survive, not how ethanol injures the mucosa. Vagal acid stimulation causes Cushing ulcer after brain injury. Gastrin hypersecretion occurs with a gastrinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, ischemia, shock, alcohol, and stress).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute gastritis risk factors include alcohol use disorder and daily NSAID use (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: forceful retching can also cause Mallory-Weiss tears.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — acute gastropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":3,"stem":"A 56-year-old man has a resection specimen from the upper abdomen. The organ has simple columnar epithelium, three layers of smooth muscle in the muscularis externa, and a serosal covering. Which organ is this?","choices":{"A":"Esophagus","B":"Stomach","C":"Duodenum","D":"Jejunum","E":"Colon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Simple columnar epithelium with an extra innermost oblique layer, giving three muscle layers, and a serosal coat is characteristic of the stomach. The esophagus has stratified squamous epithelium, two muscle layers, and mostly adventitia. The duodenum and jejunum have villi and two muscle layers. The colon has crypts and teniae coli in two muscle layers. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, histologic comparison); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (stomach muscularis externa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach has gastric glands with parietal and chief cells (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: oblique fibers aid gastric churning.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has dyspepsia, and gastric biopsy shows curved rods on the foveolar cells with chronic active inflammation. The strain carries the CagA gene. How does this toxin weaken the mucosal barrier?","choices":{"A":"Tight junction disruption","B":"COX enzyme inhibition","C":"Reduced mucosal blood flow","D":"Parietal cell autoimmunity","E":"Vagal overstimulation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"H. pylori cytotoxins such as CagA destroy tight junctions, disrupt the mucus gel, and trigger chronic active inflammation, letting acid and pepsin reach the epithelium. COX inhibition is the NSAID mechanism. Reduced mucosal blood flow is the mechanism of shock and burns. Parietal cell autoimmunity causes autoimmune gastritis. Vagal overstimulation causes Cushing ulcer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, Helicobacter pylori infection).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori increases the risk of peptic ulcer disease and MALT lymphoma (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: treat with triple therapy and confirm eradication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl03_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":4,"stem":"A 53-year-old woman with Hashimoto thyroiditis and vitiligo has fatigue, a sore smooth tongue, and numbness in both feet. Biopsy of the gastric body shows thin mucosa with few glands, while the antrum looks normal. Which of the following sets of laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Serum gastrin","Gastric acid","Serum B12"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↑","↔"],"B":["↑","↓","↓"],"C":["↔","↔","↔"],"D":["↓","↓","↓"],"E":["↔","↔","↓"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis destroys parietal cells in the body and fundus, so Choice B (↑ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↓ serum B12) is correct. Acid falls, gastrin from the spared antrum rises, and serum B12 falls, which fits the glossitis and neuropathy. Choice A (↑ serum gastrin, ↑ gastric acid, ↔ serum B12) is a gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome), in which tumor gastrin drives intact parietal cells to secrete excess acid; intrinsic factor is still made, so B12 is typically normal, and although very low duodenal pH can inactivate the pancreatic proteases that cleave R-binder and free B12 to bind intrinsic factor (inferred: Costanzo describes low duodenal pH inactivating pancreatic lipase in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and pancreatic proteases degrading R proteins), the high acid level excludes this row whatever the B12 value. Choice C (↔ serum gastrin, ↔ gastric acid, ↔ serum B12) is folate deficiency, which causes a macrocytic anemia almost identical to pernicious anemia but leaves gastric secretion and B12 unchanged and does not explain the numbness in her feet. Choice D (↓ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↓ serum B12) follows total gastrectomy, which removes the antral G cells, so gastrin falls, and removes the parietal cells, so acid and intrinsic factor are lost and B12 is not absorbed; the low gastrin separates this row from the key. Choice E (↔ serum gastrin, ↔ gastric acid, ↓ serum B12) follows resection of the distal ileum, where the B12 and intrinsic factor complex is absorbed, with normal gastric function. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, parietal cell loss, achlorhydria and hypergastrinemia); Pawlina Ch. 17 (pernicious anemia); Guyton supplemental (folic acid deficiency causes a macrocytic anemia almost identical to pernicious anemia); First Aid 2025 GI (autoimmune gastritis; Zollinger-Ellison syndrome); Costanzo Ch. 8 (antral resection decreases gastrin and H+ secretion; gastrectomy removes the source of intrinsic factor, so B12 must be injected); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, total or partial gastrectomy causes B12 deficiency); the low gastrin in row D is inferred from removal of the antrum, and the B12 value in row A is inferred from preserved intrinsic factor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune gastritis has antibodies to the parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase and intrinsic factor, affects the body and fundus, and causes hypochlorhydria, hypergastrinemia, and pernicious anemia (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: a high gastrin level with low acid points to atrophic gastritis or acid suppression rather than gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":4,"title":"SDL 4 — Physiology - GI Innervation","questions":[{"id":"sdl04_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":1,"stem":"Within the muscularis externa, contraction of the inner circular layer has which effect on a gut segment?","choices":{"A":"Narrows the lumen","B":"Shortens the segment","C":"Folds the mucosa","D":"Widens the lumen","E":"Lengthens the segment"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Contraction of the inner circular layer decreases the luminal diameter. Contraction of the outer longitudinal layer shortens the segment. Folding of the mucosa is produced by the muscularis mucosae. Widening of the lumen follows relaxation of circular muscle ahead of a bolus, and lengthening follows relaxation of longitudinal muscle; neither is produced by circular contraction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, inner circular muscle layer); Costanzo Ch. 8 (circular muscle decreases diameter, longitudinal muscle decreases length).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus between the two muscle layers controls motility (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: circular and longitudinal layers are reciprocally innervated during peristalsis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — submucosal versus myenteric plexus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":1,"stem":"Which layer of the gut wall alters the folding and surface area of the inner lining to aid local absorption and secretion?","choices":{"A":"Inner circular layer","B":"Outer longitudinal layer","C":"Muscularis mucosae","D":"Lamina propria","E":"Serosal layer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The muscularis mucosae is a thin smooth muscle layer at the base of the mucosa whose local contractions change mucosal shape and surface area. The inner circular and outer longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa move the whole segment. The lamina propria is loose connective tissue with vessels and immune cells. The serosa is not the layer described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, muscularis mucosae).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gut wall layers are mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: the submucosal plexus controls the muscularis mucosae.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — submucosal versus myenteric plexus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":1,"stem":"During peristalsis, when the circular muscle of a segment contracts, what does the longitudinal muscle of that segment do?","choices":{"A":"Contracts","B":"Relaxes","C":"Is unchanged","D":"Fatigues","E":"Depolarizes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Circular and longitudinal muscle are reciprocally innervated, so when the circular muscle of a segment contracts, its longitudinal muscle relaxes, and the segment narrows and lengthens behind the bolus. Simultaneous contraction is what reciprocal innervation prevents. The longitudinal layer changes its tone rather than staying unchanged. Fatigue is not part of the reflex. Depolarization would lead to contraction, not relaxation. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (reciprocal innervation and peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: behind the bolus, circular muscle contracts; ahead of it, circular muscle relaxes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the segment ahead of the bolus relaxes so the bolus can advance (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — submucosal versus myenteric plexus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Preganglionic sympathetic fibers to the gastrointestinal tract synapse in which location?","choices":{"A":"Myenteric plexus","B":"Submucosal plexus","C":"Dorsal root ganglia","D":"Inferior vagal ganglion","E":"Prevertebral ganglia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sympathetic preganglionic fibers are short and synapse outside the gut in four prevertebral ganglia: celiac, superior mesenteric, inferior mesenteric, and hypogastric. The myenteric and submucosal plexuses are where parasympathetic preganglionic fibers synapse and where sympathetic postganglionic fibers end. Dorsal root ganglia are not sympathetic synapse sites. The inferior vagal ganglion is not a sympathetic synapse site. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, ganglion location); Costanzo Ch. 8 (sympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic splanchnic supply is T5 to T9 for the foregut, T10 to T12 for the midgut, and L1 to L2 for the hindgut (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: the lecture also lists paravertebral chain ganglia in the sympathetic pathway.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Which description fits the parasympathetic preganglionic fibers that supply the gastrointestinal tract?","choices":{"A":"Short with prevertebral ganglia","B":"Short with intramural ganglia","C":"Long with chain ganglia","D":"Long with intramural ganglia","E":"Short with chain ganglia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Parasympathetic preganglionic fibers are long, running from the brainstem or sacral cord to the gut, and synapse in ganglia within the wall, in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses, so the postganglionic fibers are short. Short preganglionic fibers synapsing in prevertebral ganglia describe the sympathetic supply. Short fibers ending in the wall describe no autonomic division. Chain ganglia are sympathetic paravertebral ganglia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, fiber length); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasympathetic ganglia lie in or near the target organ; sympathetic ganglia lie outside it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vagal preganglionic fibers synapse in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Activation of sympathetic postganglionic fibers to the gut produces which effect?","choices":{"A":"Contraction of sphincters","B":"Relaxation of sphincters","C":"Increased gland secretion","D":"Increased wall motility","E":"Mucosal vasodilation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sympathetic activity generally inhibits gut function: it relaxes the smooth muscle of the walls, contracts GI sphincters, decreases motility, inhibits secretion, and constricts GI vessels. Relaxation of sphincters, increased gland secretion, and increased wall motility are parasympathetic effects. Mucosal vasodilation accompanies parasympathetic stimulation during digestion, whereas sympathetic activity causes vasoconstriction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, effects on GI function); Guyton Ch. 63 (sympathetic stimulation inhibits activity in the gut).","boardPrep":"Step 1: norepinephrine inhibits gut motility, mostly through inhibition of enteric neurons (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: intense sympathetic activity can stop movement of food through the gut.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"The pelvic nerve supplies parasympathetic fibers to which part of the gastrointestinal tract?","choices":{"A":"Stomach and proximal duodenum","B":"Jejunum and terminal ileum","C":"Cecum and ascending colon","D":"Upper third of esophagus","E":"Descending and sigmoid colon"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The pelvic nerve, from sacral segments S2 to S4, supplies the lower GI tract: the distal transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon, rectum, and anal canal. The stomach, duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, and ascending colon receive vagal parasympathetic fibers. The upper third of the esophagus is striated muscle supplied by vagal fibers from the nucleus ambiguus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, pelvic nerve); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation; Costanzo lists the transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon, and the lecture adds the rectum and anal canal).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hindgut parasympathetic supply is from S2 to S4 splanchnic nerves (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: sacral parasympathetic fibers coordinate defecation reflexes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — vagal innervation of the rotated stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, besides coordinating defecation reflexes, which colonic function does parasympathetic outflow through the pelvic nerve stimulate?","choices":{"A":"Mucus secretion","B":"Water absorption","C":"Bile acid uptake","D":"Motility inhibition","E":"Sphincter contraction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pelvic nerve fibers from S2 to S4 are heavily concentrated in the sigmoid, rectal, and anal regions, where they coordinate defecation reflexes and stimulate colonic mucus secretion. Water absorption by the colonic epithelium is not the function the lecture assigns to pelvic outflow. Bile acid uptake occurs mainly in the terminal ileum, which is in vagal territory. Parasympathetic activity increases rather than inhibits colonic motility. Sphincter contraction is a sympathetic effect. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, pelvic nerve distribution and functions, lines 843–850; section 2, sympathetic contraction of sphincters); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes that parasympathetic activity increases peristalsis and secretions while lowering sphincter tone (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, stomach physiology). COMLEX: sacral cord lesions impair defecation reflexes and colonic mucus secretion (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — vagal innervation of the rotated stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Which is the most distal part of the gastrointestinal tract that receives vagal parasympathetic fibers?","choices":{"A":"Distal transverse colon","B":"Proximal transverse colon","C":"Proximal descending colon","D":"Proximal sigmoid colon","E":"Rectosigmoid junction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The vagus supplies the esophagus, stomach, pancreas, liver and gallbladder, small intestine, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal transverse colon. The distal transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, and rectosigmoid region are supplied by the pelvic nerve. This boundary matches the midgut and hindgut junction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, vagus nerve distribution).","boardPrep":"Step 1: midgut parasympathetic supply is the vagus; hindgut supply is S2 to S4 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: the distal third of the transverse colon is hindgut.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — vagal innervation of the rotated stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"About what proportion of the fibers in the vagus nerve are afferent?","choices":{"A":"About 25 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 10 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Costanzo states that about 75 percent of vagal fibers are afferent and 25 percent efferent; the lecture gives 75 to 80 percent afferent. The 25 percent figure is the efferent fraction. About 50 percent does not match the vagal split. About 10 and 90 percent match no source. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (vagus is 75% afferent and 25% efferent); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, structural composition).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagovagal reflexes carry both afferent and efferent limbs in the vagus (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vagal afferents relay stretch and chemical signals from the gut.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — phases of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"In a vagovagal reflex, the vagal afferent fibers from the gut terminate in which brainstem nucleus?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal motor nucleus of vagus","B":"Nucleus ambiguus of medulla","C":"Nucleus tractus solitarius","D":"Spinal trigeminal nucleus","E":"Hypoglossal motor nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vagal afferents from the gut end in the nucleus tractus solitarius of the medulla. The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus is the origin of the efferent limb. The nucleus ambiguus, spinal trigeminal nucleus, and hypoglossal nucleus are not the termination site of vagal afferents in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, afferent and efferent pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in vagovagal reflexes both limbs travel in the vagus (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: receptive relaxation and distension-driven acid secretion are vagovagal reflexes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — phases of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Distension of the orad stomach triggers a vagovagal reflex that relaxes the gastric wall. Which transmitters do the postganglionic neurons release?","choices":{"A":"ACh and substance P","B":"VIP and nitric oxide","C":"Gastrin and CCK","D":"Secretin and somatostatin","E":"GRP and histamine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Receptive relaxation is a vagovagal reflex in which peptidergic postganglionic neurons release VIP and nitric oxide, relaxing the orad stomach so it can hold up to 1.5 L without a rise in pressure. ACh and substance P are excitatory transmitters that contract smooth muscle. Gastrin and CCK are hormones, not reflex transmitters. Secretin and somatostatin are a hormone and a paracrine inhibitor. GRP stimulates G cells, and histamine is a paracrine stimulant of parietal cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, receptive relaxation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (receptive relaxation; the neurotransmitter is VIP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagotomy eliminates receptive relaxation (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: loss of accommodation causes early satiety.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — phases of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":1,"stem":"In the peristaltic reflex, intrinsic primary afferent neurons in the gut wall receive chemical activation from which signal?","choices":{"A":"Norepinephrine from sympathetic fibers","B":"ACh from vagal efferent fibers","C":"Gastrin from antral G endocrine cells","D":"Histamine from mucosal mast cells","E":"Serotonin from enterochromaffin cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A bolus stimulates enterochromaffin cells in the mucosa to release serotonin, which binds intrinsic primary afferent neurons (IPANs) and starts the peristaltic reflex in that segment. Norepinephrine from sympathetic fibers suppresses enteric activity. ACh from vagal efferent fibers is extrinsic modulation, not the local trigger. Gastrin from antral G cells is a hormone acting on parietal cells. Histamine from mast cells is not the trigger described in the lecture. IPANs also respond directly to stretch. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, sensorimotor architecture); Costanzo Ch. 8 (5-HT binds IPANs to initiate the peristaltic reflex).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the enteric nervous system contains more than 100 million neurons (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: local reflexes work without central processing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — enteric control of secondary peristalsis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":1,"stem":"During the peristaltic reflex, excitatory enteric neurons contract the circular muscle behind the bolus. Which transmitters do they release?","choices":{"A":"VIP and nitric oxide","B":"Norepinephrine and dopamine","C":"Secretin and CCK","D":"ACh and substance P","E":"Gastrin and histamine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Excitatory enteric neurons release acetylcholine and substance P to contract muscle behind the bolus. VIP and nitric oxide are released by inhibitory neurons that relax muscle ahead of the bolus and relax sphincters. Norepinephrine is released by extrinsic sympathetic fibers and inhibits motility. Secretin and CCK are hormones. Gastrin and histamine regulate acid secretion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, motility control via the myenteric plexus); Costanzo Ch. 8 (peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetylcholine most often excites GI activity, and norepinephrine usually inhibits it (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: anticholinergic drugs slow gut motility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — enteric control of secondary peristalsis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":1,"stem":"Sympathetic postganglionic fibers suppress gut motility and secretion. Where do most of these fibers end?","choices":{"A":"On smooth muscle cells","B":"On mucosal epithelium","C":"On enteric neurons","D":"On vagal ganglia","E":"On dorsal root ganglia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sympathetic postganglionic fibers end mainly on neurons of the enteric nervous system, where norepinephrine inhibits transmitter release; only a small share acts directly on smooth muscle and vessels. Smooth muscle cells receive a minor direct input. Mucosal epithelium is controlled mainly through enteric neurons. Vagal ganglia and dorsal root ganglia are not sympathetic targets. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, sympathetic input); Guyton Ch. 63 (sympathetic fibers end mainly on enteric neurons).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic stimulation inhibits gut activity mainly through the enteric nervous system (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: sympathetic activity also contracts sphincters and constricts gut vessels (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — enteric control of secondary peristalsis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has a full-thickness colon biopsy for chronic constipation. Ganglion cells are seen between the dense connective tissue of the submucosa and the inner circular muscle layer. Which function does this network mainly control?","choices":{"A":"Muscularis externa contraction","B":"Relaxation of the GI sphincters","C":"Local secretion and blood flow","D":"Initiation of the swallow reflex","E":"Gastric receptive relaxation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ganglia between the submucosal connective tissue and the inner circular layer form the submucosal (Meissner) plexus, which controls local fluid and electrolyte secretion, absorption, local blood flow, and movement of the muscularis mucosae. Contraction of the muscularis externa and relaxation of sphincters are controlled by the myenteric plexus. The swallow reflex is initiated by the medullary swallowing center. Receptive relaxation is a vagovagal reflex of the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, submucosal plexus location, and section 5, submucosal plexus control).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the submucosal plexus integrates epithelial sensory signals to control fluid secretion and local vasodilation (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: the submucosal plexus also controls the muscularis mucosae (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — submucosal versus myenteric plexus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman has an endoscopic submucosal injection before removal of a colon polyp. The needle tip lies in dense irregular connective tissue with large vessels, just deep to the muscularis mucosae. Which neural network lies in this layer?","choices":{"A":"Auerbach plexus","B":"Celiac plexus","C":"Vagal trunk plexus","D":"Meissner plexus","E":"Pelvic plexus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dense irregular connective tissue with large vessels just deep to the muscularis mucosae is the submucosa, which contains the submucosal (Meissner) plexus. The Auerbach (myenteric) plexus lies deeper, between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers. The celiac and pelvic plexuses are extrinsic autonomic plexuses outside the gut wall. Vagal trunks lie on the esophagus and stomach, outside the wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, submucosa and submucosal plexus); First Aid 2025 GI (digestive tract anatomy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the submucosa contains the Meissner plexus and vessels; the muscularis externa contains the Auerbach plexus (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: the submucosa carries the larger vessels of the gut wall (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman in a pharmacology study receives an agent that blocks synaptic transmission in the ganglia between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers. Which function is reduced?","choices":{"A":"Peristaltic wave velocity","B":"Mucosal fluid secretion","C":"Muscularis mucosae folding","D":"Local mucosal vasodilation","E":"Epithelial absorption"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ganglia between the two muscle layers form the myenteric plexus, which sets smooth muscle tone, the rate and intensity of rhythmic contractions, and the conduction velocity of peristaltic waves. Mucosal fluid secretion, muscularis mucosae folding, and local vasodilation are controlled by the submucosal plexus. Epithelial absorption depends on enterocytes and submucosal control, not on the myenteric plexus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, myenteric plexus location, and section 5, motility control).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Auerbach plexus controls motility (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: loss of myenteric neurons causes achalasia in the esophagus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — enteric control of esophageal peristalsis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman has episodes of severe hypertension, headaches, and sweating, and imaging shows an adrenal mass. She also reports constipation. Which gastrointestinal effect of her excess catecholamine most likely contributes to the constipation?","choices":{"A":"Faster transit with relaxed sphincters","B":"Slow transit and tight sphincters","C":"Increased glandular fluid secretion","D":"Mucosal vasodilation and hyperemia","E":"Destruction of myenteric neurons"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Norepinephrine, the sympathetic postganglionic transmitter, relaxes the smooth muscle of the gut wall, contracts sphincters, decreases motility and transit, and inhibits secretion, which together most likely favor constipation; Robbins does not list constipation among pheochromocytoma features, so the clinical outcome is inferred from the mechanism. Faster transit with relaxed sphincters and increased glandular secretion are parasympathetic effects. Sympathetic activity constricts, rather than dilates, gut vessels. Norepinephrine inhibits enteric neurons but does not destroy them. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, effects on GI function); Guyton Ch. 63 (norepinephrine usually inhibits GI activity).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic activity inhibits gut motility and secretion and contracts sphincters (Costanzo Ch. 8; Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: norepinephrine acts mainly through enteric neurons (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — sympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman takes an overdose of a drug that blocks muscarinic receptors. She has a dry mouth, flushed skin, and absent bowel sounds. Which normal parasympathetic action has been blocked?","choices":{"A":"Relaxation of gut smooth muscle","B":"Vasoconstriction of gut vessels","C":"Inhibition of glandular secretion","D":"Contraction of gut smooth muscle","E":"Release of norepinephrine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Parasympathetic postganglionic cholinergic neurons release ACh onto muscarinic receptors to increase smooth muscle contraction and motility, relax sphincters, and increase glandular secretion. Blocking muscarinic receptors removes these effects, causing ileus and dry mouth. Relaxation of gut smooth muscle, vasoconstriction, and inhibition of secretion are sympathetic effects. Norepinephrine is released by sympathetic, not parasympathetic, fibers. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, neurotransmitters and effects on GI function).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetylcholine most often excites GI activity (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: parasympathetic activity relaxes GI sphincters (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — parasympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with pancreatic cancer has severe abdominal pain and receives a celiac plexus block. The injected ganglion lies outside the gut wall in front of the aorta. Which neurons have cell bodies in this ganglion?","choices":{"A":"Parasympathetic postganglionic neurons","B":"Vagal preganglionic neurons","C":"Sympathetic postganglionic neurons","D":"Enteric interneurons of the wall","E":"Dorsal root sensory neurons"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The celiac ganglion is a prevertebral sympathetic ganglion where short preganglionic fibers synapse on postganglionic neurons whose long adrenergic fibers run to the gut. Parasympathetic postganglionic cell bodies lie in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses within the gut wall. Vagal preganglionic cell bodies lie in the dorsal motor nucleus of the brainstem, and vagal fibers pass through the celiac plexus without synapsing; Bootcamp labels the celiac ganglia parasympathetic, but the lecture lists them as sympathetic prevertebral ganglia. Enteric interneurons lie in the gut wall. Dorsal root sensory neurons do not lie in the celiac ganglion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, ganglion location and fiber length); Costanzo Ch. 8 (sympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: foregut sympathetic supply runs through the T5 to T9 splanchnic nerves (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: the lecture lists the celiac, superior mesenteric, inferior mesenteric, and hypogastric ganglia as prevertebral.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has a sacral tumor that destroys the S2 to S4 nerve roots on both sides. Which part of the gastrointestinal tract loses its parasympathetic supply?","choices":{"A":"Descending colon and rectum","B":"Stomach and proximal duodenum","C":"Jejunum and terminal ileum","D":"Cecum and ascending colon","E":"Distal esophagus and stomach"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The S2 to S4 segments give rise to the pelvic nerve, which supplies the distal transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon, rectum, and anal canal. The stomach, duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, ascending colon, and esophagus receive their parasympathetic supply from the vagus, which leaves the brainstem and is spared. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, pelvic nerve distribution); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hindgut parasympathetic supply is S2 to S4 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: sacral root injury impairs defecation and colonic mucus secretion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut divisions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman undergoes truncal vagotomy, with both vagal trunks divided at the esophageal hiatus, during surgery for recurrent bleeding from a duodenal ulcer. Her sacral spinal cord and pelvic nerves are intact. Which segment of the gut loses its parasympathetic supply?","choices":{"A":"Distal transverse colon","B":"Ascending colon","C":"Descending colon","D":"Sigmoid colon","E":"Anal canal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Dividing both vagal trunks removes parasympathetic supply from the whole vagal territory, which runs from the esophagus and stomach through the small intestine, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal transverse colon. The distal transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, and anal canal receive parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic nerve, which arises from S2 to S4 and is intact in this patient. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, vagus and pelvic nerve distribution, lines 835–850); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid gives the vagus as the parasympathetic nerve of the foregut and of the midgut, which extends to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon (First Aid 2025 GI, gastrointestinal blood supply and innervation). COMLEX: after truncal vagotomy, the left colon still receives sacral parasympathetic outflow (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — anterior and posterior vagal trunks","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with a sacral myelomeningocele has absent function of the S2 to S4 cord segments. Which gastrointestinal function is most affected?","choices":{"A":"Gastric acid secretion","B":"Gallbladder contraction","C":"Pancreatic enzyme secretion","D":"Small bowel peristalsis","E":"Rectal defecation reflexes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pelvic nerve innervation from S2 to S4 is concentrated in the sigmoid colon, rectum, and anal canal, where it coordinates defecation reflexes and stimulates colonic mucus secretion. Gastric acid secretion, gallbladder contraction, and pancreatic enzyme secretion receive vagal parasympathetic input. Small bowel peristalsis is driven by the myenteric plexus with vagal modulation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, pelvic nerve).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pelvic nerve arises from the second, third, and fourth sacral cord segments (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: neurogenic bowel follows sacral cord injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — hindgut derivatives","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 53-year-old man has early satiety and epigastric fullness after small meals since a truncal vagotomy. Loss of which reflex best explains his symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Receptive relaxation","B":"Gastrocolic reflex","C":"Enterogastric reflex","D":"Peristaltic reflex","E":"Defecation reflex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Receptive relaxation is a vagovagal reflex in which distension of the orad stomach relaxes the gastric wall so it can hold a meal without a rise in pressure; vagotomy eliminates it, causing early fullness. The gastrocolic reflex increases colonic motility after a meal. The enterogastric reflex slows gastric emptying in response to duodenal contents, and its loss would not cause early satiety. The peristaltic reflex is a local enteric reflex that survives vagotomy. The defecation reflex is sacral. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, receptive relaxation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (vagotomy eliminates receptive relaxation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: receptive relaxation is a vagovagal reflex mediated by VIP (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired gastric accommodation causes early satiety.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — receptive relaxation of the orad stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman in a physiology study has a balloon inflated in her stomach, and her serum gastrin rises. Postganglionic vagal neurons release which transmitter onto the antral G cells?","choices":{"A":"VIP","B":"ACh","C":"CCK","D":"NO","E":"GRP"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gastric distension initiates vagovagal reflexes in which postganglionic neurons release ACh onto parietal cells and gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP) onto antral G cells, increasing acid secretion. VIP and nitric oxide relax the orad stomach in receptive relaxation. ACh is the transmitter acting on parietal cells rather than on G cells. CCK is a hormone from duodenal I cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, gastric secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagal stimulation releases GRP to increase gastrin (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: atropine does not block vagal stimulation of gastrin because GRP is not cholinergic (First Aid 2025 GI acid secretion diagram: atropine acts on the ACh limb).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — neural regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman notices an urge to defecate within minutes after each large meal. Which reflex is responsible?","choices":{"A":"Receptive relaxation","B":"Gastrocolic reflex","C":"Enterogastric reflex","D":"Rectosphincteric reflex","E":"Vomiting reflex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastric distension triggers the gastrocolic reflex, a long arc reflex that increases colonic motility and starts mass movements. The lecture describes it as a long arc vagovagal reflex, which is the exam convention; Costanzo instead places the afferent limb in parasympathetic nerves and the efferent limb in CCK and gastrin. Receptive relaxation relaxes the stomach itself. The enterogastric reflex slows gastric emptying. The rectosphincteric reflex relaxes the internal anal sphincter when the rectum fills, after mass movements arrive. The vomiting reflex is coordinated in the medulla. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, gastrocolic reflex); Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastrocolic reflex).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastrocolic reflex increases colonic motility and mass movements after a meal (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: mass movements carry colonic contents toward the rectum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman in a motility study receives a drug that blocks serotonin receptors on intrinsic primary afferent neurons. Which response to a bolus is reduced?","choices":{"A":"Sympathetic splanchnic outflow","B":"Gastric acid secretion","C":"Pancreatic enzyme release","D":"Local peristaltic reflex","E":"Hepatic bile formation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Serotonin released from mucosal enterochromaffin cells activates intrinsic primary afferent neurons, which start the local peristaltic reflex through enteric interneurons and motor neurons; blocking these receptors reduces it. Sympathetic splanchnic outflow arises in the spinal cord. Gastric acid secretion, pancreatic enzyme release, and bile formation are not driven by IPAN serotonin signaling. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, sensorimotor architecture); Costanzo Ch. 8 (5-HT binds IPANs).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the enteric nervous system coordinates local reflexes without central input (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: serotonin signaling links mucosal stimuli to motility.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — secondary peristalsis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man has paralytic ileus after a long abdominal operation with intense sympathetic activation. Norepinephrine suppresses his motility mainly through which effect on the enteric nervous system?","choices":{"A":"Increased vagal outflow","B":"Loss of enteric neurons","C":"Blocked nicotinic receptors","D":"Increased VIP and NO release","E":"Reduced enteric ACh release"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sympathetic postganglionic fibers end mainly on enteric neurons, and norepinephrine binds presynaptic receptors that inhibit release of excitatory transmitters such as ACh, suppressing motility. Increased vagal outflow would increase motility. Enteric neurons are inhibited, not lost. Norepinephrine does not block nicotinic receptors. Increased VIP and NO release would relax muscle by a different pathway and is not the sympathetic mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, sympathetic input); Guyton Ch. 63 (inhibitory effect of norepinephrine on enteric neurons).","boardPrep":"Step 1: strong sympathetic stimulation can inhibit gut movement enough to block passage of food (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: enterogastric reflexes also use prevertebral sympathetic ganglia (lecture SDL 6).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — sympathetic limb of the enterogastric reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman is studied after her extrinsic gut nerves are blocked pharmacologically. Luminal peptides still increase gastrin release from her antral G cells. Which neural element can still modulate the G cells?","choices":{"A":"Vagal afferent fibers","B":"Prevertebral ganglia","C":"Enteric interneurons","D":"Pelvic splanchnic fibers","E":"Spinal motor neurons"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Enteric interneurons synapse with and modulate enteroendocrine cells, regulating the release of local hormones such as gastrin, CCK, secretin, and somatostatin in response to luminal conditions, even without extrinsic input. Vagal afferents, prevertebral sympathetic ganglia, and pelvic splanchnic fibers are extrinsic and are blocked. Spinal motor neurons supply skeletal muscle. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, endocrine modulation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: G cells release gastrin in response to small peptides and amino acids (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: the enteric nervous system functions independently of extrinsic nerves.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — gastrin release triggers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":3,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman has a segment of ileum resected and examined. Which layer lies immediately outside its outer longitudinal muscle?","choices":{"A":"Mucosa","B":"Serosa","C":"Adventitia","D":"Submucosa","E":"Muscularis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Intraperitoneal segments such as the ileum are covered by a serosa, a serous membrane of simple squamous mesothelium outside the outer longitudinal muscle. The mucosa and submucosa lie inside the muscularis externa. Adventitia is the connective tissue coat of fixed, retroperitoneal segments. The muscularis is the muscle layer itself. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, serosa or adventitia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gut wall layers from lumen outward are mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: retroperitoneal segments have an adventitia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":3,"stem":"An 81-year-old man has midgut visceral pain carried with sympathetic fibers from T10 to T12. In which ganglion do the preganglionic sympathetic fibers to the midgut synapse?","choices":{"A":"Inferior mesenteric ganglion","B":"Superior cervical ganglion","C":"Hypogastric plexus ganglion","D":"Superior mesenteric ganglion","E":"Myenteric plexus ganglia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The midgut is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery with sympathetic fibers from T10 to T12, which synapse in the superior mesenteric ganglion wrapped around the superior mesenteric artery; its postganglionic axons reach the territory of that artery. The inferior mesenteric and hypogastric ganglia serve the hindgut and pelvis. The superior cervical ganglion serves the head and neck. Myenteric ganglia are intrinsic. Sources: Bootcamp Gastroenterology (midgut: SMA, T10 to T12); Foundations of Osteopathic Medicine Ch. 6 (superior mesenteric ganglion, lines 3976–3981).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the midgut has vagal parasympathetic and T10 to T12 splanchnic sympathetic supply (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: hindgut sympathetic supply is L1 to L2.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q03","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":3,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl eats a small protein meal that barely distends her stomach, yet a vagovagal reflex raises her acid secretion. Which receptors most likely start this reflex?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal chemoreceptors","B":"Wall stretch receptors","C":"Visceral pain nociceptors","D":"Mucosal thermoreceptors","E":"Carotid baroreceptors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vagovagal afferents start from mechanoreceptors that detect stretch or distension and from chemoreceptors that detect pH, osmolarity, nutrients, or mucosal irritation; with little distension, digestion products act on mucosal chemoreceptors. Stretch receptors need distension. Pain nociceptors are not the afferents of this reflex. Thermoreceptors and carotid baroreceptors are not afferents of this reflex either. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, afferent pathway and gastric secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagovagal reflexes stimulate parietal cells through ACh and G cells through GRP (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: luminal digestion products trigger the gastric phase of acid secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — phases of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":3,"stem":"An 83-year-old man has a vagovagal reflex studied after a meal. Where are the cell bodies of the vagal afferent neurons that carry the sensory limb?","choices":{"A":"Celiac ganglion","B":"Myenteric plexus","C":"Geniculate ganglion","D":"Ventral horn","E":"Nodose ganglion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Afferent axons traveling with the vagus have their cell bodies in the nodose (inferior vagal) ganglion, and their central processes end in the solitary nucleus of the medulla. The celiac ganglion is a sympathetic prevertebral ganglion. The myenteric plexus holds enteric neurons. The geniculate ganglion belongs to the facial nerve. The ventral horn holds motor neurons. Sources: Foundations of Osteopathic Medicine Ch. 6 (vagal afferent cell bodies in the nodose ganglion, lines 2891–2893 and 5126–5127).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in a vagovagal reflex both limbs travel in the vagus (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vagal afferents also sense plasma glucose and portal osmotic pressure (Foundations of Osteopathic Medicine Ch. 6).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — medullary swallowing center","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":3,"stem":"A 20-year-old woman joins a motility study of the enteric neurons that relax muscle ahead of a bolus. Which transmitter is released by these inhibitory motor neurons?","choices":{"A":"ACh","B":"GRP","C":"ATP","D":"CCK","E":"Gastrin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Inhibitory enteric motor neurons use ATP among other cotransmitters to relax muscle ahead of the bolus and relax GI sphincters. ACh is excitatory and contracts muscle behind the bolus. GRP stimulates G cells. CCK and gastrin are hormones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, motility control via the myenteric plexus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the myenteric plexus regulates muscle tone and peristaltic wave velocity (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: excitatory enteric neurons contract muscle behind a bolus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — LES relaxation ahead of the bolus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman has visceral abdominal pain carried by afferent fibers that run with the splanchnic sympathetic nerves. Where are the cell bodies of these afferent neurons?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal root ganglia","B":"Inferior vagal ganglion","C":"Celiac ganglion","D":"Myenteric plexus","E":"Dorsal horn of cord"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Visceral afferent fibers associated with the splanchnic nerves have their cell bodies in the dorsal root ganglia of spinal nerves, and their central processes end in the dorsal horn. The inferior vagal (nodose) ganglion holds vagal afferent cell bodies. The celiac ganglion holds sympathetic postganglionic neurons. The myenteric plexus holds enteric neurons. The dorsal horn receives the central processes rather than the cell bodies. Sources: Foundations of Osteopathic Medicine Ch. 6 (visceral afferents with splanchnic nerves, lines 2888–2890 and 5137–5138).","boardPrep":"Step 1: splanchnic visceral afferents end in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord (Foundations of Osteopathic Medicine Ch. 6). COMLEX: about half of sympathetic nerve fibers are afferent (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":5,"title":"SDL 5 — Physiology - GI Hormones, Paracrines, & Appetite","questions":[{"id":"sdl05_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":1,"stem":"According to Costanzo, how does a GI paracrine reach its target cell?","choices":{"A":"Portal then systemic circulation","B":"Release into a synaptic cleft","C":"Local interstitial diffusion","D":"Secretion into the gut lumen","E":"Retrograde axonal transport"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Paracrines are secreted by endocrine cells of the GI mucosa and act locally, diffusing short distances through interstitial fluid to adjacent target cells. Travel through the portal and then systemic circulation describes hormones. Release into a synaptic cleft after an action potential describes neurocrines. Secretion into the lumen describes exocrine products such as enzymes. Retrograde axonal transport carries signals within neurons, not between cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, paracrines); Costanzo Ch. 8 (classification of GI peptides).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin and histamine are the classic GI paracrines (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: H2 blockers interrupt a paracrine signal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric neurons and their neurocrines","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which GI peptide acts as a neurocrine released by enteric neurons?","choices":{"A":"GIP","B":"VIP","C":"CCK","D":"PYY","E":"Gastrin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) is synthesized in enteric neurons and released at synapses, so it is a neurocrine. GIP, CCK, and gastrin are official GI hormones secreted by K, I, and G cells into the blood. Peptide YY is a candidate hormone from L cells of the ileum and colon. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, neurocrines); Costanzo Ch. 8 (neurocrines).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a VIPoma causes watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors). COMLEX: VIP relaxes GI smooth muscle and sphincters.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric neurons and their neurocrines","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":1,"stem":"Which paracrine is released by enterochromaffin-like cells in the oxyntic mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Histamine","B":"Somatostatin","C":"Gastrin","D":"Secretin","E":"Ghrelin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ECL cells in the oxyntic region release histamine, which diffuses to neighboring parietal cells and binds H2 receptors. Somatostatin is a paracrine, but it comes from D cells. Gastrin is a hormone from antral G cells. Secretin is a hormone from duodenal S cells. Ghrelin is a hormone from gastric oxyntic cells that acts on the hypothalamus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, paracrines, and section 6, ghrelin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: histamine binds H2 receptors on parietal cells through a Gs cascade (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acid secretion pathways). COMLEX: H2 receptor antagonists reduce acid by blocking this paracrine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric neurons and their neurocrines","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":1,"stem":"According to Costanzo, S cells release secretin in response to which stimulus?","choices":{"A":"Amino acids in the stomach","B":"Glucose in the jejunum","C":"Distension of the antrum","D":"Vagal release of GRP","E":"Acid in the duodenum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Secretin is released by duodenal S cells when acidic chyme below pH 4.5 enters the duodenum, and fatty acids also stimulate it. Amino acids in the stomach, antral distension, and vagal GRP release are stimuli for gastrin. Glucose in the small intestine stimulates GIP. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, secretin); Costanzo Ch. 8 (secretin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin increases pancreatic HCO3- secretion and decreases gastric acid secretion (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: secretin also inhibits gastrin release.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":1,"stem":"Fatty acids entering the duodenum release a hormone from I cells that contracts the gallbladder. Which hormone is this?","choices":{"A":"GIP","B":"PYY","C":"VIP","D":"CCK","E":"Gastrin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cholecystokinin (CCK) is secreted by I cells of the duodenum and jejunum in response to monoglycerides, fatty acids, small peptides, and amino acids; it contracts the gallbladder, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, and stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion. GIP comes from K cells and stimulates insulin release. PYY comes from L cells and decreases appetite. VIP is a neurocrine. Gastrin comes from G cells and stimulates acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, cholecystokinin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK increases pancreatic secretion and gallbladder contraction and decreases gastric emptying (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: fatty meals provoke biliary colic through CCK.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":1,"stem":"According to Costanzo, one GI hormone is secreted in response to all three nutrient types: glucose, amino acids, and fatty acids. Which cells secrete it?","choices":{"A":"I cells","B":"S cells","C":"K cells","D":"G cells","E":"L cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"GIP is secreted by K cells of the duodenal, jejunal, and ileal mucosa and, unlike the other GI hormones, is released by glucose, amino acids, and fatty acids. I cells secrete CCK in response to fats and protein products. S cells secrete secretin in response to acid and fatty acids. G cells secrete gastrin in response to peptides and amino acids. L cells secrete GLP-1 and PYY. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (GIP); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, GIP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP increases insulin release and decreases gastric acid secretion (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: GIP is an incretin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":1,"stem":"According to Costanzo, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome can cause steatorrhea. Which mechanism explains it?","choices":{"A":"Loss of bile acid secretion","B":"Acid inactivation of lipase","C":"Gastrin block of CCK release","D":"Villous atrophy from gluten","E":"Bacterial overgrowth"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, excess acid acidifies the intestinal lumen and inactivates pancreatic lipase, so dietary fat is not digested and appears in the stool. Bile acid secretion is not reduced by gastrin. Gastrin does not block CCK release. Villous atrophy from gluten is celiac disease. Bacterial overgrowth causes malabsorption by a different mechanism. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrinoma presents with refractory ulcers, diarrhea, steatorrhea, and weight loss (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: gastrinoma is associated with MEN1.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":1,"stem":"In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, chronically high gastrin levels change the gastric oxyntic mucosa in which way?","choices":{"A":"Atrophy and parietal loss","B":"Intestinal type metaplasia","C":"Fibrosis and scarring of glands","D":"Necrosis and mucosal sloughing","E":"Hypertrophy and hyperplasia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gastrin has a trophic effect, so chronically elevated gastrin causes hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the oxyntic mucosa with marked parietal cell hyperplasia. Atrophy with parietal cell loss occurs after antrectomy or in autoimmune gastritis. Intestinal metaplasia follows chronic gastritis. Fibrosis and necrosis are not effects of gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, mucosal effect); Costanzo Ch. 8 (trophic effect of gastrin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin increases growth of the gastric mucosa (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: thickened gastric folds with refractory ulcers suggest gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":1,"stem":"Which peptic ulcer location most strongly suggests Zollinger-Ellison syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Duodenal bulb","B":"Lesser curvature","C":"Gastric antrum","D":"Proximal jejunum","E":"Gastric cardia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome causes severe recurrent ulcers in the duodenum and stomach and frequently in unusual distal sites such as the jejunum, because excess unbuffered acid reaches the small intestine. Duodenal bulb ulcers are common in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome but also in ordinary H. pylori disease, so they are less specific. Lesser curvature and antral ulcers are typical gastric ulcers. The cardia is an uncommon ulcer site with no link to gastrinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, ulcer location).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal ulcers are most common in gastrinoma, but jejunal ulcers are the most specific clue (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: ulcers that do not respond to therapy suggest gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":1,"stem":"Oral glucose releases more insulin than an intravenous glucose load that produces the same blood glucose concentration. Which hormone is mainly responsible?","choices":{"A":"GIP from K cells","B":"CCK from I cells","C":"Secretin from S cells","D":"Gastrin from G cells","E":"Ghrelin from the stomach"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Oral glucose stimulates GIP release from K cells, and GIP binds receptors on pancreatic beta cells to amplify glucose-induced insulin secretion; GLP-1 from L cells contributes. Intravenous glucose bypasses the gut and relies on direct beta cell sensing. CCK, secretin, and gastrin are not incretins in the lecture. Ghrelin increases appetite and is suppressed by food. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4, incretin action); Costanzo Ch. 8 (GIP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP increases insulin release and is stimulated by oral glucose (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: oral glucose produces a larger insulin response than intravenous glucose.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — GIP and duodenal control of the stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":1,"stem":"The greater insulin response to oral glucose than to an equivalent intravenous glucose load has which name?","choices":{"A":"Somogyi effect","B":"Incretin effect","C":"Dawn phenomenon","D":"Glucose toxicity","E":"Insulin resistance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The larger insulin release after oral glucose is the incretin effect, mediated by GIP and GLP-1. The Somogyi effect is rebound hyperglycemia after nocturnal hypoglycemia. The dawn phenomenon is early morning hyperglycemia from counterregulatory hormones. Glucose toxicity is beta cell impairment from chronic hyperglycemia. Insulin resistance is a reduced tissue response to insulin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4, incretin effect).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP and GLP-1 are incretins (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: GLP-1 analogs treat type 2 diabetes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — GIP and duodenal control of the stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":1,"stem":"Besides GIP, the incretin GLP-1 amplifies insulin release. Which cells secrete GLP-1?","choices":{"A":"K cells of duodenum and jejunum","B":"I cells of duodenum and jejunum","C":"S cells of the duodenum","D":"L cells of ileum and colon","E":"G cells of the gastric antrum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"GLP-1 is cleaved from proglucagon and secreted by L cells of the distal ileum and colon in response to carbohydrates and fats. K cells secrete GIP. I cells secrete CCK. S cells secrete secretin. Antral G cells secrete gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4, incretin action); Costanzo Ch. 8 (GLP-1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 increases insulin secretion, decreases gastric emptying, and decreases appetite (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: GLP-1 analogs promote weight loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — GIP and duodenal control of the stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Somatostatin from antral D cells diffuses to neighboring G cells. What effect does it have on them?","choices":{"A":"Stimulates gastrin release","B":"Inhibits gastrin release","C":"Stimulates histamine release","D":"Blocks gastrin receptors","E":"Destroys G cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Somatostatin binds receptors on G cells and suppresses gastrin synthesis and release. Stimulation of gastrin release is produced by peptides, distension, and vagal GRP. Somatostatin also inhibits, rather than stimulates, histamine release. It acts on G cells, not on gastrin receptors of parietal cells. It inhibits G cells without destroying them. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin effect on gastrin secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin decreases gastric acid and pepsinogen secretion and many hormones (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: octreotide is a somatostatin analog.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — somatostatin, histamine, and acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, D cells release somatostatin in response to which luminal condition?","choices":{"A":"High gastric pH","B":"Gastric distension","C":"Low gastric pH","D":"Luminal amino acids","E":"Vagal GRP release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"D cells release somatostatin in response to low luminal pH, providing negative feedback that suppresses gastrin and acid. A high gastric pH, distension, luminal amino acids, and vagal GRP stimulate gastrin release instead. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (low pH stimulates somatostatin, which inhibits parietal and G cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin is increased by acid and decreased by vagal stimulation (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: this feedback is lost in gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — somatostatin, histamine, and acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Histamine from ECL cells increases parietal cell acid secretion through which receptor and second messenger?","choices":{"A":"H2 receptor and cAMP","B":"H1 receptor and IP3","C":"CCK2 receptor and calcium","D":"M3 receptor and calcium","E":"SST receptor and Gi"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Histamine binds H2 receptors on parietal cells, activating Gs and adenylyl cyclase to raise cAMP and drive the H+/K+-ATPase. H1 receptors act through IP3 in other tissues. Gastrin acts on CCK2 receptors through calcium. ACh acts on M3 receptors through calcium. A somatostatin receptor coupled to Gi lowers cAMP, which is how somatostatin inhibits acid secretion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, histamine effect on parietal cell acid secretion); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (ECL cell histamine and D cell somatostatin pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: histamine potentiates the acid stimulating effects of ACh and gastrin (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: H2 blockers reduce acid stimulated by all three secretagogues.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — somatostatin, histamine, and acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":1,"stem":"Which peptide rises before meals and during fasting to increase food intake?","choices":{"A":"Leptin","B":"Peptide YY","C":"Ghrelin","D":"Insulin","E":"Serotonin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ghrelin is secreted by gastric oxyntic cells before meals and during fasting and is orexigenic. Leptin from adipocytes, PYY from L cells, insulin from beta cells, and serotonin are anorexigenic in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, hormones and peptides influencing appetite).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin is increased in the fasting state and in Prader-Willi syndrome and decreased after gastric bypass (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: ghrelin also increases gastric motility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":1,"stem":"Leptin is an anorexigenic signal secreted in proportion to body fat mass. Which cells secrete it?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocytes","B":"Enterocytes","C":"Beta cells","D":"Myocytes","E":"Adipocytes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Leptin is secreted by adipocytes in proportion to fat mass and activates POMC neurons while inhibiting NPY and AGRP neurons. Hepatocytes and myocytes are not listed sources of appetite peptides. Enterocytes do not secrete leptin, although L and I cells of the intestine secrete PYY, GLP-1, and CCK. Beta cells secrete insulin, another anorexigenic signal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, anorexigenic hormones).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leptin and insulin stimulate the anorexigenic pathway (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: leptin rises with fat mass.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":1,"stem":"In the orexigenic pathway, AGRP released from arcuate neurons acts at melanocortin-4 receptors in the paraventricular nucleus. How does it act there?","choices":{"A":"Competitive antagonist","B":"Partial receptor agonist","C":"Full receptor agonist","D":"Positive allosteric enhancer","E":"Irreversible agonist"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AGRP is a competitive antagonist at MC4R in the paraventricular nucleus, blocking satiety signaling while NPY activates the lateral hypothalamus. Partial, full, and irreversible agonists would activate MC4R and reduce appetite, which is the role of alpha-MSH. A positive allosteric enhancer would increase MC4R signaling. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, orexigenic pathway).","boardPrep":"Step 1: MC4R defects cause hyperphagia and early onset obesity (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: AGRP and NPY increase appetite.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with reflux takes a drug that blocks receptors for a substance released by ECL cells. Which mode of signaling does the drug interrupt?","choices":{"A":"Paracrine diffusion","B":"Endocrine circulation","C":"Neurocrine release","D":"Autocrine feedback","E":"Luminal secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Histamine from ECL cells diffuses through interstitial fluid to adjacent parietal cells, which is paracrine signaling. Endocrine signaling uses the portal and systemic circulation. Neurocrine signaling is release by neurons into a synapse. Autocrine signaling acts on the releasing cell itself. Luminal secretion releases products into the gut lumen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, paracrines); Costanzo Ch. 8 (paracrines).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H2 receptor antagonists block histamine on parietal cells (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: H2 blockers are used for reflux and peptic ulcer disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — acid suppressing drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old woman eats a fatty meal. A peptide from duodenal I cells contracts her gallbladder. How is this peptide classified?","choices":{"A":"Paracrine","B":"GI hormone","C":"Neurocrine","D":"Autocrine","E":"Exocrine enzyme"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CCK is secreted by I cells into the portal circulation, passes through the liver into the systemic circulation, and acts on distant targets, so it is a GI hormone. Paracrines act locally by diffusion. Neurocrines are released by neurons at synapses. Autocrines act on the cell that secretes them. Exocrine enzymes are secreted into the lumen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, hormones); Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastrointestinal hormones).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the four official GI hormones are gastrin, CCK, secretin, and GIP (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: CCK release explains postprandial biliary colic.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — CCK and duodenal control of the stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old man in a physiology study has electrical stimulation of the vagus nerve, and his serum gastrin rises even after atropine. The mediator is a peptide released by an action potential. How is this mediator classified?","choices":{"A":"Circulating hormone","B":"Paracrine peptide","C":"Neurocrine peptide","D":"Autocrine signal","E":"Luminal secretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gastrin-releasing peptide is released by postganglionic vagal and enteric neurons onto G cells after an action potential, so it is a neurocrine; it is not cholinergic, which is why atropine does not block it. Circulating hormones travel in the blood. Paracrines are released by endocrine cells and diffuse locally. Autocrine signals act on the releasing cell. Luminal secretions enter the gut lumen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, neurocrines).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagal stimulation increases gastrin through GRP (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: GRP is also called bombesin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes and gastric secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":2,"stem":"A 43-year-old man has a study in which acidic chyme with a pH of 4 is infused into his duodenum. His pancreatic juice volume and bicarbonate concentration rise. Which hormone is responsible?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin","B":"Motilin","C":"Ghrelin","D":"Secretin","E":"Somatostatin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acid below pH 4.5 in the duodenum releases secretin from S cells, which stimulates pancreatic ductal and biliary bicarbonate secretion to neutralize the acid. Gastrin release is inhibited by acid. Motilin is released during fasting and drives migrating motor complexes. Ghrelin increases appetite. Somatostatin is also released by acid but inhibits pancreatic secretion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, secretin); Costanzo Ch. 8 (secretin, motilin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin increases pancreatic HCO3- and decreases gastric acid (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: secretin is secreted by S cells in the duodenal mucosa (lecture SDL 5).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — secretin and duodenal acid","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man has hepatobiliary scintigraphy and receives an analog of the duodenal hormone that contracts the gallbladder. Which additional effect does this hormone produce?","choices":{"A":"Faster gastric emptying","B":"Increased gastric acid","C":"Slower gastric emptying","D":"Contraction of Oddi sphincter","E":"Inhibition of enzyme release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"CCK contracts the gallbladder, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion, and delays gastric emptying so that fat enters the duodenum slowly. Faster emptying is the opposite effect. Increased gastric acid is an action of gastrin. CCK relaxes rather than contracts the sphincter of Oddi. CCK stimulates, rather than inhibits, enzyme release. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, cholecystokinin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK decreases gastric emptying and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: CCK analogs are used to measure gallbladder ejection fraction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — CCK and gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man eats a steak dinner, and his serum gastrin rises within minutes. Which luminal component stimulates his antral G cells?","choices":{"A":"Intact dietary triglycerides","B":"Small peptides and amino acids","C":"Free fatty acids and monoglycerides","D":"Luminal acid below pH 3","E":"Glucose and galactose"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Small peptides and amino acids in the stomach, distension, and vagal GRP stimulate gastrin release. Intact triglycerides do not release gastrin. Fatty acids and monoglycerides in the duodenum release CCK. Acid below pH 3 inhibits gastrin release. Glucose stimulates GIP rather than gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, gastrin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin is increased by distension, alkalinization, amino acids, peptides, and vagal GRP, and decreased by gastric acid; the lecture uses pH below 3, while First Aid gives pH below 1.5 (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: protein meals raise gastrin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — gastric phase of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man has ulcers in the distal duodenum, chronic diarrhea, and weight loss. His fasting serum gastrin is markedly elevated while his gastric pH is low. Which tumor is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Gastrinoma","B":"Insulinoma","C":"VIPoma","D":"Somatostatinoma","E":"Glucagonoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Jejunal ulcers with high gastrin and low gastric pH indicate Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, caused by a gastrinoma. An insulinoma causes hypoglycemia. A VIPoma causes watery diarrhea with achlorhydria, so gastric pH would be high. A somatostatinoma is a delta cell tumor that does not raise gastrin. A glucagonoma causes diabetes and necrolytic migratory erythema. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, responsible tumor); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (gastrinoma and pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a gastrinoma is a gastrin secreting neuroendocrine tumor of the pancreas or duodenum (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: gastrinomas often lie in the gastrinoma triangle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — insulinoma, gastrinoma, glucagonoma, and VIPoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old man has a gastrinoma with a gastric pH near 1. In a healthy stomach, this acidity would sharply reduce gastrin release. Why does his gastrin stay high?","choices":{"A":"Somatostatin excess","B":"Parietal cell autoimmunity","C":"Loss of histamine receptors","D":"Tumor ignores pH feedback","E":"Vagal hyperactivity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastrin from the tumor is not subject to negative feedback by low gastric pH, so acid secretion becomes massive and autonomous. Somatostatin excess would lower gastrin. Parietal cell autoimmunity raises gastrin by removing acid, which is the opposite pH. Loss of histamine receptors would reduce acid. Vagal hyperactivity increases gastrin through GRP but would still be subject to acid feedback. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, H+ secretion effect).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin is decreased by gastric acid in normal physiology (lecture pH below 3; First Aid pH below 1.5) and increased in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: treatment uses proton pump inhibitors and tumor resection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — feedback control of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has recurrent duodenal ulcers despite treatment. Endoscopy shows prominent thickened folds in the gastric body, and his serum gastrin is markedly elevated. Which mechanism explains the thickened folds?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin induced edema","B":"Trophic effect of gastrin","C":"Lymphocytic infiltration","D":"Foveolar mucous hyperplasia","E":"Submucosal fibrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronically elevated gastrin from a gastrinoma has a trophic effect that causes hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the oxyntic mucosa, including parietal cell hyperplasia, which thickens the folds. Gastrin acts by growth of the mucosa, not by edema. Lymphocytic infiltration is a feature of gastritis or lymphoma. Foveolar mucous hyperplasia without high gastrin is Ménétrier disease. Submucosal fibrosis follows ulcer healing, not gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, mucosal effect); Costanzo Ch. 8 (trophic effect of gastrin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin stimulates trophic growth of the gastric oxyntic mucosa (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: refractory ulcers with thick folds warrant a fasting gastrin level.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman in a study drinks a glucose solution on one day and receives intravenous glucose adjusted to match her plasma glucose on another day. Her plasma insulin is much higher after the oral dose. What explains the difference?","choices":{"A":"Faster glucose absorption","B":"Higher peak glucose","C":"Vagal insulin inhibition","D":"Gut incretin release","E":"Reduced insulin clearance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Oral glucose stimulates GIP from K cells and GLP-1 from L cells, which amplify glucose-induced insulin secretion; intravenous glucose bypasses the gut. Faster absorption and a higher peak glucose are excluded because plasma glucose was matched. Vagal inhibition of insulin is not the mechanism. Altered insulin clearance does not explain the incretin effect. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4, route comparison and incretin action).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP release is stimulated by oral glucose (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: an oral glucose load is used by cells more rapidly than an intravenous load.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — GIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":2,"stem":"A 51-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes starts a weekly injected analog of the incretin secreted by ileal L cells. Besides improving insulin secretion, which effect helps her lose weight?","choices":{"A":"Increased ghrelin","B":"Increased glucagon release","C":"Reduced appetite","D":"Faster gastric emptying","E":"Lower leptin levels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"GLP-1 analogs amplify glucose-dependent insulin release, slow gastric emptying, and are anorexigenic, reducing appetite. Increased ghrelin would promote weight gain. GLP-1 inhibits rather than increases glucagon release. GLP-1 slows rather than speeds gastric emptying. Lower leptin would increase appetite. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4 and 6, GLP-1); Costanzo Ch. 8 (GLP-1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 analogs treat type 2 diabetes and promote weight loss (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: nausea from slowed gastric emptying is a common adverse effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — hormones that slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman eats a mixed meal, and her duodenal K cells release a hormone that amplifies her insulin secretion. Which other action does this hormone have?","choices":{"A":"Stimulates gastric acid","B":"Contracts the gallbladder","C":"Speeds gastric emptying","D":"Increases appetite","E":"Inhibits gastric acid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"GIP from K cells stimulates insulin release and also inhibits gastric H+ secretion, inhibits gastrin, and slows gastric motility. Stimulation of acid is an action of gastrin. Gallbladder contraction is an action of CCK. GIP slows rather than speeds gastric emptying. GIP is listed as anorexigenic, so it does not increase appetite. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, GIP, and section 6, anorexigenic hormones); Costanzo Ch. 8 (GIP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP decreases gastric H+ secretion and increases insulin release (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: GIP was first named gastric inhibitory peptide.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — inhibitors of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 56-year-old woman with acromegaly starts octreotide, an analog of a D cell paracrine. Her serum gastrin falls. Which mechanism explains the fall?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of G cells","B":"Inhibition of parietal cells","C":"Blockade of H2 receptors","D":"Rise in gastric pH","E":"Loss of vagal GRP"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Octreotide mimics somatostatin, which binds receptors on G cells and suppresses gastrin release. Inhibition of parietal cells lowers acid but would raise, not lower, gastrin through loss of acid feedback. Blockade of H2 receptors is the action of H2 antagonists. A rise in gastric pH would increase gastrin. Octreotide does not remove vagal GRP. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin effect on gastrin secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: octreotide is a somatostatin analog used for acromegaly, carcinoid syndrome, VIPoma, and variceal bleeding (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: octreotide can cause gallstones.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — somatostatin","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman takes a drug that blocks histamine receptors on parietal cells, and her acid secretion falls. Which second messenger falls in her parietal cells?","choices":{"A":"IP3 and calcium","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Diacylglycerol","D":"Nitric oxide","E":"Cyclic AMP"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Histamine binds H2 receptors, activating Gs and adenylyl cyclase to raise cyclic AMP and protein kinase A activity, which drives the H+/K+-ATPase; blocking H2 receptors lowers cyclic AMP. IP3 and calcium mediate ACh and gastrin. Cyclic GMP and nitric oxide mediate smooth muscle relaxation. Diacylglycerol accompanies IP3 in the calcium pathway. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, histamine effect).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H2 receptors use a Gs cascade to increase H+/K+-ATPase activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acid secretion pathways). COMLEX: H2 blockers reduce nocturnal acid secretion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — H2 receptor antagonists","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man has antral H. pylori gastritis and a duodenal ulcer. His serum gastrin is elevated and his gastric acid output is high. Loss or suppression of which gastric cell best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"G cells","B":"Parietal cells","C":"D cells","D":"ECL cells","E":"Chief cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Antral gastritis causes loss or suppression of D cell somatostatin (Bootcamp describes D cell destruction; Costanzo describes inhibited somatostatin secretion), which removes inhibition of G cells and parietal cells and raises gastrin and acid. Loss of G cells would lower gastrin. Loss of parietal cells would lower acid. Loss of ECL cells would lower histamine and acid. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and do not control gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (destruction of antral D cells lowers somatostatin and raises acid).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal ulcers are associated with H. pylori and gastrinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: eradicating H. pylori lowers ulcer recurrence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy has severe hyperphagia and obesity, and his serum leptin is undetectable because of a gene mutation. Which change in his arcuate nucleus explains his hunger?","choices":{"A":"Less POMC neuron activity","B":"Less AGRP neuron activity","C":"Less NPY neuron activity","D":"More MC4R activation","E":"Lower ghrelin secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Leptin activates POMC and CART neurons and inhibits NPY and AGRP neurons. Without leptin, POMC activity falls, alpha-MSH no longer activates MC4R, and NPY and AGRP activity rises, increasing appetite. Less AGRP activity, less NPY activity, and more MC4R activation would reduce appetite. Lower ghrelin would also reduce hunger. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, anorexigenic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (leptin stimulates the anorexigenic pathway).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leptin activates POMC and CART neurons while inhibiting NPY and AGRP neurons (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: leptin acts on the arcuate nucleus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 4 — systemic consequences of obesity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl with Prader-Willi syndrome has constant hunger, and her plasma ghrelin is markedly elevated. Which arcuate neurons does ghrelin activate to increase her food intake?","choices":{"A":"POMC and CART neurons","B":"NPY and AGRP neurons","C":"Lateral hypothalamic neurons","D":"Paraventricular MC4R neurons","E":"Brainstem NTS neurons"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ghrelin stimulates NPY and AGRP neurons in the arcuate nucleus; NPY activates the lateral hypothalamus and AGRP blocks MC4R in the paraventricular nucleus. POMC and CART neurons are anorexigenic and are activated by leptin and insulin. The lateral hypothalamus and paraventricular nucleus are downstream targets, not arcuate neurons. The NTS receives MC4R projections in the satiety pathway. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, orexigenic pathway).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin is increased in Prader-Willi syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: ghrelin rises with fasting and sleep deprivation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, stomach).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — gastric enteroendocrine cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman reports much less hunger after a gastric bypass operation. Which hormonal change most likely contributes?","choices":{"A":"Higher orexin release","B":"Lower PYY levels","C":"Higher NPY release","D":"Reduced leptin action","E":"Lower ghrelin levels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ghrelin is decreased after gastric bypass, reducing stimulation of NPY and AGRP neurons and lowering hunger. Higher orexin release, higher NPY release, and reduced leptin action would increase hunger, because orexins and NPY are orexigenic. PYY is anorexigenic, so lower PYY would also increase hunger. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, appetite hormones); First Aid 2025 GI (ghrelin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin is decreased after gastric bypass surgery (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: PYY decreases appetite by inhibiting ghrelin secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — gastric enteroendocrine cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has a tumor of the cells that secrete the GI paracrine somatostatin. Besides the GI mucosa and hypothalamus, where do these cells normally reside?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic islets","B":"Adrenal medulla","C":"Anterior pituitary","D":"Thyroid follicles","E":"Renal cortex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Somatostatin is secreted by D cells throughout the GI mucosa, by delta cells of the pancreatic islets, and by the hypothalamus. The adrenal medulla secretes catecholamines. The anterior pituitary is a target of hypothalamic somatostatin, which inhibits growth hormone, rather than a source. Thyroid follicles make thyroid hormone. The renal cortex is not a somatostatin source. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, paracrines).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatinomas arise from pancreatic delta cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors). COMLEX: somatostatin inhibits growth hormone release from the anterior pituitary.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — gastric enteroendocrine cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman in a pancreatic function study receives secretin, and her bicarbonate output rises modestly. Adding CCK raises bicarbonate output much further. Which action of CCK explains this?","choices":{"A":"Stimulation of gastrin release","B":"Gallbladder contraction","C":"Potentiation of secretin","D":"Inhibition of secretin release","E":"Stimulation of ghrelin release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"CCK potentiates secretin-induced pancreatic bicarbonate secretion, so the two together produce more bicarbonate than secretin by itself. CCK does not stimulate gastrin release or inhibit secretin release. CCK is anorexigenic, so it does not stimulate ghrelin. Gallbladder contraction is a real CCK action, but it does not raise pancreatic bicarbonate output. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, cholecystokinin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK increases pancreatic secretion and gallbladder contraction (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: secretin mainly raises pancreatic bicarbonate while CCK mainly raises enzyme output (lecture SDL 5).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — CCK and secretin","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has hypercalcemia from parathyroid hyperplasia and peptic ulcers that do not heal with treatment. His fasting gastrin is high and his gastric pH is low. Which test best confirms the suspected tumor?","choices":{"A":"Oral glucose tolerance test","B":"Urea breath test","C":"Serum glucagon level","D":"Secretin stimulation test","E":"Dexamethasone suppression"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Refractory ulcers with high gastrin, low gastric pH, and MEN1 features suggest a gastrinoma, and the secretin stimulation test follows fasting gastrin and gastric pH in the workup. An oral glucose tolerance test evaluates diabetes. A urea breath test detects H. pylori. A serum glucagon level is used for glucagonoma. Dexamethasone suppression evaluates Cushing syndrome. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (gastrinoma diagnostics: fasting serum gastrin and gastric pH, then secretin stimulation test).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zollinger Ellison syndrome is caused by a gastrin secreting neuroendocrine tumor in the pancreas or duodenum (lecture SDL 7). COMLEX: screen for MEN1 in a patient with gastrinoma and hypercalcemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":3,"stem":"In a 74-year-old man, oral glucose releases gut incretins that amplify insulin secretion. Through which receptor type do these incretins act on his pancreatic beta cells?","choices":{"A":"G protein coupled receptors","B":"Receptor tyrosine kinases","C":"Ligand gated ion channels","D":"Nuclear hormone receptors","E":"JAK STAT cytokine receptors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"GIP and GLP-1 bind G protein coupled receptors on beta cells to amplify glucose-induced insulin secretion. Receptor tyrosine kinases mediate insulin action on target tissues. Ligand gated ion channels mediate fast synaptic transmission. Nuclear hormone receptors bind steroid and thyroid hormones. JAK STAT receptors bind cytokines and growth hormone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4, incretin action).","boardPrep":"Step 1: oral glucose releases GIP and GLP 1 from intestinal K and L cells (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: incretins amplify insulin release in a glucose-dependent manner.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — GIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman takes famotidine, and her acid response to a protein meal falls. Which signals normally stimulate release of the paracrine whose action this drug opposes?","choices":{"A":"Secretin and GIP","B":"Gastrin and ACh","C":"Somatostatin and PGE2","D":"Leptin and insulin","E":"VIP and nitric oxide"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ECL cells release histamine in response to gastrin and ACh, and histamine then binds H2 receptors on parietal cells and potentiates the direct effects of ACh and gastrin. Secretin and GIP inhibit acid secretion. Somatostatin and prostaglandins inhibit acid secretion. Leptin and insulin are anorexigenic signals. VIP and nitric oxide relax GI smooth muscle. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, histamine effect on parietal cell acid secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acid secretion is stimulated by ACh, gastrin, and histamine and inhibited by somatostatin, secretin, prostaglandins, and GIP (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, parietal cells). COMLEX: H2 blockers blunt acid responses to meals.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":3,"stem":"A 16-year-old boy has early onset obesity and hyperphagia from a loss of function mutation in melanocortin-4 receptors. Besides increased appetite, which change is expected?","choices":{"A":"Higher sympathetic tone","B":"Lower ghrelin secretion","C":"Higher leptin sensitivity","D":"Faster gastric emptying","E":"Lower energy expenditure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MC4R activation in the paraventricular nucleus decreases food intake and increases energy expenditure through projections to the NTS and increased sympathetic activity. Loss of MC4R function therefore lowers energy expenditure and sympathetic tone. Lower ghrelin would reduce appetite. MC4R loss does not increase leptin sensitivity, since leptin acts upstream of MC4R. Gastric emptying is not an MC4R effect in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, anorexigenic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (ghrelin from the gastric fundus is orexigenic and stimulates NPY and AGRP neurons (lecture SDL 5).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin from the gastric fundus is orexigenic and stimulates NPY and AGRP neurons (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: alpha-MSH acts at MC4R to promote satiety.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 4 — systemic consequences of obesity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl05_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":4,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman has new diabetes and gallstones and no prior abdominal surgery. CT shows a mass in the pancreas, and she has lost weight. Which of the following sets of laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Serum gastrin","Gastric acid","Fecal fat"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↑","↑"],"B":["↓","↓","↑"],"C":["↓","↓","↔"],"D":["↔","↔","↔"],"E":["↑","↓","↔"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"A somatostatinoma releases excess somatostatin from tumor delta cells, so Choice B (↓ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↑ fecal fat) is correct. Somatostatin suppresses gastrin release from G cells and inhibits parietal cell acid secretion, causing hypochlorhydria, and it inhibits pancreatic secretion, causing steatorrhea; the diabetes and gallstones reflect inhibition of insulin release and gallbladder contraction. Choice A (↑ serum gastrin, ↑ gastric acid, ↑ fecal fat) is a gastrinoma, which raises gastrin and acid, and the acid entering the duodenum inactivates pancreatic lipase and causes steatorrhea. Choice C (↓ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↔ fecal fat) follows resection of the gastric antrum, which removes the G cells so gastrin and acid fall. Choice D (↔ serum gastrin, ↔ gastric acid, ↔ fecal fat) is a glucagonoma, which causes diabetes, weight loss, and necrolytic migratory erythema without changing gastric secretion. Choice E (↑ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↔ fecal fat) is autoimmune atrophic gastritis, in which loss of parietal cells lowers acid and removes feedback on G cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin inhibits gastrin secretion; section 3, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome); Costanzo Ch. 8 (antral resection decreases gastrin and H+ secretion; acid inactivates pancreatic lipase in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome); Robbins (SDL 19 pathology text: somatostatinomas cause diabetes, cholelithiasis, steatorrhea, and hypochlorhydria); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors: gastrinoma steatorrhea, glucagonoma, and somatostatinoma); First Aid 2025 GI (somatostatin inhibits gastric acid, pancreatic secretion, gallbladder contraction, and insulin release). Rows C, D, and E list ↔ for analytes their sources do not describe as changed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin inhibits gastric acid, pancreatic secretion, gallbladder contraction, and insulin and glucagon release (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: octreotide, a somatostatin analog, can cause gallstones and steatorrhea.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":6,"title":"SDL 6 — Physiology - GI Motility: Chewing, Swallowing, Esophageal and Gastric Motility","questions":[{"id":"sdl06_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which sensory receptors detect the bolus and initiate the involuntary swallowing reflex?","choices":{"A":"Gastric stretch receptors","B":"Carotid sinus baroreceptors","C":"Laryngeal chemoreceptors","D":"Esophageal pain receptors","E":"Tonsillar pillar receptors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tactile receptors around the pharyngeal opening, especially on the tonsillar pillars, detect the bolus pushed back by the tongue and start the automatic swallowing reflex. Gastric stretch receptors drive gastric reflexes. Carotid sinus baroreceptors sense blood pressure. Laryngeal chemoreceptors trigger cough, not swallowing. Esophageal pain receptors do not start swallowing. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, voluntary stage); Costanzo Ch. 8 (oral phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the oral phase of swallowing is voluntary and the pharyngeal and esophageal phases are reflexes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: oropharyngeal dysphagia points to neural or striated muscle disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — striated and smooth muscle of the esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cranial nerves carry the afferent limb of the swallowing reflex from the pharynx to the medulla?","choices":{"A":"CN VII and CN XII","B":"CN X and CN XI","C":"CN III and CN IV","D":"CN V and CN IX","E":"CN VI and CN XII"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sensory impulses from pharyngeal tactile receptors travel in the trigeminal (CN V) and glossopharyngeal (CN IX) nerves to the tractus solitarius in the medullary swallowing center. The other nerve pairs are not the afferent limb in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, involuntary pharyngeal stage).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the swallowing center lies in the medulla (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: brainstem strokes impair swallowing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — striated and smooth muscle of the esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":1,"stem":"During the pharyngeal stage of swallowing, which event prevents food from refluxing into the nasal cavity?","choices":{"A":"Epiglottis tilting back","B":"Soft palate elevation","C":"Vocal cord closure","D":"Upper sphincter relaxation","E":"Tongue base retraction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The soft palate elevates to close the posterior nares and prevent reflux into the nasal cavity. The epiglottis swings back and the vocal cords approximate to protect the airway, not the nose. Relaxation of the upper esophageal sphincter opens the esophagus. Tongue movement pushes the bolus back in the voluntary stage. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, pharyngeal stage); Costanzo Ch. 8 (pharyngeal phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: breathing is inhibited during the pharyngeal phase (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: nasal regurgitation suggests palatal weakness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — striated and smooth muscle of the esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":1,"stem":"Primary esophageal peristalsis is initiated by which event?","choices":{"A":"Esophageal distension","B":"Refluxed gastric acid","C":"Gastric receptive relaxation","D":"Duodenal fat delivery","E":"A voluntary swallow"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Primary peristalsis begins with the voluntary act of swallowing as a continuation of the pharyngeal peristaltic wave, coordinated by the medullary swallowing center. Esophageal distension and refluxed gastric contents initiate secondary peristalsis. Receptive relaxation is a gastric response to the bolus. Duodenal fat slows gastric emptying. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, primary esophageal peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the LES opens and the orad stomach relaxes as the primary wave travels down (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: primary waves propel the swallowed bolus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how long does primary peristalsis take to carry a bolus through the esophagus in an upright person?","choices":{"A":"5 to 8 seconds","B":"8 to 10 seconds","C":"1 to 2 seconds","D":"20 to 30 seconds","E":"60 to 90 seconds"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Transit takes about 5 to 8 seconds upright because gravity assists the wave. Lying down or stationary, it takes about 8 to 10 seconds. The pharyngeal stage, not esophageal transit, lasts under 2 seconds. Twenty seconds or more is far longer than normal transit. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, primary esophageal peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gravity accelerates esophageal transit when sitting or standing (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: delayed transit suggests a motility disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, secondary esophageal peristalsis can still occur after the vagal supply is interrupted. Which mechanism allows this?","choices":{"A":"Medullary swallowing center","B":"Intrinsic myenteric circuits","C":"Somatic motor reflex arcs","D":"Sympathetic chain ganglia input","E":"Voluntary cortical input"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Intrinsic myenteric networks can drive secondary peristaltic waves on their own after a brief period of adaptation, even when vagal supply is interrupted. The medullary swallowing center acts through the vagus and is cut off. Somatic motor reflexes do not drive smooth muscle peristalsis. Sympathetic input inhibits motility. Voluntary cortical input starts primary peristalsis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, secondary esophageal peristalsis); Costanzo Ch. 8 (secondary wave is mediated by the enteric nervous system).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary peristalsis clears residual food and refluxed contents (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired clearance prolongs acid exposure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, interstitial cells of Cajal in the caudad stomach generate slow waves at about what rate?","choices":{"A":"12 per minute","B":"8 to 10 per minute","C":"1 per minute","D":"3 to 5 per minute","E":"20 to 30 per minute"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Interstitial cells of Cajal generate slow waves of about 3 to 5 per minute in the caudad stomach. About 12 per minute is the duodenal rate in Costanzo. Eight to 10, 1, and 20 to 30 per minute match no gastric source. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, basic electrical rhythm); Costanzo Ch. 8 (slow wave frequency).","boardPrep":"Step 1: slow waves are not action potentials; action potentials on top of them cause phasic contractions (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: gastric pacing disorders slow emptying.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — muscle layers of the gut wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":1,"stem":"What does the term retropulsion describe in gastric motility?","choices":{"A":"Antral contents jetting backward","B":"Chyme entering the duodenum","C":"Bile refluxing into the stomach","D":"Fundic receptive relaxation","E":"Duodenal acid inhibiting emptying"},"correct":"A","explanation":"As a peristaltic ring nears the closed pylorus, most antral contents are squirted backward through the ring toward the body, mixing them with acid. Chyme entering the duodenum is emptying. Bile reflux is retrograde duodenal flow. Receptive relaxation is fundic accommodation. Duodenal acid acts through enterogastric feedback. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, retropulsion and mechanical grinding).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the caudad stomach mixes and grinds, and the orad stomach receives the bolus (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: solids leave the stomach more slowly than liquids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — muscle layers of the gut wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how does the circular muscle wall of the pylorus compare with the antral wall before it?","choices":{"A":"About the same thickness","B":"10 to 20 percent thicker","C":"50 to 100 percent thicker","D":"Two to three times thinner","E":"Five times thicker"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The pyloric sphincter is 50% to 100% thicker than the preceding antral wall. Equal thickness, 10 to 20 percent, thinner walls, and five times thicker do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, pyloric resistance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is hypertrophy of this circular muscle (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: hypertrophic pyloric stenosis presents with nonbilious projectile vomiting at about 2 to 6 weeks of age (First Aid 2025 GI).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — muscle layers of the gut wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":1,"stem":"CCK is the most potent hormonal inhibitor of gastric emptying. Besides slowing antral propulsion, which effect does it produce?","choices":{"A":"Increased antral propulsion","B":"Faster fundic contraction","C":"Relaxed pyloric sphincter","D":"Increased gastric acid","E":"Increased pyloric tone"},"correct":"E","explanation":"CCK slows antral propulsion and increases pyloric sphincter tone, giving time for bile emulsification and pancreatic digestion of fat. Increased antral propulsion and faster fundic contraction would speed emptying. A relaxed pyloric sphincter would let chyme pass faster. CCK does not act by raising gastric acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, hormonal feedback); Costanzo Ch. 8 (CCK inhibits gastric emptying).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK decreases gastric emptying (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: fatty meals stay in the stomach longer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how quickly do enterogastric reflexes act after duodenal stimulation?","choices":{"A":"Within 5 minutes","B":"Within 2 hours","C":"Within 30 seconds","D":"Within 30 minutes","E":"Within 24 hours"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Neural enterogastric reflexes from the duodenal wall act within 30 seconds through myenteric, sympathetic, and vagal routes. Five minutes, 30 minutes, and longer intervals are slower than these neural reflexes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, enterogastric reflexes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal feedback keeps chyme delivery within intestinal digestive and buffering capacity (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: rapid emptying overwhelms the duodenum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":1,"stem":"Acid that lowers duodenal pH below 4.5 releases a hormone that inhibits gastric motility and acid secretion. Which hormone and source is this?","choices":{"A":"Serotonin from EC cells","B":"GIP from K cells","C":"Gastrin from G cells","D":"Secretin from S cells","E":"Motilin from M cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Duodenal S cells release secretin in response to acid below pH 4.5; secretin inhibits gastrin release, gastric acid secretion, and gastric motility until bicarbonate neutralizes the chyme. Serotonin from enterochromaffin cells activates enteric sensory neurons rather than acting as this duodenal hormone. GIP is released by fat, amino acids, and carbohydrate. Gastrin stimulates acid. Motilin is released during fasting and drives interdigestive motility. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, hormonal feedback); Costanzo Ch. 8 (motilin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP exerts a mild inhibitory effect on gastric motility and emptying (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: duodenal acid slows gastric emptying.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what is the resting pressure of the lower esophageal sphincter?","choices":{"A":"5 mm Hg","B":"100 mm Hg","C":"30 mm Hg","D":"Minus 5 mm Hg","E":"60 mm Hg"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The resting LES is tonically contracted at about 30 mm Hg, which overcomes the negative intrathoracic pressure of the middle esophagus. Five mm Hg would be too low to prevent reflux. A negative value describes intrathoracic esophageal pressure. One hundred and 60 mm Hg are higher than normal resting tone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, LES resting tone).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GERD is inappropriate relaxation of the LES (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD). COMLEX: smoking, alcohol, and caffeine are GERD risk factors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — GERD pathogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure provides extrinsic pinch valve reinforcement of the lower esophageal sphincter during coughing or straining?","choices":{"A":"Abdominal rectus muscles","B":"Diaphragmatic crura","C":"Upper esophageal sphincter","D":"Intercostal muscles","E":"Pyloric sphincter"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Skeletal muscle of the diaphragmatic crura surrounds the LES at the esophageal hiatus and reinforces it during inspiration, coughing, and straining. Rectus muscles raise abdominal pressure but do not encircle the LES. The upper esophageal sphincter keeps air out of the upper esophagus. Intercostal muscles move the chest wall. The pyloric sphincter controls gastric emptying. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, diaphragmatic crural reinforcement).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a sliding hiatal hernia moves the gastroesophageal junction into the mediastinum and raises GERD risk (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, sliding hiatal hernia). COMLEX: obesity raises intra-abdominal pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — GERD pathogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, increased intra-abdominal pressure helps prevent reflux by compressing which structure?","choices":{"A":"Distal esophageal segment","B":"Thoracic esophageal body","C":"Wall of the gastric fundus","D":"Upper esophageal sphincter","E":"Pyloric sphincter muscle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A 1 to 2 cm segment of distal esophagus lies in the abdomen, so rising intra-abdominal pressure compresses it inward, creating a flap valve closure. The thoracic esophagus is exposed to intrathoracic pressure. Compression of the fundus would push contents upward. The upper esophageal sphincter and pylorus are not compressed by intra-abdominal pressure in this mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, intra-abdominal esophageal segment).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the 1 to 2 cm abdominal esophagus is compressed by intra-abdominal pressure (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: chronically raised intra-abdominal pressure from obesity or pregnancy and hiatal hernia still promote GERD (lecture SDL 8; Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — GERD pathogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":2,"stem":"A 73-year-old man has a stroke in the medulla, and liquids enter his trachea during swallowing. Failure of which pharyngeal stage event best explains the aspiration?","choices":{"A":"Soft palate elevation","B":"Lower sphincter relaxation","C":"Secondary esophageal waves","D":"Gastric receptive relaxation","E":"Airway closure by larynx"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In the pharyngeal stage the vocal cords approximate, the larynx moves up and forward, and the epiglottis covers the laryngeal opening to prevent aspiration; this sequence is coordinated by the medullary swallowing center. Soft palate elevation protects the nasal cavity. LES relaxation, secondary peristalsis, and receptive relaxation occur after the bolus leaves the pharynx. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, pharyngeal stage).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the swallowing center coordinates CN V, IX, X, and XII (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: aspiration risk is high after brainstem stroke.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — brainstem nuclei of vagal reflexes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has anterior neck surgery that tethers the hyoid, and food sticks at the top of his esophagus. Which normal event opens the upper esophageal sphincter?","choices":{"A":"VIP release at the LES","B":"Secondary peristalsis","C":"Gastric receptive relaxation","D":"Laryngeal stretch","E":"Epiglottic closure"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Upward laryngeal movement stretches and relaxes the upper esophageal sphincter, opening the entrance to the esophagus. VIP release relaxes the lower, not upper, sphincter. Secondary peristalsis begins in the esophagus after the bolus enters. Receptive relaxation occurs in the orad stomach. Epiglottic closure protects the airway. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, pharyngeal stage); Costanzo Ch. 8 (upper esophageal sphincter relaxes in the pharyngeal phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the upper esophageal sphincter is striated muscle (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired laryngeal elevation causes oropharyngeal dysphagia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — striated muscle of the upper esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has a videofluoroscopic swallow study, and his breathing pauses briefly with each swallow. Which mechanism causes the pause?","choices":{"A":"Voluntary cortical breath holding","B":"Reflex vocal cord paralysis","C":"Medullary respiratory inhibition","D":"Fatigue of the phrenic nerve","E":"Nasal block by the epiglottis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Medullary signals temporarily inhibit respiration throughout the pharyngeal stage, which lasts under 2 seconds. The pause is reflex, not voluntary. The vocal cords approximate actively rather than becoming paralyzed. Phrenic nerve fatigue would not cycle with each swallow. The soft palate, not the epiglottis, closes the nasal passage. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, pharyngeal stage); Costanzo Ch. 8 (breathing is inhibited during the pharyngeal phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pharyngeal phase is a reflex controlled in the medulla (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired coordination of breathing and swallowing raises aspiration risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — medullary nuclei","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":2,"stem":"A 75-year-old woman has nighttime reflux, and esophageal manometry records a peristaltic wave that begins in the midesophagus without a swallow. What started this wave?","choices":{"A":"A voluntary pharyngeal swallow","B":"Distension of the wall","C":"Gastric receptive relaxation","D":"Cortical motor command","E":"Duodenal acid reflex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Secondary peristalsis is started locally by distension of the esophageal wall from residual food or refluxed gastric contents and does not require a swallow. A voluntary swallow starts primary peristalsis in the pharynx. Receptive relaxation is a gastric response. A cortical command is part of the voluntary stage. Duodenal acid acts on gastric emptying. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, secondary esophageal peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary peristalsis is mediated by the enteric nervous system and partly by vagovagal reflexes (lecture SDL 6; Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: nocturnal reflux is cleared by secondary waves.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — intrinsic enteric circuits","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man swallows a large bolus that the primary wave does not fully clear. A second wave clears it without another swallow. Where does this second wave begin?","choices":{"A":"In the upper pharyngeal wall","B":"At the lower sphincter","C":"In the gastric fundus","D":"At the site of distension","E":"At the cricopharyngeus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A secondary peristaltic contraction begins at the point of distension and travels downward until the lumen is cleared. Waves starting in the pharynx are primary waves. The lower sphincter relaxes rather than starting the wave. The gastric fundus is beyond the esophagus. The cricopharyngeus is part of the upper esophageal sphincter. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (secondary peristaltic contraction begins at the point of distention); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, secondary peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary waves repeat until the lumen is cleared (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: impaired secondary peristalsis worsens reflux esophagitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — the peristaltic reflex","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old man joins a research study and receives an inhibitor of an enzyme that uses arginine. As his primary wave travels down, his lower esophageal sphincter fails to relax ahead of the bolus. Which transmitter normally causes this relaxation?","choices":{"A":"Acetylcholine","B":"Substance P","C":"Norepinephrine","D":"Histamine","E":"Nitric oxide"},"correct":"E","explanation":"As the primary wave travels caudally, peptidergic myenteric inhibitory neurons release VIP and nitric oxide to relax the LES and orad stomach ahead of the bolus. Acetylcholine and substance P are excitatory and contract smooth muscle. Norepinephrine increases sphincter tone. Histamine is a gastric paracrine for acid secretion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, primary peristalsis); Costanzo Ch. 8 (VIP and NO relax the LES).","boardPrep":"Step 1: achalasia is loss of inhibitory neurons containing NO and VIP in the myenteric plexus (First Aid 2025 GI achalasia). COMLEX: nitrates relax esophageal smooth muscle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man has a gastric emptying study. Solid food leaves his stomach only after it has been broken into particles smaller than 1 mm. Which mechanism grinds the solids?","choices":{"A":"Fundic relaxation","B":"Duodenal segmentation","C":"Antral retropulsion","D":"Migrating complexes","E":"Pyloric relaxation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Strong antral rings push contents against a nearly closed pylorus, so most chyme is squirted back toward the body, shearing solids into particles under 1 mm. Fundic relaxation stores the meal. Duodenal segmentation mixes chyme after it leaves the stomach. Migrating motor complexes clear the fasting stomach. Pyloric relaxation would let solids leave without grinding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, retropulsion and mechanical grinding).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the caudad stomach has the thickest muscle and produces the strongest contractions (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired antral grinding delays solid emptying.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — circular and longitudinal muscle","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has a gastric emptying study. Liquid leaves her stomach quickly, while solid food stays until it is mixed into chyme. Which feature explains the difference?","choices":{"A":"Tonic pyloric contraction","B":"Weak tone in the fundus","C":"Faster gastric slow waves","D":"Acidic duodenal contents","E":"Absent antral peristalsis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The thick, tonically contracted pyloric sphincter lets fluids pass easily but restricts solids until they are mixed into fluid chyme. Weak fundic tone would affect storage, not particle size. Faster slow waves would increase contraction rate for both phases. Duodenal acid slows emptying of all contents. Absent antral peristalsis would slow both liquids and solids. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, pyloric resistance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pyloric sphincter is circular smooth muscle (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: gastric emptying studies use radiolabeled solid meals.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — the pyloric circular muscle","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old man has antral manometry after a meal. For about one fifth of the recording, powerful antral contractions generate 50 to 70 cm H2O of pressure. What is this pattern called?","choices":{"A":"Mixing waves","B":"Pyloric pump","C":"Mass movement","D":"Segmentation","E":"Receptive relaxation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For about 20% of the time after a meal, gastric peristalsis turns into powerful contractions called the pyloric pump, generating 50 to 70 cm H2O and driving emptying. Mixing waves are weak constrictor waves that do not empty the stomach. Mass movements occur in the colon. Segmentation mixes small intestinal contents. Receptive relaxation lowers orad gastric pressure. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, basic electrical rhythm and propulsion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pyloric pump generates 50 to 70 cm H2O and drives emptying (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: enterogastric reflexes inhibit the pyloric pump.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman feels full for hours after fatty meals but not after meals of fruit and bread. Which response in her duodenum most slows emptying after the fatty meal?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin release by peptides","B":"GIP release by glucose","C":"CCK release by lipids","D":"Motilin release by fasting","E":"Ghrelin release by fasting"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fatty acids and monoglycerides release CCK from I cells, and CCK is the most potent hormonal inhibitor of gastric emptying. Gastrin is released in the stomach and stimulates acid. GIP has only a mild inhibitory effect on emptying. Motilin and ghrelin rise during fasting. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, hormonal feedback).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK increases gallbladder contraction and decreases gastric emptying (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: low fat meals help patients with delayed emptying.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — CCK","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old man drinks a hyperosmolar sports drink, and it empties from his stomach more slowly than the same volume of water. Which mechanism slows emptying of the drink?","choices":{"A":"Duodenal osmoreceptor reflex","B":"Gastric acid hypersecretion","C":"Pyloric sphincter relaxation","D":"Faster antral slow waves","E":"Vagal receptive relaxation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Duodenal receptors monitor osmolality, and hypertonic chyme triggers enterogastric reflexes that inhibit the pyloric pump and increase pyloric tone. Gastric acid secretion does not depend on drink osmolality. Pyloric relaxation and faster antral waves would speed emptying. Receptive relaxation accommodates both drinks equally. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, duodenal stimuli and enterogastric reflexes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal distension, acid, osmolality, and fat and protein products slow gastric emptying (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: isotonic fluids empty faster than hypertonic ones.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — reflex control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 77-year-old man has a vagotomy, and duodenal distension still inhibits his antral contractions within seconds. Which routes of the enterogastric reflex remain?","choices":{"A":"Vagal efferent pathways","B":"Circulating gastrin release","C":"Pelvic splanchnic reflexes","D":"Somatic spinal reflexes","E":"Myenteric and sympathetic"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Enterogastric reflexes act through intrinsic myenteric circuits, sympathetic nerves via prevertebral ganglia, and the vagus; after vagotomy the myenteric and sympathetic routes remain. Vagal efferents are cut. Gastrin stimulates rather than inhibits gastric activity. Pelvic splanchnic fibers supply the distal colon. Somatic reflexes do not control gastric smooth muscle. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, enterogastric reflexes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic activity decreases peristalsis and increases sphincter tone (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, general principles). COMLEX: vagotomy also removes receptive relaxation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — sympathetic effects on motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with a sliding hiatal hernia has daily heartburn. Her gastroesophageal junction lies above the diaphragm. Which antireflux mechanism has she lost?","choices":{"A":"Abdominal segment compression","B":"Esophageal cardiac gland mucus","C":"Secondary peristaltic clearance","D":"Upper sphincter tone","E":"Gastric receptive relaxation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When the gastroesophageal junction moves into the mediastinum, the distal esophagus no longer lies in the abdomen, so intra-abdominal pressure no longer compresses it into a flap valve, and crural reinforcement is lost. Cardiac gland mucus and secondary peristalsis are separate clearance and buffering defenses. Upper sphincter tone keeps air out of the esophagus. Receptive relaxation is gastric accommodation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, intra-abdominal segment and crural reinforcement); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (sliding hiatal hernia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sliding hiatal hernia is the most common type and increases GERD risk (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, sliding hiatal hernia). COMLEX: obesity and pregnancy are risk factors.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — sliding hiatal hernia in GERD pathogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man has heartburn and regurgitation. Manometry shows normal resting sphincter pressure, and pH monitoring shows acid exposure episodes that coincide with periods of complete sphincter relaxation without a preceding swallow. Which mechanism explains his reflux?","choices":{"A":"Excess LES resting tone","B":"Absent gastric acid","C":"Strong crural contraction","D":"Transient LES relaxations","E":"Rapid gastric emptying"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Abnormal transient relaxations of the LES permit gastric reflux even when resting tone is normal. Excess resting tone would oppose reflux and suggests achalasia. Absent acid would reduce injury. Strong crural contraction reinforces the barrier. Rapid gastric emptying would lower the volume available to reflux. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, LES resting tone); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (GERD is inappropriate relaxation of the LES).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GERD presents with burning retrosternal pain, regurgitation, and cough (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD). COMLEX: proton pump inhibitors are first line.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — reflux esophagitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 21-year-old woman in a manometry study has a zone at the gastroesophageal junction with pressure higher than in both the esophagus and the stomach. Which property produces this zone?","choices":{"A":"Cholinergic mucosal secretion","B":"Tonic circular contraction","C":"Longitudinal muscle shortening","D":"Submucosal venous engorgement","E":"Constant VIP release"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The LES is circular smooth muscle at the distal esophagus that stays tonically contracted at rest, creating a pressure barrier above both intrathoracic esophageal and gastric pressure. Mucosal secretion does not generate sphincter pressure. Longitudinal shortening does not create a sphincter zone. Venous engorgement is not the mechanism. VIP relaxes the LES. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, LES resting tone); Costanzo Ch. 8 (LES resting pressure exceeds esophageal and gastric pressure).","boardPrep":"Step 1: LES resting pressure exceeds intrathoracic and gastric pressure (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: low LES pressure predisposes to GERD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — esophageal motility disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":3,"stem":"A 20-year-old man has a small stroke involving the medullary nucleus that moves his tongue, and he cannot push a chewed bolus back toward his pharynx. Which cranial nerve carries this motor output?","choices":{"A":"Facial motor nerve","B":"Hypoglossal nerve","C":"Spinal accessory nerve","D":"Phrenic nerve","E":"Abducens nerve"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The swallowing center coordinates motor output through cranial nerves V, IX, X, and XII and upper cervical nerves; the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) moves the tongue, which rolls the bolus back against the hard palate. The facial nerve is not in the lecture list of swallowing motor nerves. The spinal accessory nerve supplies neck and shoulder muscles. The phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm. The abducens nerve moves the eye. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, voluntary and pharyngeal stages).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the oral phase of swallowing is voluntary (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: tongue weakness causes oral phase dysphagia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — medullary nuclei","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":3,"stem":"An 18-year-old woman has esophageal manometry during a swallow. At what point does her lower esophageal sphincter open?","choices":{"A":"Just after the UES closes","B":"After the bolus enters the stomach","C":"Before the pharyngeal stage","D":"After gastric contraction","E":"During exhalation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The LES opens just after the upper esophageal sphincter returns to resting tone, so it is open for the whole time the bolus moves down the esophagus. Opening only after the bolus enters the stomach would block transit. Opening before the pharyngeal stage would precede the swallow. Gastric contraction is not the trigger. Respiratory phase does not time LES opening. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (esophageal motility, step 3: the LES opens just after the UES returns to resting tone); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, primary peristalsis: relaxation ahead of the advancing bolus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the LES stays open for the whole esophageal transit (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: failure of LES relaxation is achalasia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — vagal transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":3,"stem":"A 77-year-old woman has gastric manometry after a meal. Where do the weak constrictor mixing waves of her stomach begin?","choices":{"A":"Gastric fundus","B":"Pyloric sphincter","C":"Midstomach","D":"Duodenal bulb","E":"Gastric cardia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Weak peristaltic mixing waves originate in the midstomach and travel toward the antrum every 15 to 20 seconds, intensifying as they reach the thick caudad wall. The fundus is part of the thin walled orad stomach that stores food. The pyloric sphincter resists outflow. The duodenal bulb lies beyond the stomach. The cardia surrounds the gastroesophageal junction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, basic electrical rhythm and propulsion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the orad stomach receives the meal and the caudad stomach mixes and grinds it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the caudad stomach wall is thickest.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — regions of the stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":3,"stem":"An 80-year-old man has a gastric emptying study after a meal. Besides acid and fat, duodenal chyme with which property slows emptying?","choices":{"A":"Isotonic osmolality","B":"Neutral pH near 7","C":"Low fat and protein","D":"Small duodenal volume","E":"Hypotonic osmolality"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Duodenal receptors monitor distension, acidity, hypertonic or hypotonic osmolality, fat and protein products, and irritation, and each can slow emptying. Isotonic chyme, a neutral pH, low fat and protein content, and a small duodenal volume do not trigger this feedback. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, duodenal stimuli).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK, secretin, and GIP slow gastric emptying (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: duodenal irritation also slows gastric emptying (lecture SDL 6).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — duodenal hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":3,"stem":"A 19-year-old man has esophageal manometry that maps his lower esophageal sphincter. About how far above the gastric junction does this sphincter zone extend?","choices":{"A":"About 10 cm","B":"About 25 cm","C":"About 0.5 cm","D":"About 3 cm","E":"About 15 cm"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The LES is the circular smooth muscle at the distal end of the esophagus, extending about 3 cm above the junction with the stomach. Ten, 15, and 25 cm would include much of the thoracic esophagus. Half a centimeter is shorter than the sphincter zone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, LES resting tone).","boardPrep":"Step 1: LES pressure exceeds gastric pressure at rest (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: GERD presents with heartburn and regurgitation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — reflux esophagitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":3,"stem":"An 82-year-old woman has antral recordings that show contractions occurring at a fixed maximum rate. What sets this maximum rate?","choices":{"A":"Slow wave frequency","B":"Vagal firing rate","C":"Serum gastrin level","D":"Pyloric resistance","E":"Fundic muscle tone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The frequency of slow waves generated by interstitial cells of Cajal sets the frequency of action potentials and therefore the maximum frequency of contractions. Vagal firing and gastrin change the number of action potentials and the force of each contraction, not the maximum rate. Pyloric resistance controls outflow. Fundic tone affects storage. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (frequency of slow waves); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, basic electrical rhythm).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neural and hormonal input changes the number of action potentials on each slow wave (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: stronger antral contractions speed gastric mixing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric pacemaking","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl06_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":4,"stem":"A 73-year-old woman has Raynaud phenomenon, tight thickened skin over her fingers, heartburn, and dysphagia to both solids and liquids. Esophageal manometry is performed. Which of the following sets of manometry findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["LES resting pressure","LES relaxation","Distal contractions"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↓","↓"],"B":["↔","↔","↑"],"C":["↓","↔","↓"],"D":["↓","↔","↔"],"E":["↔","↔","↔"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Systemic sclerosis causes smooth muscle atrophy and fibrosis of the distal esophagus, so Choice C (↓ LES resting pressure, ↔ LES relaxation, ↓ distal contractions) is correct. The weak sphincter loses the positive pressure barrier that normally prevents reflux, and the weak distal smooth muscle clears refluxed acid poorly, producing heartburn and dysphagia. Choice A (↑ LES resting pressure, ↓ LES relaxation, ↓ distal contractions) is achalasia, from loss of inhibitory myenteric neurons. Choice B (↔ LES resting pressure, ↔ LES relaxation, ↑ distal contractions) is distal esophageal spasm, with uncoordinated high amplitude contractions. Choice D (↓ LES resting pressure, ↔ LES relaxation, ↔ distal contractions) is reflux from LES incompetence with intact peristalsis. Choice E (↔ LES resting pressure, ↔ LES relaxation, ↔ distal contractions) is cardiovascular dysphagia from external compression by an enlarged left atrium, which has normal manometry. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, LES resting tone and LES incompetence); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (esophageal disorders of motility: achalasia, distal esophageal spasm, cardiovascular dysphagia, scleroderma esophageal dysmotility; GERD); First Aid 2025 GI (achalasia). Cells shown as ↔ are values the sources do not describe as changed; LES resting pressure in row D is inferred from the lecture term incompetence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: scleroderma esophageal dysmotility shows ↓ LES resting pressure and ↓ esophageal contractions, and achalasia shows ↑ LES resting pressure with ↓ contractions (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal disorders of motility). COMLEX: scleroderma is a risk factor for GERD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — esophageal motility disorders","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":7,"title":"SDL 7 — Physiology - Salivary and Gastric Secretion","questions":[{"id":"sdl07_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the tonicity of the initial saliva secreted by acinar cells?","choices":{"A":"Hypotonic to plasma","B":"Hypertonic to plasma","C":"Varies with the diet","D":"Twice plasma osmolarity","E":"Isotonic to plasma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acinar cells secrete an initial saliva that is isotonic, with Na+, K+, Cl−, and HCO3− concentrations similar to plasma. Saliva becomes hypotonic later, in the ducts. It does not become hypertonic or reach twice plasma osmolarity, and diet does not set its tonicity. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, acinar cell secretion); Costanzo Ch. 8 (formation of saliva, step 1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serous salivary secretions contain alpha amylase and lipase (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, salivary glands). COMLEX: the parotid gland is primarily serous.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":1,"stem":"Which property of salivary ductal cells allows the final saliva to become hypotonic?","choices":{"A":"Active water secretion","B":"Potassium reabsorption","C":"Rapid acinar flow","D":"Low water permeability","E":"Bicarbonate absorption"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ductal cells absorb more NaCl than they secrete KHCO3, and because they are relatively impermeable to water, water does not follow the absorbed solute, so the saliva becomes hypotonic. Ducts do not secrete water. They secrete rather than reabsorb potassium and bicarbonate. Rapid flow makes saliva closer to isotonic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, ductal cell modification); Costanzo Ch. 8 (formation of saliva, step 2).","boardPrep":"Step 1: final saliva is hypotonic with lower Na+ and Cl− and higher K+ and HCO3− than plasma (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the submandibular gland makes most of the saliva in the mouth (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, salivary glands).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":1,"stem":"What does contraction of salivary myoepithelial cells accomplish?","choices":{"A":"Secretes salivary amylase","B":"Expels stored saliva","C":"Reabsorbs ductal sodium","D":"Dilates gland arterioles","E":"Lowers saliva tonicity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Myoepithelial (basket) cells lie between the basal membranes of acinar and ductal cells and the basal lamina; on neural stimulation they contract, compressing the acini and shortening the ducts to eject saliva. Acinar cells secrete amylase. Ductal cells reabsorb sodium and lower tonicity. Bradykinin from kallikrein dilates the arterioles. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, myoepithelial cell function); Costanzo Ch. 8 (structure of the salivary glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasympathetic stimulation contracts myoepithelial cells (lecture SDL 7). COMLEX: the sublingual gland is primarily mucous (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, salivary glands).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which second messenger pathway mediates parasympathetic stimulation of salivary acinar cells?","choices":{"A":"IP3 and Ca2+","B":"Cyclic AMP","C":"Cyclic GMP","D":"Nitric oxide","E":"Tyrosine kinase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Postganglionic parasympathetic neurons release ACh onto muscarinic M3 receptors, which act through IP3 and intracellular Ca2+ to produce a large volume of watery, enzyme rich saliva. Cyclic AMP is the sympathetic beta receptor messenger. Cyclic GMP, nitric oxide, and tyrosine kinase are not the salivary parasympathetic pathway. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, parasympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the parasympathetic input to the salivary glands travels in CN VII and CN IX (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: anticholinergic drugs cause dry mouth.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic ganglia and transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Where do sympathetic preganglionic fibers to the salivary glands synapse?","choices":{"A":"Submandibular ganglion","B":"Pterygopalatine ganglion","C":"Inferior cervical ganglion","D":"Geniculate ganglion","E":"Superior cervical ganglion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sympathetic preganglionic fibers arise from thoracic segments T1 to T3 and synapse in the superior cervical ganglion; postganglionic fibers release norepinephrine onto the glands. The submandibular ganglion is a parasympathetic relay for the submandibular gland. The pterygopalatine, inferior cervical, and geniculate ganglia are not the salivary sympathetic relay in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, sympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (sympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: both parasympathetic and sympathetic stimulation increase saliva, and parasympathetic control is dominant (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: saliva is regulated by autonomic nerves rather than GI hormones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic ganglia and transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which factor decreases parasympathetic drive to the salivary glands?","choices":{"A":"Oral tactile input","B":"Dehydration","C":"Sour taste","D":"Smell of food","E":"Conditioned reflexes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Parasympathetic drive to the salivary glands is decreased by fear, sleep, and dehydration. It is increased by food, taste (especially sour stimuli), smell, tactile oral stimuli, nausea, and conditioned reflexes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, parasympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (factors that modulate parasympathetic input).","boardPrep":"Step 1: salivary stasis from dehydration is associated with salivary duct stones (First Aid 2025 GI, sialolithiasis). COMLEX: Pavlov conditioned reflexes increase salivation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic ganglia and transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme in the parietal cell cytoplasm generates the H+ that is secreted into the gastric lumen?","choices":{"A":"Adenylyl cyclase","B":"Phospholipase C","C":"Protein kinase A","D":"Carbonic anhydrase","E":"Histidine decarboxylase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Carbonic anhydrase combines CO2 and H2O into H2CO3, which dissociates into H+ for secretion and HCO3− for absorption into the blood. Adenylyl cyclase, phospholipase C, and protein kinase A are signaling enzymes that regulate the pump. Histidine decarboxylase makes histamine. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, intracellular generation of H+ and HCO3−); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion, step 1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells secrete gastric acid and intrinsic factor (First Aid 2025 GI, gastrointestinal secretory products). COMLEX: gastric pH is normally between 1 and 2 (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":1,"stem":"Through which apical pathway does chloride enter the gastric lumen during HCl secretion?","choices":{"A":"Chloride bicarbonate exchanger","B":"Sodium chloride cotransporter","C":"Chloride ATPase pump","D":"Potassium chloride symport","E":"Passive chloride channels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cl− diffuses passively into the canalicular lumen through apical Cl− channels and combines with secreted H+ to form HCl. The chloride bicarbonate exchanger sits on the basolateral membrane and brings Cl− into the cell. Sodium chloride and potassium chloride cotransport and a chloride ATPase are not part of the lecture mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, apical active transport and Cl− channels); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion, step 2).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the parietal cell apical membrane contains the H+/K+ ATPase and Cl− channels (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vomiting gastric acid causes hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis (First Aid 2025 GI).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":1,"stem":"Where does the bicarbonate generated in parietal cells go during acid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Into the gastric lumen","B":"Into the canaliculus","C":"Into gastric venous blood","D":"Into parietal cell vesicles","E":"Into the mucus gel layer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"HCO3− made by carbonic anhydrase leaves across the basolateral membrane into the blood, producing the alkaline tide. It is not secreted into the lumen or canaliculus, where H+ goes. It is not stored in vesicles. Bicarbonate trapped in surface mucus comes from surface epithelial cells, not parietal cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, basolateral transport and the alkaline tide); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion, step 3).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the alkaline tide raises blood pH after gastric acid secretion, such as after meals or vomiting (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: absorbed HCO3− is later secreted back into the gut in pancreatic juice (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what explains potentiation among the gastric acid secretagogues?","choices":{"A":"Different second messengers","B":"Shared receptor binding","C":"Common G protein coupling","D":"Competitive receptor antagonism","E":"Increased gastrin clearance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Histamine acts through cAMP while ACh and gastrin act through IP3/Ca2+, and ACh and gastrin also release histamine from ECL cells, so combined stimuli give more acid than the sum of their separate effects. Each agent has its own receptor, so they do not share binding or one G protein. Antagonism and gastrin clearance reduce rather than amplify the response. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, potentiation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (potentiation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbonic anhydrase in parietal cells supplies the H+ that the apical pump secretes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: combined secretagogues produce more acid than expected.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — somatostatin and histamine actions on acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, gastrin from antral G cells binds its receptors on which two cell types in the oxyntic mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Chief and ECL cells","B":"Parietal and D cells","C":"Chief and mucous cells","D":"Parietal and ECL cells","E":"Mucous and D cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastrin binds its receptors on both parietal cells and ECL cells, so it can stimulate parietal cells directly and also act through ECL cells. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and are not the gastrin targets named in the lecture. D cells release somatostatin, which inhibits acid. Mucous cells secrete mucus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, secretagogues and receptor pathways); Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastrin acts on parietal cells and ECL cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin is released from G cells of the gastric antrum (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: PPIs are used to treat Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI, proton pump inhibitors).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — somatostatin and histamine actions on acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":1,"stem":"Which receptor mechanism does somatostatin use directly on parietal cells to reduce acid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Gq protein and IP3","B":"Receptor tyrosine kinase","C":"Inhibitory G protein","D":"Ligand gated channel","E":"Nuclear receptor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Somatostatin binds Gi coupled receptors on parietal cells, inhibiting adenylyl cyclase and lowering cAMP, which antagonizes histamine. Gq and IP3 are the ACh and gastrin pathway. Tyrosine kinase receptors, ligand gated channels, and nuclear receptors are not the somatostatin mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, inhibitory pathways); Costanzo Ch. 8 (inhibition of HCl secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: D cells release somatostatin, which acts through a Gi cascade to reduce H+/K+ ATPase activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, parietal cell physiology). COMLEX: lower cAMP reduces protein kinase A activity in the parietal cell (lecture SDL 7).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — somatostatin and histamine actions on acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of meal-induced acid secretion occurs in the gastric phase?","choices":{"A":"About 60 percent","B":"About 30 percent","C":"About 10 percent","D":"About 90 percent","E":"About 45 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The gastric phase accounts for about 60% of the acid response, the cephalic phase about 30%, and the intestinal phase about 10%. Ninety and 45 percent do not match any phase. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, phases of gastric acid secretion); Costanzo Ch. 8 (phases of HCl secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastric phase is triggered by distension and protein digestion products (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the intestinal phase accounts for about 10 percent of acid secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Which gastric mucosal defense rapidly seals small erosions?","choices":{"A":"Pepsin secretion","B":"Mucosal fibrosis","C":"Cell restitution","D":"Parietal hyperplasia","E":"Gastrin release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Rapid epithelial cell restitution seals small erosions, alongside mucus and trapped HCO3−, tight junctions, and mucosal blood flow. Pepsin is a damaging factor. Fibrosis is scarring rather than a rapid defense. Parietal cell hyperplasia and gastrin increase acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, gastric mucosal barrier).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bismuth and sucralfate bind the gastric mucosa and give physical protection from acid (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: peptic ulcers reflect an imbalance of protective and damaging factors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Which H. pylori enzyme lets the organism survive in gastric acid?","choices":{"A":"Catalase","B":"Urease","C":"Lipase","D":"Protease","E":"Mucinase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"H. pylori urease converts urea into ammonia and CO2, forming an alkaline ammonia cloud that protects it from luminal acid. Catalase, lipase, protease, and mucinase are not the lecture mechanism for acid survival. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, peptic ulcer disease and H. pylori); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (urease hydrolyzes urea to form ammonia, which neutralizes gastric acid).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the urea breath test detects H. pylori urease activity (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: H. pylori is treated with triple or quadruple therapy that includes a PPI (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":2,"stem":"An 81-year-old woman is dehydrated, and her salivary flow falls below 1 mL/min. Compared with her saliva at a high flow rate, which change in her final saliva is expected?","choices":{"A":"Higher sodium concentration","B":"Higher chloride concentration","C":"Higher bicarbonate concentration","D":"Higher potassium concentration","E":"Isotonic osmolarity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"At low flow rates, saliva spends more time in contact with ductal cells, so more Na+ and Cl− are reabsorbed and more K+ is secreted; final saliva is most hypotonic with the highest K+. Sodium and chloride are lowest at low flow. Bicarbonate is lowest at low flow because its secretion is selectively stimulated during active salivation. Isotonic saliva is seen at high flow. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, effect of flow rate on electrolyte composition); Costanzo Ch. 8 (effect of flow rate on composition of saliva).","boardPrep":"Step 1: at the highest flow rates saliva most closely resembles plasma (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: dehydration reduces parasympathetic drive to the salivary glands.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy chews sour candy, and his salivary flow rises to about 4 mL/min. His salivary bicarbonate is higher than at rest even though the saliva spends less time in the ducts. Which mechanism explains this?","choices":{"A":"Ductal bicarbonate reabsorption","B":"Higher plasma bicarbonate","C":"Water reabsorption by ducts","D":"Slower acinar fluid secretion","E":"Stimulated ductal secretion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HCO3− is the exception to the contact time rule: its ductal secretion is selectively stimulated when saliva production is stimulated, so its concentration rises with flow rate. Ducts secrete rather than reabsorb bicarbonate. Plasma bicarbonate does not change with a sour stimulus. Ductal cells are relatively impermeable to water. Acinar secretion speeds up, not slows, during stimulation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, effect of flow rate on electrolyte composition); Costanzo Ch. 8 (HCO3− is not described by the contact time explanation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sour taste increases parasympathetic drive to the salivary glands (lecture SDL 7). COMLEX: salivary bicarbonate buffers ingested food.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman in a physiology study has saliva collected at a low flow rate, and its Na+ concentration is far below that of plasma. Through which luminal membrane transporter do her ductal cells take up this Na+?","choices":{"A":"Sodium potassium pumps","B":"Sodium glucose carriers","C":"Epithelial sodium channels","D":"Sodium hydrogen exchangers","E":"Sodium phosphate carriers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The luminal membrane of salivary ductal cells contains epithelial Na+ channels (ENaC), CFTR Cl− channels, Cl−/HCO3− exchange, and H+/K+ exchange; Na+ enters through ENaC and is pumped out across the basolateral membrane. The Na+/K+ ATPase sits on the basolateral membrane, not the luminal one. Sodium glucose, sodium hydrogen, and sodium phosphate carriers are not the ductal luminal transporters listed. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (formation of saliva, step 2: ductal luminal and basolateral transporters); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, ductal cell modification).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CFTR is the luminal Cl− channel of salivary ductal cells (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: saliva is not a simple ultrafiltrate of plasma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 75-year-old man starts an anticholinergic drug for bladder spasm and now has a dry mouth during meals. Blockade of which receptors on his salivary cells best explains the loss of watery saliva?","choices":{"A":"Beta adrenergic receptors","B":"Alpha adrenergic receptors","C":"Histamine H2 receptors","D":"Muscarinic M3 receptors","E":"Dopamine D2 receptors"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Parasympathetic postganglionic neurons release ACh onto muscarinic M3 receptors on acinar and ductal cells, and the IP3/Ca2+ cascade produces a high volume of watery, enzyme rich saliva; blocking M3 removes this dominant drive. Beta and alpha adrenergic receptors mediate the smaller sympathetic response. H2 and D2 receptors are not the salivary parasympathetic receptors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, parasympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: atropine blocks muscarinic receptors (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: parasympathetic control of saliva is dominant.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man gives a stressful speech and notices that his saliva becomes thick and scant. Activation of which pathway in his salivary cells produces this protein rich secretion?","choices":{"A":"M3 receptors raising IP3","B":"Nicotinic receptors on acini","C":"H2 receptors raising cAMP","D":"Gi receptors lowering cAMP","E":"Beta receptors raising cAMP"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Postganglionic sympathetic fibers from the superior cervical ganglion release norepinephrine onto beta adrenergic receptors, which activate adenylyl cyclase and raise cAMP, producing a smaller volume of viscous, protein and mucin rich saliva. M3 and IP3 mediate the watery parasympathetic response. Nicotinic receptors are on ganglion cells, not acini. H2 receptors are on parietal cells. A Gi pathway would lower cAMP. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, sympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (sympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic preganglionic fibers to the salivary glands arise from T1 to T3 (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: sympathetic alpha receptors are also present on acinar cells.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man in a study has parasympathetic stimulation of his submandibular gland, and gland blood flow rises sharply along with secretion. Which acinar product mediates this vasodilation?","choices":{"A":"Alpha amylase","B":"Lingual lipase","C":"Kallikrein","D":"Mucin glycoprotein","E":"Secretory IgA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"During high salivary activity, acinar cells secrete kallikrein, which cleaves high molecular weight kininogen into bradykinin, a potent local vasodilator that accounts for the high salivary blood flow. Alpha amylase and lingual lipase begin carbohydrate and lipid digestion. Mucin lubricates food. Secretory IgA is an immune product. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, parasympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (organic constituents of saliva).","boardPrep":"Step 1: maximal salivary blood flow is more than 10 times that of exercising muscle when corrected for size (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the submandibular duct is Wharton duct (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, salivary glands).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old man has parietal cells studied during acid secretion. As each HCO3− ion leaves across the basolateral membrane, which ion enters the cell in exchange?","choices":{"A":"Sodium","B":"Chloride","C":"Potassium","D":"Hydrogen","E":"Phosphate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The basolateral membrane of the parietal cell carries a Cl−/HCO3− exchanger, so HCO3− leaves for the blood as Cl− enters; that Cl− then diffuses into the lumen through apical Cl− channels to accompany the secreted H+. Na+ and K+ are moved by the basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase, not by this exchanger. H+ is pumped into the lumen. Phosphate is not part of HCl secretion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion: basolateral Cl−/HCO3− exchangers, apical Cl− channels); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, basolateral transport). The direction of Cl− entry is inferred from the exchanger swapping Cl− for HCO3−.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the parietal cell basolateral membrane also contains the Na+/K+ ATPase (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: net HCl secretion is paired with net HCO3− absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman has gastric glands studied after surgery. Blocking the apical K+ channels of her parietal cells lowers acid output even though the pump is intact. Why?","choices":{"A":"Carbonic anhydrase is inhibited","B":"Chloride channels close","C":"Pump loses its exchange ion","D":"Histamine release falls","E":"Bicarbonate exits apically"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The H+/K+ ATPase secretes H+ in exchange for luminal K+, and K+ recycles back into the canaliculus through apical K+ channels; without that recycling, little K+ is available for exchange and H+ secretion falls. Carbonic anhydrase, chloride channels, and histamine release are not directly affected. Bicarbonate exits across the basolateral membrane. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, apical active transport and Cl− channels).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the H+/K+ ATPase is a primary active transporter that moves H+ and K+ against their gradients (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: proton pump inhibitors block this pump.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old man takes omeprazole for a gastric ulcer, and his acid output falls in response to every stimulus, including histamine and gastrin. Which step does the drug block?","choices":{"A":"Apical proton pump","B":"Parietal H2 receptors","C":"Parietal M3 receptors","D":"CCK2 receptors","E":"Carbonic anhydrase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Proton pump inhibitors such as omeprazole irreversibly block the apical H+/K+ ATPase, the final step of acid secretion shared by every secretagogue, which suppresses both meal stimulated and nocturnal acid secretion. H2, M3, and CCK2 receptor blockade would each remove one stimulus. Carbonic anhydrase is not the drug target. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, pharmacologic inhibition); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion, step 2).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PPIs can cause malabsorption of vitamin B12, iron, calcium, and magnesium (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: omeprazole inhibits CYP2C19.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — acid suppressing drug classes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman takes cimetidine, and her acid response to vagal stimulation and to gastrin falls much more than expected from blocking one secretagogue. Which mechanism explains this larger effect?","choices":{"A":"Direct M3 receptor blockade","B":"Lost histamine potentiation","C":"CCK2 receptor blockade","D":"Proton pump inhibition","E":"Gastrin release inhibition"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Histamine potentiates the actions of ACh and gastrin, so an H2 blocker removes both the direct histamine effect and the histamine-potentiated effects of ACh and gastrin. Cimetidine does not block M3 or CCK2 receptors, inhibit the proton pump, or stop gastrin release. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, pharmacologic inhibition); Costanzo Ch. 8 (potentiation and H2 receptor blocking agents).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cimetidine inhibits cytochrome P450 and has antiandrogenic effects (First Aid 2025 GI, H2 blockers). COMLEX: famotidine and nizatidine are other H2 antagonists.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — acid suppressing drug classes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man has gastric pH recorded through a meal. The pH stays relatively high while food is in his stomach but drops sharply once the meal empties into the duodenum. What explains the drop?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin secretion rises","B":"Somatostatin release falls","C":"Vagal tone increases","D":"Pepsin activity increases","E":"Less food buffer remains"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Food buffers secreted H+, so gastric contents are less acidic while the meal is present; once chyme moves into the small intestine, buffering falls and continued H+ secretion lowers pH, which then inhibits gastrin and triggers somatostatin release to slow acid secretion. Gastrin falls and somatostatin rises as pH drops. Vagal tone falls rather than rises as the meal leaves, and pepsin activity does not lower pH. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (inhibition of HCl secretion); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, inhibitory pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: distension of the stomach drives gastric phase acid secretion through vagovagal and local reflexes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the cephalic phase is mediated by the vagus (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — somatostatin inhibits gastrin secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":2,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman in a study has her vagus nerves stimulated, and ACh binds muscarinic receptors on her parietal cells. Which enzyme does this receptor activate to drive acid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Phospholipase C","B":"Adenylyl cyclase","C":"Guanylyl cyclase","D":"Tyrosine kinase","E":"Lipoxygenase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"M3 receptors activate phospholipase C, which liberates diacylglycerol and IP3; IP3 releases intracellular Ca2+, and Ca2+ and diacylglycerol activate protein kinases that drive H+ secretion. Adenylyl cyclase is the histamine H2 pathway. Guanylyl cyclase, tyrosine kinase, and lipoxygenase are not the parietal M3 pathway. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (substances that alter HCl secretion); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, secretagogues and receptor pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACh acts on M3 receptors through a Gq cascade to increase H+/K+ ATPase activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, parietal cell physiology). COMLEX: atropine blocks direct vagal stimulation of parietal cells.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes and gastric secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"An 85-year-old man has H. pylori infection and a duodenal ulcer. The infection also spreads to the duodenum. Which duodenal defense does it impair?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic enzyme output","B":"Bile salt secretion","C":"Duodenal villus height","D":"Local bicarbonate output","E":"Duodenal mucosal IgA"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Spread of H. pylori to the duodenum inhibits duodenal HCO3− secretion, so acid delivered from the stomach is poorly neutralized and erodes the mucosa. Pancreatic enzymes, bile salts, villus height, and mucosal IgA are not the described mechanism. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (duodenal ulcers); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, peptic ulcer disease and H. pylori infection).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal ulcers are caused by H. pylori and gastrinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: H. pylori is diagnosed by urea breath test, fecal antigen, or biopsy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"An 80-year-old woman has an H. pylori gastric ulcer. Her measured net acid secretion is lower than normal, and her serum gastrin is raised. What explains the low net acid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Parietal cell autoimmunity","B":"Back leak into the mucosa","C":"Gastrin receptor loss","D":"Excess somatostatin release","E":"Vagal nerve injury"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In gastric ulcers the mucosal barrier is defective, so some secreted H+ leaks back into the damaged mucosa and net H+ secretion is lower than normal; the reduced net acid removes feedback on G cells and raises gastrin. Autoimmunity against parietal cells causes atrophic gastritis. Gastrin receptor loss, excess somatostatin, and vagal injury are not the described mechanism. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastric ulcers and Table 8.5).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori releases CagA toxin after attaching to epithelial cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: chronic NSAID use is a cause of gastric ulcers (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman in a study smells and chews food and then spits it out, and her acid output rises before food reaches her stomach. Which vagal actions drive this response?","choices":{"A":"ACh and GRP release","B":"Gastrin and CCK release","C":"Secretin and GIP release","D":"Motilin and GIP release","E":"Histamine and somatostatin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the cephalic phase, vagal efferents release ACh onto parietal cells and GRP onto antral G cells, so acid rises directly and through gastrin. Gastrin is released secondarily via GRP rather than by the vagus itself, and CCK is not part of the cephalic phase. Secretin, GIP, and motilin are not vagal transmitters. Histamine comes from ECL cells, and somatostatin inhibits acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, phases of gastric acid secretion); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cephalic phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cephalic phase accounts for about 30% of meal stimulated acid secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: smell, taste, chewing, and conditioned reflexes start the cephalic phase (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes and gastric secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy chews bread until it tastes sweet, but digestion of the starch by his salivary enzyme stops soon after he swallows. Why?","choices":{"A":"Pepsin degrades the starch","B":"Gastric acid inactivates it","C":"Intrinsic factor binds it","D":"Pancreatic lipase blocks it","E":"Bile salts inhibit it"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acinar cells secrete alpha amylase, which begins starch digestion in the mouth, but salivary amylase is inactivated by the low pH of the gastric contents, so it plays little role overall. Pepsin digests protein, not starch. Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12. Pancreatic lipase and bile salts act in the duodenum on fat. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, acinar cell secretion); Costanzo Ch. 8 (digestion of carbohydrates: salivary amylase is inactivated by low gastric pH).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic amylase completes starch digestion in the small intestine (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: lingual lipase begins the digestion of lipids in the mouth (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy is badly frightened before a school play, and his sympathetic activity is high. His mouth is dry even though sympathetic input stimulates his acinar cells. Which sympathetic effect also limits his fluid output?","choices":{"A":"Beta receptor blockade","B":"Ductal water absorption","C":"Reduced gland blood flow","D":"Myoepithelial relaxation","E":"Acinar cell apoptosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sympathetic stimulation raises cAMP and produces a small volume of viscous saliva, but concurrent sympathetic vasoconstriction reduces local blood supply and blunts overall fluid output; fear also lowers parasympathetic drive. Norepinephrine activates rather than blocks beta receptors. Ductal water absorption is not a sympathetic effect. Stimulation contracts rather than relaxes myoepithelial cells. Fear does not kill acinar cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, sympathetic pathway and parasympathetic pathway).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic preganglionic fibers to the salivary glands arise from spinal segments T1 to T3 (lecture SDL 7). COMLEX: salivary glands have a very high blood flow that rises when saliva production is stimulated (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q03","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":3,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl in a study receives an infusion of CCK, and her parietal cells secrete acid much as they do in response to gastrin. Which mechanism explains this?","choices":{"A":"Both bind CCK2 receptors","B":"CCK triggers vagal ACh","C":"CCK releases antral gastrin","D":"Both bind H2 receptors","E":"CCK inhibits D cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gastrin acts on parietal cells through CCK2 receptors, which have equal affinity for gastrin and CCK, whereas CCK1 receptors are specific for CCK; CCK can therefore act at the same receptor. CCK does not act by releasing vagal ACh or antral gastrin. H2 receptors bind histamine. Inhibiting D cells is not the described mechanism. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastrin binds CCK2 receptors on parietal cells); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, secretagogues and receptor pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin acts through a Gq cascade to increase H+/K+ ATPase activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, parietal cell physiology). COMLEX: CCK1 receptors are specific for CCK (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":3,"stem":"An 86-year-old man in a study receives somatostatin, and his acid output falls more than its direct parietal cell effect predicts. Which paracrine action adds to the fall?","choices":{"A":"More vagal ACh release","B":"Blocking of H2 receptors","C":"Inhibited carbonic anhydrase","D":"Less ECL histamine release","E":"Closed apical chloride channels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Besides its direct action on parietal cells, somatostatin acts by a paracrine mechanism to inhibit histamine release from ECL cells, reducing the stimulatory action of histamine. It does not raise vagal ACh, block H2 receptors, inhibit carbonic anhydrase, or close chloride channels. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, inhibitory pathways); Costanzo Ch. 8 (inhibition of HCl secretion, indirect pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the somatostatin analog octreotide is used to manage carcinoid tumors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, carcinoid tumor management). COMLEX: somatostatin inhibits acid secretion by both direct and indirect pathways (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — somatostatin inhibits gastrin secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 17-year-old girl has healthy gastric mucosa. Some pepsin from her gastric juice diffuses into the surface mucus layer. Why does it not digest her epithelium?","choices":{"A":"High pH inactivates it","B":"Mucus digests the pepsin","C":"Tight junctions bind it","D":"Prostaglandins degrade it","E":"Blood flow carries it off"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Surface cells secrete mucus that traps HCO3−, creating a relatively alkaline layer at the cell surface; pepsin that penetrates this layer is inactivated in the high HCO3− environment. Mucus does not digest pepsin. Tight junctions limit back-diffusion of acid rather than bind pepsin. Prostaglandins do not degrade pepsin. Mucosal blood flow removes back-diffusing H+, not pepsin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, gastric mucosal barrier); Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastric diffusion barrier).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the alkaline mucus gel neutralizes acid and inactivates pepsin to prevent autodigestion (lecture SDL 2). COMLEX: H+ and pepsin together erode the mucosa in peptic ulcer disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 78-year-old woman has a duodenal ulcer, and imaging shows no pancreatic or duodenal tumor. Serum gastrin is measured while fasting and again after a test meal. Which pattern is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Low fasting and normal after meals","B":"Low fasting and low after meals","C":"High fasting and normal after meals","D":"Normal fasting and low after meals","E":"Normal fasting and high after meals"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In persons with duodenal ulcers, baseline gastrin may be normal, but gastrin secretion in response to a meal is increased. Low fasting and low or normal meal values do not fit duodenal ulcer disease. A high fasting value with a normal meal response, or a normal fasting value with a low meal response, is not the described pattern. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (duodenal ulcers: normal baseline gastrin, increased meal response; Zollinger-Ellison syndrome). The fasting and meal values in the wrong rows are not described by the sources.","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal ulcer pain improves with meals, whereas gastric ulcer pain follows meals (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: a gastrinoma can sit in the pancreas or duodenum within the gastrinoma triangle (lecture SDL 7).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl07_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":4,"stem":"An 83-year-old woman with osteoarthritis has taken a nonprescription anti-inflammatory pill at high dose every day for several months and now has burning epigastric pain. Endoscopy shows multiple gastric erosions. Which of the following sets of gastric mucosal findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Acid secretion","Bicarbonate secretion","Mucosal blood flow"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↔","↔"],"B":["↑","↔","↔"],"C":["↑","↓","↓"],"D":["↔","↔","↓"],"E":["↑","↓","↔"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"NSAIDs inhibit cyclooxygenase and lower PGE2, so Choice C (↑ acid secretion, ↓ bicarbonate secretion, ↓ mucosal blood flow) is correct. PGE2 normally acts through Gi receptors on parietal cells to oppose histamine, stimulates mucus and HCO3− secretion, and maintains mucosal blood flow; losing it tips the balance toward damaging factors. In the barrier-loss view, which Robbins emphasizes, prostaglandins support nearly every mucosal defense (mucus, bicarbonate, blood flow, and restitution), so the injury comes mainly from weakened protection rather than from extra acid; Robbins notes that prostaglandins also reduce acid secretion, so their loss fits the ↑ acid that Bootcamp gives. Choice A (↓ acid secretion, ↔ bicarbonate secretion, ↔ mucosal blood flow) is an H2 receptor antagonist such as famotidine, which reversibly blocks parietal cell H2 receptors and lowers acid secretion. Choice B (↑ acid secretion, ↔ bicarbonate secretion, ↔ mucosal blood flow) is a Cushing ulcer, in which brain injury and raised intracranial pressure increase vagal stimulation of parietal cells. Choice D (↔ acid secretion, ↔ bicarbonate secretion, ↓ mucosal blood flow) is a Curling ulcer, in which burns or trauma cause hypovolemia and reduced gastric blood flow. Choice E (↑ acid secretion, ↓ bicarbonate secretion, ↔ mucosal blood flow) is antral H. pylori infection in duodenal ulcer disease, in which loss of D cell somatostatin raises gastrin and acid delivery while duodenal bicarbonate secretion falls. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, lines 1368–1370, prostaglandins bind Gi receptors on parietal cells; section 5, PGE2 preserves the mucosal barrier; SDL 9, section 2, lines 1715–1719, antral H. pylori in duodenal ulcer disease; SDL 10, section 1, H2 receptor antagonists); Costanzo Ch. 8 (PGE2 stimulates HCO3− and mucus secretion and increases mucosal blood flow); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (line 1044, NSAIDs lower PGE2, raising H+ secretion and lowering bicarbonate secretion and gastric blood flow; lines 1045–1046, Cushing and Curling ulcers); Robbins Ch. 17 (NSAIDs remove prostaglandin support for mucus, bicarbonate, blood flow, and restitution, and prostaglandins reduce acid secretion). The ↔ cells in rows A, B, D, and E are inferred because the sources do not describe those values as changed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: misoprostol is used for NSAID ulcer prophylaxis and is contraindicated in pregnancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: Curling ulcers follow burns and hypovolemia, and Cushing ulcers follow brain injury (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — NSAID associated ulcer treatment and prevention","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":8,"title":"SDL 8 — Pathology of the Esophagus","questions":[{"id":"sdl08_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, loss of which neurons causes primary achalasia?","choices":{"A":"Excitatory cholinergic neurons","B":"Vagal sensory neurons","C":"Sympathetic adrenergic neurons","D":"Interstitial pacemaker cells","E":"Nitric oxide neurons"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Primary achalasia results from idiopathic degeneration or autoimmune loss of nitric oxide producing inhibitory enteric neurons in the myenteric plexus, so the LES fails to relax and the distal esophagus loses peristalsis. Loss of excitatory cholinergic neurons would weaken contraction. Vagal sensory, sympathetic, and pacemaker cells are not the lesion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, achalasia); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (achalasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: achalasia shows a bird beak sign on barium swallow (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, achalasia). COMLEX: achalasia raises the risk of esophageal cancer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where does a B ring of the distal esophagus lie?","choices":{"A":"In the upper cervical esophagus","B":"At the squamocolumnar junction","C":"At the cricopharyngeal muscle","D":"In the middle thoracic esophagus","E":"Within the gastric antrum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"B rings lie at the squamocolumnar junction and have gastric cardia mucosa on their undersurface, whereas A rings lie above the gastroesophageal junction and are covered by squamous mucosa. Upper esophageal webs occur in Plummer Vinson syndrome. The cricopharyngeus, midesophagus, and antrum are not ring sites. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal webs and rings); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Schatzki ring).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schatzki rings are associated with hiatal hernia and GERD (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal ring). COMLEX: rings cause intermittent solid food dysphagia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":1,"stem":"What dysphagia pattern does progressive esophageal stenosis typically produce?","choices":{"A":"Painful swallowing of liquids","B":"Solids and liquids from the onset","C":"Liquids worse than solids","D":"Solids first and later liquids","E":"Episodic dysphagia for meat"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Stenosis narrows the lumen progressively, so mechanical dysphagia begins with solids and later involves liquids as the caliber narrows, with appetite and weight usually preserved unless the lesion is malignant. Dysphagia to solids and liquids from onset is the motor pattern of achalasia. Episodic meat impaction fits rings. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: strictures follow GERD, caustic ingestion, or radiation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal stricture). COMLEX: barium swallow shows narrowing at the stricture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what failure during severe retching causes a Mallory Weiss tear?","choices":{"A":"Pylorus fails to close","B":"Vagal tone is lost","C":"Gastric acid is excessive","D":"Diaphragm fails to contract","E":"LES fails to relax first"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mallory Weiss tears occur when reflex relaxation of the LES fails to precede antiperistaltic gastric expulsion, so the wall near the gastroesophageal junction is overstretched and lacerated. Pyloric closure, vagal tone, excess acid, and diaphragm contraction are not the lecture mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, mucosal lacerations).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mallory Weiss syndrome is associated with alcohol use and bulimia nervosa (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Mallory Weiss syndrome). COMLEX: these tears account for up to 10% of upper GI bleeding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium and protection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":1,"stem":"What does Candida esophagitis look like at endoscopy?","choices":{"A":"Punched out volcanic ulcers","B":"Stacked circular mucosal rings","C":"Salmon pink mucosal tongues","D":"Gray white pseudomembranes","E":"Dilated submucosal veins"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Candida forms adherent gray white pseudomembranes of matted hyphae, pseudohyphae, and inflammatory cells over eroded mucosa. Punched out ulcers, stacked rings, salmon pink tongues, and dilated veins are not the Candida appearance. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, infectious esophagitis); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (esophageal candidiasis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Candida histology shows pseudohyphae and budding yeast (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal candidiasis). COMLEX: infectious esophagitis causes prominent odynophagia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium and protection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the minimum eosinophil count on esophageal biopsy that supports eosinophilic esophagitis?","choices":{"A":"At least 2 per HPF","B":"At least 15 per HPF","C":"At least 5 per HPF","D":"At least 10 per HPF","E":"At least 50 per HPF"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eosinophilic esophagitis shows dense intraepithelial eosinophils, at least 15 per high power field, forming superficial clusters with spongiosis. Reflux esophagitis has only scattered intraepithelial eosinophils, so counts of 2, 5, or 10 per field are below the cutoff, and 50 per field exceeds the minimum. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, eosinophilic esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophilic esophagitis is driven by Th2 inflammation to food antigens (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, eosinophilic esophagitis). COMLEX: it is associated with atopic conditions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium and protection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cell on biopsy is the histologic hallmark of Barrett esophagus?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cells","B":"Parietal cells","C":"Chief cells","D":"Paneth cells","E":"Keratinocytes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Barrett esophagus is simple columnar epithelium containing distinctive goblet cells with pale blue mucin vacuoles. Parietal and chief cells belong to gastric glands. Paneth cells are not the lecture hallmark. Keratinocytes would indicate squamous epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of Barrett esophagus); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Barrett esophagus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in Barrett esophagus the Z line extends proximally (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: Barrett mucosa may undergo malignant transformation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing reflux","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"What process replaces esophageal squamous epithelium in Barrett esophagus?","choices":{"A":"Squamous hyperplasia","B":"Gastric glandular atrophy","C":"Intestinal metaplasia","D":"Fibrous scarring","E":"Epithelial apoptosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Persistent acid injury reprograms esophageal stem cells toward a columnar fate, so intestinal metaplasia replaces the squamous lining with acid resistant columnar epithelium. Hyperplasia keeps squamous epithelium. Atrophy, scarring, and apoptosis do not produce columnar epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of Barrett esophagus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Barrett esophagus is a complication of chronic GERD (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD). COMLEX: endoscopy shows salmon pink mucosa above the junction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing reflux","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, mutation of which gene is a key step in progression of Barrett dysplasia to adenocarcinoma?","choices":{"A":"APC","B":"TP53","C":"BRCA1","D":"RB1","E":"VHL"},"correct":"B","explanation":"TP53 mutation is among the genetic alterations that drive Barrett dysplasia to invasive esophageal adenocarcinoma in the lecture. APC, BRCA1, RB1, and VHL are not listed for this progression. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of esophageal adenocarcinoma; section 5, pathogenesis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal adenocarcinoma arises in the distal third (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: GERD and obesity are adenocarcinoma risk factors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing reflux","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":1,"stem":"Where do esophageal veins drain the blood shunted to them from the portal circulation?","choices":{"A":"Splenic vein","B":"Inferior mesenteric vein","C":"Hepatic veins","D":"Renal veins","E":"Azygos system"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At the esophagogastric junction, portal blood shunted at the junction enters esophageal veins that drain into the azygos system. The splenic and inferior mesenteric veins feed the portal system. Hepatic veins drain the liver. Renal veins drain the kidneys. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, portosystemic collateral vessels).","boardPrep":"Step 1: varices are dilated submucosal veins at a portocaval anastomosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: variceal bleeding causes hematemesis and shock.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — layers of the esophageal wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what share of patients with cirrhosis develop esophageal varices?","choices":{"A":"About 5 to 10 percent","B":"About 15 to 20 percent","C":"About 40 to 60 percent","D":"About 80 to 90 percent","E":"About 1 to 2 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Varices develop in about 40% to 60% of patients with cirrhosis. The lower and higher ranges do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, clinical significance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal hypertension dilates submucosal veins (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: cirrhosis commonly follows alcohol use, viral hepatitis, or steatohepatitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — layers of the esophageal wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the immediate mortality of each episode of variceal bleeding?","choices":{"A":"About 15 to 30 percent","B":"About 1 to 2 percent","C":"About 3 to 5 percent","D":"About 50 to 60 percent","E":"About 80 to 90 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Each episode of variceal bleeding carries an immediate mortality of 15% to 30%, and survivors face an 80% two year risk of rebleeding. The other ranges do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, clinical significance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: variceal bleeding presents abruptly with hematemesis and melena (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: hemorrhagic shock may follow.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — layers of the esophageal wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"Where in the esophagus does squamous cell carcinoma most often arise?","choices":{"A":"Distal third","B":"Gastric cardia","C":"Middle third","D":"Upper sphincter","E":"Abdominal segment"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Squamous cell carcinoma arises mainly in the middle third (about 50%) and upper third, whereas adenocarcinoma lies in the distal third and often invades the gastric cardia. The upper sphincter and abdominal segment are not the usual sites. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, anatomic distribution); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (squamous cell carcinoma: proximal two thirds).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma is the most common esophageal cancer worldwide (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, squamous cell carcinoma). COMLEX: barium swallow may show an apple core lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which infection is associated with a reduced risk of esophageal adenocarcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Human papillomavirus","B":"Epstein Barr virus","C":"Cytomegalovirus","D":"Helicobacter pylori","E":"Herpes simplex virus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The lecture notes that H. pylori infection is associated with a reduced risk of esophageal adenocarcinoma, and that alcohol is not a risk factor. Human papillomavirus is listed by Bootcamp as a squamous cell carcinoma risk factor. Epstein Barr virus, cytomegalovirus, and herpes simplex virus are not described as protective. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, major risk factors); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (squamous cell carcinoma risk factors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenocarcinoma is the most common esophageal cancer in the US (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: adenocarcinoma shows a strong male predominance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic feature identifies esophageal squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Keratin pearls","B":"Goblet cell glands","C":"Signet ring cells","D":"Mucin vacuoles","E":"Cribriform glands"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Squamous cell carcinoma shows nests of malignant squamous cells with keratin pearls and intercellular bridges. Goblet cells, signet ring cells, mucin vacuoles, and cribriform glands are glandular features rather than squamous ones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, morphologic features); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (squamous cell carcinoma histopathology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma risk factors include smoking, alcohol, and hot beverages (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, squamous cell carcinoma). COMLEX: both esophageal cancers present with progressive dysphagia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 37-year-old woman who grew up in rural Brazil has dysphagia to both solids and liquids, regurgitation of undigested food at night, and a bird beak narrowing on barium swallow. Which agent most likely caused her disease?","choices":{"A":"Trypanosoma cruzi","B":"Candida albicans","C":"Herpes simplex virus","D":"Cytomegalovirus","E":"Helicobacter pylori"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Secondary achalasia arises from Chagas disease, in which Trypanosoma cruzi destroys the myenteric plexus; it can also follow diabetic neuropathy, amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, or malignancy. Candida, herpes simplex virus, and cytomegalovirus cause infectious esophagitis with odynophagia. H. pylori infects the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, achalasia); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Chagas disease secondary achalasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: T. cruzi is shed in the feces of the reduviid bug (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Chagas disease). COMLEX: Chagas disease can cause megaesophagus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has had episodes over several years of a steak bolus sticking low in his chest, each resolving without trouble swallowing liquids. Which lesion best explains this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Primary achalasia","B":"Esophageal adenocarcinoma","C":"Scleroderma dysmotility","D":"Esophageal varices","E":"Distal esophageal ring"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Rings cause episodic, nonprogressive mechanical dysphagia triggered by poorly chewed solid boluses, the steakhouse syndrome. Achalasia causes dysphagia to solids and liquids from onset. Cancer causes progressive dysphagia with weight loss. Scleroderma causes motor dysphagia with reflux. Varices bleed rather than obstruct. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal webs and rings).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schatzki B rings lie at the squamocolumnar junction (lecture SDL 8). COMLEX: rings present with dysphagia and regurgitation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has fatigue, a sore smooth tongue, cracks at the corners of her mouth, and dysphagia high in her throat. Her blood count shows a microcytic anemia. Which syndrome is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Pharyngeal Zenker diverticulum","B":"Plummer Vinson syndrome","C":"Mallory Weiss syndrome","D":"Boerhaave syndrome","E":"Barrett esophagus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Upper esophageal webs with iron deficiency anemia, glossitis, and cheilosis make up Plummer Vinson syndrome. Zenker diverticulum is a pouch with halitosis and regurgitation. Mallory Weiss and Boerhaave syndromes follow vomiting. Barrett esophagus is distal metaplasia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal webs and rings); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Plummer Vinson syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Plummer Vinson syndrome raises the risk of squamous cell carcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Plummer Vinson syndrome). COMLEX: webs occur most often in women over 40.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 21-year-old man vomits repeatedly after a night of binge drinking and then vomits bright red blood. His vital signs are stable, and the bleeding stops on its own. Which lesion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Ruptured submucosal vein","B":"Bleeding gastric ulcer","C":"Longitudinal mucosal tear","D":"Ulcerated squamous carcinoma","E":"Candida pseudomembranes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A Mallory Weiss tear is a longitudinal mucosal laceration crossing the gastroesophageal junction after severe retching, presenting as painful hematemesis that usually heals quickly. Variceal rupture needs portal hypertension. A gastric ulcer is not linked to the retching sequence. Carcinoma and candidiasis do not fit the sudden onset after vomiting. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, mucosal lacerations); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Mallory Weiss syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: endoscopy shows longitudinal tears near the gastroesophageal junction (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Mallory Weiss syndrome). COMLEX: prolonged coughing is also an association.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric propulsion and retropulsion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-year-old man who is otherwise healthy has severe pain on swallowing. Endoscopy shows punched out ulcers, and biopsy of the ulcer edge shows multinucleated squamous cells with nuclear molding. Which organism is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Herpes simplex virus","B":"Cytomegalovirus","C":"Candida albicans yeast","D":"Trypanosoma cruzi","E":"Helicobacter pylori"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Herpes simplex virus causes punched out, volcanic ulcers with multinucleated squamous cells, Cowdry A inclusions, and nuclear molding at the ulcer edge, and it can occur in healthy hosts. Cytomegalovirus causes shallow linear ulcers. Candida forms pseudomembranes. T. cruzi causes achalasia. H. pylori infects the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, infectious esophagitis); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (herpes esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: herpes esophagitis is usually caused by HSV 1 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, herpes esophagitis). COMLEX: odynophagia is prominent in infectious esophagitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 13-year-old boy with asthma and eczema has repeated food impaction. Endoscopy shows stacked circular rings and linear furrows, and his symptoms persist after 8 weeks of a PPI. Which condition is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Acid reflux esophagitis","B":"Eosinophilic esophagitis","C":"Herpes simplex esophagitis","D":"Candida esophagitis","E":"Multiple Schatzki rings"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eosinophilic esophagitis is a Th2 driven allergic disorder linked to atopy that causes food impaction, a ringed feline esophagus with furrows and exudates, and symptoms that are refractory to PPI therapy alone. Reflux esophagitis responds to acid suppression. Herpes and Candida cause odynophagia with ulcers or plaques. Schatzki rings are distal and nonprogressive and do not cause furrows, atopy, or PPI refractory symptoms. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, eosinophilic esophagitis); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (eosinophilic esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: endoscopy may show trachealization of the esophagus (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, eosinophilic esophagitis). COMLEX: infants present with feeding intolerance and vomiting.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has heartburn that worsens when she lies down after meals. Endoscopy shows hyperemia, and biopsy of the lower esophagus shows elongated lamina propria papillae. Which other finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cell metaplasia","B":"Keratin pearl formation","C":"Cowdry A inclusions","D":"Basal zone hyperplasia","E":"Fungal pseudohyphae"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Reflux esophagitis shows scattered intraepithelial eosinophils, basal zone hyperplasia above 20% of epithelial thickness, and papillae extending into the upper third of the epithelium. Goblet cells, keratin pearls, Cowdry A inclusions, and pseudohyphae are not features of reflux injury. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, reflux esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GERD risk factors include obesity, pregnancy, hiatal hernia, and smoking (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD). COMLEX: GERD is treated with lifestyle change and a PPI.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing reflux","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with long-standing Barrett esophagus has surveillance biopsies. The glands show rounded hyperchromatic nuclei, loss of nuclear polarity, atypical mitoses, and gland within gland patterns, but the basement membrane is intact. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Low grade dysplasia","B":"Invasive adenocarcinoma","C":"High grade dysplasia","D":"Reflux esophagitis","E":"Squamous dysplasia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"High grade dysplasia shows marked atypia, rounded hyperchromatic nuclei, loss of polarity, atypical mitoses, and cribriform architecture, while the basement membrane is not breached. Low grade dysplasia has elongated nuclei limited to the basal aspect of glands. Invasion of the lamina propria defines carcinoma. Reflux esophagitis is inflammatory. Squamous dysplasia arises in squamous epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, progression to dysplasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic reflux causes intestinal metaplasia with simple columnar epithelium (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: Barrett esophagus needs endoscopic surveillance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with Barrett esophagus has a new nodule in the distal esophagus. Biopsy shows atypical glands that have breached the basement membrane and invaded the lamina propria and submucosa. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"High grade dysplasia","B":"Invasive adenocarcinoma","C":"Squamous cell carcinoma","D":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumor","E":"Leiomyoma of the wall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Once dysplastic Barrett cells breach the basement membrane and invade the lamina propria and submucosa, the lesion is invasive esophageal adenocarcinoma. High grade dysplasia lacks invasion. Squamous cell carcinoma forms squamous nests. Stromal tumors and leiomyomas are mesenchymal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of esophageal adenocarcinoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenocarcinoma histology shows glandular tumor with intestinal differentiation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: esophageal cancer presents with progressive dysphagia for solids then liquids and rapid weight loss (lecture SDL 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman with alcoholic cirrhosis suddenly vomits a large amount of blood and becomes hypotensive. Which lesion is the most likely source?","choices":{"A":"Mallory Weiss mucosal tear","B":"Ulcerated Barrett adenocarcinoma","C":"Ruptured esophageal varices","D":"Candida pseudomembranes","E":"Eosinophilic esophagitis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cirrhosis raises portal pressure and creates varices beneath thin squamous mucosa that rupture with massive, life threatening hematemesis. A Mallory Weiss tear needs prior retching and is usually self limited. Carcinoma, Candida, and eosinophilic esophagitis rarely cause sudden massive bleeding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, clinical significance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: variceal bleeding can cause melena or hematochezia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: survivors have a high risk of rebleeding.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — management of variceal hemorrhage","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old man has cirrhosis and portal hypertension. Which vein carries portal blood into his esophageal collaterals at the esophagogastric junction?","choices":{"A":"Superior rectal vein","B":"Paraumbilical vein","C":"Inferior vena cava","D":"Right renal vein","E":"Left gastric vein"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At the esophagogastric junction, blood shunts from the left gastric (coronary) vein into the esophageal veins. The superior rectal and paraumbilical veins form other portosystemic collaterals. The inferior vena cava and renal veins are systemic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, portosystemic collateral vessels).","boardPrep":"Step 1: varices form at portocaval anastomoses (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: varices also form in the proximal stomach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — management of variceal hemorrhage","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with cirrhosis has large varices in her distal esophagus. Which anatomic feature makes these vessels especially prone to rupture?","choices":{"A":"Thick muscular venous walls","B":"Low pressure within the veins","C":"Deep location in the adventitia","D":"Thin overlying squamous mucosa","E":"Dense surrounding fibrosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Varices lie immediately beneath thin squamous mucosa under high hydrostatic pressure, so they rupture easily and bleed massively. Their walls are thin, pressure is high, and they lie in the subepithelial and submucosal plexuses rather than the adventitia. Fibrosis does not protect them. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, development of collaterals and clinical significance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: varices lie in the subepithelial and submucosal venous plexuses of the distal esophagus (lecture SDL 8). COMLEX: varices develop in many patients with cirrhosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman who grew up in northern Iran drinking very hot tea has progressive dysphagia and weight loss. Endoscopy shows an ulcerated esophageal mass. Which tumor is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Squamous cell carcinoma","B":"Esophageal adenocarcinoma","C":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumor","D":"Esophageal leiomyoma","E":"Neuroendocrine tumor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common esophageal cancer worldwide, concentrated in belts such as Iran and central China, and linked to very hot beverages. Adenocarcinoma arises from Barrett mucosa. Stromal tumors, leiomyomas, and neuroendocrine tumors are not linked to hot tea. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, epidemiology, risk factors, and anatomic distribution).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zenker diverticulum is listed as a squamous cell carcinoma risk factor (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, squamous cell carcinoma). COMLEX: both cancers have poor five year survival.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman with long-standing reflux and obesity has a mass in her distal esophagus that extends into the gastric cardia. Which histology is expected on biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Nests with keratin pearls","B":"Spindle cells with KIT","C":"Small round blue cells","D":"Malignant mucin glands","E":"Sheets of lymphoid cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Esophageal adenocarcinoma, linked to GERD, Barrett esophagus, and obesity, arises in the distal third and shows mucin producing malignant glands or signet ring cells. Keratin pearls, spindle cells, small round cells, and lymphoid sheets are not the expected pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, morphologic features).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenocarcinoma risk factors include Barrett esophagus, smoking, GERD, and obesity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: endoscopy with biopsy confirms the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman has a midesophageal lesion in which atypical cells replace the full thickness of the surface lining but do not invade. Which lesion is this?","choices":{"A":"Barrett metaplasia","B":"Basal zone hyperplasia","C":"Reflux esophagitis","D":"Candida esophagitis","E":"Squamous dysplasia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Squamous cell carcinoma arises from squamous dysplasia, also called carcinoma in situ, driven by cell cycle gene mutations. Barrett metaplasia is columnar. Basal zone hyperplasia and reflux esophagitis are reactive. Candida esophagitis forms pseudomembranes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, pathogenesis and precursor lesions).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tylosis is a squamous cell carcinoma risk factor (lecture SDL 8). COMLEX: early lesions appear as gray white plaques.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman swallowed lye as a child and now has slowly worsening dysphagia. Endoscopy shows a localized circumferential narrowing. Which change in the wall causes the narrowing?","choices":{"A":"Myenteric neuron loss","B":"Submucosal fibrosis","C":"Mucosal fold duplication","D":"Muscularis hypertrophy","E":"Venous plexus dilation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Esophageal stenosis is incomplete luminal opening from fibrous thickening of the submucosa with atrophy of the muscularis propria and mucosal damage; acquired forms follow caustic ingestion, GERD, radiation, or systemic sclerosis. Myenteric neuron loss causes achalasia. Fold duplication is not a stenosis mechanism. The muscularis atrophies rather than hypertrophies. Dilated venous plexuses are varices. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: caustic ingestion is a risk factor for esophageal stricture (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal stricture). COMLEX: caustic strictures also raise squamous cell carcinoma risk (lecture SDL 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has sudden severe chest pain after forceful vomiting. He is tachypneic and hypotensive, with crepitus in his neck and air around the mediastinum on CT. Which injury explains the air?","choices":{"A":"Full thickness wall tear","B":"Superficial mucosal tear","C":"Variceal wall rupture","D":"Candida mucosal erosion","E":"Eosinophilic microabscess"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Boerhaave syndrome is a transmural esophageal rupture from a sudden rise in intraesophageal pressure during vomiting or straining, so air escapes and causes pneumomediastinum, mediastinitis, and shock. A superficial tear is confined to the mucosa and does not perforate the esophageal wall, so air does not enter the mediastinum. Variceal rupture, Candida erosions, and eosinophilic microabscesses do not breach the wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, mucosal lacerations); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Boerhaave syndrome: transmural perforation, subcutaneous or mediastinal emphysema).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Boerhaave syndrome usually involves the distal third of the esophagus (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Boerhaave syndrome). COMLEX: barium for contrast esophagography may cause mediastinitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Boerhaave syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl receiving chemotherapy has odynophagia, and endoscopy shows shallow linear ulcers. Her physician suspects cytomegalovirus. Where should the biopsy target to find viral inclusions?","choices":{"A":"Squamous cells at the margin","B":"Superficial keratin layer","C":"Ulcer base endothelium","D":"Overlying pseudomembrane","E":"Normal mucosa nearby"},"correct":"C","explanation":"CMV inclusions, both nuclear and cytoplasmic, sit within endothelial cells and stromal fibroblasts in the ulcer base, so the base is sampled. Multinucleated squamous cells at the ulcer edge are the herpes finding. A pseudomembrane suggests Candida. The keratin layer and nearby normal mucosa do not hold CMV inclusions. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, infectious esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CMV inclusions are both intranuclear and cytoplasmic (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, CMV esophagitis). COMLEX: infectious esophagitis affects immunocompromised patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q04","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has esophageal adenocarcinoma arising in Barrett mucosa, and his tumor is tested for a targetable oncogene that is commonly amplified during Barrett progression. Which gene is it?","choices":{"A":"ERBB2","B":"MYCN","C":"ABL1","D":"PIK3CA","E":"BRAF"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Progression of Barrett dysplasia to adenocarcinoma involves tumor suppressor loss and oncogene amplification such as ERBB2 or KRAS, and ERBB2 (HER2) is a targetable receptor. MYCN, ABL1, PIK3CA, and BRAF are not the oncogenes listed for this progression. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of esophageal adenocarcinoma; section 5, pathogenesis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: high grade dysplasia in Barrett mucosa shows loss of nuclear polarity and atypical mitoses (lecture SDL 8). COMLEX: tobacco and obesity raise adenocarcinoma risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman has cirrhosis. Besides fixed fibrosis, which active change inside her liver raises resistance to portal flow?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic artery dilation","B":"Lower splanchnic inflow","C":"Reduced endothelin levels","D":"Sinusoidal constriction","E":"Portal vein thrombolysis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intrahepatic resistance rises from fixed fibrosis and from active sinusoidal vasoconstriction caused by less endothelial NO and more endothelin 1; splanchnic arterial vasodilation then raises portal inflow. Hepatic artery dilation is not the mechanism. Splanchnic inflow rises rather than falls. Endothelin rises rather than falls. Thrombolysis would open rather than block the portal vein. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, pathogenesis of portal hypertension).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal hypertension dilates submucosal veins at portocaval anastomoses (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: cirrhosis commonly follows alcohol use or viral hepatitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — vasoactive agents for variceal hemorrhage","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old man from western Kenya has drunk mursik, a fermented milk, for decades and now has squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus. Which carcinogen in the drink is implicated?","choices":{"A":"Aflatoxin B1","B":"Vinyl chloride","C":"Benzene vapor","D":"Asbestos fiber","E":"Acetaldehyde"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The lecture lists mursik as a squamous cell carcinoma risk factor because of its acetaldehyde content. Aflatoxin, vinyl chloride, benzene, and asbestos are linked to other cancers. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, major risk factors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tylosis and caustic strictures are squamous cell carcinoma risk factors (lecture SDL 8). COMLEX: squamous cell carcinoma is more common in African American men in Western nations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":9,"title":"SDL 9 — Pathology of the Stomach","questions":[{"id":"sdl09_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which feature of the inflammatory infiltrate typifies acute reactive gastropathy?","choices":{"A":"Few or absent neutrophils","B":"Neutrophilic pit abscesses","C":"Dense plasma cell band","D":"Gland centered lymphocytes","E":"Eosinophil microabscesses"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Reactive gastropathy from NSAIDs, alcohol, bile reflux, or ischemia shows edema, vascular congestion, erosions, and foveolar hyperplasia with a notable paucity or absence of neutrophils. Pit abscesses and a superficial plasma cell rich infiltrate are features of H. pylori gastritis. A deep gland centered lymphocytic infiltrate is autoimmune gastritis. Eosinophil microabscesses are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, acute or reactive gastropathy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs, alcohol, smoking, and physiologic stress cause gastritis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastritis general principles). COMLEX: gastritis is evaluated by H. pylori testing and upper endoscopy with biopsy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where are H. pylori organisms concentrated in a gastric biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Deep oxyntic glands","B":"Submucosal lymphatics","C":"Muscularis propria","D":"Lamina propria capillaries","E":"Superficial mucus layer"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Spiral bacilli of H. pylori are concentrated in the superficial mucus layer and are seen on H&E or silver and immunostains. They do not live in the deep glands, submucosal lymphatics, muscularis propria, or lamina propria vessels. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, H. pylori gastritis morphology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori is a gram negative motile spiral bacillus that may be seen on silver stain (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: chronic H. pylori infection raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma and MALT lymphoma (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, at about what age does autoimmune atrophic gastritis typically present?","choices":{"A":"Around age 20","B":"Around age 35","C":"Around age 60","D":"Around age 45","E":"Around age 80"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis typically presents around age 60 with manifestations of vitamin B12 deficiency. The other ages do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, autoimmune atrophic gastritis clinical manifestations).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic atrophic gastritis can follow H. pylori or pernicious anemia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic gastritis). COMLEX: autoimmune gastritis is associated with Hashimoto thyroiditis, type 1 diabetes, and Graves disease (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which zone forms the deepest layer of a chronic peptic ulcer base?","choices":{"A":"Fibrinoid necrotic debris","B":"Neutrophilic infiltrate","C":"Granulation tissue","D":"Dense fibrous scar","E":"Foveolar hyperplasia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A peptic ulcer shows four zones from the surface down: fibrinoid necrotic debris, an active neutrophilic infiltrate, vascularized granulation tissue, and deep dense fibrous scar. Foveolar hyperplasia is a reactive gastropathy feature, not an ulcer zone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, morphologic features of PUD).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric ulcers most often lie on the lesser curvature (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: gastric ulcers carry a higher malignancy risk than duodenal ulcers (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — mucosal protection and peptic ulcer pathophysiology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, when does peptic ulcer pain classically occur?","choices":{"A":"Within minutes of swallowing","B":"Mostly on waking each morning","C":"One to three hours after eating","D":"Steady through day and night","E":"Mainly during strenuous exercise"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Peptic ulcer pain is epigastric burning or aching that often occurs 1 to 3 hours after meals or at night and is relieved by food or antacids. Pain within minutes of swallowing suggests an esophageal cause. The other patterns are not the described timing. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, clinical features of PUD).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori infection and NSAIDs are the main causes of peptic ulcer disease (lecture SDL 9). COMLEX: gastric ulcers lead to weight loss and duodenal ulcers to weight gain (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — mucosal protection and peptic ulcer pathophysiology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how does antral H. pylori infection lead to duodenal ulcer disease?","choices":{"A":"Reduced mucosal prostaglandin synthesis","B":"Autonomous tumor gastrin release","C":"Autoimmune parietal cell loss","D":"Increased duodenal bicarbonate output","E":"Reduced D cell somatostatin release"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In duodenal ulcer disease, antral H. pylori inflammation inhibits D cell somatostatin secretion, removing the brake on G cells; the resulting uninhibited gastrin release causes parietal cell hyperplasia and greater acid delivery to the duodenum, while duodenal bicarbonate secretion falls. Reduced mucosal prostaglandin synthesis is the mechanism of NSAIDs, which inhibit cyclooxygenase and lower mucus, bicarbonate, and blood flow. Autonomous tumor gastrin release is Zollinger-Ellison syndrome from a gastrinoma. Autoimmune parietal cell loss causes atrophic gastritis with low acid rather than duodenal ulcers. Duodenal bicarbonate output falls rather than rises in H. pylori duodenal ulcer disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, roles of H. pylori and NSAIDs in PUD and ZES mechanism, lines 1714–1752); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (peptic ulcer disease).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists H. pylori in about 90 percent of duodenal ulcers and about 70 percent of gastric ulcers (First Aid 2025 GI, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that NSAIDs inhibit cyclooxygenase, lowering PGE2, bicarbonate secretion, and gastric blood flow (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute erosive gastritis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — mucosal protection and peptic ulcer pathophysiology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which gastric polyps account for up to 75 percent of all gastric polyps?","choices":{"A":"Fundic gland polyps","B":"Gastric adenomas","C":"Hyperplastic polyps","D":"Hamartomatous polyps","E":"Neuroendocrine polyps"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Inflammatory and hyperplastic polyps account for up to 75% of gastric polyps and arise as reactive responses to chronic mucosal injury, most often chronic H. pylori gastritis. Fundic gland polyps are less common. Adenomas are up to 10% of gastric polyps. Hamartomatous and neuroendocrine polyps are not the majority. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, inflammatory and hyperplastic polyps).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic gastritis can cause gastric mucosal atrophy with loss of glands and metaplasia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic gastritis). COMLEX: hyperplastic polyps peak at ages 50 to 60 (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which histologic finding characterizes a fundic gland polyp?","choices":{"A":"Cystic oxyntic glands","B":"Corkscrew foveolar glands","C":"Dysplastic tubular glands","D":"Sheets of signet ring cells","E":"Lymphoepithelial lesions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fundic gland polyps are composed of cystically dilated glands lined by parietal, chief, and foveolar cells with minimal inflammation. Elongated corkscrew foveolar glands in an inflamed lamina propria are hyperplastic polyps. Dysplastic glands define adenomas. Signet ring cells are diffuse carcinoma. Lymphoepithelial lesions are MALT lymphoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, fundic gland polyps).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric adenocarcinoma is the most common gastric malignancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: fundic gland polyps lie in the body and fundus (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what risk of invasive adenocarcinoma do gastric adenomas larger than 2 cm carry?","choices":{"A":"Up to 1 percent","B":"Up to 30 percent","C":"Up to 5 percent","D":"Up to 60 percent","E":"Up to 90 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastric adenomas carry up to a 30% risk of harboring or progressing to invasive adenocarcinoma when larger than 2 cm. The other values do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, gastric adenomas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori infection raises the risk of gastric malignancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: gastric adenomas are most common in men aged 50 to 60 (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which molecular subtype of intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma features TP53 mutations and RTK RAS amplifications?","choices":{"A":"Microsatellite instability","B":"Epstein Barr virus type","C":"Genomically stable type","D":"Hypermutated POLE type","E":"Chromosomal instability"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The chromosomal instability subtype is characterized by TP53 mutations, Wnt pathway mutations, SMAD4 mutations, and RTK RAS pathway amplifications such as ERBB2, KRAS, EGFR, and MET. Microsatellite instability and Epstein Barr virus association are separate subtypes. Genomically stable and POLE types are not described in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, pathogenesis and molecular alterations).","boardPrep":"Step 1: EBV is a risk factor for gastric malignancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: intestinal type tumors form glands (tubular or papillary) (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":1,"stem":"Spread of gastric adenocarcinoma to the left supraclavicular lymph node produces which named finding?","choices":{"A":"Krukenberg tumor","B":"Virchow node","C":"Sister Mary Joseph nodule","D":"Irish node","E":"Blumer shelf"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The left supraclavicular sentinel node is the Virchow node. A Krukenberg tumor is ovarian metastasis, a Sister Mary Joseph nodule is a periumbilical node, an Irish node is the left axillary node, and a Blumer shelf is a deposit in the pouch of Douglas. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, metastatic patterns); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Virchow node, Sister Mary Joseph nodule, Krukenberg tumor). Irish node and Blumer shelf names are not used in the lecture; their sites are.","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric malignancy may present with the Leser Trélat sign or acanthosis nigricans (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: gastric cancer is difficult to detect early (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where in the stomach do intestinal type adenocarcinomas mainly arise?","choices":{"A":"Fundus and greater curve","B":"Cardia near the junction","C":"Body along the greater curvature","D":"Antrum or lesser curvature","E":"Pylorus and duodenal bulb"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma is located predominantly in the antrum or lesser curvature and forms bulky exophytic or ulcerated masses of malignant glands. Diffuse type tumors infiltrate the wall without a discrete mass. The other sites are not the described location. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, morphologic patterns); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (intestinal type: lesser curvature).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma occurs at an older age than diffuse type (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: gastric malignancy can cause gastric outlet obstruction and upper GI bleeding (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which marker pattern fits the cells of gastric MALT lymphoma?","choices":{"A":"CD20 positive CD5 negative","B":"CD5 and CD23 positive","C":"CD10 and BCL6 positive","D":"CD3 positive CD20 negative","E":"CD117 and DOG1 positive"},"correct":"A","explanation":"MALT lymphoma cells express mature B cell markers CD19 and CD20, are negative for CD5 and CD10, are CD43 positive in about 25% of cases, and show light chain restriction. CD5 positivity and CD10 positivity are excluded. CD3 marks T cells. CD117 and DOG1 mark GIST. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastric lymphomas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: MALT lymphoma occurs at an older age and follows persistent H. pylori inflammation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MALT lymphoma). COMLEX: MALT lymphoma is about 5% of gastric malignancies (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — mucosal specializations and MALT","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, gastrointestinal stromal tumors arise from which cells?","choices":{"A":"Smooth muscle myocytes","B":"Cajal pacemaker cells","C":"Enterochromaffin cells","D":"Peripheral Schwann cells","E":"Stromal myofibroblasts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GISTs are the most common abdominal mesenchymal tumor and arise from the interstitial cells of Cajal, the pacemaker cells of the gut. Enterochromaffin type cells give rise to neuroendocrine tumors. Smooth muscle cells, Schwann cells, and myofibroblasts are not the described origin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastrointestinal stromal tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIST is most common in the stomach but can arise elsewhere in the GI tract (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GIST). COMLEX: GIST occurs at an older age (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric pacemaking and propulsion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which staining result supports the diagnosis of a gastric neuroendocrine tumor?","choices":{"A":"CD117 and DOG1 staining","B":"CD20 and CD19 staining","C":"Desmin and actin staining","D":"Chromogranin A staining","E":"S100 and SOX10 staining"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastric neuroendocrine tumors express synaptophysin and chromogranin A, and the diagnosis is made by histology with this positivity. CD117 and DOG1 mark GIST. CD20 and CD19 mark B cell lymphoma. Desmin, actin, S100, and SOX10 are not the described markers. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, neuroendocrine tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carcinoid syndrome causes watery diarrhea, flushing, and bronchospasm (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, carcinoid tumor). COMLEX: urinary 5 HIAA is used to diagnose carcinoid tumors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man had a partial gastrectomy with a gastrojejunal anastomosis decades ago and has chronic bile reflux. A gastric biopsy shows mucosal edema, vascular congestion, and corkscrew foveolar hyperplasia. Which diagnosis fits best?","choices":{"A":"Helicobacter gastritis","B":"Reactive gastropathy","C":"Autoimmune gastritis","D":"Menetrier disease","E":"MALT lymphoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bile reflux injures the epithelium directly, and the reactive gastropathy pattern is edema, congestion, foveolar hyperplasia with corkscrew profiles, and few or no neutrophils. H. pylori gastritis has neutrophils and plasma cells. Autoimmune gastritis has a deep lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate in the body. Ménétrier disease causes giant folds with protein loss. MALT lymphoma is a dense lymphoid infiltrate. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, acute or reactive gastropathy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: previous gastric surgery raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: management of gastritis includes avoiding NSAIDs, tobacco, and alcohol (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute erosive gastritis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — gastric mucosal barrier and its disruption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has chronic dyspepsia, and a silver stain of his antral biopsy shows curved rods. Which inflammatory finding marks active disease in this gastritis?","choices":{"A":"Eosinophilic microabscesses","B":"Noncaseating granulomas","C":"Deep lymphoid gland injury","D":"Mast cell infiltrates","E":"Intraepithelial neutrophils"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In H. pylori gastritis, intraepithelial and lamina propria neutrophils form pit abscesses, alongside a superficial infiltrate of plasma cells, lymphocytes, and macrophages. Eosinophilic microabscesses and granulomas are not the described pattern. Deep gland centered lymphocytic injury is autoimmune gastritis. Mast cell infiltrates are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, H. pylori gastritis morphology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori is treated with triple therapy (amoxicillin, clarithromycin, and a PPI) or quadruple therapy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: H. pylori infection usually starts in the antrum and spreads to the body (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — H. pylori eradication regimens","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes has a macrocytic anemia. Biopsy of the gastric body shows a deep gland centered infiltrate of lymphocytes and plasma cells without prominent neutrophils, with goblet and Paneth cells in the mucosa. Which other change is expected in her oxyntic mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Foveolar hyperplasia","B":"Neutrophilic pit abscesses","C":"Spiral surface bacilli","D":"Loss of chief cells","E":"Signet ring cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis shows thinning of the body and fundic mucosa, a deep gland centered lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate, extensive loss of parietal and chief cells, intestinal metaplasia, and neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia. Foveolar hyperplasia is a reactive or Ménétrier change. Pit abscesses and spiral bacilli are H. pylori gastritis. Signet ring cells are diffuse carcinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, autoimmune atrophic gastritis morphology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pernicious anemia raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: autoimmune gastritis is linked to HLA predisposition (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — consequences of gastric cell loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with a known posterior duodenal ulcer now has constant epigastric pain radiating to his back, and his serum lipase is raised. Which complication is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Penetration into pancreas","B":"Free perforation","C":"Gastric outlet obstruction","D":"Acute ulcer hemorrhage","E":"Malignant transformation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Penetration of an ulcer into an adjacent structure such as the pancreas causes constant pain radiating to the back and can raise pancreatic enzymes. Free perforation causes peritonitis and free air. Outlet obstruction causes vomiting. Hemorrhage causes melena or hematemesis. Duodenal ulcers are usually benign. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, complications of PUD); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (duodenal ulcers usually benign).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a posterior duodenal ulcer can bleed from the gastroduodenal artery (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, ulcer hemorrhage). COMLEX: H. pylori and gastrinoma cause duodenal ulcers (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman has endoscopy for epigastric pain. A 1.5 cm round gastric lesion has clean, flat, nonelevated borders and a smooth base, and biopsy shows necrotic debris over granulation tissue and scar. Which lesion is this?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal type carcinoma","B":"Ulcerated stromal tumor","C":"Gastric MALT lymphoma","D":"Linitis plastica","E":"Benign peptic ulcer"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A peptic ulcer is classically a solitary, small, round to oval punched out defect with clean nonelevated borders and a smooth flat base, with zones of necrotic debris, neutrophils, granulation tissue, and scar. Intestinal type carcinoma forms bulky or ulcerated masses of malignant glands. A stromal tumor is a submucosal mass. MALT lymphoma is a lymphoid infiltrate. Linitis plastica thickens the whole wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, morphologic features of PUD; section 4, morphologic patterns).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs and H. pylori cause gastric ulcers (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: PUD is managed with a PPI or H2 antagonist (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute erosive gastritis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman with years of ulcer disease near the pylorus now vomits undigested food several hours after meals, and a succussion splash is heard. Which change most likely causes her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Pyloric muscle hypertrophy","B":"Vagal motor neuron loss","C":"Twisting of the stomach","D":"Pyloric channel scarring","E":"Swallowed bezoar formation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Peptic ulcers can cause gastric outlet obstruction from edema or fibrous scarring. Pyloric muscle hypertrophy is a congenital infant disorder. Vagal motor neuron loss, gastric volvulus, and bezoars are not ulcer complications in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, complications of PUD).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric outlet obstruction is a complication of gastric malignancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: intractable pain is also a complication of PUD (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric mixing, propulsion, and pyloric resistance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-year-old girl whose father had colectomy for hundreds of colon adenomas has many small polyps in her gastric body and fundus made of cystically dilated glands. Which gene is most likely mutated in her germline?","choices":{"A":"CDH1","B":"STK11","C":"APC","D":"SMAD4","E":"KRAS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Syndromic fundic gland polyps arise with germline APC mutations in familial adenomatous polyposis, which is dominant and fits her father; they can develop dysplasia. CDH1 causes hereditary diffuse gastric cancer. STK11 and SMAD4 cause hamartomatous polyposis syndromes. KRAS is a somatic oncogene. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, fundic gland polyps). The STK11 and SMAD4 syndromes are not in the lecture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomatous polyps are the most common colonic polyps (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, colonic polyps). COMLEX: fundic gland polyps can also arise with MUTYH mutations (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has taken omeprazole for ten years. Endoscopy shows several small smooth polyps in the gastric body, and biopsy confirms the polyp type linked to his acid suppression. What is their malignant potential?","choices":{"A":"Negligible cancer risk","B":"Risk similar to adenomas","C":"High risk needing gastrectomy","D":"Risk rising after 1 cm","E":"Frequent HER2 amplification"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sporadic and PPI associated fundic gland polyps carry no significant risk of malignant transformation, unlike syndromic polyps in FAP. Adenomas carry a high risk. Hyperplastic polyp risk rises in pedunculated polyps over 1 cm. HER2 amplification is a carcinoma change. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, fundic gland polyps; hyperplastic polyps; gastric adenomas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PPIs are used for gastritis, PUD, GERD, and stress ulcer prophylaxis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: sporadic fundic gland polyps have increased with widespread PPI use (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — drug classes for acid suppression","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman has chronic atrophic gastritis. Endoscopy shows a solitary 2.5 cm velvety antral polyp, and biopsy shows enlarged elongated hyperchromatic nuclei with pseudostratification and crowded glands. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Repeat biopsy in five years","B":"Complete endoscopic resection","C":"PPI therapy without resection","D":"Reassurance and no follow up","E":"Antibiotics without resection"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is a gastric adenoma, a true neoplastic polyp with epithelial dysplasia that arises on chronic gastritis with atrophy and metaplasia; its risk of harboring or progressing to invasive carcinoma requires complete endoscopic resection and surveillance. Delayed biopsy, acid suppression, reassurance, or antibiotics leave the dysplastic lesion in place. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, gastric adenomas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric malignancy is diagnosed by endoscopy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: high grade dysplasia carries a substantial risk of progression to invasive adenocarcinoma (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — Barrett esophagus and dysplasia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has early satiety and weight loss. Imaging shows a diffusely thickened, rigid stomach wall without a discrete mass, and biopsy shows single discohesive cells infiltrating the wall. What mainly stiffens the wall?","choices":{"A":"Submucosal edema","B":"Smooth muscle hypertrophy","C":"Amyloid deposition","D":"Desmoplastic stroma","E":"Dense lymphoid infiltrate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma infiltrates as individual mucin filled cells and elicits an intense desmoplastic stromal reaction that stiffens and thickens the wall without a mass, giving the leather bottle appearance of linitis plastica. Edema, muscle hypertrophy, amyloid, and lymphoid infiltrates are not the described cause. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, morphologic patterns).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma has obscure borders and poorly cohesive cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: early satiety is especially common in diffuse type cancer (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man grew up eating salt preserved and smoked fish and has smoked for decades. He now has a bulky antral adenocarcinoma of the intestinal type. Which dietary carcinogen class is most implicated?","choices":{"A":"Aflatoxin B1","B":"Acrylamide","C":"Nitrosamines","D":"Benzene","E":"Vinyl chloride"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors include chronic H. pylori gastritis, a high salt diet, N nitroso compounds or nitrosamines, and smoking. Aflatoxin is linked to liver cancer. Acrylamide, benzene, and vinyl chloride are not the listed gastric risk factors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, risk factors and precursor lesions); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (nitrosamines, smoked and salt preserved foods).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric cancer metastasizes to the periumbilical node as a Sister Mary Joseph nodule (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: tobacco use raises gastric cancer risk (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 5 — esophageal cancer risk factors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has bilateral solid ovarian masses, and the ovarian tissue contains mucin filled cells with nuclei pushed to the periphery. Her stomach wall is thickened. Which term describes the ovarian lesions?","choices":{"A":"Brenner tumor","B":"Krukenberg tumor","C":"Dysgerminoma","D":"Mature teratoma","E":"Granulosa cell tumor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Metastasis of gastric signet ring cell carcinoma to both ovaries is a Krukenberg tumor. Brenner tumors, dysgerminomas, teratomas, and granulosa cell tumors are primary ovarian tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, metastatic patterns); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Krukenberg tumor). The primary ovarian tumor types are not in the lecture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma occurs at a younger age (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: gastric cancer can spread to the pouch of Douglas (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman has early stage gastric marginal zone B cell lymphoma arising in mucosa infected with H. pylori. Which first treatment can produce complete remission?","choices":{"A":"Trastuzumab therapy","B":"Total gastrectomy","C":"Antibiotic therapy","D":"Octreotide therapy","E":"Radiation therapy first"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Early stage H. pylori dependent MALT lymphomas frequently achieve complete remission after antibiotic eradication of H. pylori, because chronic antigen stimulation drives them. Trastuzumab targets HER2. Gastrectomy is not first line. Octreotide is a somatostatin analog for neuroendocrine tumors. Radiation is reserved for resistant tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastric lymphomas); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (MALT lymphoma: eradication therapy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: quadruple therapy for H. pylori uses metronidazole, tetracycline, bismuth, and a PPI (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: MALT lymphoma presents with dyspepsia, epigastric pain, or GI bleeding (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — H. pylori eradication regimens","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with autoimmune gastritis has several small yellow submucosal nodules in the gastric body. Biopsy shows uniform cells with salt and pepper chromatin, and staining confirms neuroendocrine origin. Which finding best predicts an indolent course?","choices":{"A":"Low Ki 67 index","B":"TP53 and RB1 loss","C":"High mitotic count","D":"Liver metastases","E":"KIT gene mutation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Well differentiated neuroendocrine tumors have a low Ki 67 proliferative index, and small gastric NETs associated with autoimmune gastritis have an indolent course managed by endoscopic resection. High Ki 67 with TP53 and RB1 mutations marks poorly differentiated carcinoma. Liver metastases signal aggressive disease and carcinoid syndrome. KIT mutations drive GIST. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, neuroendocrine tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about one third of carcinoid tumors metastasize, usually to the liver (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, carcinoid tumor). COMLEX: carcinoid tumors are managed with resection or a somatostatin analog such as octreotide (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, carcinoid tumor).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has melena. CT shows a 9 cm gastric submucosal mass with liver lesions, and biopsy shows spindle cells that stain strongly for CD117 and DOG1. Which drug is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Rituximab","B":"Trastuzumab","C":"Octreotide","D":"Cisplatin","E":"Imatinib"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Unresectable or metastatic GISTs with KIT or PDGFRA mutations are treated with tyrosine kinase inhibitors such as imatinib. Rituximab targets CD20 on B cells. Trastuzumab targets HER2. Octreotide treats neuroendocrine tumors. Cisplatin is not the described therapy. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastrointestinal stromal tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: c KIT and PDGFRA mutations cause aberrant receptor tyrosine kinase activity in GIST (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GIST). COMLEX: GIST malignant risk correlates with size, mitotic count, and location (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric pacemaking and propulsion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old man has leg edema and a low serum albumin. Endoscopy shows giant cerebriform folds in the gastric body, and biopsy shows marked foveolar hyperplasia with atrophy of the oxyntic glands. Overactivity of which signaling pathway causes this disease?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin via CCK2","B":"Histamine via H2","C":"Hedgehog via Smoothened","D":"Wnt via Frizzled","E":"TGF alpha via EGFR"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ménétrier disease results from increased TGF alpha binding the EGF receptor, which drives foveolar hyperplasia and mucus overproduction with parietal and chief cell atrophy; the protein losing gastropathy causes hypoalbuminemia and edema. Gastrin and histamine stimulate acid secretion rather than foveolar hyperplasia. Hedgehog and Wnt signaling are not the described pathway. Sources: Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Ménétrier disease: TGF alpha binds EGFR, protein losing gastropathy, foveolar hyperplasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ménétrier disease shows thick gastric folds resembling cerebral gyri on imaging (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Ménétrier disease). COMLEX: Ménétrier disease raises the risk of gastric malignancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — gastric epithelial cell types","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old man has refractory ulcers, and imaging for a suspected gastrinoma searches his pancreas. Besides the pancreas, where do these tumors commonly arise?","choices":{"A":"Gastric antrum","B":"Gastric fundus","C":"Splenic hilum","D":"Duodenal wall","E":"Distal ileum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastrinomas arise in the non beta cell pancreas, the duodenum, or peripancreatic soft tissue, the region called the gastrinoma triangle. Antral G cells make normal gastrin but are not the described tumor site. The fundus, splenic hilum, and distal ileum are not gastrinoma sites in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome pathogenesis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture links Zollinger-Ellison syndrome to MEN1 (lecture SDL 9, neuroendocrine tumors). COMLEX: gastric NETs can arise in the hypergastrinemia of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — insulinoma, gastrinoma, glucagonoma, and VIPoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman has a gastric polyp that arose on chronic H. pylori gastritis. It shows elongated corkscrew foveolar glands in an edematous, inflamed lamina propria. Which feature most raises its risk of dysplasia?","choices":{"A":"Pedunculated and over 1 cm","B":"Location in the fundus","C":"Many tiny sessile polyps","D":"Patient age under thirty years","E":"Smooth surface mucosa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps carry a dysplasia risk of 1% to 20%, which rises significantly in pedunculated polyps larger than 1 cm. Fundic location, multiple tiny polyps, young age, and a smooth surface are not the described risk features. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, inflammatory and hyperplastic polyps).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori infection raises the risk of peptic ulcer disease (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: hyperplastic polyps are most common in the antrum (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — H. pylori eradication regimens","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":3,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman has a sporadic diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma that has lost E-cadherin protein, but sequencing finds no mutation in the gene that encodes it. Which change most likely silenced the gene?","choices":{"A":"Gene amplification","B":"Activating point mutation","C":"Promoter hypermethylation","D":"Balanced translocation","E":"Microsatellite expansion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma is driven by loss of the E-cadherin gene in about half of sporadic cases, either by loss of function mutation or by promoter hypermethylation, an epigenetic change that silences an intact sequence. Amplification and activating mutations increase gene activity. Balanced translocations and microsatellite expansions are not the described mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, pathogenesis and molecular alterations).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma shows signet ring cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: hereditary diffuse gastric cancer arises de novo without identified precursor lesions (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — molecular progression in Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":3,"stem":"A 73-year-old man has gastric MALT lymphoma. Which fusion protein is produced by the most characteristic translocation of this tumor?","choices":{"A":"API2 MALT1 fusion","B":"BCR ABL1 fusion","C":"PML RARA fusion","D":"MYC IGH fusion","E":"EWSR1 FLI1 fusion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The t(11;18)(q21;q21) translocation forms the API2 MALT1 fusion protein. BCR ABL1 is chronic myeloid leukemia, PML RARA is acute promyelocytic leukemia, MYC IGH is Burkitt lymphoma, and EWSR1 FLI1 is Ewing sarcoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastric lymphomas). The other fusions are not in the lecture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MALT lymphoma forms lymphoid follicles within the gastric mucosa (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MALT lymphoma). COMLEX: MALT lymphoma is diagnosed by endoscopic biopsy with IgH clonality testing (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — mucosal lymphoid tissue","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":3,"stem":"A 13-year-old girl has a paraganglioma and a gastric stromal tumor. Tumor testing finds no KIT or PDGFRA mutation. Which defect most likely drives her tumors?","choices":{"A":"CDH1 mutation","B":"SDH deficiency","C":"APC mutation","D":"Menin gene mutation","E":"RET mutation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GISTs without KIT or PDGFRA mutations can be driven by succinate dehydrogenase complex mutations, which are linked to Carney Stratakis syndrome with paraganglioma. CDH1 causes diffuse gastric cancer. APC causes FAP. MEN1 and RET cause endocrine neoplasia syndromes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastrointestinal stromal tumors). The paraganglioma link of Carney Stratakis syndrome is not stated in the lecture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIST mutations in KIT and PDGFRA are mutually exclusive (lecture SDL 9). COMLEX: gastric GISTs are less aggressive than small intestinal GISTs (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric pacemaking and propulsion","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":10,"title":"SDL 10 — Pharmacology of acid-peptic diseases, antiemetics, and variceal hemorrhage","questions":[{"id":"sdl10_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how long does acid suppression from a single proton pump inhibitor dose last despite a plasma half life of about 1.5 hours?","choices":{"A":"About 90 minutes","B":"About 6 hours","C":"About 3 days","D":"About 2 weeks","E":"About 24 hours"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Proton pump inhibitors have a short plasma half life of about 1.5 hours but act for about 24 hours because they bind the H+/K+ ATPase covalently and irreversibly, so acid secretion returns only as new pumps are made. The shorter and longer durations do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, PPI pharmacokinetics).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors irreversibly inhibit the H+/K+ ATPase on parietal cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: omeprazole and esomeprazole inhibit CYP2C19 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which antisecretory drug class requires a dose reduction in renal insufficiency?","choices":{"A":"Proton pump inhibitors","B":"Alginate raft formers","C":"H2 receptor antagonists","D":"Calcium carbonate antacids","E":"Dopamine D2 antagonists"},"correct":"C","explanation":"H2 receptor antagonists are cleared by hepatic metabolism and renal secretion, so their dose is reduced in renal insufficiency. Proton pump inhibitors are cleared by hepatic CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 and need no renal adjustment. Alginates and antacids act locally, although magnesium and aluminum accumulate in renal failure. Dopamine D2 antagonists are prokinetics rather than antisecretory drugs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, H2 receptor antagonist pharmacokinetics).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H2 antagonists reversibly inhibit H2 receptors on parietal cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: H2 antagonists are used for gastritis, PUD, GERD, and stress ulcer prophylaxis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how far apart should antacid doses be spaced from tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, or iron?","choices":{"A":"Ten minutes","B":"Six hours","C":"Twelve hours","D":"Two hours","E":"One full day"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Antacids bind other drugs such as tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, and iron, so the lecture calls for a 2 hour separation. The other intervals do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, antacid adverse effects and interactions).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aluminum hydroxide causes constipation and hypophosphatemia, while magnesium hydroxide causes diarrhea (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: calcium carbonate can cause milk alkali syndrome and rebound acidity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, why is bismuth quadruple therapy now recommended as first line H. pylori treatment?","choices":{"A":"Clarithromycin resistance","B":"Amoxicillin allergy rates","C":"Shorter treatment course","D":"Fewer daily pill doses","E":"Metronidazole resistance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bismuth quadruple therapy is first line because clarithromycin resistance is rising, and triple therapy is now limited to regions where resistance is below 15 percent. The quadruple regimen lasts 10 to 14 days with several daily doses, so it is not shorter or simpler, and it contains metronidazole. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, H. pylori eradication regimens).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori quadruple therapy combines metronidazole, tetracycline, bismuth, and a PPI (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori). COMLEX: bismuth also treats traveler diarrhea and can darken the stool (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antidiarrheal medications).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — H. pylori gastritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how long must a proton pump inhibitor be held before a test that confirms H. pylori eradication?","choices":{"A":"Two days","B":"Six hours","C":"Six months","D":"Two weeks","E":"Twelve hours"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PPIs must be held for 2 weeks before a urea breath test, fecal antigen test, or biopsy to avoid false negative results, and testing is done at least 4 weeks after the antibiotics end. The shorter and longer intervals do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, role of PPI in eradication).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bismuth and sucralfate bind the gastric mucosa and raise bicarbonate secretion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: sucralfate requires an acidic environment to work (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — peptic ulcer disease and its complications","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what sustained intragastric pH threshold prevents acid inactivation of pepsin and preserves clot formation?","choices":{"A":"Below pH 2","B":"Above pH 6","C":"Near pH 3","D":"Near pH 4","E":"Above pH 10"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sustained elevation of intragastric pH above 6.0 prevents acid inactivation of pepsin and preserves clotting by optimizing platelet aggregation and coagulation. Lower values still allow clot breakdown, and a pH above 10 is not a lecture target. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, reduction of recurrent ulcer bleeding risk).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors raise the risk of C. difficile infection and aspiration pneumonia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: proton pump inhibitors are used for GI bleeding and stress ulcer prophylaxis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — peptic ulcer disease and its complications","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone and area postrema do aprepitant and fosaprepitant block?","choices":{"A":"Substance P receptors","B":"Dopamine D2 receptors","C":"Muscarinic M1 receptors","D":"Histamine H1 receptors","E":"Cannabinoid CB1 receptors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aprepitant and fosaprepitant cross the blood brain barrier and block central NK1 receptors, the receptors for substance P. Dopamine D2 receptors are blocked by prochlorperazine and metoclopramide. M1 and H1 receptors are blocked by scopolamine and the antihistamines. Dronabinol and nabilone are CB1 agonists. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, NK1 receptor antagonists).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NK1 antagonists such as aprepitant act centrally and treat chemotherapy induced emesis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications). COMLEX: NK1 antagonists are combined with a 5 HT3 antagonist and dexamethasone for highly emetogenic chemotherapy (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what property makes palonosetron better than older agents for delayed chemotherapy induced emesis?","choices":{"A":"Extra NK1 blockade","B":"Central CB1 agonism","C":"Long 40 hour half life","D":"Added D2 receptor blockade","E":"Peripheral restriction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Palonosetron is a second generation 5 HT3 antagonist with higher receptor affinity and a 40 hour half life, which makes it superior for delayed CINV. It does not block NK1 or D2 receptors or act at CB1 receptors, and it acts both peripherally and centrally. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, serotonin 5 HT3 receptor antagonists).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5 HT3 antagonists cause QT prolongation, headache, and serotonin syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications). COMLEX: 5 HT3 antagonists are used for chemotherapy, postoperative, and radiation induced emesis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, aprepitant inhibits CYP3A4. What change is required in the dexamethasone given with it?","choices":{"A":"Double the dose","B":"Reduce dose by half","C":"Give it intravenously","D":"Stop dexamethasone","E":"Give it at bedtime"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aprepitant and netupitant inhibit CYP3A4 and raise dexamethasone levels, so the dexamethasone dose is reduced by 50 percent. Raising the dose, changing the route or timing, and stopping the steroid are not what the lecture describes, because dexamethasone potentiates NK1 antagonists. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, NK1 adverse effects and interactions).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aprepitant also lowers the warfarin INR (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: dexamethasone used as an antiemetic can cause insomnia and hyperglycemia (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how does octreotide reduce portal inflow in acute variceal bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Inhibits glucagon release","B":"Activates V1a receptors","C":"Blocks beta 1 receptors","D":"Releases nitric oxide","E":"Directly constricts arterioles"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Somatostatin and octreotide cause selective splanchnic vasoconstriction by inhibiting the release of glucagon and other vasodilatory peptides, which lowers portal inflow without systemic vasoconstriction. Vasopressin and terlipressin activate V1a receptors. Beta 1 blockade is how nonselective beta blockers reduce cardiac output. Nitric oxide is a splanchnic vasodilator that worsens portal hypertension. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, somatostatin and octreotide mechanism).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal hypertension dilates submucosal portocaval veins that form esophageal varices (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: variceal bleeding begins abruptly with hematemesis or melena and can cause hemorrhagic shock (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — somatostatin actions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which drug must be given with vasopressin in variceal bleeding to offset its systemic and coronary effects?","choices":{"A":"Phenylephrine","B":"Nitroglycerin","C":"Propranolol","D":"Terlipressin","E":"Norepinephrine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vasopressin causes severe systemic and coronary vasoconstriction with hypertension and myocardial ischemia, so it requires co administration of IV nitroglycerin. Phenylephrine and norepinephrine add vasoconstriction. Propranolol is contraindicated during active bleeding. Terlipressin is a V1 agonist with greater V1 selectivity and fewer side effects, not a drug that offsets vasopressin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, vasopressin adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vasopressin also causes free water retention and hyponatremia (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: terlipressin has greater V1 selectivity and fewer side effects than vasopressin (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — esophageal varices and portal hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, to what resting heart rate is propranolol titrated in chronic variceal management?","choices":{"A":"40 to 45 bpm","B":"70 to 75 bpm","C":"80 to 85 bpm","D":"90 to 95 bpm","E":"55 to 60 bpm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The dose is titrated to reduce the resting heart rate by 25 percent or to 55 to 60 bpm. Lower targets risk symptomatic bradycardia, and higher ones give too little portal pressure reduction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, clinical role in chronic settings).","boardPrep":"Step 1: TIPS is a management option for portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: lactulose and rifaximin treat hepatic encephalopathy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — esophageal varices and portal hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has heartburn four times a week for six months. Endoscopy shows erosive esophagitis without Barrett mucosa. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Famotidine as needed","B":"Metoclopramide daily","C":"Antacids as needed","D":"Alginate after meals","E":"Daily omeprazole"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Frequent heartburn on 3 or more days a week and erosive esophagitis call for a proton pump inhibitor, which heals 85 to 90 percent of erosive esophagitis when taken 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast. H2 blockers heal less than half and suit mild infrequent symptoms. Metoclopramide is not recommended as monotherapy. Antacids and alginates give only short temporary relief. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, PPI and H2 blocker indications).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors reduce absorption of vitamin B12, iron, calcium, and magnesium and can cause acute interstitial nephritis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: GERD is managed with lifestyle changes and a proton pump inhibitor (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophagus).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — reflux injury and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man takes an over the counter drug for heartburn. Over three months he develops tender breast enlargement, and his warfarin effect rises. Which drug is he most likely taking?","choices":{"A":"Pantoprazole","B":"Famotidine","C":"Cimetidine","D":"Sucralfate","E":"Nizatidine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cimetidine inhibits androgen binding and raises prolactin, causing gynecomastia, impotence, and galactorrhea, and it inhibits CYP1A2, 2C9, 2D6, and 3A4, which raises warfarin levels. Famotidine and nizatidine lack these effects. Pantoprazole is a PPI without antiandrogen effects. Sucralfate is a local mucosal protectant. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, H2 receptor antagonist adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cimetidine causes CYP450 inhibition, antiandrogenic effects, and CNS effects (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: H2 blockers can cause confusion in elderly or ICU patients with renal or hepatic impairment (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with long standing diabetes has persistent regurgitation and delayed gastric emptying despite a proton pump inhibitor. An adjunct drug is added that blocks central and peripheral dopamine receptors. Which adverse effect most limits its long term use?","choices":{"A":"Osmotic diarrhea","B":"Tardive dyskinesia","C":"Milk alkali syndrome","D":"Interstitial nephritis","E":"Hypomagnesemia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist that crosses the blood brain barrier, and central D2 blockade causes extrapyramidal effects including dystonia, parkinsonism, and tardive dyskinesia. Osmotic diarrhea is from magnesium antacids. Milk alkali syndrome is from excess calcium. Interstitial nephritis and hypomagnesemia are long term PPI risks. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, prokinetic agents).","boardPrep":"Step 1: metoclopramide raises LES tone and gastric contractions and causes hyperprolactinemia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: metoclopramide is contraindicated in small bowel obstruction (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman who is 12 weeks pregnant has rheumatoid arthritis and a past bleeding ulcer. She must continue an NSAID and needs ulcer prophylaxis. Which drug should be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Omeprazole","B":"Famotidine","C":"Pantoprazole","D":"Misoprostol","E":"Sucralfate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Misoprostol is a synthetic PGE1 analog that raises mucus and bicarbonate secretion and mucosal blood flow, but it is an abortifacient and is contraindicated in pregnancy, and it also causes dose dependent cramping and diarrhea. A once daily PPI is the alternative cotherapy for high risk NSAID users. Famotidine and sucralfate act on acid or the mucosa without abortifacient effects. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, NSAID ulcer prevention in high risk patients).","boardPrep":"Step 1: misoprostol raises bicarbonate and mucus, lowers parietal cell acid secretion, and raises gastric blood flow (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: misoprostol is used for NSAID induced PUD prophylaxis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — prostaglandins in mucosal protection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with a duodenal ulcer and a positive urea breath test starts a regimen of two antimicrobials, bismuth, and a twice daily acid suppressant. How does the acid suppressant improve eradication?","choices":{"A":"Antibiotic stability","B":"Thicker protective mucus","C":"Bacterial urease blockade","D":"Faster gastric emptying","E":"Stronger IgA secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PPIs suppress acid and raise gastric pH, which increases antibiotic stability and lowers the minimal inhibitory concentration against H. pylori. They do not thicken mucus, block urease directly, speed emptying, or raise IgA. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, role of PPI in eradication).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori triple therapy combines amoxicillin, clarithromycin, and a PPI (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori). COMLEX: after the antibiotics, the PPI is continued once daily for 4 to 6 weeks to heal the ulcer (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori and NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has hematemesis. Endoscopy shows a duodenal ulcer with a visible vessel that is treated endoscopically. Which drug regimen best lowers his risk of rebleeding?","choices":{"A":"Oral famotidine at bedtime","B":"IV PPI bolus and infusion","C":"IV octreotide infusion","D":"Oral sucralfate slurry","E":"IV vasopressin infusion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ulcers with high risk stigmata such as active bleeding, a visible vessel, or an adherent clot are treated with an IV PPI bolus followed by a continuous infusion for 72 hours, or high dose oral PPI. Bedtime famotidine and sucralfate give too little acid suppression. Octreotide and vasopressin are vasoactive drugs for variceal bleeding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, high dose or IV PPI therapy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peptic ulcer disease is a cause of upper GI bleeding along with varices and Mallory Weiss tears (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, upper GI bleeding). COMLEX: a duodenal ulcer can erode the gastroduodenal artery (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — complications of peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman who is 10 weeks pregnant has nausea and vomiting most mornings. Diet changes have not helped. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Meclizine by mouth","B":"Dronabinol by mouth","C":"Prochlorperazine suppository","D":"Aprepitant with dexamethasone","E":"Doxylamine with pyridoxine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Doxylamine with pyridoxine is the H1 antihistamine combination the lecture names for nausea and vomiting of pregnancy. Meclizine is for motion sickness and vertigo. Dronabinol is second line for refractory CINV. Prochlorperazine is a broad spectrum D2 antagonist with extrapyramidal effects. Aprepitant with dexamethasone is for highly emetogenic CINV. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, H1 antihistamines and anticholinergics).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H1 antihistamines and anticholinergics cause sedation, dry mouth, blurred vision, and urinary retention (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: prochlorperazine blocks D2, muscarinic, and histamine receptors and causes extrapyramidal symptoms (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man is leaving on a week long sailing trip and became very nauseated on his last boat trip. Which option best prevents his symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Scopolamine patch","B":"Ondansetron tablets","C":"Aprepitant capsules","D":"Dexamethasone tablets","E":"Dronabinol capsules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Motion sickness is prevented by blocking M1 and H1 receptors in the vestibular apparatus and solitary nucleus, as with a transdermal scopolamine patch, meclizine, or dimenhydrinate. 5 HT3 antagonists such as ondansetron are ineffective for motion sickness. Aprepitant is for CINV, and dexamethasone potentiates other antiemetics in CINV and PONV. Dronabinol is for refractory CINV and AIDS cachexia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, H1 antihistamines and anticholinergics).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dronabinol and nabilone are CB1 agonists that can cause euphoria, sedation, and orthostatic hypotension (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: cannabinoids also stimulate appetite in AIDS cachexia (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man taking sertraline for depression receives repeated doses of ondansetron after surgery. He becomes agitated with tremor, clonus, sweating, and a temperature of 39.2 °C. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Acute dystonia","B":"Serotonin syndrome","C":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","D":"Anticholinergic toxicity","E":"Thyroid storm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"5 HT3 antagonists carry a risk of serotonin syndrome when combined with SSRIs or SNRIs, and clonus, tremor, agitation, and fever fit it. Acute dystonia is an extrapyramidal effect of D2 blockers. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome follows dopamine blockade and causes rigidity rather than clonus. Anticholinergic toxicity causes dry skin rather than sweating. Thyroid storm is not linked to these drugs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, 5 HT3 antagonist adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: metoclopramide blocks D2 and 5 HT3 receptors centrally and stimulates 5 HT4 receptors peripherally (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications). COMLEX: metoclopramide can cause neuroleptic malignant syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — GI hormones, paracrines, and neurocrines","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with alcohol related cirrhosis has hematemesis, a pulse of 118/min, and a blood pressure of 88/54 mm Hg. An infusion of octreotide is started before endoscopy. Why is his usual propranolol held?","choices":{"A":"Blunts compensatory reflexes","B":"Raises portal vein inflow","C":"Worsens hepatic encephalopathy","D":"Interacts with octreotide","E":"Constricts coronary arteries"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Nonselective beta blockers are contraindicated in acute active variceal bleeding because they blunt the compensatory cardiovascular reflexes needed during hemorrhagic shock. They lower rather than raise portal inflow. Encephalopathy and an octreotide interaction are not the reason given. Coronary vasoconstriction is a vasopressin effect. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, contraindications and adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the esophageal portocaval anastomosis joins the left gastric vein to the distal esophageal veins (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: octreotide is given immediately for suspected variceal bleeding and continued for 3 to 5 days (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — esophageal varices","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with cirrhosis and medium varices that have never bled starts nadolol. Besides lowering cardiac output, which effect of the drug reduces portal inflow?","choices":{"A":"Direct V1a agonism","B":"Reduced glucagon release","C":"Unopposed alpha 1 tone","D":"More nitric oxide release","E":"Stellate cell relaxation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nonselective beta blockers lower portal pressure by beta 1 blockade, which cuts cardiac output, and by beta 2 blockade of vasodilatory receptors, which leaves alpha 1 receptors unopposed to constrict the splanchnic bed. V1a agonism is the vasopressin mechanism. Reduced glucagon release is the octreotide mechanism. Nitric oxide dilates the splanchnic bed. Stellate cell relaxation is not the described mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, nonselective beta blocker mechanism).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carvedilol, nadolol, and propranolol prevent variceal bleeding (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: beta blockers are contraindicated in severe asthma or COPD because of bronchospasm (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — esophageal varices and portal hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with cirrhosis survived a variceal bleed two months ago and takes propranolol. What should be combined with it to prevent another bleed?","choices":{"A":"Chronic octreotide infusion","B":"Daily oral metoprolol","C":"Monthly vasopressin doses","D":"Endoscopic band ligation","E":"Balloon tamponade"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secondary prophylaxis combines a nonselective beta blocker with endoscopic variceal band ligation. Octreotide and vasopressin are short term acute therapies. Metoprolol is cardioselective and far less effective. Balloon tamponade is a temporary rescue measure for active bleeding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, clinical role in chronic settings).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a hepatic venous pressure gradient measurement assesses portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: primary prophylaxis is for medium or large varices that have not bled (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portocaval collaterals","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old man on hemodialysis takes an antacid several times a day for heartburn and has had loose stools since starting it. He develops weakness, hyporeflexia, and hypotension. Which ion has most likely accumulated?","choices":{"A":"Calcium","B":"Aluminum","C":"Magnesium","D":"Phosphate","E":"Bicarbonate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Magnesium and aluminum from antacids accumulate in renal failure, and magnesium excess causes hyporeflexia, hypotension, cardiac effects, and respiratory failure. Calcium excess follows calcium carbonate use and can cause milk alkali syndrome. Aluminum also accumulates in renal failure but causes constipation rather than loose stools. Aluminum hydroxide lowers phosphate. Bicarbonate excess follows sodium bicarbonate use. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, antacid adverse effects); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (gastric pharmacology, lines 1203–1209).","boardPrep":"Step 1: magnesium hydroxide can cause hyporeflexia, hypotension, cardiac effects, and respiratory failure (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: antacids give rapid relief within 5 to 15 minutes that lasts only 1 to 2 hours (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman has taken a proton pump inhibitor daily for eight years and now has a low trauma hip fracture. Which drug effect best explains her fracture risk?","choices":{"A":"Reduced vitamin D synthesis","B":"Reduced calcium absorption","C":"Increased renal calcium loss","D":"Direct osteoclast activation","E":"Low estrogen production"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Long term PPI use decreases absorption of calcium, non heme iron, and vitamin B12 and raises the risk of osteoporotic hip and wrist fractures. PPIs do not block vitamin D synthesis, raise renal calcium loss, activate osteoclasts directly, or lower estrogen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, PPI adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: long term PPI use also causes benign fundic gland polyps (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: PPIs are cleared by hepatic CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 and need no renal dose change (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — consequences of gastric cell loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":3,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman with severe hip osteoarthritis had a bleeding gastric ulcer last year. She needs long term anti inflammatory treatment. Which regimen carries the lowest risk of another ulcer?","choices":{"A":"Celecoxib with a PPI","B":"Naproxen with famotidine","C":"Ibuprofen with sucralfate","D":"Celecoxib with antacids","E":"Aspirin with misoprostol"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Selective COX 2 inhibitors combined with a PPI carry the lowest ulcer risk in high risk patients. Famotidine and sucralfate give less protection than a PPI with nonselective NSAIDs. Antacids give only brief relief. Misoprostol protects but aspirin remains a nonselective COX inhibitor, and misoprostol causes cramping and diarrhea. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, prevention in high risk patients).","boardPrep":"Step 1: risk factors for NSAID ulcers include prior ulcer or bleeding, advanced age, and anticoagulant, antiplatelet, or corticosteroid use (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: if NSAIDs are stopped, H2 blockers or PPIs for 4 to 8 weeks heal the ulcer (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — prostaglandins in mucosal protection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q04","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman starting cisplatin receives four drug antiemetic prophylaxis that includes an atypical antipsychotic. Which receptor types does this added drug block?","choices":{"A":"Histamine and muscarinic","B":"Cannabinoid and opioid","C":"Neurokinin and GABA","D":"Adrenergic and glutamate","E":"Dopamine and serotonin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Olanzapine is part of four drug high risk CINV prophylaxis and blocks D2, 5 HT2C, and 5 HT3 receptors. Histamine and muscarinic blockade is the antihistamine and anticholinergic mechanism. Cannabinoids are CB1 agonists. NK1 antagonists block substance P but not GABA. Adrenergic and glutamate receptors are not the described targets. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, dopamine D2 antagonists and olanzapine).","boardPrep":"Step 1: D2 antagonist antiemetics cause extrapyramidal symptoms, sedation, hyperprolactinemia, and QT prolongation (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: dexamethasone potentiates 5 HT3 and NK1 antagonists in CINV and PONV (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with cirrhosis has large varices that have never bled. He also has severe asthma with frequent attacks. Which effect makes propranolol a poor choice for him?","choices":{"A":"Bronchospasm","B":"Hyperkalemia","C":"Hepatotoxicity","D":"Hyperglycemia","E":"Tachycardia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Nonselective beta blockers block bronchial beta 2 receptors and can cause bronchospasm, so they are contraindicated in severe asthma or COPD. Hyperkalemia and hepatotoxicity are not among the lecture-listed adverse effects, they can mask hypoglycemia rather than cause hyperglycemia, and they cause bradycardia rather than tachycardia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, contraindications and adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: beta 1 blockade lowers cardiac output and portal inflow (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: cardioselective beta blockers such as atenolol and metoprolol are far less effective for varices (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — esophageal varices","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with cirrhosis is given a vasopressin infusion for variceal bleeding. Two days later he is confused, and his serum sodium is 124 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). What is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Renal sodium wasting","B":"Adrenal insufficiency","C":"Osmotic diuresis","D":"Free water retention","E":"Gastrointestinal sodium loss"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Vasopressin causes free water retention that produces hyponatremia, along with systemic and coronary vasoconstriction. Renal sodium wasting, adrenal insufficiency, osmotic diuresis, and GI sodium loss are not the described effects of vasopressin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, vasopressin adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: octreotide is given as a 50 mcg bolus then 50 mcg per hour (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: long term octreotide can cause gallstones (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portocaval collaterals","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":11,"title":"SDL 11 — Embryology of the liver, gallbladder and pancreas","questions":[{"id":"sdl11_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which tissue secretes the FGF2 that starts liver bud outgrowth?","choices":{"A":"Septum transversum","B":"Paraxial mesoderm","C":"Cardiac mesoderm","D":"Surface ectoderm","E":"Neural crest"},"correct":"C","explanation":"FGF2 comes from the cardiac mesoderm and nearby vascular endothelial cells at the site of liver bud outgrowth. The septum transversum secretes BMPs, not FGF2. Ectoderm is one of the repressing tissues. Paraxial mesoderm and neural crest are not FGF2 sources for liver induction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, inductive signaling by FGF2 and BMPs, lines 2169–2174); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 731–735.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the liver parenchyma arises from foregut endoderm, and its connective tissue comes from mesoderm of the septum transversum (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: endoderm forms the primitive gut tube, the GI tract, and the liver (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — foregut divisions and the liver bud","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the main role of the BMPs secreted by the septum transversum during liver induction?","choices":{"A":"Raising endoderm competence","B":"Repressing hepatic genes","C":"Forming bile ducts directly","D":"Inducing pancreatic buds","E":"Blocking FGF2 signaling"},"correct":"A","explanation":"BMPs from the septum transversum enhance the competence of prospective liver endoderm to respond to FGF2. Hepatic genes are repressed by ectoderm, noncardiac mesoderm, and the notochord. BMPs do not form bile ducts, induce the pancreatic buds, or block FGF2. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, BMPs, lines 2175–2177); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 735–737.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ventral mesogastrium gives rise to the liver, lesser omentum, and falciform ligament (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: the liver weighs about 10 percent of body weight at week 10 (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — foregut divisions and the liver bud","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which transcription factors control differentiation of liver bud cells into hepatocytes and biliary cells?","choices":{"A":"PAX4 and PAX6","B":"HNF3 and HNF4","C":"SOX2 and CDXC","D":"PDX1 and SHH","E":"NKX2 and GATA4"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After the hepatic field is specified, liver bud cells become hepatocyte and biliary lineages under control of the hepatocyte nuclear transcription factors HNF3 and HNF4. PAX4 and PAX6 specify pancreatic endocrine cells. SOX2 specifies esophagus and stomach and CDXC the intestine. PDX1 is the pancreatic factor, and SHH must be repressed for pancreas to form. NKX2 and GATA4 are not the described factors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, downstream differentiation, lines 2178–2181); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 738–740.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SOX2 specifies the esophagus and stomach, PDX1 the duodenum, and CDXC the small and large intestine (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the bare area of the liver touches the central tendon of the diaphragm (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — foregut divisions and the liver bud","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of extrahepatic biliary atresia cases are the perinatal form?","choices":{"A":"About 10 percent","B":"About 25 percent","C":"About 50 percent","D":"About 95 percent","E":"About 80 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The perinatal form makes up about 80 percent of cases. The tree forms normally and is then destroyed after birth by suspected viral, toxic, or autoimmune injury. The fetal form is less common and reflects abnormal development or failed recanalization. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 2, perinatal and fetal forms, lines 2187–2193).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliary atresia is a fibro obliterative disease of the extrahepatic biliary tree (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary atresia). COMLEX: biliary atresia shows raised AST, ALT, GGT, and ALP (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary atresia).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, by what age does uncorrected extrahepatic biliary atresia lead to secondary biliary cirrhosis?","choices":{"A":"1 to 2 weeks","B":"2 to 3 years","C":"8 to 10 years","D":"3 to 6 months","E":"12 to 18 months"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Uncorrected obstruction leads to secondary biliary cirrhosis by 3 to 6 months of age, which is why drainage must be restored early. The other ages do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 2, morphologic features, lines 2194–2197).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ultrasound in biliary atresia shows an absent or irregular gallbladder or common bile duct (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary atresia). COMLEX: infants present within 2 to 6 weeks of birth with neonatal cholestasis (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, extrahepatic biliary atresia is the leading indication for which procedure in children?","choices":{"A":"Small bowel transplantation","B":"Kidney transplantation","C":"Liver transplantation","D":"Pancreas transplantation","E":"Heart transplantation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Biliary atresia accounts for one third of neonatal cholestasis and 50 to 60 percent of pediatric liver transplants. The other transplants are not linked to it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 2, clinical significance, lines 2201–2203).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliary atresia is the most common reason for pediatric liver transplantation (First Aid 2025 GI biliary atresia). COMLEX: 15 to 20 percent of infants with biliary atresia have patent proximal ducts and a correctable defect (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which part of the pancreas forms from the ventral bud?","choices":{"A":"Tail of pancreas","B":"Body of pancreas","C":"Neck of pancreas","D":"Uncinate process","E":"Accessory duct"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ventral bud forms the uncinate process and the inferior and posterior head. The dorsal bud forms the body, tail, and superior and anterior head, and the accessory duct comes from the proximal dorsal duct. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, bud fusion and derivatives, lines 2220–2223); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 815–817.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the dorsal pancreatic bud lies in the dorsal mesentery and the ventral bud lies next to the bile duct (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: visceral mesoderm around the pancreatic buds forms the pancreatic connective tissue (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal development and the major papilla","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, between which gestational weeks do the parenchyma and ducts of the two pancreatic buds fuse?","choices":{"A":"Weeks 5 to 8","B":"Weeks 2 to 3","C":"Weeks 10 to 12","D":"Weeks 16 to 20","E":"Weeks 30 to 36"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ventral bud comes to lie below and behind the dorsal bud, and their parenchyma and ducts fuse between weeks 5 and 8. The other intervals do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, bud fusion, lines 2217–2219).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in about 10 percent of people the pancreatic duct systems fail to fuse and the double system persists (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: pancreas divisum is usually asymptomatic but raises pancreatitis risk (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal development and the major papilla","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, the accessory pancreatic duct of Santorini derives from which duct segment?","choices":{"A":"Distal dorsal duct","B":"Proximal dorsal duct","C":"Entire ventral duct","D":"Proximal ventral duct","E":"Common hepatic duct"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The accessory duct of Santorini comes from the proximal dorsal duct and either obliterates or drains at the minor papilla. The main duct of Wirsung forms from the distal dorsal duct and the entire ventral duct. The common hepatic duct is biliary. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, ductal anatomy, lines 2224–2231); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 817–822.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the main pancreatic duct and bile duct enter the duodenum together at the major papilla, the ampulla of Vater (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the accessory duct, when present, drains at the minor papilla (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal development and the major papilla","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which structures produce the two signals, one of them FGF2, that permit pancreatic specification of the foregut endoderm?","choices":{"A":"Septum transversum and heart","B":"Neural tube and somites","C":"Liver bud and yolk sac","D":"Ectoderm and allantois","E":"Notochord and dorsal aorta"},"correct":"E","explanation":"FGF2 and activin, a TGF beta family member, come from the notochord and the endothelium of the dorsal aorta. The septum transversum and cardiac mesoderm signal liver induction. The other pairs are not the described sources. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, repression of SHH, lines 2235–2237); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 843–845.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic islets develop by the ninth week and scatter through the gland (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: glucagon and somatostatin cells also arise from pancreatic parenchyma (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which signal must be repressed in foregut endoderm for the pancreas to be specified?","choices":{"A":"Retinoic acid","B":"Beta catenin","C":"Sonic hedgehog","D":"Notch receptor","E":"Fibronectin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"FGF2 and activin repress sonic hedgehog in the endoderm destined to form the dorsal pancreatic bud, which permits pancreatic specification. Retinoic acid, beta catenin, Notch, and fibronectin are not the described targets. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, repression of SHH, lines 2235–2237); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 843–845.","boardPrep":"Step 1: activin is a member of the TGF beta family (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: insulin secretion begins at about the 12th week (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, which tissue induces the ventral pancreatic bud?","choices":{"A":"Paraxial mesoderm","B":"Visceral mesoderm","C":"Surface ectoderm","D":"Cardiac mesoderm","E":"Dorsal aorta"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ventral pancreatic bud is induced by visceral mesoderm, which then upregulates PDX1. Cardiac mesoderm induces the liver. Paraxial mesoderm, surface ectoderm, and the dorsal aorta are not the described inducers of the ventral bud. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 845–846; Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, PDX1 upregulated under mesodermal influence, lines 2238–2239).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cells expressing both PAX4 and PAX6 become beta, delta, and gamma cells (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: cells expressing PAX6 alone become alpha cells (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the most common congenital anomaly of the pancreas?","choices":{"A":"Annular pancreas","B":"Ectopic pancreas","C":"Pancreatic agenesis","D":"Pancreatic hypoplasia","E":"Pancreas divisum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pancreas divisum is the most common congenital pancreatic anomaly, with a 3 to 10 percent incidence. Annular pancreas and ectopic pancreas are less common, and agenesis from PDX1 loss is rare. Hypoplasia is not a listed anomaly. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, pancreas divisum, lines 2264–2266).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreas divisum is failed fusion of the dorsal and ventral bud duct networks (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies). COMLEX: in pancreas divisum the ventral and dorsal parts fail to fuse at 7 weeks (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic anomalies).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — congenital pancreatic anomalies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which part of the duodenum does an annular pancreas encircle?","choices":{"A":"First part","B":"Third part","C":"Fourth part","D":"Second part","E":"Duodenal bulb"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A bifid ventral bud whose left portion migrates the wrong way forms a ring of normal pancreas around the second part of the duodenum. The first part and bulb lie proximal, and the third and fourth parts lie distal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, annular pancreas, lines 2254–2258).","boardPrep":"Step 1: annular pancreas is associated with polyhydramnios and feeding regurgitation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies). COMLEX: about one third of annular pancreas cases are asymptomatic (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — congenital pancreatic anomalies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where is ectopic pancreatic tissue most often found?","choices":{"A":"Stomach and duodenum","B":"Esophagus and colon","C":"Spleen and liver","D":"Ileum and appendix","E":"Gallbladder and lung"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ectopic pancreas is found in about 2 percent of autopsies, most often in the submucosa or mucosa of the stomach and duodenum, then the jejunum, Meckel diverticulum, and ileum. The other sites are not the favored ones. Langman also stresses gastric mucosa and Meckel diverticulum. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, ectopic pancreas, lines 2274–2278); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 873–876.","boardPrep":"Step 1: accessory pancreatic tissue can lie anywhere from the distal esophagus to the tip of the primary intestinal loop (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: ectopic pancreas is found in about 2 percent of autopsies (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — congenital pancreatic anomalies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman at 7 weeks of gestation asks how her embryo forms a liver. Ectoderm, noncardiac mesoderm, and the notochord all release signals that reach the foregut endoderm. What is their effect on that endoderm?","choices":{"A":"Activation of liver genes","B":"Conversion to pancreas","C":"Induction of bile ducts","D":"Repression of liver genes","E":"Promotion of sinusoids"},"correct":"D","explanation":"All foregut endoderm can express liver genes, but signals from ectoderm, noncardiac mesoderm, and especially the notochord repress them. Liver forms only where FGF2 lifts this repression, so the induction works by removing a repressor. These signals do not activate liver genes, convert endoderm to pancreas, or build ducts or sinusoids. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, repressor signals, lines 2163–2168); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 727–731 and Fig. 15.21 legend.","boardPrep":"Step 1: induction can occur by removal of a repressor signal rather than direct activation (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the liver bud appears in the middle of the third week at the distal foregut (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman at 6 weeks of gestation asks why only one region of her embryo's gut forms a liver. Her physician describes an experiment in which endoderm from the future stomach region of the gut tube is placed beside the tissue that normally induces the liver bud, and the transplanted endoderm begins to express liver-specific genes. Which property of the endoderm does this experiment show?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic potential limited to the liver bud","B":"Reprogramming by migrating mesoderm cells","C":"Fusion with septum transversum cells","D":"Hepatic potential acquired from ectoderm","E":"Hepatic potential throughout the foregut"},"correct":"E","explanation":"All foregut endoderm inherently has the potential to express liver-specific genes, so endoderm from another foregut region forms liver once it receives the inductive signal. If hepatic potential were limited to the liver bud, the transplanted stomach-region endoderm could not respond. The hepatocytes arise from the endoderm itself, not from reprogrammed mesoderm or fusion with septum transversum cells, which instead contribute BMPs, connective tissue, and Kupffer cells. Ectoderm releases repressing signals rather than conferring hepatic potential. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, foregut endoderm potential and inductive signaling, lines 2162–2177); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 727–735.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the liver bud arises as an outgrowth of endodermal epithelium at the distal foregut (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: hepatic induction depends on signals from the cardiac mesoderm, nearby endothelium, and septum transversum (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman at 8 weeks of gestation asks how her embryo's liver began. Her physician explains that, a few weeks earlier, FGF2 released beside the ventral foregut started liver bud formation in that endoderm. By which mechanism did FGF2 start hepatic gene expression?","choices":{"A":"Inducing sonic hedgehog in endoderm","B":"Blocking repressor signals","C":"Converting nearby mesoderm into hepatocytes","D":"Stimulating PDX1 expression in endoderm","E":"Promoting apoptosis of endoderm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"FGF2 from the cardiac mesoderm and nearby endothelial cells suppresses the action of repressor signals, derepressing the hepatic field so that the foregut endoderm expresses liver-specific genes; BMPs from the septum transversum enhance the competence of the endoderm to respond. Sonic hedgehog induction is not the hepatic mechanism, and repression of SHH is instead a step in pancreatic specification. Hepatocytes come from endoderm, not from converted mesoderm. PDX1 is the master factor for the pancreas rather than the liver. Apoptosis of endoderm creates vacuoles during duodenal recanalization and does not specify liver. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, inductive signaling by FGF2 and BMPs, lines 2169–2177; section 4, SHH repression and PDX1, lines 2235–2241); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 727–735.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in liver induction, BMPs from the septum transversum enhance the competence of the endoderm to respond to FGF2 (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: HNF3 and HNF4 then direct hepatocyte and biliary differentiation of liver bud cells (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-week-old boy has jaundice, dark urine, clay colored stools, and hepatomegaly. Direct bilirubin and GGT are high. Ultrasound shows no visible gallbladder. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Physiologic jaundice","B":"Breast milk jaundice","C":"Dubin Johnson syndrome","D":"Biliary atresia","E":"Crigler Najjar syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Persistent conjugated jaundice with acholic stools, dark urine, hepatomegaly, and an absent gallbladder 2 to 6 weeks after birth fits extrahepatic biliary atresia. Physiologic and breast milk jaundice and Crigler Najjar syndrome raise unconjugated bilirubin with normal stools. Dubin Johnson syndrome raises conjugated bilirubin but has normal stools, a visible gallbladder, and a dark liver. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 2, clinical presentation, lines 2198–2200); First Aid 2025 GI (biliary atresia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliary atresia often presents after 2 weeks of life with jaundice, dark urine, and acholic stools (First Aid 2025 GI biliary atresia). COMLEX: biliary atresia accounts for one third of neonatal cholestasis (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — conjugated versus unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-week-old girl has persistent conjugated jaundice and pale stools. An intraoperative cholangiogram shows fibrotic, obliterated extrahepatic bile ducts. Which treatment should be done now to restore bile drainage?","choices":{"A":"Ursodeoxycholic acid therapy","B":"Exchange transfusion","C":"Kasai portoenterostomy","D":"Phototherapy sessions","E":"Cholecystectomy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A Kasai portoenterostomy restores bile drainage before irreversible cirrhosis develops. Ursodiol does not open an obliterated tree. Exchange transfusion and phototherapy treat unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia. Cholecystectomy does not bypass the blocked ducts. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 2, clinical significance, lines 2203–2205).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the fetal form of biliary atresia reflects failed ductal recanalization (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: intrahepatic biliary duct atresia is rare and may follow fetal infection (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-week-old boy has rising direct bilirubin, acholic stools, and hepatomegaly, and ultrasound shows an irregular gallbladder. A liver biopsy is taken before surgery. Which finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Periportal ground glass cells","B":"Portal ductular reaction","C":"Centrilobular necrosis","D":"Interface plasma cells","E":"Copper laden hepatocytes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Biopsy in extrahepatic biliary atresia shows portal edema, ductular reactions, and neutrophilic infiltrates, with progressive fibrosis of the extrahepatic ducts. Ground glass hepatocytes suggest hepatitis B. Centrilobular necrosis suggests ischemia or toxins. Interface plasma cells suggest autoimmune hepatitis. Copper laden hepatocytes suggest Wilson disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 2, morphologic features, lines 2194–2196).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliary atresia causes jaundice and scleral icterus in the first weeks of life (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary atresia). COMLEX: biliary atresia is associated with an absent or abnormal gallbladder on ultrasound (First Aid 2025 GI biliary atresia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 5 — bile flow through canaliculi and ducts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has a pancreaticoduodenectomy, and the surgeon notes that the common bile duct runs behind the duodenum. Which developmental event explains this position?","choices":{"A":"Duodenal rotation","B":"Liver bud regression","C":"Gallbladder descent","D":"Ventral mesentery loss","E":"Splenic migration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The bile duct first enters the duodenum anteriorly, but as the duodenum rotates to the right and becomes C shaped, its entrance shifts posteriorly, so the bile duct passes behind the duodenum. The ventral pancreatic bud follows the same path. The other events are not the described cause. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 686–689 and 809–814; Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, rotation around the duodenum, lines 2213–2215).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pancreas and most of the duodenum become attached to the posterior abdominal wall (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the hepatoduodenal ligament carries the portal triad (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, liver anatomy).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation and the mesenteries","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has ERCP, and contrast injected at the major papilla fills the main pancreatic duct to the tail. Which embryonic ducts formed this channel?","choices":{"A":"Ventral and proximal dorsal ducts","B":"Dorsal duct and cystic duct","C":"Ventral duct and bile duct","D":"Ventral and distal dorsal ducts","E":"Proximal and distal dorsal ducts"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The main duct of Wirsung forms from the distal part of the dorsal duct joined to the entire ventral duct, and it enters the duodenum with the bile duct at the major papilla. The proximal dorsal duct becomes the accessory duct. The cystic and bile ducts are biliary. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, main pancreatic duct, lines 2225–2228); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 817–823.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ventral bud forms the uncinate process and inferior head of the pancreas (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the dorsal bud forms the body, tail, and superior head of the pancreas (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — exocrine pancreas and its ducts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman has a first trimester ultrasound, and her physician explains how the fetal pancreas forms. After the ventral bud moves around the duodenum, where does it come to lie relative to the dorsal bud?","choices":{"A":"Above and in front","B":"Directly anterior to it","C":"To the left of it","D":"Behind the stomach","E":"Below and behind it"},"correct":"E","explanation":"As the duodenum rotates to the right, the ventral bud moves dorsally and comes to lie immediately below and behind the dorsal bud before the two fuse. Above, in front, anterior, or left positions and a site behind the stomach do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, bud fusion, lines 2217–2218); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 812–815.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pancreas forms from two endodermal buds of the duodenal lining (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: the tail of the pancreas lies in the splenorenal ligament with the splenic vessels (First Aid 2025 GI ligaments).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-month-old boy has a loss of function variant in a transcription factor gene, leaving only one major islet lineage able to form. Which islet hormone can still be produced normally?","choices":{"A":"Glucagon release","B":"Insulin release","C":"Somatostatin release","D":"Pancreatic polypeptide release","E":"Amylin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cells expressing both PAX4 and PAX6 become beta, delta, and gamma cells, while cells expressing PAX6 alone become alpha cells. Without PAX4, alpha cells that make glucagon can still form. Insulin and amylin both come from beta cells, somatostatin from delta cells, and pancreatic polypeptide from gamma or PP cells, and all three lineages need PAX4. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, PAX4 and PAX6, lines 2242–2250); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 846–850.","boardPrep":"Step 1: beta cells make insulin, delta cells somatostatin, and gamma cells pancreatic polypeptide (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: alpha cells make glucagon (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn girl of consanguineous parents has neonatal diabetes and fatty stools, and imaging shows no pancreatic tissue. Both alleles of one homeobox gene are inactivated. Which gene is it?","choices":{"A":"PAX6","B":"SOX2","C":"HNF4","D":"CDX2","E":"PDX1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"PDX1 is the master homeobox factor for pancreatic outgrowth and differentiation, and homozygous loss of function causes pancreatic agenesis. PAX6 specifies endocrine cells. SOX2 specifies esophagus and stomach. HNF4 controls hepatocyte and biliary differentiation. CDX2 is an intestinal factor. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, PDX1, lines 2238–2241); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, pancreatic agenesis, lines 2281–2284).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PDX1 is upregulated in pancreatic endoderm under mesodermal influence (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: pancreatic agenesis is a rare anomaly with complete failure of pancreatic development (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — congenital pancreatic anomalies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes is 6 weeks pregnant and asks how her embryo's pancreas gets started. Her physician explains that two secreted signals from nearby tissues act on the dorsal foregut endoderm and permit it to become pancreas. One of them is FGF2. Which signal is the other?","choices":{"A":"Noggin","B":"Chordin","C":"Activin","D":"Follistatin","E":"Sonic hedgehog"},"correct":"C","explanation":"FGF2 and activin, a TGF beta family member, from the notochord and dorsal aorta endothelium repress sonic hedgehog in the foregut endoderm and so permit pancreatic specification. Noggin and chordin are BMP antagonists and are not the signals the lecture gives for pancreatic specification. Follistatin binds and neutralizes activin rather than acting with FGF2. Sonic hedgehog is the signal that must be repressed for the pancreas to form, so it is the target rather than the partner signal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, repression of sonic hedgehog by FGF2 and activin, lines 2235–2237); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 826–850.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PDX1 is the master pancreatic homeobox factor, and homozygous loss causes pancreatic agenesis (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: PAX6 without PAX4 yields alpha cells, the glucagon-producing islet cells (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has recurrent pancreatitis without alcohol use or gallstones. MRCP shows most of the gland draining through a narrow minor papilla, and a small duct drains only part of the head. Which developmental error explains this?","choices":{"A":"Abnormal ventral bud rotation","B":"Failed ductal fusion","C":"Ectopic pancreatic rests","D":"Absent ventral bud","E":"Duodenal recanalization failure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancreas divisum results from failure of the dorsal and ventral duct systems to fuse, so the dorsal bulk drains through the small minor papilla via the duct of Santorini, and about 5 percent develop chronic pancreatitis. Abnormal ventral bud rotation causes annular pancreas. Ectopic rests are misplaced tissue. An absent ventral bud and failed duodenal recanalization are not the described cause. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, pancreas divisum, lines 2264–2272).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreas divisum raises the risk of pancreatitis and abdominal pain (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies). COMLEX: most cases of pancreas divisum are asymptomatic (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-day-old girl vomits with every feed. Imaging shows a double bubble, and surgery finds a ring of normal pancreatic tissue around the duodenum. Which developmental error explains this?","choices":{"A":"Failed ductal fusion","B":"Failed duodenal recanalization","C":"Split ventral bud rotation","D":"Pyloric muscle hypertrophy","E":"Incomplete midgut rotation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Annular pancreas forms when a bifid ventral bud splits and its left portion migrates opposite to the right, encircling the duodenum. Failed ductal fusion is pancreas divisum. Failed recanalization causes duodenal atresia without a pancreatic ring. Pyloric hypertrophy and midgut malrotation are not ring lesions. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, annular pancreas, lines 2254–2262); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 856–862.","boardPrep":"Step 1: annular pancreas is associated with trisomy 21 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies). COMLEX: annular pancreas can cause duodenal stenosis and complete obstruction (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has a small submucosal nodule in the gastric antrum with central umbilication. Biopsy shows normal pancreatic acini, ducts, and islets. Which complication can this lesion cause?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal bleeding","B":"Duodenal atresia","C":"Biliary cirrhosis","D":"Neonatal diabetes","E":"Hepatic abscess"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ectopic pancreas is usually incidental but can cause local pain, inflammation, or mucosal bleeding. Duodenal atresia is a recanalization defect. Biliary cirrhosis follows biliary obstruction. Neonatal diabetes fits pancreatic agenesis. Hepatic abscess is not a described complication. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, ectopic pancreas, lines 2276–2280).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ectopic pancreatic nests may show all the histologic features of the pancreas (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: ectopic pancreas can cause localized abdominal pain and inflammation (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 5 — normal gastric histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman is 6 weeks pregnant by her last menstrual period, so her embryo is about 4 weeks old. In her embryo, which cells beside the gut tube at the liver bud site secrete FGF2?","choices":{"A":"Notochord sheath cells","B":"Surface ectoderm cells","C":"Endothelial cells","D":"Neural crest cells","E":"Somitic mesoderm cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"FGF2 that lifts repression of hepatic genes comes from cardiac mesoderm and from blood vessel forming endothelial cells next to the gut tube at the liver bud site. The notochord and ectoderm release repressing signals. Neural crest and somitic mesoderm are not described FGF2 sources. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, inductive signaling by FGF2 and BMPs, lines 2170–2174); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 731–735.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cardiac mesoderm and nearby endothelium instruct gut endoderm to express liver genes by inhibiting an inhibitor (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: liver cords intermingle with the vitelline and umbilical veins, which form the hepatic sinusoids (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 7-week-old girl has conjugated jaundice and acholic stools, and surgery finds fibrotic, obliterated extrahepatic ducts. Among infants with this defect, about what share have patent proximal ducts and a correctable lesion?","choices":{"A":"15 to 20 percent","B":"1 to 2 percent","C":"40 to 50 percent","D":"70 to 80 percent","E":"95 to 99 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Langman states that 15 to 20 percent of infants with extrahepatic biliary atresia have patent proximal ducts and a correctable defect, while the rest usually need a liver transplant. The other shares do not match. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 782–785.","boardPrep":"Step 1: extrahepatic biliary atresia occurs in about 1 in 15,000 live births (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: biliary atresia shows jaundice, dark urine, acholic stools, and hepatomegaly (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary atresia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a mass in the tail of the pancreas on CT. Which embryonic structure formed this part of the gland?","choices":{"A":"Ventral pancreatic bud","B":"Hepatic diverticulum","C":"Cystic duct outgrowth","D":"Septum transversum","E":"Dorsal pancreatic bud"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The dorsal bud forms the body, tail, and superior and anterior head of the pancreas. The ventral bud forms the uncinate process and inferior head. The hepatic diverticulum forms the liver, the cystic outgrowth forms the gallbladder, and the septum transversum gives mesoderm for the liver. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, dorsal bud derivatives, lines 2222–2223).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the tail of the pancreas comes to lie against the posterior body wall in the dorsal mesogastrium (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the ventral bud lies close to the bile duct outgrowth (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":3,"stem":"Which two transcription factor genes must be expressed in pancreatic parenchymal precursors to specify the islet endocrine lineage?","choices":{"A":"HNF3 and HNF4","B":"PAX4 and PAX6","C":"SOX2 and CDXC","D":"PDX1 and SHH","E":"NKX2 and GATA4"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Expression of the paired box genes PAX4 and PAX6 specifies the endocrine lineage. HNF3 and HNF4 control hepatocyte and biliary lineages. SOX2 and CDXC pattern the gut tube. PDX1 drives overall pancreatic outgrowth, and SHH must be repressed for pancreas to form. NKX2 and GATA4 are not the described factors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, endocrine lineage specification, lines 2242–2245).","boardPrep":"Step 1: homozygous PDX1 mutations cause pancreatic agenesis (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: islet cells scatter throughout the pancreas after forming from parenchymal tissue (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":3,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman learns that MRCP shows pancreas divisum. She asks how often people with this anomaly develop chronic pancreatitis. About what share do?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 25 percent","C":"About 50 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 95 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Most cases of pancreas divisum are asymptomatic, but about 5 percent develop chronic pancreatitis from inadequate drainage through the minor papilla. The higher shares do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, pancreas divisum, lines 2270–2272).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in pancreas divisum the duct of Wirsung drains only a small portion of the head (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: pancreas divisum has a 3 to 10 percent incidence (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — congenital pancreatic anomalies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has a CT for kidney stones that incidentally shows a ring of pancreatic tissue around the duodenum. About what share of people with this anomaly have no symptoms?","choices":{"A":"About one tenth","B":"About one half","C":"About two thirds","D":"About one third","E":"About nine tenths"},"correct":"D","explanation":"About one third of annular pancreas cases are asymptomatic, while others develop duodenal stenosis or complete obstruction with vomiting in neonates or adults. The other shares do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, annular pancreas, lines 2259–2262).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an annular pancreas forms a ring of normal pancreatic tissue around the duodenum (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: annular pancreas can present with vomiting in neonates or adults (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":12,"title":"SDL 12 — Histology of the Liver","questions":[{"id":"sdl12_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of total hepatic blood flow does the portal vein supply?","choices":{"A":"About 70 to 75 percent","B":"About 25 to 30 percent","C":"About 10 to 15 percent","D":"About 45 to 50 percent","E":"About 90 to 95 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The portal vein supplies about 70 to 75 percent of hepatic blood flow as deoxygenated, nutrient rich blood from the GI tract and spleen, and the hepatic artery supplies the other 25 to 30 percent as oxygen rich blood. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, hepatic blood flow pathway, lines 2299–2302).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the liver has a dual blood supply from the portal vein and the proper hepatic artery (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, liver anatomy). COMLEX: the hepatoduodenal ligament carries the hepatic portal vein, proper hepatic artery, and common bile duct (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, liver anatomy).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how thick are normal adult hepatocyte plates?","choices":{"A":"Two cells thick","B":"Three cells thick","C":"Five cells thick","D":"Ten cells thick","E":"One cell thick"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Adult hepatocytes form branching, anastomosing plates that are normally one cell thick and radiate from the central vein, with sinusoids between neighboring plates. Thicker plates are not the adult pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, hepatocyte plates and sinusoids, lines 2294–2297).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatocytes have a central round nucleus with prominent nucleoli and stain PAS positive from glycogen (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture). COMLEX: hepatocytes carry out metabolism, detoxification, and protein synthesis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, into which vessels does the central vein drain first?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic veins","B":"Portal venules","C":"Interlobular veins","D":"Sublobular veins","E":"Splenic veins"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The central vein empties into sublobular veins, which join the hepatic veins and then the inferior vena cava. Portal and interlobular veins bring blood into the lobule rather than draining it, and the splenic vein feeds the portal vein. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, centripetal flow and venous drainage, lines 2303–2308).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal vein blood passes through interlobular veins and sinusoids to the central vein and hepatic vein, a summary that skips the sublobular veins (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, flow of blood and bile). COMLEX: Budd Chiari syndrome causes ischemic zone 3 injury (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what forms the short axis of the liver acinus?","choices":{"A":"Line joining two central veins","B":"Sublobular vein branches","C":"Interlobular bile ducts","D":"Bile canaliculi network","E":"Distributing portal vessels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The short axis of the acinus is formed by the penetrating distributing vessels from portal triads between two classic lobules, and the long axis runs between the two nearest central veins. Sublobular veins drain the lobule, and bile ducts and canaliculi carry bile. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, structure of the liver acinus, lines 2312–2316).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 1 is the first zone to meet incoming blood and has the highest oxygen and metabolic activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: zone 1 is affected first by viral hepatitis and ingested toxins such as cocaine (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what gross appearance results from chronic passive congestion of the liver?","choices":{"A":"Hobnail liver","B":"Nutmeg liver","C":"Bronze liver","D":"Green liver","E":"Zahn infarct"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ischemic or congestive injury causes centrilobular necrosis and a mottled gross appearance termed nutmeg liver. A hobnail surface fits cirrhosis, bronze color fits iron overload, green color fits cholestasis, and a Zahn infarct is not the described pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, ischemic susceptibility, lines 2327–2333).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 3 is affected first by ischemia because it is least oxygenated, as in congestive hepatopathy (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists Budd Chiari syndrome and chronic alcohol use as zone 3 injuries (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what overall shape does the liver acinus have?","choices":{"A":"Diamond shaped","B":"Hexagonal prism","C":"Spherical cluster","D":"Cylindrical tube","E":"Rectangular block"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The liver acinus is a diamond or lozenge shaped functional unit defined along microvascular lines. The hexagonal prism is the classic lobule. Spherical, cylindrical, and rectangular shapes are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, structure of the liver acinus, line 2312).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 2 has intermediate oxygen tension and is the zone affected by yellow fever (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: the classic lobule is a hexagon with portal tracts at its six angles (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, up to what diameter are the fenestrae of hepatic sinusoidal endothelium?","choices":{"A":"About 10 nm","B":"About 10 µm","C":"About 0.1 nm","D":"About 1 µm","E":"About 100 µm"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sinusoidal endothelial cells have large fenestrae up to about 1 µm without diaphragms, wide gaps between cells, and no continuous basal lamina. The other sizes do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium, lines 2345–2349).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sinusoids are lined by a single permeable, poorly selective layer of endothelial cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture). COMLEX: the apical surface of hepatocytes faces bile canaliculi and the basolateral surface faces sinusoids (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, hepatocyte microvilli in the space of Disse raise the surface area for exchange by up to how much?","choices":{"A":"Twofold","B":"Tenfold","C":"Sixfold","D":"Fiftyfold","E":"Hundredfold"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Numerous hepatocyte microvilli project into the space of Disse and increase the surface area for metabolic exchange up to sixfold. The other values do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, space of Disse, lines 2350–2354).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatic stellate cells lie in the perisinusoidal space of Disse (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture). COMLEX: the space of Disse lies between the sinusoidal endothelium and the hepatocytes (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, excess fluid in the space of Disse drains into which space to form hepatic lymph?","choices":{"A":"Space of Mall","B":"Space of Retzius","C":"Bile canaliculi","D":"Central vein lumen","E":"Canals of Hering"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Excess fluid in the space of Disse drains into the periportal spaces of Mall and forms hepatic lymph. The space of Retzius is retropubic. Bile canaliculi and the canals of Hering carry bile. The central vein carries blood. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, plasma hepatocyte exchange, lines 2362–2363).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatocytes release albumin, clotting factors, and lipoproteins into the space of Disse (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: hepatocytes take up glucose, amino acids, lipids, and toxins from plasma in the space of Disse (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of total liver cells are Kupffer cells?","choices":{"A":"About 2 percent","B":"About 15 percent","C":"About 40 percent","D":"About 60 percent","E":"About 85 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Kupffer cells make up about 15 percent of liver cells and 80 to 90 percent of the tissue macrophages in the body. The other shares do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, Kupffer cell location and nature, lines 2366–2370).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells are hepatic sinusoidal macrophages that carry out phagocytosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture). COMLEX: Kupffer cells clear bacteria and damaged or senescent red cells (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, in what chemical form is dietary vitamin A stored in the liver?","choices":{"A":"Retinoic acid","B":"Free retinol","C":"Beta carotene","D":"Retinyl esters","E":"Retinal aldehyde"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Quiescent hepatic stellate cells take up and esterify dietary vitamin A and store it as retinyl esters in cytoplasmic lipid droplets. Retinol is released bound to retinol binding protein when blood levels fall. The other forms are not the stored form. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, vitamin A storage, lines 2384–2388).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stellate cells store vitamin A when quiescent and produce extracellular matrix when activated (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture). COMLEX: activated stellate cells are responsible for hepatic fibrosis (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which two cytokines drive fibrogenic activation in chronic liver injury?","choices":{"A":"IL 2 and IL 4","B":"IFN gamma and IL 12","C":"TGF beta and PDGF","D":"IL 5 and IL 13","E":"GM CSF and IL 3"},"correct":"C","explanation":"TGF beta and PDGF released by activated Kupffer cells and injured hepatocytes turn stellate cells into proliferative, fibrogenic myofibroblasts. The other pairs are lymphocyte or hematopoietic cytokines not described here. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, activation and collagen deposition, lines 2389–2393).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells release IL 1, IL 6, TNF alpha, and IL 8 in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: zone 3 is usually affected first in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what diameter are bile canaliculi?","choices":{"A":"About 5 to 10 µm","B":"About 0.5 to 1 µm","C":"About 50 to 100 µm","D":"About 1 to 2 mm","E":"About 10 to 20 nm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bile canaliculi are tiny intercellular channels about 0.5 to 1 µm across, formed by apposed grooves between hepatocytes. The other sizes do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, bile synthesis and canaliculi, lines 2403–2407).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile flows from canaliculi to bile ductules, the hepatic duct, and the common bile duct (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, flow of blood and bile). COMLEX: hepatocytes secrete bile across their apical membranes (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which short channels at the lobule margin are lined by both hepatocytes and cuboidal cholangiocytes?","choices":{"A":"Bile canaliculi","B":"Interlobular ducts","C":"Canals of Hering","D":"Spaces of Mall","E":"Sublobular veins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Canaliculi empty into canals of Hering at the lobule margin, which are uniquely lined by both hepatocytes and cholangiocytes and have contractile activity. Canaliculi are lined only by hepatocyte membranes. Interlobular ducts are fully cholangiocyte lined. The spaces of Mall hold lymph, and sublobular veins carry blood. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, intrahepatic biliary pathway, lines 2409–2412).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile ductules collect bile from the canals of Hering and are lined completely by cholangiocytes (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: ductules pass into the periportal space before joining interlobular ducts (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where do the right and left hepatic ducts unite to form the common hepatic duct?","choices":{"A":"Ampulla of Vater","B":"Gallbladder neck","C":"Minor papilla","D":"Caudate lobe","E":"Porta hepatis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The right and left hepatic ducts unite at the porta hepatis to form the common hepatic duct. The ampulla of Vater and minor papilla open into the duodenum, the gallbladder neck leads to the cystic duct, and the caudate lobe is a liver lobe. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, major hepatic ducts, lines 2417–2420).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the portal triad of the lobule holds a hepatic arteriole, an interlobular vein, and a bile ductule (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture). COMLEX: tight junctions on hepatocytes prevent mixing of bile with sinusoidal blood (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has a liver biopsy. At one angle of a hexagonal lobule, the pathologist identifies branches of the portal vein and hepatic artery next to an interlobular bile duct. Which other structures normally run with these?","choices":{"A":"Sinusoids and Kupffer cells","B":"Lymphatics and nerves","C":"Lymph nodes and ganglia","D":"Hepatic venules","E":"Smooth muscle bundles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A portal triad holds terminal branches of the portal vein and hepatic artery and an interlobular bile duct, along with lymphatic vessels and nerve fibers. Sinusoids and Kupffer cells lie between the plates. Lymph nodes and ganglia are not in portal tracts. Hepatic venules lie at the lobule center. Smooth muscle bundles are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, portal triad components, lines 2291–2293).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists the portal vein, hepatic artery, bile ducts, and lymphatics in the portal triad (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture). COMLEX: the classic lobule is about 1 to 2 mm across (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":2,"stem":"A 37-year-old woman has a liver transplant, and her hepatic artery thromboses while portal flow continues. Which feature of hepatic inflow has she mainly lost?","choices":{"A":"Nutrient rich blood","B":"Gut derived toxins","C":"Splenic venous return","D":"Oxygen rich blood","E":"Most of total flow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The hepatic artery brings oxygen rich blood and 25 to 30 percent of flow, while the portal vein brings most of the flow as deoxygenated, nutrient rich blood from the GI tract and spleen. Losing the artery removes mainly the oxygen rich supply. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, hepatic blood flow pathway, lines 2299–2303).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatic artery blood passes through hepatic arterioles and sinusoids to the central vein (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, flow of blood and bile). COMLEX: blood from both inflow vessels opens into sinusoids at the lobule periphery (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man has contrast injected into a small portal vein branch during angiography. Where does the contrast first enter the hepatic sinusoids?","choices":{"A":"Lobule center","B":"Space of Disse","C":"Lobule periphery","D":"Bile canaliculi","E":"Sublobular veins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Terminal branches of the portal vein and hepatic artery open into the sinusoids at the periphery of the lobule, and blood then flows centripetally toward the central vein. The center is where blood leaves. The space of Disse holds plasma outside the sinusoid, canaliculi carry bile, and sublobular veins drain the lobule. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, hepatic blood flow and centripetal flow, lines 2302–2306).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mixed blood flows centripetally from the portal triads toward the central vein (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: the hepatic veins drain into the inferior vena cava (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portocaval collaterals","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has cardiogenic shock for six hours. Two days later his aminotransferases are markedly raised. A liver biopsy is taken. Which pattern is expected?","choices":{"A":"Periportal necrosis","B":"Midzonal necrosis","C":"Portal tract fibrosis","D":"Interface hepatitis","E":"Pericentral necrosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Zone 3 hepatocytes receive blood already depleted of oxygen, so shock and hypotension cause centrilobular or pericentral necrosis. Periportal injury fits direct toxins or viral hepatitis. Midzonal necrosis fits yellow fever. Fibrosis and interface hepatitis are chronic patterns. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, ischemic susceptibility, lines 2327–2331).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists ischemic injury such as Budd Chiari syndrome among zone 3 lesions (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: ischemic hepatitis follows hypotension, shock, or heart failure (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — hepatic venous outflow and ischemic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man has acute viral hepatitis, and a liver biopsy shows the most intense inflammation in one part of the acinus. Which part is it most likely?","choices":{"A":"Subcapsular region","B":"Midzonal zone 2","C":"Central vein wall","D":"Periportal zone 1","E":"Sublobular septa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Direct acting toxins and viral hepatitis often injure the periportal zone 1, which meets incoming blood first. Zone 2 is the yellow fever pattern. The subcapsular region, the central vein wall, and sublobular septa are not described sites. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, toxic susceptibility, lines 2338–2340).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists autoimmune hepatitis and alpha 1 antitrypsin deficiency among zone 1 lesions (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: zone 1 is best oxygenated and most resistant to circulatory compromise (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man is exposed to carbon tetrachloride at work, and a liver biopsy shows necrosis of the hepatocytes around the central veins. Which property of these hepatocytes best explains their injury?","choices":{"A":"High P450 enzyme content","B":"Highest oxygen tension","C":"Absent Kupffer cells","D":"Thin sinusoidal flow","E":"Densest bile canaliculi network"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Zone 3 has high concentrations of cytochrome P450 enzymes, so toxins that need bioactivation, such as carbon tetrachloride and acetaminophen, form reactive metabolites there and cause centrilobular necrosis. Zone 3 has the lowest, not highest, oxygen. Kupffer cells, sinusoidal flow, and canaliculi do not explain the zone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, toxic susceptibility, lines 2334–2338).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 3 is the main site of drug detoxification by CYP450 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists halothane, CCl4, and rifampin as zone 3 toxins (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has normal liver function, and his hepatocytes make albumin continuously. By which route does newly made albumin reach the blood?","choices":{"A":"Bile canaliculi to ducts","B":"Lymph to thoracic duct","C":"Canals of Hering to hepatic veins","D":"Space of Disse to sinusoid","E":"Central vein directly"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hepatocytes release albumin, clotting factors, and lipoproteins into the space of Disse, and these enter sinusoidal blood through the fenestrated, gapped endothelium. Canaliculi and canals of Hering carry bile, lymph carries only excess Disse fluid, and hepatocytes do not secrete straight into the central vein. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, plasma hepatocyte exchange, lines 2355–2362).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the basolateral surface of hepatocytes faces the sinusoids (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture). COMLEX: large macromolecules and plasma proteins pass freely from the sinusoid into the space of Disse (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman has a research liver study showing that large lipoproteins pass freely between sinusoidal plasma and hepatocytes. Besides large fenestrae and wide cell gaps, which feature allows this?","choices":{"A":"Tight endothelial junctions","B":"Thick glycocalyx layer","C":"Diaphragm covered pores","D":"Pericyte wrapped capillaries","E":"No continuous basal lamina"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The sinusoidal endothelium has large fenestrae without diaphragms, wide intercellular gaps, and no continuous basal lamina, so even large macromolecules pass freely into the space of Disse. Tight junctions, a thick glycocalyx, diaphragms, and pericyte coverage would restrict exchange. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, discontinuous endothelium and exchange, lines 2345–2358).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatic sinusoids are discontinuous capillaries (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: hepatocyte microvilli project into the space of Disse (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with cirrhosis has a biopsy showing dense collagen in the perisinusoidal space and sinusoids that have lost their fenestrae. Which hepatocyte function is most directly impaired by this change?","choices":{"A":"Bile acid conjugation","B":"Plasma exchange","C":"Glycogen synthesis","D":"Drug glucuronidation","E":"Ammonia to urea"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Collagen in the space of Disse with loss of fenestrae, called sinusoidal capillarization, blocks the free passage of plasma between sinusoids and hepatocytes. Conjugation, glycogen synthesis, glucuronidation, and the urea cycle are intracellular functions, not the exchange step this structure provides. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, plasma hepatocyte exchange, lines 2355–2358); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, sinusoidal capillarization, lines 2394–2396).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cirrhosis features bridging fibrosis from activated stellate cells and regenerative nodules (First Aid 2025 GI cirrhosis). COMLEX: activated stellate cells contract sinusoids and contribute to portal hypertension (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — fibrosis in chronic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has a gut barrier defect that lets bacteria and endotoxin into his portal blood. Which liver cells are the first line of defense against them?","choices":{"A":"Stellate cells","B":"Cholangiocytes","C":"Endothelial cells","D":"Pit cells","E":"Kupffer cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Kupffer cells are resident sinusoidal macrophages that act as the first line of defense against gut bacteria, endotoxin, and particles in portal blood, recognizing PAMPs with pattern recognition receptors. Stellate cells store vitamin A and make collagen. Cholangiocytes line ducts. Endothelial cells line sinusoids. Pit cells are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, phagocytosis and innate defense, lines 2371–2375).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells make up 80 to 90 percent of the tissue macrophages in the body (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: Kupffer cells recognize endotoxin with pattern recognition receptors (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — bacterial and parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with chronic hepatitis has a biopsy in which perisinusoidal cells have lost their lipid droplets, express smooth muscle actin, and lay down type I and type III collagen. Which cell type is this?","choices":{"A":"Kupffer cells","B":"Cholangiocytes","C":"Stellate cells","D":"Endothelial cells","E":"Pit cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Activated hepatic stellate cells lose their vitamin A lipid droplets, become myofibroblasts with alpha smooth muscle actin, and deposit type I and type III collagen in the space of Disse, causing fibrosis. Kupffer cells release the activating cytokines but do not make this matrix. Cholangiocytes, endothelial cells, and pit cells are not the fibrogenic cell. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, activation and collagen deposition, lines 2389–2398).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stellate cells sit in the space of Disse between hepatocytes and endothelial cells (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: chronic liver injury, reactive oxygen species, and cytokines activate stellate cells (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman has chronic hemolysis, and her Kupffer cells take up many senescent red cells. After hemoglobin breakdown, how is the released iron sent back to the blood?","choices":{"A":"Through ferroportin","B":"Through DMT1 channels","C":"Through hepcidin pores","D":"Through hemopexin","E":"Through haptoglobin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Kupffer cells break down hemoglobin and store iron in ferritin or export it through ferroportin to bind transferrin for erythropoiesis. DMT1 is an apical iron importer, hepcidin regulates ferroportin rather than forming pores, and hemopexin and haptoglobin bind heme and hemoglobin in plasma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, iron recycling, lines 2376–2380).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells store iron safely in ferritin (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: exported iron binds transferrin for systemic erythropoiesis (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — hemochromatosis and iron overload","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman has a liver biopsy, and the histology instructor traces bile flow within a classic lobule. In which direction does bile move?","choices":{"A":"Toward central veins","B":"Into sinusoids","C":"Toward portal triads","D":"Into space of Disse","E":"Into sublobular veins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile flows centrifugally, starting near the central vein and moving outward to the portal triads, opposite to the centripetal flow of blood toward the central vein. Bile does not enter sinusoids, the space of Disse, or sublobular veins. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, directional contrast, lines 2421–2425).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile and blood flow in opposite directions in the lobule (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: interlobular ducts converge into larger intrahepatic ducts (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":2,"stem":"A 56-year-old woman has an autoimmune disease that destroys the small interlobular bile ducts. Where are these ducts located in the liver?","choices":{"A":"Around central veins","B":"Within portal triads","C":"In space of Disse","D":"Between hepatocyte plates","E":"In sublobular septa"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bile ductules join interlobular bile ducts within the portal triads of the portal canals. The central veins carry blood, the space of Disse holds plasma, canaliculi rather than ducts run between hepatocytes, and sublobular septa are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, interlobular bile ducts, lines 2415–2416).","boardPrep":"Step 1: interlobular bile ducts run in the portal canals with the portal vein and hepatic artery branches (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: intrahepatic bile ductules are lined completely by cholangiocytes (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary cholangitis and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man has a cholestatic drug injury in which bile leaks from canaliculi into the blood. Which structures normally seal bile canaliculi from the vascular space?","choices":{"A":"Tight junctions","B":"Desmosomes","C":"Gap junctions","D":"Hemidesmosomes","E":"Adherens junctions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bile canaliculi are formed by apposed grooves between hepatocytes and are sealed from the vascular space by tight junctions. Desmosomes and adherens junctions anchor cells, gap junctions allow small molecule passage, and hemidesmosomes anchor cells to the basal lamina. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, bile synthesis and canaliculi, lines 2403–2407).","boardPrep":"Step 1: canals of Hering have contractile activity that propels bile (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: canaliculi are formed by apposed grooves between adjacent hepatocytes (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":3,"stem":"A 51-year-old woman has hepatic venous outflow obstruction. Normally, into which vessel do the hepatic veins empty?","choices":{"A":"Inferior vena cava","B":"Superior vena cava","C":"Hemiazygos vein","D":"Left renal vein","E":"Superior mesenteric vein"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Blood drains from the sinusoids into the central vein, then sublobular veins, then hepatic veins, and finally the inferior vena cava. The superior vena cava, hemiazygos vein, and left renal vein do not receive hepatic venous blood, and the superior mesenteric vein feeds the portal vein. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, centripetal flow and venous drainage, lines 2306–2308).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal vein blood passes through sinusoids to the central vein and on to the hepatic vein (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, flow of blood and bile). COMLEX: hepatic venous outflow obstruction injures zone 3 first (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — hepatic venous outflow obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old man returns from South America with fever, jaundice, and bleeding, and is diagnosed with yellow fever. Which acinar zone is classically injured?","choices":{"A":"Periportal zone 1","B":"Pericentral zone 3","C":"Panlobular injury","D":"Midzonal zone 2","E":"Zone 1 and 3 together"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Yellow fever classically causes midzonal, zone 2 injury. Zone 1 is injured by viral hepatitis and ingested toxins such as cocaine. Zone 3 is injured by ischemia and bioactivated toxins. Yellow fever injury is midzonal rather than panlobular or split between zones 1 and 3. Sources: Bootcamp Gastroenterology (hepatic zones, lines 1527–1528); First Aid 2025 GI (liver tissue architecture, fa_gastro_cache lines 594–595). The lecture describes zone 2 as intermediate in oxygen and substrates (SDL 12, section 2, lines 2321–2322).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 2 has intermediate oxygen tension and metabolic activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: zone 3 has the highest concentration of cytochrome P450 (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — hepatic injury, scarring, repair, and liver failure","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has a liver biopsy examined by electron microscopy. The sinusoidal endothelium shows large pores without diaphragms and wide gaps between cells. Which capillary type is this?","choices":{"A":"Continuous type","B":"Diaphragmed fenestrated","C":"Blood brain barrier type","D":"Glomerular type","E":"Discontinuous type"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hepatic sinusoids have discontinuous endothelium with large fenestrae up to 1 µm that lack diaphragms, wide intercellular gaps, and no continuous basal lamina. Continuous and blood brain barrier capillaries have tight junctions and no pores. Diaphragmed fenestrated capillaries line gut mucosa and endocrine glands. Glomerular capillaries have fenestrae on a thick basement membrane. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium, lines 2345–2349).","boardPrep":"Step 1: excess Disse fluid drains to the periportal spaces and forms hepatic lymph (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: discontinuous sinusoids allow plasma proteins to reach hepatocytes without obstruction (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has a low dietary intake of vitamin A for months. When his blood level falls, the liver releases stored vitamin A into the blood bound to which carrier?","choices":{"A":"Ceruloplasmin","B":"Thyroxine binding globulin","C":"Retinol binding protein","D":"Vitamin D binding protein","E":"Cortisol binding globulin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When systemic vitamin A drops, stellate cells release retinol bound to retinol binding protein into the blood. Ceruloplasmin carries copper, and the binding globulins and vitamin D binding protein carry thyroxine, cortisol, and vitamin D. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, vitamin A storage, lines 2384–2388).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stellate cells hold vitamin A in cytoplasmic lipid droplets (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: stellate cells lose their lipid droplets when they become myofibroblasts (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 2 — fat soluble vitamins A and D","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":3,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman studies liver histology with her tutor, who asks how the walls of a bile canaliculus are formed. What forms them?","choices":{"A":"Apposed hepatocyte grooves","B":"Cholangiocyte lined tubes","C":"Endothelial lined channels","D":"Kupffer cell processes","E":"Stellate cell processes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bile canaliculi are intercellular channels formed by apposed grooves in the membranes of adjacent hepatocytes and sealed by tight junctions. Cholangiocytes line ductules and ducts, endothelium lines sinusoids, and Kupffer and stellate cells do not form canaliculi. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, bile synthesis and canaliculi, lines 2403–2407).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile canaliculi empty into canals of Hering at the lobule margin (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: the right and left hepatic ducts form the common hepatic duct (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has a needle liver biopsy, and the pathologist measures one classic hexagonal lobule. About what size is a classic lobule across?","choices":{"A":"10 to 20 µm","B":"1 to 2 mm","C":"50 to 100 µm","D":"1 to 2 cm","E":"5 to 10 cm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The classic hepatic lobule is a roughly hexagonal prism about 1 to 2 mm across, centered on a central vein with portal tracts at its six angles. The micrometer sizes are cell to tissue scale, far below a lobule, since a hepatocyte is only about 20 to 30 µm, and the centimeter sizes match whole lobes or masses. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, classic hepatic lobule structure, lines 2287–2290).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the classic lobule centers on a terminal hepatic venule (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: First Aid describes the lobule as a central vein with portal triads on its edges (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":13,"title":"SDL 13 — Histology of the Gallbladder, and pancreas","questions":[{"id":"sdl13_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what type of epithelium lines the gallbladder mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Simple cuboidal epithelium","B":"Stratified squamous epithelium","C":"Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium","D":"Simple columnar epithelium","E":"Transitional epithelium"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The gallbladder is lined by a single layer of tall columnar absorptive cells with short apical microvilli, apical tight junctions, and abundant mitochondria. Cuboidal, stratified squamous, pseudostratified ciliated, and transitional linings are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, simple columnar epithelium, lines 2428–2431).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder epithelial cells have apical tight junctions that seal the lumen (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the gallbladder has a fundus, body, infundibulum, and neck (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary anatomy).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which two layers are missing from the gallbladder wall?","choices":{"A":"Muscularis externa and serosa","B":"Muscularis mucosae and submucosa","C":"Lamina propria and adventitia","D":"Epithelium and lamina propria","E":"Adventitia and muscularis externa"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The gallbladder lacks both a muscularis mucosae and a submucosa, so the lamina propria rests directly on the muscularis externa. It has an epithelium, a lamina propria, a muscularis externa, and an adventitia on the liver side with serosa on the free surface. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, absence of muscularis mucosae and submucosa, lines 2438–2440).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lamina propria of the gallbladder rests directly upon the muscularis externa (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the free surface of the gallbladder is covered by visceral peritoneum (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what does the gallbladder lamina propria noticeably lack?","choices":{"A":"Lymphatic vessels","B":"Fenestrated capillaries","C":"Plasma cells","D":"Resident lymphocytes","E":"Small venules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The gallbladder lamina propria is cellular loose connective tissue rich in fenestrated capillaries, small venules, lymphocytes, and plasma cells, but it noticeably lacks lymphatic vessels. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, lamina propria, lines 2435–2437).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the empty gallbladder mucosa forms deep temporary folds that flatten as the organ fills (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: calculous cholecystitis follows gallstone impaction in the cystic duct and thickens the gallbladder wall (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which pump on the lateral membranes of gallbladder epithelial cells drives active electrolyte transport?","choices":{"A":"Hydrogen potassium ATPase","B":"Calcium ATPase pumps","C":"Sodium glucose cotransporter","D":"Chloride bicarbonate exchanger","E":"Sodium potassium ATPase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lateral membrane Na+/K+ ATPase pumps drive transport of Na+, Cl- and HCO3- into the lateral intercellular space. The hydrogen potassium ATPase is the gastric proton pump. Calcium pumps, sodium glucose cotransport, and a chloride bicarbonate exchanger are not named as the driver. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, active electrolyte transport, lines 2456–2459).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder cells move Na+, Cl- and HCO3- into the lateral intercellular compartment (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: solute build up in the narrow lateral space sets up an osmotic gradient with the lumen (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which membrane water channels carry water across gallbladder epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"Aquaporins 1 and 8","B":"Aquaporins 2 and 3","C":"Aquaporins 4 and 5","D":"Aquaporins 0 and 7","E":"Connexin channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Water crosses the apical and basolateral membranes of gallbladder epithelial cells through aquaporin 1 and aquaporin 8. The other aquaporin pairs are not described for the gallbladder, and connexins form gap junctions. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, aquaporin mediated water absorption, lines 2464–2467).","boardPrep":"Step 1: water movement in the gallbladder is passive and follows the osmotic gradient (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: bile is stored within the gallbladder between meals (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary physiology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of the water in incoming hepatic bile does the gallbladder remove?","choices":{"A":"About 50 percent","B":"About 90 percent","C":"About 25 percent","D":"About 10 percent","E":"About 70 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Coupled electrolyte and water reabsorption removes about 90 percent of the water from hepatic bile. The lower figures understate the concentrating power of the gallbladder. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, net bile concentration, lines 2472–2474).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder concentration raises bile salts, cholesterol, and bilirubin together (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: gallbladder stasis promotes sludge and gallstone formation (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how is the exocrine pancreas classified as a gland?","choices":{"A":"Compound tubuloacinar serous gland","B":"Simple branched tubular mucous gland","C":"Compound tubular mixed mucous gland","D":"Simple coiled tubular gland","E":"Compound alveolar seromucous gland"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The exocrine pancreas is a compound tubuloacinar serous gland divided into ill defined lobules by thin septa from its capsule. Simple branched, simple coiled, mucous, and seromucous patterns describe other glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, overall organization, lines 2478–2479).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the exocrine pancreas makes up the majority of pancreatic mass (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: thin septa from the capsule divide the pancreas into ill defined lobules (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes and autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what makes the basal cytoplasm of pancreatic acinar cells strongly basophilic?","choices":{"A":"Zymogen granules","B":"Abundant smooth ER","C":"Abundant rough ER","D":"Abundant glycogen","E":"Golgi complexes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The basal cytoplasm is ergastoplasm rich in rough ER and ribosomes, which stains strongly basophilic. Zymogen granules fill the acidophilic apex. Smooth ER, glycogen, and Golgi complexes are not the cause. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, acinar cell histology, lines 2481–2483).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic acinar cells are pyramidal epithelial cells that contain zymogen granules (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: acinar cells have a basal round nucleus under an acidophilic granular apex (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes and autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, pale centroacinar cells mark the intra-acinar start of which ducts?","choices":{"A":"Interlobular ducts","B":"Striated ducts","C":"Main pancreatic duct","D":"Intercalated ducts","E":"Intralobular ducts"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Centroacinar cells are the intra-acinar origin of the intercalated ducts, a feature unique to the pancreas. Intralobular and interlobular ducts lie farther downstream, striated ducts belong to salivary glands, and the main duct is the final collecting channel. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, centroacinar cells, lines 2487–2491).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic secretions pass from acini to centroacinar cells, intercalated ducts, and pancreatic ducts (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: centroacinar cells lack zymogen granules and ergastoplasm and stain faintly (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes and autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which cells release secretin when acid enters the small bowel?","choices":{"A":"Duodenal I cells","B":"Duodenal S cells","C":"Jejunal K cells","D":"Gastric G cells","E":"Ileal L cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Secretin is released by duodenal S cells when gastric acid lowers duodenal pH below about 4.5. I cells release CCK, K cells release GIP, G cells release gastrin, and L cells release GLP 1 and PYY. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, secretin source and trigger, lines 2507–2508).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin increases pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and bile secretion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GI hormones). COMLEX: secretin also decreases parietal cell H+ secretion and gastric motility (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GI hormones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, CCK1 receptors on the pancreatic target cells of CCK activate which signaling pathway?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"IP3 and calcium","D":"JAK STAT signaling","E":"Tyrosine kinase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"CCK binds CCK1 receptors on acinar cells and activates the IP3 and intracellular Ca2+ pathway, causing zymogen granule exocytosis. Cyclic AMP is the secretin pathway in ductal cells. Cyclic GMP, JAK STAT, and tyrosine kinase signaling are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, CCK target cell and pathway, lines 2519–2520).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK increases gallbladder contraction and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GI hormones). COMLEX: CCK also slows gastric emptying (First Aid 2025 GI GI hormones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which phase accounts for about 80 percent of total pancreatic secretion?","choices":{"A":"Cephalic phase","B":"Gastric phase","C":"Interdigestive phase","D":"Oral phase","E":"Intestinal phase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Secretin, CCK, and vagal ACh act together during the intestinal phase, which accounts for about 80 percent of total pancreatic secretion. The cephalic and gastric phases contribute less, and the interdigestive and oral phases are not the main period. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, coordinated action, lines 2524–2527).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin and CCK act synergistically with vagal ACh to deliver an alkaline enzyme rich juice (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: somatostatin inhibits the exocrine pancreas (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of the pancreas is endocrine tissue?","choices":{"A":"80 to 85 percent","B":"10 to 15 percent","C":"1 to 2 percent","D":"25 to 30 percent","E":"5 to 8 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The endocrine pancreas makes up only 1 to 2 percent of the organ as scattered islets, while the exocrine pancreas makes up 80 to 85 percent. The other figures are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, histologic differentiation, lines 2533–2536).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic endocrine function is glucose regulation by insulin, glucagon, and somatostatin (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreas physiology). COMLEX: islets are most concentrated in the tail of the pancreas (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where in a human islet are beta cells mainly located?","choices":{"A":"Islet periphery","B":"Capsule surface","C":"Acinar lumen","D":"Central core","E":"Duct walls"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Beta cells, about 60 to 70 percent of islet cells, lie mainly in the central core of the islet. Alpha and delta cells lie mainly at the periphery. Capsules, acinar lumens, and duct walls are not islet locations. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, beta cells, lines 2540–2541).","boardPrep":"Step 1: beta cells make up about 60 to 70 percent of islet cells and produce insulin (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: islet cells form irregular cords invested by fenestrated capillaries (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which electron microscopic feature marks the 325 nm granules of islet delta cells?","choices":{"A":"Crystalline polyhedral core","B":"Round eccentric core","C":"Clear peripheral halo","D":"Lamellar body","E":"Homogeneous matrix"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Delta cell granules are about 325 nm with a homogeneous matrix. Beta cell granules have a crystalline polyhedral core with a clear halo, and alpha cell granules have a dense round eccentric core. Lamellar bodies are not islet granules. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, delta cells, lines 2548–2551).","boardPrep":"Step 1: delta cells make up about 5 to 10 percent of islet cells and sit at the islet periphery (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: PP cells make pancreatic polypeptide, which inhibits intestinal motility and pancreatic secretion (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with gallstones and chronic cholecystitis has a cholecystectomy. Histology shows deep mucosal outpouchings that extend through the lamina propria into the muscularis externa. What are these structures?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal crypts of Lieberkuhn","B":"Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses","C":"Submucosal Brunner glands","D":"Periportal canals of Hering","E":"Pancreatic acinar rests"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses are deep mucosal invaginations that extend into or through the muscularis externa. They arise from epithelial hyperplasia and herniation under luminal pressure, and trapped bile and bacteria promote chronic inflammation and stones. Crypts of Lieberkuhn and Brunner glands belong to the intestine, canals of Hering belong to the liver, and acinar rests are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses, lines 2448–2452).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile stagnation in mucosal outpouchings promotes chronic gallbladder inflammation and gallstones (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: a porcelain gallbladder is a calcified wall from chronic cholecystitis (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 4 — chronic cholecystitis and porcelain gallbladder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman has a cholecystectomy, and the surgeon dissects the gallbladder off the liver bed. Which layer covers the surface that was attached to the liver?","choices":{"A":"Visceral serosa","B":"Submucosal layer","C":"Muscularis mucosae","D":"Fibrous adventitia","E":"Parietal peritoneum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The attached surface is covered by a connective tissue adventitia carrying large vessels, lymphatics, and nerves, while the free surface has a smooth serosa of visceral peritoneum. The gallbladder lacks a submucosa and a muscularis mucosae, and parietal peritoneum lines the body wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, adventitia versus serosa, lines 2444–2447).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the adventitia on the liver side carries large blood vessels, lymphatics, and nerves (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: acalculous cholecystitis is due to stasis, hypoperfusion, or infection in critically ill patients (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man eats a fatty meal, and his gallbladder squeezes bile into the cystic duct. Which wall component produces this contraction?","choices":{"A":"Interlacing smooth muscle","B":"Circular skeletal muscle","C":"Muscularis mucosae","D":"Myoepithelial cells","E":"Longitudinal skeletal muscle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The muscularis externa is made of randomly oriented interlacing smooth muscle bundles with elastic and collagen fibers, and its contraction reduces luminal volume to expel bile. The gallbladder has no skeletal muscle or muscularis mucosae, and myoepithelial cells belong to glands such as salivary glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, muscularis externa, lines 2441–2443).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after a fatty meal, CCK triggers gallbladder contraction that can force a stone into the cystic duct (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology). COMLEX: biliary colic worsens after meals because CCK drives gallbladder contraction (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary colic).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with gallstones asks how her gallbladder changes the bile it stores. By about how much are bile salts concentrated during storage?","choices":{"A":"2 to 3 times","B":"100 to 200 times","C":"4 to 5 times","D":"50 to 60 times","E":"10 to 20 times"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Removal of about 90 percent of the water concentrates bile salts, cholesterol, and bilirubin up to 10 to 20 times. The smaller figures understate, and the larger figures overstate, the effect. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, net bile concentration, lines 2472–2474).","boardPrep":"Step 1: most gallstones form within the gallbladder (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis). COMLEX: raised cholesterol, reduced bile salts, and gallbladder stasis all cause sludge or stones (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — formation and composition of gallstones","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old man has a cholecystectomy, and the pathologist explains how the gallbladder absorbs fluid. After isotonic fluid crosses the basal lamina into the lamina propria, where is it taken up?","choices":{"A":"Lymphatic capillaries","B":"Fenestrated capillaries","C":"Lumen of the cystic duct","D":"Bile in the gallbladder lumen","E":"Muscularis externa"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hydrostatic pressure in the lateral spaces pushes isotonic fluid across the basal lamina, where fenestrated capillaries rapidly absorb it. The lamina propria noticeably lacks lymphatics, fluid does not return to the lumen or cystic duct, and the muscularis does not absorb it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, hydrostatic fluid movement, lines 2468–2471).","boardPrep":"Step 1: water build up distends the lateral intercellular space and raises local hydrostatic pressure (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the lamina propria of the gallbladder has fenestrated capillaries and small venules (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has a cholecystectomy, and her surgeon explains how a living gallbladder concentrates bile. Water moves from the lumen through membrane water channels into the lateral intercellular space. What drives this water movement?","choices":{"A":"Active water pumping","B":"Bile salt uptake","C":"Osmotic gradient","D":"Vesicular transport","E":"Ciliary beating"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Actively pumped electrolytes collect in the lateral space and create an osmotic gradient, and water follows passively through aquaporins. Water is not pumped actively, bile salts are concentrated rather than absorbed, and vesicles and cilia are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, osmotic gradient and water absorption, lines 2460–2467).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder epithelial cells have abundant apical and basal mitochondria for active transport (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the striated border of gallbladder cells is formed by short apical microvilli (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has recurrent pneumonia, poor weight gain, greasy stools, and a raised sweat chloride. Which apical protein of his pancreatic duct cells is most likely defective?","choices":{"A":"ENaC sodium channel","B":"Aquaporin water channel","C":"Voltage gated K channel","D":"Chloride bicarbonate exchanger","E":"CFTR chloride channel"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ductal cells secrete Na+ and HCO3- rich fluid through CFTR Cl- channels working with apical Cl-/HCO3- exchangers. A defective CFTR reduces this watery output. The Cl-/HCO3- exchanger is the partner of CFTR but is not the defective protein in cystic fibrosis. ENaC, aquaporins, and potassium channels are not the described apical secretory step. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, ductal cell secretory function, lines 2500–2503).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ductal secretion brings duodenal pH to about 8.0 for pancreatic enzyme activity (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: pancreatic fluid is isotonic with water, bicarbonate, and other electrolytes (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has a pancreatic biopsy. In H&E sections, pale flattened cells sit in the center of each acinus and lack zymogen granules. What do these cells mainly secrete?","choices":{"A":"Sodium bicarbonate fluid","B":"Trypsinogen and proelastase","C":"Insulin and pancreatic polypeptide","D":"Mucin and lysozyme","E":"Pancreatic amylase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Centroacinar cells secrete the initial isotonic aqueous fluid rich in sodium and bicarbonate. Acinar cells secrete proenzymes and amylase, islet cells secrete insulin and pancreatic polypeptide, and mucin and lysozyme are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, centroacinar cells, lines 2487–2493).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intercalated ducts secrete bicarbonate (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: at high postprandial flow, the bicarbonate to chloride ratio of pancreatic fluid rises (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has acute pancreatitis with raised serum enzymes. Which pancreatic enzymes do acinar cells secrete in active form rather than as proenzymes?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin and elastase","B":"Chymotrypsin and trypsin","C":"Carboxypeptidase and elastase","D":"Amylase and lipase","E":"Enteropeptidase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acinar cells secrete proteases as proenzymes such as trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase, and proelastase, but release pancreatic amylase and lipases as active enzymes. Enteropeptidase is a brush border enzyme rather than an acinar product. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, acinar cell secretory function, lines 2484–2486).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic fluid contains zymogens such as trypsinogen and chymotrypsinogen (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: pancreatic proteases are secreted as proenzymes also called zymogens (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic secretions).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes and autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man eats a meal, and acidic chyme drops his duodenal pH below 4.5. Which pancreatic secretion does the resulting hormone mainly stimulate?","choices":{"A":"Low volume enzyme rich juice","B":"Mucus rich interlobular secretion","C":"Insulin rich islet output","D":"Chloride rich low flow fluid","E":"High volume bicarbonate fluid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acid releases secretin from S cells, which acts on ductal and centroacinar cells to produce a copious watery fluid rich in sodium bicarbonate with little enzyme. Low volume enzyme rich juice is the CCK response, and chloride rich fluid is the low flow basal pattern. Interlobular goblet cells add little mucus, and islet insulin is not an exocrine output of secretin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, secretin, lines 2506–2514).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin is released by acid and fatty acids in the duodenal lumen (First Aid 2025 GI GI hormones). COMLEX: S cells that release secretin are found in the duodenum (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, enteroendocrine cells).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 53-year-old woman eats a meal rich in fat and protein, and fatty acids and phenylalanine stimulate duodenal I cells. Which pancreatic cells are the main target of the released hormone?","choices":{"A":"Ductal cells","B":"Centroacinar cells","C":"Acinar cells","D":"Beta cells","E":"Stellate cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"I cells release CCK, which binds CCK1 receptors on acinar cells and triggers zymogen granule exocytosis for a low volume enzyme rich secretion. Ductal and centroacinar cells answer mainly to secretin, beta cells are endocrine, and stellate cells are not the target. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, cholecystokinin, lines 2515–2523).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK stimulates zymogen release from acinar cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: First Aid places CCK producing I cells in the duodenum and jejunum (First Aid 2025 GI GI hormones).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with suspected gastrinoma has a secretin stimulation test. In the pancreas, secretin binds ductal cell receptors that raise which intracellular messenger?","choices":{"A":"IP3 and calcium","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Nitric oxide","D":"Cyclic AMP","E":"Diacylglycerol"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secretin binds receptors on ductal and centroacinar cells and stimulates adenylyl cyclase to raise intracellular cAMP. IP3 and calcium are the CCK pathway in acinar cells. Cyclic GMP, nitric oxide, and diacylglycerol are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, secretin target cell and pathway, lines 2509–2511).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zollinger Ellison workup uses fasting gastrin and gastric pH, then a secretin stimulation test (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastrinoma). COMLEX: secretin stimulates bicarbonate release from ductal cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl has new type 1 diabetes from autoimmune loss of insulin producing islet cells. On electron microscopy, which granule appearance marked the destroyed cells?","choices":{"A":"Round eccentric core","B":"Polyhedral core with halo","C":"Homogeneous granule matrix","D":"Large zymogen type granule","E":"Lamellated concentric layers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Beta cell granules are about 300 nm with a dense crystalline polyhedral core surrounded by a clear halo. A round eccentric core marks alpha cells, a homogeneous matrix marks delta cells, large zymogen granules belong to acinar cells, and lamellated layers are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, beta cells, lines 2540–2543).","boardPrep":"Step 1: islets are pale staining, highly vascular clusters scattered among dark serous acini (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the pancreas holds about 1 to 3 million islets of Langerhans (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — insulinoma, the beta cell tumor, contrasted with beta cell loss in type 1 diabetes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man has a pancreatic islet tumor that secretes excess glucagon. In a normal human islet, where do the cells that make this hormone mainly sit?","choices":{"A":"Islet periphery","B":"Central islet core","C":"Duct epithelium","D":"Acinar lumen","E":"Capsular septa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Alpha cells, about 15 to 20 percent of islet cells, sit primarily at the islet periphery in humans and make glucagon, which stimulates glycogenolysis and raises blood glucose. Beta cells occupy the central core. Ducts, acinar lumens, and septa are not islet sites. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, alpha cells, lines 2544–2547).","boardPrep":"Step 1: alpha cell granules are about 250 nm with a dense round eccentric core (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: glucagon raises blood glucose by stimulating glycogenolysis (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with a delta cell tumor of the pancreas has new hyperglycemia and fatty stools. Inhibition of which outputs by the excess hormone explains both findings?","choices":{"A":"Glucagon and bicarbonate output","B":"Amylin and mucin secretion","C":"Insulin and enzyme secretion","D":"Pancreatic polypeptide output","E":"Gastrin and secretin release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Delta cells make somatostatin, which suppresses insulin and glucagon release as well as exocrine secretion. Less insulin raises glucose, and less enzyme output causes fatty stools. Less glucagon would lower rather than raise glucose, and amylin, mucin, pancreatic polypeptide, gastrin, and secretin do not explain both findings. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, delta cells, lines 2548–2551).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatinomas arise from delta cells with autonomous somatostatin release (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, somatostatinoma). COMLEX: somatostatin decreases insulin, glucagon, CCK, and secretin release (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, somatostatin).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — somatostatin actions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":3,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has a fasting ultrasound, and her empty gallbladder mucosa shows many deep folds. What happens to these folds as the gallbladder fills with bile?","choices":{"A":"They flatten out","B":"They deepen further","C":"They form villi","D":"They calcify","E":"They become polyps"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the empty or partly filled state the gallbladder mucosa forms many deep temporary folds, which flatten out as the organ distends with bile. The folds do not deepen, form villi, calcify, or become polyps. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, mucosal folds, lines 2432–2434).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gallbladder lies in the right upper quadrant beneath the liver (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary anatomy). COMLEX: the gallbladder gets sensory innervation from the phrenic nerve (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary anatomy).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder storage and concentration","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has gallbladder bile that is far more concentrated than his hepatic bile. Which solutes do his gallbladder epithelial cells actively pump into the lateral intercellular spaces?","choices":{"A":"Potassium and hydrogen ions","B":"Calcium and magnesium ions","C":"Conjugated bile salts and bilirubin","D":"Glucose and free amino acids","E":"Sodium chloride and bicarbonate"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gallbladder epithelial cells actively transport Na+, Cl- and HCO3- across their lateral membranes into the intercellular space, and water follows. Potassium and hydrogen, calcium and magnesium, and nutrients are not the described solutes, and bile salts and bilirubin stay in the lumen and become concentrated. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, active electrolyte transport, lines 2456–2459).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cystic duct joins the common hepatic duct to form the common bile duct (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary anatomy). COMLEX: the gallbladder is supplied by the cystic artery (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary anatomy).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman has a biopsy of a large interlobular pancreatic duct. Besides simple columnar lining cells, which occasional cells are expected in its epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Paneth and intestinal tuft cells","B":"Parietal and gastric chief cells","C":"Goblet and enteroendocrine cells","D":"Ciliated and respiratory basal cells","E":"Hepatic Kupffer and stellate cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Larger interlobular ducts are lined by simple columnar epithelium with occasional enteroendocrine and goblet cells, while intercalated ducts have low cuboidal cells continuous with centroacinar cells. Paneth, parietal, chief, ciliated, Kupffer, and stellate cells belong to other organs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, ductal cells, lines 2494–2499).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the main pancreatic duct empties into the major duodenal papilla (First Aid 2025 GI pancreas embryology figure, fa_gastro_cache lines 198–204; also Bootcamp merged_with_ocr line 2961). COMLEX: the accessory pancreatic duct opens at the minor papilla (First Aid 2025 GI pancreas embryology figure, fa_gastro_cache lines 198–204; also Bootcamp merged_with_ocr line 2962).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old man drinks a protein shake, and duodenal I cells release their hormone in response to specific amino acids. Besides phenylalanine and methionine, which amino acid is an especially strong trigger?","choices":{"A":"Glycine","B":"Tryptophan","C":"Glutamine","D":"Asparagine","E":"Hydroxyproline"},"correct":"B","explanation":"I cells release CCK in response to fatty acids, monoglycerides, peptides, and amino acids, especially phenylalanine, methionine, and tryptophan. Glycine, glutamine, asparagine, and hydroxyproline are not named. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, CCK source and trigger, lines 2516–2518).","boardPrep":"Step 1: I cells release CCK in the duodenum and jejunum (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, enteroendocrine cells). COMLEX: CCK is released in response to fatty acids and amino acids (First Aid 2025 GI hormones).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman dies of heart failure, and at autopsy the pathologist counts pancreatic islets region by region. In which region are islets most concentrated?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic head","B":"Pancreatic neck","C":"Uncinate process","D":"Pancreatic tail","E":"Pancreatic body"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Islets of Langerhans are scattered among the exocrine acini and are most concentrated in the tail. The head, neck, uncinate process, and body hold fewer islets. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, histologic differentiation, lines 2535–2538).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic veins drain into the portal circulation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic vasculature, merged_with_ocr line 2966). COMLEX: the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery give rise to the pancreaticoduodenal arteries (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic vasculature, merged_with_ocr line 2965).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":3,"stem":"A 23-year-old man has a biopsy of the pancreas. A rare islet cell type makes up a small minority of islet cells and is also scattered in the exocrine tissue. Its hormone slows intestinal motility. Which hormone is it?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic polypeptide","B":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide","C":"Gastric inhibitory peptide","D":"Glucagon like peptide 1","E":"Somatostatin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PP or F cells make up 1 to 2 percent of islet cells, are also scattered in the exocrine pancreas, and make pancreatic polypeptide, which inhibits intestinal motility and pancreatic enzyme and bicarbonate secretion. VIP, GIP, and GLP 1 come from other cells, and somatostatin comes from delta cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, PP cells, lines 2552–2554).","boardPrep":"Step 1: L cells in the ileum release GLP 1, GLP 2, and PYY (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, enteroendocrine cells). COMLEX: K cells in the duodenum and jejunum release GIP (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, enteroendocrine cells).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":14,"title":"SDL 14 — Physiology - Pancreas, Biliary Secretion, and Liver","questions":[{"id":"sdl14_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which brush border enzyme of duodenal epithelial cells first converts trypsinogen to trypsin?","choices":{"A":"Carboxypeptidase B","B":"Enteropeptidase","C":"Phospholipase A2","D":"Chymotrypsin","E":"Aminopeptidase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Enteropeptidase, also called enterokinase, sits in the brush border glycocalyx of duodenal cells and cleaves a hexapeptide from trypsinogen to form trypsin. Carboxypeptidase B, phospholipase A2, and chymotrypsin are activated by trypsin afterward, and aminopeptidase is not the trigger. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, enteropeptidase mediated trypsin activation, lines 2565–2569).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enteropeptidase is a brush border enzyme on duodenal and jejunal mucosa (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic enzymes). COMLEX: activation of pancreatic proteases begins in the duodenal lumen rather than in the gland (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which pancreatic enzyme group is secreted already active rather than as a zymogen?","choices":{"A":"Phospholipase A2","B":"Carboxypeptidase A","C":"Elastase","D":"Chymotrypsin","E":"Nucleases"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pancreatic proteases are secreted only as inactive proenzymes, while alpha amylase, lipases, cholesterol ester hydrolase, and nucleases are secreted active. Phospholipase A2, carboxypeptidase A, elastase, and chymotrypsin act only after their zymogens are cleaved by trypsin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, synthesis and secretion of pancreatic enzymes, lines 2558–2564).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic alpha amylase is secreted in active form (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic enzymes). COMLEX: pancreatic enzymes are made on rough ER, pass through the Golgi, and are stored in apical zymogen granules (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which trypsin inhibitor do acinar and ductal cells package directly into zymogen granules?","choices":{"A":"PRSS1","B":"SERPINA1","C":"CTRC","D":"SPINK1","E":"MRP2"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acinar and ductal cells co-package serine protease inhibitor Kazal type 1, SPINK1, into zymogen granules, where it inactivates up to 20 percent of prematurely formed trypsin. PRSS1 is listed with SPINK1 as a pancreatitis gene but is not the co-packaged inhibitor, SERPINA1 is not described here, CTRC aids trypsin self degradation, and MRP2 moves conjugated bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, trypsin inhibitors, lines 2600–2603).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PRSS1 and SPINK1 mutations are causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: chronic pancreatitis is linked to alcohol use, tobacco use, cystic fibrosis, and hypertriglyceridemia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, up to about what bicarbonate concentration can stimulated pancreatic juice reach?","choices":{"A":"About 24 mEq/L","B":"About 70 mEq/L","C":"About 145 mEq/L","D":"About 300 mEq/L","E":"About 10 mEq/L"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Centroacinar and intercalated duct cells make an isotonic juice with bicarbonate up to about 140 to 145 mEq/L when stimulated. Plasma bicarbonate is about 24 mEq/L, and the other values are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, cellular mechanism of ductal HCO3 secretion and flow rate, lines 2612–2614, 2640–2643).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stimulated pancreatic juice is isotonic and alkaline (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: at high flow, pancreatic juice has a reciprocal fall in chloride as bicarbonate rises (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — CFTR and intestinal chloride secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which intracellular enzyme in pancreatic duct cells joins CO2 and water to begin making bicarbonate?","choices":{"A":"Adenylyl cyclase","B":"Carbonic anhydrase","C":"Carboxypeptidase A","D":"Glutaminase","E":"Pyruvate carboxylase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Carbonic anhydrase combines CO2 with water to form carbonic acid, which dissociates into H+ and HCO3-. Adenylyl cyclase makes cAMP after secretin binds. Carboxypeptidase A is a protease, and glutaminase and pyruvate carboxylase are not described here. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, carbonic anhydrase activity, lines 2615–2619).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duct cells also take up HCO3 across the basolateral membrane by Na+ HCO3 cotransport (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: generated HCO3 leaves across the apical membrane in exchange for luminal Cl- (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — CFTR and intestinal chloride secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how do Na+ and K+ concentrations in pancreatic juice behave as flow rate changes?","choices":{"A":"Rise with flow","B":"Halve at low flow","C":"Exceed plasma twofold","D":"Stay equal to plasma","E":"Track chloride levels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Na+ and K+ in pancreatic juice stay constant and equal to plasma at all flow rates, while HCO3- and Cl- change reciprocally. They do not rise, halve, double, or follow chloride. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, flow rate and ionic composition, lines 2635–2639).","boardPrep":"Step 1: at low basal flow, pancreatic juice is lower in HCO3 and higher in Cl- (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: Bootcamp states that low flow between meals lowers the HCO3 to Cl- ratio (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — CFTR and intestinal chloride secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of the postprandial pancreatic enzyme response comes from the cephalic phase?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 80 percent","D":"About 35 percent","E":"About 20 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The cephalic phase gives about 20 percent of the postprandial enzyme response, the gastric phase about 5 to 10 percent, and the intestinal phase about 70 to 80 percent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, phases of pancreatic secretion, lines 2658, 2668, 2674).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cephalic stimuli include sight, smell, taste, chewing, swallowing, and conditioned reflexes (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: the intestinal phase gives about 70 to 80 percent of the total pancreatic response (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the primary stimulus of the gastric phase of pancreatic secretion?","choices":{"A":"Sight and smell of food","B":"Acidic duodenal chyme","C":"Stomach wall distention","D":"Duodenal fatty acids","E":"Rising blood glucose"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mechanical distention of the stomach wall by food drives the gastric phase through long vagovagal reflexes and enteric circuits. Sight and smell drive the cephalic phase, acidic chyme and fatty acids drive the intestinal phase, and blood glucose is not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, gastric phase, lines 2668–2673).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastric phase maintains a low volume, enzyme rich acinar secretion (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: gastric phase reflexes release ACh onto acinar cells (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what mediator acts directly on acinar cells during the cephalic phase of pancreatic secretion?","choices":{"A":"Vagal acetylcholine","B":"Sympathetic norepinephrine","C":"Circulating secretin","D":"Circulating CCK","E":"Enteric VIP release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the cephalic phase, central impulses travel in the vagus nerve and release ACh from postganglionic vagal endings directly onto acinar cells. Secretin and CCK are released later in the intestinal phase, and norepinephrine and VIP are not described here. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, cephalic phase pathways, lines 2661–2663).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cephalic stimulation yields a moderate enzyme rich secretion with very little water (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: CCK acts on acinar CCK1 receptors and through vagovagal reflexes in the intestinal phase (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of the solutes in bile are bile salts?","choices":{"A":"About 4 percent","B":"About 2 percent","C":"About 50 percent","D":"About 40 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile salts make up about 50 percent of bile solutes, phospholipids mainly lecithin about 40 percent, cholesterol about 4 percent, and bile pigments about 2 percent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, bile formation and composition, lines 2695–2697).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatocytes produce bile continuously, about 0.5 to 1.0 L per day (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: bile is composed of bile salts, phospholipids, cholesterol, bilirubin, water, and ions (First Aid 2025 GI bile).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — lipid digestion and emulsification","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which hepatocyte enzyme converts cholesterol into the primary bile acids?","choices":{"A":"HMG CoA reductase","B":"Heme oxygenase","C":"Lipoprotein lipase","D":"7alpha hydroxylase","E":"Acyl CoA synthetase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hepatocytes make cholic and chenodeoxycholic acid from cholesterol through 7alpha hydroxylase, the rate limiting enzyme. HMG CoA reductase makes cholesterol, heme oxygenase breaks down heme, lipoprotein lipase acts on plasma triglycerides, and acyl CoA synthetase activates fatty acids. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, primary bile acids, lines 2702–2703).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol 7alpha hydroxylase catalyzes the rate limiting step of bile acid synthesis (First Aid 2025 GI bile). COMLEX: the primary bile acids are cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — lipid digestion and emulsification","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what change do intestinal bacteria make to primary bile acids to form secondary bile acids?","choices":{"A":"Dehydroxylation at C7","B":"Conjugation with glycine","C":"Glucuronidation at C3","D":"Oxidation at C12","E":"Sulfation at C24"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intestinal bacteria dehydroxylate primary bile acids at C7, turning cholic acid into deoxycholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid into lithocholic acid. Conjugation with glycine or taurine happens in the liver. The other changes are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, secondary bile acids, lines 2704–2706).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the secondary bile acids are deoxycholic acid and lithocholic acid (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids). COMLEX: bacteria in the ileum and colon deconjugate and modify bile salts to form secondary bile acids (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — lipid digestion and emulsification","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of secreted bile salts pass on to the terminal ileum after lipid absorption?","choices":{"A":"About 50 percent","B":"About 20 percent","C":"About 95 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 5 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After lipid digestion and absorption in the jejunum, about 95 percent of secreted bile salts pass down to the terminal ileum for active reabsorption. The other figures are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, terminal ileal reabsorption, lines 2758–2759).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the terminal ileum is the site of bile salt reabsorption and vitamin B12 absorption (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, small intestine). COMLEX: reabsorbed bile salts return to the liver in portal blood for re-secretion into bile (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which carrier moves bile salts from the ileal lumen into enterocytes?","choices":{"A":"Sodium bile salt cotransporter","B":"Sodium glucose cotransporter","C":"Proton peptide cotransporter","D":"Chloride bicarbonate exchanger","E":"Multidrug resistance protein 2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ileal enterocytes take up bile salts actively through Na+ bile salt cotransporters, called ASBT. Sodium glucose and proton peptide cotransport carry sugars and peptides, the chloride bicarbonate exchanger moves anions, and MRP2 exports conjugated bilirubin into bile. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, terminal ileal reabsorption, lines 2760–2761).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp traces reuptake at the terminal ileum into the portal circulation and back to the liver (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids). COMLEX: hepatocytes extract returning bile salts efficiently through basolateral carriers (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about how large is the total bile salt pool?","choices":{"A":"About 25 to 30 g","B":"About 2.5 to 3 g","C":"About 0.2 to 0.3 g","D":"About 12 to 15 g","E":"About 100 g"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The bile salt pool is about 2.5 to 3.0 g and recirculates several times per meal, for a daily flux of 12 to 30 g. The 25 to 30 g and 12 to 15 g values fall within the daily flux range rather than the pool, 100 g is far too large, and 0.2 to 0.3 g is too small. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, regulation of bile acid synthesis, lines 2766–2767).","boardPrep":"Step 1: returning bile salts inhibit the rate limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: bile acid sequestrants bind bile acids in the gut lumen to prevent bile acid diarrhea (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acid sequestrants).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of bilirubin comes from the breakdown of aging red blood cells?","choices":{"A":"About 15 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 30 percent","D":"About 65 percent","E":"About 85 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"About 85 percent of bilirubin comes from aging red cells broken down by macrophages in the spleen, liver, and bone marrow, and about 15 percent from hepatic hemoproteins such as P450 cytochromes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, bilirubin production, lines 2780–2783).","boardPrep":"Step 1: RBC destruction releases hemoglobin, which yields heme for bilirubin production (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bilirubin metabolism). COMLEX: unconjugated bilirubin is lipid soluble and travels to the liver bound to albumin (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bilirubin metabolism).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 3 — hereditary hyperbilirubinemias","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which macrophage enzyme converts heme to biliverdin?","choices":{"A":"Biliverdin reductase","B":"Heme oxygenase","C":"Glucuronyl transferase","D":"Beta glucuronidase","E":"Ferrochelatase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Macrophage heme oxygenase turns heme into biliverdin, which biliverdin reductase then reduces to unconjugated bilirubin. Glucuronyl transferase conjugates bilirubin in hepatocytes, bacterial beta glucuronidase deconjugates it in the gut, and ferrochelatase builds heme. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, bilirubin production, lines 2784–2786).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliverdin is green and bilirubin is yellow brown (First Aid 2025 GI bilirubin). COMLEX: indirect bilirubin is unconjugated and water insoluble (First Aid 2025 GI bilirubin).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 3 — hereditary hyperbilirubinemias","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which transporter moves conjugated bilirubin across the canalicular membrane into bile?","choices":{"A":"MRP2","B":"ASBT","C":"OATP","D":"CFTR","E":"NTCP"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Conjugated bilirubin is actively pumped across the canalicular membrane by MRP2, multidrug resistant protein 2. ASBT takes up bile salts in the ileum, CFTR is a chloride channel, and OATP and NTCP are basolateral uptake carriers not described for this step. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, hepatic conjugation and excretion, lines 2797–2799).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dubin Johnson syndrome is a defective MRP2 transporter with reduced excretion of conjugated bilirubin (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias). COMLEX: Dubin Johnson and Rotor syndromes are excretion defects (First Aid 2025 GI jaundice).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 3 — hereditary hyperbilirubinemias","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with heavy alcohol use has acute pancreatitis after a small amount of trypsin is activated inside the gland. Which built in property of trypsin normally limits this damage?","choices":{"A":"Autolysis aided by CTRC","B":"Acinar cell apoptosis","C":"Bicarbonate neutralization","D":"Lysosomal colocalization","E":"Bile salt inhibition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Trypsinogen and trypsin carry cleavage sites that allow autocatalytic self degradation, helped by chymotrypsin C or caldecrin, if trypsin is activated early. Apoptosis, bicarbonate, and bile salts do not inactivate trypsin, and lysosomal colocalization is not described as protective. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, trypsin self inactivation, lines 2604–2607).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zymogens stay unactivated until they leave the ducts and meet enteropeptidase in the small intestine (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: chronic alcohol use and tobacco use are listed causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis etiology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with chronic pancreatitis asks why her enzyme capsules need more than lipase. Her physician explains that duodenal bile salts inhibit lipase on its own. Which cofactor, activated by trypsin, binds lipase so it can still work?","choices":{"A":"Elastase","B":"Lecithin","C":"Albumin","D":"Colipase","E":"Secretin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Trypsin converts procolipase to colipase, which binds pancreatic lipase and lets it digest lipid in the presence of inhibitory bile salts. Elastase is a protease, lecithin is a phospholipid, albumin carries bilirubin in blood, and secretin is a hormone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, cascade activation by trypsin, lines 2578–2580).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes triglycerides into 2 monoglycerides and free fatty acids (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: trypsin also activates prophospholipase A2 (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — lipid digestion and emulsification","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with chronic pancreatitis takes enzyme capsules with meals. Which capsule protease removes single amino acids from the C terminal end of peptides?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic trypsin","B":"Pancreatic chymotrypsin","C":"Pancreatic elastase","D":"Brush border aminopeptidase","E":"Carboxypeptidase B"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Carboxypeptidases A and B are exopeptidases that remove single amino acids one at a time from the C terminal end. Trypsin, chymotrypsin, and elastase are endopeptidases that cut internal bonds, and aminopeptidase is a brush border enzyme rather than a pancreatic capsule protease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, digestive functions of activated enzymes, lines 2582–2585).","boardPrep":"Step 1: trypsin also cleaves more trypsinogen into active trypsin in a positive feedback loop (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic enzymes). COMLEX: pancreatic alpha amylase produces disaccharides and trisaccharides from starch and glycogen (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — pancrelipase for exocrine insufficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis has poor pancreatic bicarbonate output. Normally, how does her defective apical channel support bicarbonate secretion?","choices":{"A":"Pumping bicarbonate into blood","B":"Secreting enzymes into acini","C":"Absorbing sodium from the lumen","D":"Recycling chloride to the lumen","E":"Generating carbonic acid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The apical CFTR Cl- channel lets Cl- recycle back into the lumen, keeping luminal Cl- high enough for continuous Cl-/HCO3- exchange. CFTR does not pump bicarbonate into blood, secrete enzymes, absorb sodium, or make carbonic acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, role of CFTR Cl- channels, lines 2623–2627).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystic fibrosis is a listed cause of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis etiology). COMLEX: secretin raises duct cell cAMP to open CFTR and drive Cl-/HCO3 exchange (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — CFTR and intestinal chloride secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with suspected chronic pancreatitis has a secretin stimulation test. As his duct cells secrete bicarbonate, how does the venous blood leaving his pancreas change?","choices":{"A":"It becomes more alkaline","B":"It stays unchanged","C":"It loses chloride","D":"It gains bile salts","E":"It becomes more acidic"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The H+ formed with each HCO3- is pumped across the basolateral membrane by Na+/H+ exchange into capillaries, acidifying pancreatic venous blood. It does not become alkaline or stay unchanged, and chloride and bile salts are not the described change. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, basolateral H+ extrusion, lines 2628–2631).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sodium and water follow HCO3 paracellularly across tight junctions into the duct lumen (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes pancreatic fluid as isotonic, with water, bicarbonate, and electrolytes (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic fluid).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — secretin and CCK regulation of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has pancreatic juice collected during a secretin test, and her flow rises sharply. How does the anion content of the juice change?","choices":{"A":"Chloride up and bicarbonate down","B":"Bicarbonate up and chloride down","C":"Both anions rise together","D":"Neither anion changes","E":"Bicarbonate down and chloride flat"},"correct":"B","explanation":"At high flow, centroacinar and ductal cells secrete large volumes with bicarbonate rising to about 140 to 145 mEq/L and a reciprocal fall in chloride. Low flow shows the opposite pattern, and the two anions change in opposite directions rather than together, not at all, or with one held flat. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, flow rate and ionic composition, lines 2635–2644).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin is released when duodenal pH falls below 4.5 (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: ACh and CCK potentiate secretin driven bicarbonate secretion through IP3 and Ca2+ (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has a truncal vagotomy. Which phase of pancreatic secretion is most completely lost?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal phase","B":"Interdigestive phase","C":"Cephalic phase","D":"Gastric phase","E":"Ileal phase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The cephalic phase depends on central impulses carried by the vagus nerve to release ACh onto acinar cells. The gastric phase also uses vagovagal and enteric reflexes, and the intestinal phase relies mostly on secretin and CCK. Interdigestive and ileal phases are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, cephalic phase, lines 2658–2667).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the vagus nerve is cranial nerve X and carries cephalic impulses to the pancreas (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: the celiac ganglia carry autonomic supply to the pancreas, with parasympathetic input from the vagus (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreas anatomy).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — vagal parasympathetic innervation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman smells lunch cooking, and her pancreas secretes enzymes that mostly stay in the duct system until later. Why do they stay there?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition by somatostatin","B":"Very low fluid volume","C":"Ductal reabsorption of enzymes","D":"Absent zymogen granules","E":"Low enzyme synthesis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The cephalic response is a moderate enzyme rich secretion with very little water or electrolyte volume, so most enzymes stay stored in the ducts until fluid flow increases later. Somatostatin, ductal enzyme reabsorption, absent granules, and low synthesis are not the described reason. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, cephalic phase secretory response, lines 2664–2667).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the cephalic phase, ACh is released from postganglionic vagal endings onto acinar cells (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: somatostatin inhibits secretin and CCK release (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, somatostatin).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — vagal parasympathetic innervation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man eats a large meal, and chyme distends his duodenum and excites stretch and chemical receptors in the duodenal wall. Which pathway then amplifies the effects of secretin and CCK?","choices":{"A":"Vagovagal reflexes","B":"Enterogastric reflex","C":"Gastrocolic reflex","D":"Sympathetic reflexes","E":"Myenteric peristalsis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the intestinal phase, distention and chemical stimuli excite vagal afferents and trigger efferent ACh release, a vagovagal reflex that potentiates both secretin and CCK. The enterogastric and gastrocolic reflexes, sympathetic reflexes, and peristalsis are not described here. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, intestinal phase mechanisms, lines 2686–2688).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intestinal phase stimuli include acidic chyme and fat and protein digestion products (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: CCK stimulates massive zymogen exocytosis from acinar cells in the intestinal phase (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with gallstones asks how her bile salts can work in the duodenum. They stay ionized and water soluble at duodenal pH. Which feature explains this?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial dehydroxylation","B":"Albumin binding in bile","C":"Low pKa after conjugation","D":"Micelle core packing","E":"Bicarbonate buffering"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Conjugation with glycine or taurine lowers the pKa from about 7 to between 1 and 4, so bile salts are fully ionized and water soluble at duodenal pH (3 to 5 in the lecture). Bacterial dehydroxylation makes secondary bile acids, albumin carries bilirubin in blood, and micelles and buffering do not set the pKa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, conjugation into bile salts, lines 2707–2711).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acids are conjugated in the liver with glycine or taurine to form amphipathic bile salts (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids). COMLEX: conjugation with glycine or taurine makes bile acids water soluble (First Aid 2025 GI bile).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — lipid digestion and emulsification","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old man eats a fatty meal, and his bile salts break large fat globules into tiny emulsified droplets. By up to about how much does the lipid surface area increase?","choices":{"A":"Up to 10 times","B":"Up to 2 times","C":"Up to 100 times","D":"Up to 1 million times","E":"Up to 1000 times"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bile salts lower interfacial tension, and intestinal mixing breaks fat into droplets under 1 µm, raising surface area up to 1000 fold for pancreatic lipase. Their negative charges repel and keep the droplets from merging. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, role of bile salts in emulsification, lines 2716–2721).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp states that emulsification raises surface area for pancreatic enzymes and increases fat digestion and absorption (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acid function). COMLEX: bile salts have a hydrophobic steroid nucleus and hydrophilic negatively charged groups (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — lipid digestion and emulsification","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman digests a fatty meal, and mixed micelles carry lipids across the unstirred water layer to her enterocytes. Where do the bile salts end up once the lipids enter the cells?","choices":{"A":"In the gut lumen","B":"Inside the enterocyte","C":"In the portal blood","D":"In the lymph lacteals","E":"In the brush border"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At the brush border, monoglycerides and fatty acids leave the micelle and diffuse into enterocytes, while bile salts are left behind in the lumen for later ileal reabsorption. They do not enter the jejunal enterocyte, portal blood, lacteals, or brush border with the lipids. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, role of bile salts in micelle formation, lines 2730–2733).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile functions include absorption of lipids and fat soluble vitamins (First Aid 2025 GI bile). COMLEX: bile is the main route for eliminating cholesterol and bilirubin (First Aid 2025 GI bile).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — lipid absorption and processing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with Crohn disease has his terminal ileum resected and develops steatorrhea even though his liver now makes more bile acids. Why does steatorrhea occur?","choices":{"A":"Synthesis is shut off","B":"Synthesis cannot match losses","C":"Lipase is destroyed in bile","D":"Micelles form too easily","E":"Gallbladder stops storing bile"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Less bile salt returns to the liver, which relieves inhibition and raises synthesis, but even maximal synthesis falls behind heavy fecal loss. The pool shrinks, lipid solubilization fails, and steatorrhea follows. Synthesis rises rather than stopping, and the other choices are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, regulation of bile acid synthesis, lines 2770–2774).","boardPrep":"Step 1: malabsorption of bile salts at the distal ileum, as in Crohn disease, prevents normal fat absorption (First Aid 2025 GI bile). COMLEX: ileal bile salt loss lets dietary calcium bind fat, so free oxalate is absorbed and oxalate stones form (First Aid 2025 GI bile).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman fasts overnight, and her liver bile flows into her gallbladder for storage. What are the states of her sphincter of Oddi and gallbladder?","choices":{"A":"Open with gallbladder contracted","B":"Open with gallbladder relaxed","C":"Closed with gallbladder relaxed","D":"Closed with gallbladder contracted","E":"Spasming with ductal reflux"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Between meals the sphincter of Oddi is closed and the gallbladder is relaxed, so continuously made bile flows into the gallbladder. After a meal CCK reverses both, contracting the gallbladder and relaxing the sphincter. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, gallbladder storage, lines 2738–2740).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi so bile is ejected in pulsatile spurts (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: vagal ACh adds to CCK in stimulating gallbladder emptying (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — gallbladder concentration of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man eats three meals a day. About how many times does his bile salt pool recirculate with each meal?","choices":{"A":"2 to 3 times","B":"10 to 12 times","C":"Once per day","D":"5 to 6 times","E":"20 to 30 times"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The bile salt pool of about 2.5 to 3.0 g recirculates 2 to 3 times per meal, for 12 to 30 g per day. Once per day is too few, and the larger counts are too many. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, regulation of bile acid synthesis, lines 2766–2767).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 95 percent of bile salts reach the terminal ileum for reuptake (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: portal blood carries returning bile salts back to hepatocytes (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old man with sickle cell disease has jaundice during a hemolytic crisis. Which finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Strongly positive urine bilirubin","B":"Absent urine urobilinogen","C":"Pale clay colored stools","D":"Negative urine bilirubin","E":"Raised conjugated bilirubin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hemolytic jaundice raises mainly unconjugated bilirubin, which is albumin bound and is not filtered by glomeruli, so urine bilirubin is negative. Urine urobilinogen rises and stools are dark. Strong urine bilirubin, absent urobilinogen, and pale stools fit obstruction, and conjugated bilirubin stays normal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, prehepatic jaundice, lines 2813–2823).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hemolysis is a cause of unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia (First Aid 2025 GI jaundice). COMLEX: jaundice appears when total serum bilirubin exceeds about 2.0 to 2.5 mg/dL (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — black pigment gallstones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has a pancreatic head mass that completely blocks her common bile duct. Which stool and urine pattern is expected?","choices":{"A":"Dark stools with clear urine","B":"Dark stools and raised urobilinogen","C":"Pale stools and dark urine","D":"Normal stools and normal urine","E":"Green stools and red urine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Complete obstruction keeps bile out of the gut, so stools are pale and urine urobilinogen is absent, while water soluble conjugated bilirubin spills into urine and makes it dark and tea colored. Dark stools and raised urobilinogen fit hemolysis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, posthepatic jaundice, lines 2835–2846).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obstructive jaundice with a palpable nontender gallbladder is the Courvoisier sign (First Aid 2025 GI biliary structures). COMLEX: Bootcamp states that about 80 percent of urobilinogen becomes stercobilin for fecal excretion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bilirubin metabolism).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old man has mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia that appears during fasting. Which hepatocyte enzyme of the smooth ER is mildly deficient?","choices":{"A":"HMOX1","B":"UGT1A1","C":"BLVRA","D":"CYP7A1","E":"CYP2E1"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gilbert syndrome is an inherited mild UGT1A1 deficiency that lowers bilirubin glucuronidation in the smooth ER and raises unconjugated bilirubin. HMOX1 makes biliverdin, BLVRA makes bilirubin, CYP7A1 is the rate limiting 7 alpha hydroxylase of bile acid synthesis, and CYP2E1 is induced by alcohol, so none of them conjugates bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, hepatic conjugation and hepatic jaundice, lines 2793–2796, 2824–2827).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crigler Najjar type I has severely reduced UGT1A1 activity and type II less severe loss (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias). COMLEX: phenobarbital raises UGT1A1 expression and may help Crigler Najjar type II (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — hereditary hyperbilirubinemias","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":3,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman with chronic pancreatitis takes enzyme capsules with a fatty meal. When phospholipase A2, activated by trypsin, cleaves a fatty acid from dietary phospholipid, what product remains?","choices":{"A":"Monoglycerides","B":"Free cholesterol","C":"Lecithin","D":"Lysolecithin","E":"Glycerol"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Phospholipase A2 cleaves fatty acids from phospholipids to yield lysolecithin. Monoglycerides come from lipase acting on triglycerides, free cholesterol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase, lecithin is the starting phospholipid, and glycerol is not the described product. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, lipolytic enzymes, lines 2588–2592).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol ester hydrolase converts cholesterol esters to free cholesterol and fatty acids (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: endopeptidases such as trypsin, chymotrypsin, and elastase cleave internal peptide bonds (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — lipid digestion and emulsification","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":3,"stem":"A 37-year-old man with chronic pancreatitis has a secretin stimulation test. Besides making it inside the cell, by which basolateral route do his duct cells take up extra bicarbonate from blood?","choices":{"A":"Sodium bicarbonate symport","B":"Chloride bicarbonate exchange","C":"Sodium proton exchange","D":"Paracellular diffusion","E":"Aquaporin water channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Extra HCO3- enters the ductal cell across the basolateral membrane by Na+/HCO3- cotransport. The Cl-/HCO3- exchanger sits on the apical side, Na+/H+ exchange removes H+ basolaterally, sodium and water move paracellularly into the lumen, and aquaporins carry water. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, carbonic anhydrase activity, lines 2618–2619).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the H+ made with HCO3 leaves duct cells by basolateral Na+/H+ exchange and Na+/K+ ATPase (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: secretin is the primary stimulant of aqueous HCO3 secretion (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — Na, Cl, and HCO3 transport along the intestine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":3,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman eats lunch. During the gastric phase of pancreatic secretion, what kind of secretion do her acinar cells make?","choices":{"A":"High volume bicarbonate fluid","B":"Low volume enzyme juice","C":"Pure electrolyte secretion","D":"Mucus rich ductal secretion","E":"Bile rich duodenal reflux"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The gastric phase uses long vagovagal reflexes and enteric circuits that release ACh onto acinar cells and keep up a low volume, enzyme rich secretion. High volume bicarbonate fluid is the secretin response of the intestinal phase. The other choices are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, gastric phase, lines 2668–2673).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastric phase gives about 5 to 10 percent of the postprandial enzyme response (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: intestinal phase amino acid triggers include phenylalanine, methionine, and tryptophan (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 59-year-old man recovering from a cholecystectomy absorbs fat normally after a fatty meal. About what size are the mixed micelles that carry his lipid digestion products?","choices":{"A":"30 to 60 nm","B":"3 to 6 µm","C":"3 to 6 nm","D":"300 to 600 nm","E":"0.3 to 0.6 mm"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mixed micelles are cylindrical complexes about 3 to 6 nm across, made of bile salts with lecithin, fatty acids, monoglycerides, cholesterol, and lysolecithin. Emulsified droplets are larger, under 1 µm, and the other sizes are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, role of bile salts in micelle formation, lines 2723–2726).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in a micelle, hydrophobic lipids sit inside and the charged polar groups of bile salts face outward (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: negative charges on surface bile salts keep emulsified droplets from merging again (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — lipid digestion and emulsification","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has gallstones, and her biliary pain begins after a fatty meal when duodenal I cells release their hormone. Besides contracting her gallbladder, what else does this hormone do?","choices":{"A":"Sphincter of Oddi relaxation","B":"Sphincter of Oddi contraction","C":"Pyloric sphincter relaxation","D":"Ileocecal valve closure","E":"Gallbladder wall relaxation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"CCK from I cells contracts the gallbladder wall and at the same time relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, so concentrated bile spurts into the duodenum. It does not contract the sphincter of Oddi or relax the gallbladder wall, and the pylorus and ileocecal valve are not the described targets. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, CCK mediated gallbladder ejection, lines 2748–2756).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliary colic worsens after meals because CCK drives gallbladder contraction against an obstructing stone (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary colic). COMLEX: CCK is released when fatty acids, monoglycerides, and amino acids enter the proximal duodenum (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old man on long courses of broad spectrum antibiotics notices lighter stools. Normally, which bacterial product gives stool its brown color?","choices":{"A":"Biliverdin","B":"Bilirubin","C":"Urobilinogen","D":"Hemosiderin","E":"Stercobilin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic bacteria oxidize most urobilinogen to stercobilin and urobilin, which give feces their normal dark brown color. Biliverdin is the green heme breakdown product, bilirubin is deconjugated and reduced in the gut, urobilinogen is colorless, and hemosiderin is a tissue iron store. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, intestinal metabolism and excretion, lines 2800–2805).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 20 percent of urobilinogen is reabsorbed and a small share reaches the urine as yellow urobilin (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: bacterial beta glucuronidases deconjugate bilirubin in the terminal ileum and colon (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":15,"title":"SDL 15 — Pathology - Infectious and Inflammatory Liver Diseases","questions":[{"id":"sdl15_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":1,"stem":"In acute and chronic hepatitis, what does Robbins call the pyknotic eosinophilic remnants of apoptotic hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Mallory hyaline","B":"Acidophil bodies","C":"Russell bodies","D":"Ground glass cells","E":"Psammoma bodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Irreversible hepatocyte injury by apoptosis leaves pyknotic, eosinophilic acidophil bodies ringed by mononuclear cells. Robbins notes that these were first described in yellow fever and named Councilman bodies, and by convention keeps that eponym for yellow fever; in other hepatitis the term is acidophil bodies. Mallory hyaline is clumped intermediate filament in ballooned cells, ground glass cells mark chronic hepatitis B, and Russell and psammoma bodies are not liver apoptosis findings. Sources: Robbins ch 18, hepatocyte apoptosis (SDL 15–19 course reading, p. 6); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, hepatocyte responses to injury, lines 2850–2856).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp and the lecture use Councilman bodies more loosely, for the eosinophilic inclusions of acute viral hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute viral hepatitis); expect either name. COMLEX: confluent necrosis is zonal loss of contiguous hepatocytes, such as zone 3 perivenular necrosis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 4 — Kupffer and stellate cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which cell is mainly responsible for collagen deposition and scar formation in the injured liver?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic stellate cells","B":"Resident Kupffer cells","C":"Portal tract fibroblasts","D":"Ductal cholangiocytes","E":"Sinusoidal endothelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hepatic stellate or Ito cells are the main collagen depositing cells. Cytokines such as TNF alpha and TGF beta from Kupffer cells and injured hepatocytes activate them, and they become myofibroblasts that lay down type I and III collagen in the space of Disse. Kupffer cells signal but do not make the scar. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, scar formation and hepatic stellate cells, lines 2857–2865).","boardPrep":"Step 1: collagen in the space of Disse causes loss of endothelial fenestrations, called sinusoidal capillarization (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: PDGF drives proliferation of activated stellate cells (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 4 — Kupffer and stellate cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, acute liver failure is encephalopathy and coagulopathy arising within what time of the first injury?","choices":{"A":"Within 2 weeks","B":"Within 52 weeks","C":"Within 6 weeks","D":"Within 26 weeks","E":"Within 5 years"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute liver failure is acute liver illness with encephalopathy and coagulopathy within 26 weeks of the first injury in a patient without preexisting liver disease, and it shows massive necrosis with a small shrunken liver. The other windows are not the lecture definition. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, acute liver failure, lines 2873–2876).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in acute liver failure, portal hypertension develops over days to weeks and shows as ascites (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: First Aid lists drug injury such as acetaminophen, ischemic hepatitis, viral hepatitis, and autoimmune hepatitis as causes of very high aminotransferases (First Aid 2025 GI liver markers).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 4 — Kupffer and stellate cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what genome and structure does hepatitis A virus have?","choices":{"A":"Enveloped partially double stranded DNA","B":"Enveloped positive sense RNA","C":"Nonenveloped positive sense RNA","D":"Enveloped circular defective RNA","E":"Nonenveloped negative sense RNA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"HAV is a small nonenveloped single stranded positive sense RNA picornavirus. Partially double stranded DNA is HBV, enveloped positive sense RNA is HCV, enveloped circular defective RNA is HDV, and no nonenveloped hepatotropic virus is negative sense. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis A virus, lines 2898–2901).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgG anti HAV persists for life and confers long term immunity (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: HAV spreads by the fecal oral route and does not cause chronic liver disease (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about how long after exposure do anti-HCV antibodies emerge in hepatitis C infection?","choices":{"A":"3 to 6 days","B":"3 to 6 weeks","C":"1 to 3 weeks","D":"3 to 6 months","E":"6 to 12 months"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In hepatitis C, HCV RNA becomes detectable within 1 to 3 weeks of infection along with elevated aminotransferases, and anti-HCV antibodies emerge 3 to 6 weeks after exposure. One to 3 weeks is the timing of HCV RNA, not antibody. Three to 6 days is too early for either marker. Three to 6 months and 6 to 12 months are too late; persistent HCV RNA beyond 6 months instead defines chronic infection. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis C serological markers and timing, lines 2943–2946).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists HBV and HCV as the viral hepatitides classically associated with hepatocellular carcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma). COMLEX: First Aid lists HCV among the causes of cirrhosis linked to hepatocellular carcinoma (First Aid 2025 GI, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which marker is crucial for diagnosing acute hepatitis B after HBsAg clears but before anti HBs appears?","choices":{"A":"IgG anti HBs","B":"IgG anti HBc","C":"IgM anti HAV","D":"IgG anti HDV","E":"IgM anti HBc"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In the window period after HBsAg clears and before anti HBs appears, IgM anti HBc identifies acute infection. IgG anti HBc and anti HBs mark past or resolved infection, and the HAV and HDV antibodies belong to other viruses. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis B serological markers, lines 2921–2926).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HBeAg and HBV DNA indicate active replication and high infectivity (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: anti HBs rises after resolution and is the basis of the hepatitis B vaccine (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which organisms most commonly ascend the biliary tree to cause cholangitis and pyogenic liver abscess?","choices":{"A":"Enteric coliforms","B":"Staphylococcus aureus","C":"Group A streptococci","D":"Anaerobic clostridia","E":"Pseudomonas species"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteric bacteria, most commonly coliforms such as E. coli along with enterococci, ascend the obstructed biliary tree and can extend into the liver to form pyogenic abscesses. The other organisms are not named as the usual cause. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, ascending cholangitis pathogenesis, lines 2989–2992).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Charcot triad of cholangitis is jaundice, fever, and right upper quadrant pain (First Aid 2025 GI acute cholangitis). COMLEX: the Reynolds pentad adds altered mental status and shock to the Charcot triad (First Aid 2025 GI acute cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction and cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what morphologic finding characterizes cholestasis of sepsis?","choices":{"A":"Zone 3 necrosis","B":"Interface hepatitis","C":"Ground glass hepatocytes","D":"Ductular bile plugs","E":"Onion skin fibrosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cholestasis of sepsis shows ductular or cholangiolar cholestasis, with dark bile plugs in dilated canals of Hering and ductules at the portal parenchymal interface and little inflammation. Zone 3 necrosis, interface hepatitis, ground glass cells, and onion skin fibrosis are other patterns. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, sepsis and cholestasis of sepsis, lines 2998–3005).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sepsis injures the liver by direct bacterial action, hypoperfusion, or the host response to circulating endotoxin (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: complications of ascending cholangitis include sepsis and intrahepatic abscess (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction and cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which imaging feature is characteristic of hydatid cysts in the liver?","choices":{"A":"Air fluid level","B":"Central stellate scar","C":"Peripheral target sign","D":"Septated hemorrhage","E":"Calcified cyst wall"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Echinococcus larval tapeworms form large hydatid cysts that characteristically show calcification of the cyst wall on imaging. A central stellate scar suggests focal nodular hyperplasia, and the other signs are not the described feature. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, echinococcosis, lines 3016–3019).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hydatid cysts carry a risk of rupture, anaphylaxis, and daughter cyst spread (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: liver flukes inhabit intrahepatic bile ducts and cause hepatolithiasis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction and cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which autoantibodies characterize type 1 autoimmune hepatitis?","choices":{"A":"LKM1 and LC1","B":"AMA and gp210","C":"ANA and ASMA","D":"ANCA and MPO","E":"Anti CCP and RF"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Type 1 autoimmune hepatitis shows antinuclear and anti smooth muscle actin antibodies, sometimes with anti SLA or LPA, and high IgG. LKM1 and LC1 mark type 2. AMA points to primary biliary cholangitis, and ANCA and anti CCP fit other diseases. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, type 1 autoimmune hepatitis, lines 3048–3052).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists hypergammaglobulinemia with raised IgG in autoimmune hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, autoimmune hepatitis). COMLEX: type 1 autoimmune hepatitis is linked to thyroiditis, type 1 diabetes, and celiac disease (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which enzyme is the target of LKM1 antibodies in type 2 autoimmune hepatitis?","choices":{"A":"CYP2D6","B":"CYP2E1","C":"CYP3A4","D":"CYP1A2","E":"CYP2C9"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anti LKM1 antibodies in type 2 autoimmune hepatitis are directed against cytochrome P450 CYP2D6. CYP2E1 makes NAPQI from acetaminophen, and the other isoforms are not described as autoantigens here. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, type 2 autoimmune hepatitis, lines 3056–3058).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that autoimmune hepatitis is often positive for anti smooth muscle or anti LKM1 antibodies (First Aid 2025 GI autoimmune hepatitis). COMLEX: type 2 autoimmune hepatitis mainly affects children and adolescents (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of autoimmune hepatitis patients are female?","choices":{"A":"About 25 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 95 percent","D":"About 78 percent","E":"About 10 percent"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Autoimmune hepatitis occurs mainly in females, about 78 percent, with a genetic tie to HLA alleles such as HLA DR3 in Europeans. The other figures are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, pathogenesis and triggers, lines 3027–3031).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes dense lymphoplasmacytic infiltration with periportal piecemeal necrosis in autoimmune hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, autoimmune hepatitis). COMLEX: autoimmune hepatitis can progress to acute liver failure, cirrhosis, or hepatocellular carcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, autoimmune hepatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which cytochrome converts excess acetaminophen into its toxic metabolite?","choices":{"A":"CYP3A4","B":"CYP2D6","C":"CYP2E1","D":"CYP2C9","E":"CYP1A2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After an overdose saturates glucuronidation and sulfation, acetaminophen is shunted to CYP2E1, which forms NAPQI. The other isoforms are not the described route. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, acute necrosis pathogenesis, lines 3083–3086).","boardPrep":"Step 1: induction of CYP2E1 by chronic alcohol or anticonvulsants increases acetaminophen susceptibility (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists zone 3 centrilobular necrosis in acetaminophen toxicity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acetaminophen toxicity).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — acinar zones and their injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which feature describes idiosyncratic drug induced hepatotoxicity?","choices":{"A":"Highly predictable","B":"Not dose dependent","C":"Reproducible in animals","D":"Immediate after exposure","E":"Seen in most users"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Idiosyncratic hepatotoxicity is unpredictable, not dose dependent, occurs in only a small minority of exposed people, and has variable latency. Predictable, reproducible, rapid injury is direct intrinsic toxicity. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, general pathogenesis and categories, lines 3067–3074).","boardPrep":"Step 1: idiosyncratic susceptibility comes from polymorphisms in drug metabolizing enzymes, transporters, or HLA alleles (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: direct hepatotoxicity is predictable, dose dependent, and occurs shortly after exposure (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — acinar zones and their injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which drug is listed as a cause of microvesicular steatosis from mitochondrial toxicity?","choices":{"A":"Amiodarone","B":"Methotrexate","C":"Isoniazid","D":"Oxaliplatin","E":"Valproate"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Valproate, tetracycline, high dose aspirin, and nucleoside analogs such as zidovudine cause microvesicular steatosis. Amiodarone causes steatohepatitis, methotrexate fibrosis, isoniazid acute hepatitis, and oxaliplatin sinusoidal obstruction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, microvesicular steatosis, lines 3117–3120).","boardPrep":"Step 1: microvesicular steatosis presents with liver dysfunction, hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, and encephalopathy without overt necrosis (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: methotrexate, retinoids, and enalapril are listed causes of periportal or pericellular fibrosis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — acinar zones and their injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with cirrhosis has new confusion and a nonrhythmic flapping of his outstretched wrists. Which mechanism best explains these signs?","choices":{"A":"Low serum albumin","B":"Raised blood ammonia","C":"High serum bilirubin","D":"Low platelet count","E":"High portal pressure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hepatic encephalopathy, with confusion and asterixis, comes from raised blood ammonia when hepatic urea cycle detoxification fails. Low albumin, high bilirubin, low platelets, and portal pressure do not cause asterixis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, hepatic encephalopathy, lines 2877–2881).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists hepatic encephalopathy and asterixis as neurologic signs of liver disease (First Aid 2025 GI cirrhosis signs). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised ammonia with high PT or INR and low albumin in cirrhosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cirrhosis diagnostics).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with chronic liver failure from long standing cholestasis has severe, constant itching. What most likely causes her itching?","choices":{"A":"Histamine from mast cells","B":"High serum ammonia","C":"Excess estrogen","D":"Low clotting factors","E":"Retained bile salts"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Persistent cholestasis in chronic liver failure causes profound pruritus from systemic buildup of retained bile salts and solutes. Ammonia causes encephalopathy, excess estrogen causes spider angiomas and gynecomastia, low clotting factors cause bleeding, and mast cell histamine is not the described cause. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, chronic liver failure and pruritus, lines 2889–2892).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists jaundice, conjunctival icterus, pruritus, and fetor hepaticus in cirrhosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cirrhosis presentation). COMLEX: First Aid lists jaundice among the integumentary signs of liver disease (First Aid 2025 GI cirrhosis signs).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with cirrhosis has gynecomastia, palmar erythema, and spider angiomas on his chest. Which change best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"Excess testosterone output","B":"Raised serum ammonia levels","C":"Impaired estrogen clearance","D":"Portal venous congestion","E":"Reduced clotting factors"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyperestrogenemia from impaired hepatic estrogen clearance causes palmar erythema, spider angiomas, hypogonadism, and gynecomastia. Testosterone is not raised, ammonia causes encephalopathy, portal congestion causes ascites and varices, and low clotting factors cause bleeding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, chronic liver failure, lines 2892–2894).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists gynecomastia, hypogonadism, anovulation, and palmar erythema in cirrhosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cirrhosis presentation). COMLEX: First Aid lists spider angiomas and palmar erythema among the integumentary signs of liver disease (First Aid 2025 GI cirrhosis signs).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man still has detectable HBsAg 8 months after an acute hepatitis illness. Which state does this finding define?","choices":{"A":"Hepatitis window period","B":"Resolved infection","C":"Acute HDV coinfection","D":"Chronic hepatitis B","E":"Prior HBV vaccination"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Persistence of HBsAg beyond 6 months defines chronic HBV infection. The window period has no HBsAg, resolved infection and vaccination show anti HBs without HBsAg, and HDV coinfection is usually a self limited acute illness. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis B serological markers, lines 2927–2929).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in adults, about 5 to 10 percent of HBV infections progress to chronic hepatitis (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: perinatal HBV transmission leads to chronic infection in about 90 percent of infants (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman who is 30 weeks pregnant develops severe acute hepatitis during a waterborne outbreak in South Asia. Which virus is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Hepatitis E","B":"Hepatitis A","C":"Hepatitis B","D":"Hepatitis C","E":"Hepatitis D"},"correct":"A","explanation":"HEV spreads by the fecal oral route in waterborne epidemics, and acute HEV in pregnant women carries a mortality approaching 20 percent. HAV is also fecal oral, but the lecture ties this pregnancy mortality to HEV, and HBV, HCV, and HDV are parenteral. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis E virus, lines 2975–2978).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HEV is a nonenveloped positive sense RNA virus in the Hepeviridae family (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: chronic HEV occurs only in immunocompromised hosts such as organ transplant recipients (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — liver disease in pregnancy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old man who is a long standing HBsAg carrier and injects drugs develops a new acute hepatitis, and IgM anti HDV is positive. What outcome is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Rapid HBsAg clearance","B":"Lifelong HDV immunity","C":"Mild self limited illness","D":"Clearance of both viruses","E":"Chronic HDV infection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HDV superinfection of a chronic HBsAg carrier leads to chronic HDV in more than 80 percent of cases, with rapid progression to severe hepatitis, cirrhosis, and HCC. Coinfection with HBV and HDV at the same time is the usually self limited pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis D virus, lines 2960–2964).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HDV encodes a single internal polypeptide, the delta antigen (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: anti HDV and HBsAg persist in chronic delta hepatitis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman has fever, right upper quadrant pain, and jaundice, and enteric bacteria grow from her bile. Which condition sets the stage for this infection?","choices":{"A":"Portal vein thrombosis","B":"Biliary obstruction","C":"Right heart failure","D":"Alcohol overuse","E":"Hepatic artery spasm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Extrahepatic or intrahepatic biliary obstruction from stones, strictures, or tumors creates stagnant bile that lets bacteria multiply and ascend, causing ascending cholangitis. Portal thrombosis, heart failure, alcohol, and arterial spasm are not the described setup. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, ascending cholangitis cause and risk factors, lines 2985–2988).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp explains that biliary obstruction impairs the mechanisms that keep bacteria out of the biliary tree (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists conjunctival icterus, pale stools, and dark urine in acute cholangitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction and cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man from Southeast Asia often eats undercooked freshwater fish and has flukes in his intrahepatic bile ducts. Which tumor is he at greatly increased risk for?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocellular carcinoma","B":"Gallbladder adenoma","C":"Hepatic angiosarcoma","D":"Pancreatic cystadenoma","E":"Cholangiocarcinoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Opisthorchis and Clonorchis flukes live in intrahepatic bile ducts and cause chronic biliary inflammation, hepatolithiasis, and a dramatically increased risk of cholangiocarcinoma. The other tumors are not the described risk. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, liver flukes, lines 3012–3015).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists Clonorchis sinensis infection as a risk factor for cholangiocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholangiocarcinoma). COMLEX: First Aid lists liver fluke infection such as Clonorchis as a cholangiocarcinoma risk factor (First Aid 2025 GI biliary tumors).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — HCC versus other hepatic malignancies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man has bloody dysentery and weeks later a liver abscess that drains thick anchovy paste fluid. Which organism most likely caused it?","choices":{"A":"Entamoeba histolytica","B":"Echinococcus granulosus","C":"Schistosoma mansoni","D":"Clonorchis sinensis","E":"Giardia lamblia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Entamoeba histolytica trophozoites breach the colonic mucosa, causing amebic dysentery, and travel by portal blood to form liver abscesses full of necrotic anchovy paste debris. Echinococcus forms hydatid cysts, Schistosoma causes portal fibrosis, Clonorchis lives in bile ducts, and Giardia does not invade. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, amebiasis, lines 3020–3023).","boardPrep":"Step 1: amebiasis spreads by the fecal oral route (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: bacteria can also reach the liver by portal hematogenous spread or direct extension (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 5 — enteric protozoa","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with thyroiditis has chronic liver inflammation with high IgG and circulating autoantibodies. Which histologic feature is most characteristic of her liver disease?","choices":{"A":"Onion skin fibrosis","B":"Florid duct lesion","C":"Ground glass hepatocytes","D":"Interface hepatitis","E":"Zone 3 necrosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Autoimmune hepatitis shows prominent interface hepatitis, dense lymphoplasmacytic infiltrates with plasma cell clusters, emperipolesis, and hepatocyte rosettes. Onion skin fibrosis fits PSC, the florid duct lesion fits PBC, ground glass cells fit chronic HBV, and zone 3 necrosis fits toxic or ischemic injury. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, morphologic features, lines 3036–3042).","boardPrep":"Step 1: emperipolesis is intact lymphocytes or plasma cells engulfed within hepatocytes (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: First Aid describes a portal and periportal lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate in autoimmune hepatitis (First Aid 2025 GI autoimmune hepatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl has autoimmune hepatitis with anti LKM1 antibodies and no ANA. Which other autoantibody may also be present?","choices":{"A":"Anti mitochondrial","B":"Anti liver cytosol 1","C":"Anti smooth muscle","D":"Anti neutrophil cytoplasm","E":"Anti tissue transglutaminase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Type 2 autoimmune hepatitis shows anti LKM1 and or anti liver cytosol 1 antibodies, with ANA and smooth muscle antibodies typically absent. Anti mitochondrial antibodies fit PBC, anti smooth muscle fits type 1, and the other antibodies fit vasculitis and celiac disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, type 2 autoimmune hepatitis, lines 3054–3059).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists ANA and anti smooth muscle in type 1 and anti LKM1 in type 2 autoimmune hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, autoimmune hepatitis). COMLEX: serum IgG is elevated in both types of autoimmune hepatitis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman of European descent develops autoimmune hepatitis after a course of minocycline. Which HLA allele is most linked to her disease?","choices":{"A":"HLA B27","B":"HLA DQ2","C":"HLA DR3","D":"HLA B51","E":"HLA DR2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Autoimmune hepatitis is linked to HLA DR3 in Europeans, HLA DR4 in Japanese, and HLA DRB1 in South Americans, and drugs such as minocycline are proposed triggers. HLA B27, DQ2, B51, and DR2 are tied to other diseases. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, pathogenesis and triggers, lines 3029–3033).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proposed triggers of autoimmune hepatitis include viral infections, drugs, and vaccinations (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: CD4 helper T cells drive B cells to make autoantibodies in autoimmune hepatitis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — associated conditions and characteristic serum antibodies of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman takes a large acetaminophen overdose and two days later has very high aminotransferases. Depletion of which protective molecule allows the toxic metabolite to injure her hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Glutathione","B":"Glucuronic acid","C":"Sulfate stores","D":"Cytochrome P450","E":"Bilirubin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Excess NAPQI depletes glutathione and then binds cellular proteins, damaging mitochondria and killing zone 3 hepatocytes. Glucuronidation and sulfation are the normal pathways that are saturated, cytochrome P450 makes NAPQI, and bilirubin is not protective here. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, acute necrosis pathogenesis, lines 3083–3089).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetaminophen is the most common cause of acute liver failure requiring transplantation in the United States (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: Bootcamp states that depleted glutathione raises NAPQI and causes hepatocyte necrosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acetaminophen toxicity).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — acinar zones and their injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man had high dose chemotherapy for a bone marrow transplant and now has tender hepatomegaly, weight gain, ascites, and rising bilirubin. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Budd Chiari syndrome","B":"Portal vein thrombosis","C":"Veno occlusive disease","D":"Acute viral hepatitis","E":"Drug induced cholestasis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sinusoidal obstruction syndrome, or veno occlusive disease, follows high dose cytotoxic drugs, with toxic sinusoidal endothelial injury and fibrous obliteration of terminal hepatic venules. Budd Chiari involves the large hepatic veins, and the others do not fit this post transplant pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, sinusoidal obstruction syndrome, lines 3137–3146).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyrrolizidine alkaloids in bush tea also cause sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: azathioprine, oxaliplatin, and cyclophosphamide are listed causes of sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man taking isoniazid for latent tuberculosis develops fever, rash, eosinophilia, and jaundice with ALT more than 5 times normal. Which injury pattern is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Microvesicular steatosis","B":"Zone 3 confluent necrosis","C":"Bland canalicular cholestasis","D":"Acute inflammatory hepatitis","E":"Sinusoidal endothelial injury"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Isoniazid, nitrofurantoin, phenytoin, methyldopa, and checkpoint inhibitors cause idiosyncratic acute inflammatory hepatitis with lymphoplasmacytic inflammation, fever, rash, eosinophilia, and ALT at least 5 times normal. The other patterns belong to other drugs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, acute inflammatory hepatitis, lines 3108–3116).","boardPrep":"Step 1: immune checkpoint inhibitors are listed causes of idiosyncratic acute inflammatory hepatitis (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes eosinophilia with halothane induced liver injury (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, inhaled anesthetic toxicity).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with acute liver failure has easy bruising and a prolonged prothrombin time. What is the main cause of her bleeding tendency?","choices":{"A":"Low clotting factor output","B":"Platelet antibody destruction","C":"Dietary vitamin C deficiency","D":"Excess heparin release","E":"Bone marrow aplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Coagulopathy in liver failure comes from defective hepatic synthesis of clotting factors II, V, VII, IX, X, XI, XII, and fibrinogen and from failure to clear activated factors. Platelet antibodies, vitamin C deficiency, heparin, and marrow aplasia are not the described cause. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, coagulopathy, lines 2882–2885).","boardPrep":"Step 1: liver failure coagulopathy predisposes to easy bruising, severe bleeding, and DIC (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised PT or INR and low platelets in cirrhosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cirrhosis diagnostics).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — complications of alcohol-associated liver disease and MASLD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":3,"stem":"A 23-year-old man who injects drugs has chronic hepatitis C and starts a drug aimed at the viral RNA dependent RNA polymerase. Which viral protein does it target?","choices":{"A":"NS3","B":"NS5A","C":"NS5B","D":"NS4A","E":"Core"},"correct":"C","explanation":"NS5B is the HCV RNA dependent RNA polymerase, and its high error rate creates genotypes and quasispecies. NS3 and NS4A form the protease, NS5A is part of the replication complex, and core is structural. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis C viral proteins, lines 2938–2942).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 80 to 90 percent of HCV infections become chronic, and about 20 percent progress to cirrhosis over 20 to 30 years (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: anti HCV antibodies appear 3 to 6 weeks after exposure, and HCV RNA appears within 1 to 3 weeks (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":3,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman who swam in freshwater lakes in Africa as a child now has portal hypertension and portal fibrosis without cirrhosis. Which infection is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Clonorchiasis","B":"Echinococcosis","C":"Amebic abscess","D":"Schistosomiasis","E":"Hepatic fascioliasis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Schistosoma spreads through freshwater with snail vectors. Adult worms live in mesenteric veins, and eggs lodge in portal venules, causing granulomas, portal fibrosis, and noncirrhotic portal hypertension. Clonorchiasis affects bile ducts, echinococcosis forms cysts, amebiasis forms abscesses, and fascioliasis is not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, schistosomiasis, lines 3007–3011).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists cirrhosis and schistosomiasis as separate intrahepatic causes of portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: First Aid lists schistosomiasis among causes of portal hypertension (First Aid 2025 GI portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — helminth transmission and life cycles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has autoimmune hepatitis, and her biopsy shows a dense lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate. Which cells work with CD4 helper T cells to kill the hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophil granulocytes","B":"Activated mast cells","C":"Resident Kupffer cells","D":"Natural killer cells","E":"CD8 cytotoxic T cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In autoimmune hepatitis, CD4 helper T cells activate B cells to make autoantibodies, while CD8 cytotoxic T cells work with CD4 cells to attack hepatocytes at the interface. Neutrophils, mast cells, Kupffer cells, and NK cells are not the described effectors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, pathogenesis and triggers, lines 3033–3035).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatocyte rosettes are regenerating hepatocytes arranged around dilated canaliculi (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: First Aid notes autoimmune hepatitis may present with fatigue, nausea, or pruritus (First Aid 2025 GI autoimmune hepatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":3,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy is given aspirin for influenza and days later has vomiting, hypoglycemia, and encephalopathy. Which liver finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Macrovesicular steatosis","B":"Microvesicular steatosis","C":"Zone 3 confluent necrosis","D":"Portal interface hepatitis","E":"Pericellular bridging fibrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"High dose aspirin in children causes Reye syndrome, a mitochondrial toxicity with hepatocytes packed with tiny fat droplets around a central nucleus. Macrovesicular fat pushes the nucleus aside, zone 3 necrosis fits acetaminophen, interface hepatitis fits autoimmune disease, and bridging fibrosis is chronic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, microvesicular steatosis, lines 3117–3126).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes Reye syndrome as mitochondrial injury with hyperammonemia, encephalopathy, and cerebral edema (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Reye syndrome). COMLEX: First Aid links Reye syndrome to aspirin given for viral infections such as VZV and influenza (First Aid 2025 GI Reye syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with long standing hepatitis C has a liver biopsy showing fibrous septa that encircle nodules of surviving hepatocytes. What does this pattern define?","choices":{"A":"Cirrhosis","B":"Steatosis","C":"Cholestasis","D":"Hemosiderosis","E":"Amyloidosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chronic persistent injury causes ongoing parenchymal loss and fibrous septa that encircle surviving hepatocytes, forming the regenerative nodules that define cirrhosis. Steatosis is fat, cholestasis is bile buildup, and hemosiderosis and amyloidosis are deposition diseases. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, regeneration versus cirrhosis, lines 2866–2870).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after acute uncomplicated injury or partial hepatectomy, hepatocytes regenerate without scarring (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: if the injury stops, metalloproteinases can partly degrade fibrous bands (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":16,"title":"SDL 16 — Pathology - Steatotic, Inherited, and Cholestatic Liver Diseases","questions":[{"id":"sdl16_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about how much daily alcohol intake for 10 to 20 years is the usual threshold for alcohol associated liver disease?","choices":{"A":"At least 8 g per day","B":"At least 20 g per day","C":"At least 200 g per day","D":"At least 80 g per day","E":"At least 40 g per day"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The lecture gives 80 g or more of ethanol per day for 10 to 20 years as the usual threshold for alcohol associated liver disease, with women more susceptible at lower intake. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, risk factors, lines 3149–3154).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists macrovesicular steatosis, neutrophils, ballooning, and Mallory Denk bodies in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp adds pericellular and sinusoidal fibrosis in advanced alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which proteins make up the Mallory hyaline seen in ballooned hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Keratin 8 and 18","B":"Desmin and vimentin","C":"Actin and myosin","D":"Tau and amyloid beta","E":"Collagen types I and III"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Mallory hyaline, or Mallory Denk bodies, are tangled clumps of the intermediate filaments keratin 8 and 18 in ballooned hepatocytes. Desmin, vimentin, actin, myosin, tau, amyloid beta, and collagen are not its components. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, histologic findings, lines 3163–3171).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp gives an AST to ALT ratio above 1.5 to 1 with values under 500 U/L in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised GGT, bilirubin, and ferritin with leukocytosis in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what AST to ALT ratio is typical of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease?","choices":{"A":"2 or greater","B":"About 1.5","C":"Less than 1","D":"Above 5","E":"Between 3 and 4"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In MASLD the ALT usually exceeds the AST, so the AST to ALT ratio is less than 1. A ratio of 2 or more points to alcohol associated disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, AST to ALT ratio, lines 3176–3181).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp gives MASLD aminotransferases under 500 U/L with an AST to ALT ratio below 1 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists central obesity, type 2 diabetes, hypertension, high triglycerides, and low HDL as MASLD causes (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which HFE mutation is the most frequent cause of hereditary hemochromatosis?","choices":{"A":"H63D","B":"C282Y","C":"G551D","D":"F508del","E":"V600E"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hereditary hemochromatosis is most often an autosomal recessive HFE mutation on chromosome 6, most frequently the C282Y cysteine to tyrosine change. H63D is a less frequent HFE variant, and the others are CFTR and BRAF mutations. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, hereditary hemochromatosis, lines 3185–3190).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists Vibrio vulnificus, Yersinia enterocolitica, and Listeria as infections favored by iron overload (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists therapeutic phlebotomy, deferoxamine, deferasirox, and deferiprone as treatment (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which gene and chromosome are mutated in Wilson disease?","choices":{"A":"HFE on chromosome 6","B":"ATP7A on the X chromosome","C":"SERPINA1 on chromosome 14","D":"HAMP on chromosome 19","E":"ATP7B on chromosome 13"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Wilson disease is an autosomal recessive loss of function mutation in ATP7B on chromosome 13, a hepatocyte copper transporting ATPase. HFE causes hemochromatosis, SERPINA1 causes AAT deficiency, HAMP encodes hepcidin, and ATP7A is a different copper transporter. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, Wilson disease, lines 3213–3217).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists Fanconi syndrome as a complication of Wilson disease (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Wilson disease). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists a low copper diet, penicillamine, trientine, and zinc salts as management (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Wilson disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what circulating AAT level do PiZZ homozygotes have?","choices":{"A":"About 10 percent of normal","B":"About 50 percent of normal","C":"About 30 percent of normal","D":"About 1 percent of normal","E":"About 75 percent of normal"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AAT deficiency is an autosomal codominant SERPINA1 disorder, and PiZZ homozygotes have circulating AAT levels only about 10 percent of normal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, AAT deficiency, lines 3237–3240).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes PAS positive, diastase resistant inclusions in periportal zone 1 hepatocytes in AAT deficiency (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, AAT deficiency). COMLEX: the lecture calls AAT deficiency the most common inherited liver disease diagnosed in infants and children (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what serum bilirubin level is typical of Crigler Najjar type I?","choices":{"A":"Below 3 mg/dL","B":"Above 20 mg/dL","C":"About 5 mg/dL","D":"Below 1 mg/dL","E":"About 10 mg/dL"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Crigler Najjar type I causes severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, typically above 20 mg/dL. Gilbert syndrome is usually below 3 mg/dL, and type II is usually below 20 mg/dL. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Crigler Najjar type I, lines 3258–3266).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes that phototherapy converts unconjugated bilirubin into a water soluble form excreted in urine and bile (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Crigler Najjar syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp states that liver transplant is curative in Crigler Najjar syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Crigler Najjar syndrome).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about how much normal UGT1A1 activity remains in Gilbert syndrome?","choices":{"A":"About 3 percent","B":"About 10 percent","C":"About 30 percent","D":"About 70 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gilbert syndrome reduces UGT1A1 expression to about 30 percent of normal activity, most often from a TATA box promoter mutation. Crigler Najjar type II keeps under 10 percent, and type I has none. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Gilbert syndrome, lines 3275–3283).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp calls Gilbert syndrome the most common inherited hyperbilirubinemia syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Gilbert syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that Gilbert syndrome usually presents in adolescence with a mild course (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Gilbert syndrome).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which transporters are deficient in Rotor syndrome?","choices":{"A":"MRP2 transporter","B":"UGT1A1 enzyme","C":"ATP7B transporter","D":"BSEP transporter","E":"OATP1B1 and 1B3"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Rotor syndrome is an autosomal recessive deficiency of OATP1B1 and OATP1B3, impairing hepatic reuptake and storage of conjugated bilirubin. MRP2 is defective in Dubin Johnson syndrome, and UGT1A1 in Gilbert and Crigler Najjar syndromes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Rotor syndrome, lines 3294–3302).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised total urinary coproporphyrin in Rotor syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Rotor syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that some conjugated bilirubin returns to the sinusoids and is taken up by downstream hepatocytes via OATP1B1 and 1B3 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which laboratory pattern accompanies large bile duct obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Isolated raised AST","B":"Raised ALP and GGT","C":"Raised lipase and amylase","D":"Raised indirect bilirubin","E":"Raised CK and aldolase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Large bile duct obstruction raises serum alkaline phosphatase, GGT, and conjugated bilirubin, with jaundice, pruritus, dark urine, and pale stools. Indirect bilirubin, isolated AST, lipase, and muscle enzymes do not define it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, clinical features, lines 3313–3318).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes choledocholithiasis pain usually lasts more than 4 hours (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, choledocholithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp adds that the pain may radiate to the back or right scapula with nausea and diaphoresis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, choledocholithiasis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which nodule pattern marks biliary cirrhosis after uncorrected choledocholithiasis?","choices":{"A":"Garland shaped nodules","B":"Hobnail surface nodules","C":"Nutmeg mottled pattern","D":"Jigsaw puzzle nodules","E":"Onion skin layering"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Uncorrected choledocholithiasis causes progressive periductal fibrosis and biliary cirrhosis with irregular jigsaw puzzle shaped regenerative nodules. Garland shaped cirrhosis is linked to PBC in the lecture, nutmeg liver is passive congestion, onion skin fibrosis is PSC, and a hobnail surface is not the lecture pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, choledocholithiasis, lines 3320–3324).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture lists surgical or ischemic strictures and PSC as adult causes of large duct obstruction (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture lists pancreatic head adenocarcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma as malignant adult causes (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the morphologic hallmark of ascending cholangitis?","choices":{"A":"Granulomas around ducts","B":"Concentric periductal fibrosis","C":"Plasma cell interface hepatitis","D":"Eosinophilic duct infiltrates","E":"Neutrophils in duct epithelium"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ascending cholangitis shows neutrophilic infiltration within bile duct epithelium and lumens. Granulomatous duct injury fits PBC, concentric fibrosis fits PSC, and plasma cell interface hepatitis fits autoimmune hepatitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, ascending cholangitis, lines 3325–3331).","boardPrep":"Step 1: severe ascending cholangitis can progress to pyogenic liver abscesses and Gram negative sepsis (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture lists acute on chronic liver failure and death as outcomes of severe cases (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what fraction of PBC patients are women?","choices":{"A":"About 50 percent","B":"About 30 percent","C":"About 70 percent","D":"About 90 percent","E":"About 10 percent"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PBC has a striking female predilection, about 90 percent women at a 9 to 1 ratio, peaking at 40 to 50 years. PSC instead is about 70 percent male. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, associated conditions and demographics, lines 3336–3344).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists ursodeoxycholic acid and cholestyramine, then liver transplant, for PBC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists hyperpigmentation, xanthomas, and hepatomegaly in PBC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which antigen do the antimitochondrial antibodies of PBC target?","choices":{"A":"PDC E2 subunit","B":"CYP2D6 enzyme","C":"Thyroid peroxidase","D":"Tissue transglutaminase","E":"Proteinase 3"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AMA, present in 95 percent of PBC, targets the PDC E2 subunit of pyruvate dehydrogenase. CYP2D6 is the LKM1 target, and thyroid peroxidase, tissue transglutaminase, and proteinase 3 belong to other autoimmune diseases. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, characteristic serum antibodies, lines 3358–3363).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists antimitochondrial antibodies with hypergammaglobulinemia in PBC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes a dense lymphocytic portal infiltrate with granulomas on PBC biopsy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the lifetime risk of cholangiocarcinoma in primary sclerosing cholangitis?","choices":{"A":"About 2 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 20 percent","D":"About 80 percent","E":"About 5 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PSC uniquely carries a high lifetime risk of cholangiocarcinoma of about 20 percent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, clinical presentations and risks, lines 3368–3372).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp links PSC to ulcerative colitis, HLA B8, and HLA DR3 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary sclerosing cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists supportive care then liver transplant as PSC management (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary sclerosing cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man who drinks a fifth of whiskey daily has AST 240 U/L (reference 12–38 U/L) and ALT 100 U/L (reference 10–40 U/L), and his CK is normal. Which mechanism best explains why his AST exceeds his ALT?","choices":{"A":"Reduced renal AST clearance","B":"Bile duct leakage of ALT","C":"Selective hepatic ALT degradation","D":"Low hepatic pyridoxal phosphate","E":"Skeletal muscle AST release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In alcohol associated disease the AST to ALT ratio is usually 2 or more because ethanol depletes hepatic pyridoxal 5 phosphate, and because ethanol injures hepatocyte mitochondria. Renal clearance, ductal leak, selective ALT degradation, and muscle release, which a normal CK argues against, are not the lecture mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, AST to ALT ratio, lines 3176–3181).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised PT or INR, low albumin, and low platelets in advanced alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists acute liver failure, cirrhosis, and HCC as complications (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with central obesity and type 2 diabetes who does not drink has hepatomegaly and fatty liver on imaging. Which mechanism best explains the fat in her hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Increased VLDL export","B":"More peripheral lipolysis","C":"Excess NADH from ethanol","D":"Reduced insulin resistance","E":"Copper driven radicals"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In MASLD, insulin resistance increases lipolysis in adipose tissue, raising free fatty acid delivery to the liver, where triglyceride synthesis causes steatosis. VLDL export removes fat, ethanol derived NADH is the alcohol mechanism, and copper radicals fit Wilson disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, pathogenesis, lines 3155–3162).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes triglyceride and free fatty acid buildup causing ROS, mitochondrial dysfunction, and inflammation that progress to MASH (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that MASLD is often asymptomatic apart from hepatomegaly (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with heavy alcohol use has a liver biopsy compared with that of a patient with MASH. Which finding is more prominent in his biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Mainly microvesicular fat","B":"Granulomatous duct injury","C":"Periportal iron granules","D":"Lymphoplasmacytic rosettes","E":"Abundant Mallory hyaline"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Both diseases show steatosis, ballooning, and chicken wire pericellular fibrosis, but Mallory hyaline and neutrophilic infiltrates are more prominent in alcoholic hepatitis than in MASH. Fat is mainly macrovesicular, and the other findings belong to PBC, hemochromatosis, and autoimmune hepatitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, histologic findings, lines 3163–3171).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists jaundice, tender hepatomegaly, anorexia, and fever in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists hepatic encephalopathy in severe alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has diabetes, slate gray skin, cardiomyopathy, and cirrhosis. His transferrin saturation is very high. Which mechanism best explains his disease?","choices":{"A":"Excess dietary heme iron","B":"Defective ceruloplasmin","C":"Blocked biliary iron excretion","D":"Low hepcidin synthesis","E":"Reduced ferroportin activity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In hereditary hemochromatosis, HFE mutations impair hepatic hepcidin synthesis. Low hepcidin leaves ferroportin uninhibited in enterocytes and macrophages, so iron is hyperabsorbed. Reduced ferroportin activity would lower absorption, and ceruloplasmin defects fit Wilson disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, hemochromatosis pathogenesis, lines 3191–3196).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised iron, ferritin, and transferrin saturation with low TIBC in hereditary hemochromatosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists chronic transfusions and chronic liver disease as causes of secondary hemochromatosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old boy has a new tremor, depression, and Coombs negative hemolytic anemia. His serum ceruloplasmin is low. Which eye finding is most likely on slit lamp exam?","choices":{"A":"Kayser Fleischer rings","B":"Cherry red macular spot","C":"Upward lens dislocation","D":"Blue tinted sclerae","E":"Conjunctival Bitot spots"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Wilson disease leaks free copper into the blood, which deposits in Descemet membrane at the corneal limbus as green to brown Kayser Fleischer rings. Cherry red spots, lens dislocation, blue sclerae, and Bitot spots fit other disorders. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, Wilson disease, lines 3223–3236).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists low haptoglobin and raised indirect bilirubin with a negative direct Coombs test in Wilson disease (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Wilson disease). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists low ceruloplasmin, raised 24 hour urine copper, and raised free serum copper (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Wilson disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — clinical features of acute liver failure","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-month-old boy has cholestatic hepatitis. His biopsy shows round cytoplasmic globules in periportal hepatocytes that stay pink after diastase digestion with PAS. Which mechanism best explains his liver injury?","choices":{"A":"Unchecked elastase in liver","B":"Copper buildup in cells","C":"Iron overload of cells","D":"Viral infection of cells","E":"Misfolded protein in ER"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In AAT deficiency the PiZ protein misfolds and is retained in the endoplasmic reticulum, a toxic gain of function that triggers ER stress and hepatocyte death. Unchecked elastase injures the lungs, not the liver. Copper and iron fit Wilson disease and hemochromatosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, AAT deficiency, lines 3241–3254).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp links abnormal AAT in hepatocytes to cell stress, inflammation, cirrhosis, and HCC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, AAT deficiency). COMLEX: the lecture gives a 2 to 3 percent HCC risk in adult AAT deficiency (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old girl has unconjugated bilirubin of 28 mg/dL (reference 0.1–1.0 mg/dL total) that persists despite phototherapy. Her hemoglobin and reticulocyte count are normal, and her direct Coombs test is negative. Which defect is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Absent UGT1A1 activity","B":"Mildly reduced UGT1A1","C":"Defective MRP2 export","D":"Absent OATP1B1 reuptake","E":"Excess heme oxygenase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Profound unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia above 20 mg/dL with hemolysis excluded fits Crigler Najjar type I, a complete loss of UGT1A1 activity that risks kernicterus. Type II has residual activity that phenobarbital can induce. MRP2 and OATP defects raise conjugated bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Crigler Najjar syndromes, lines 3258–3274).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes persistent neonatal jaundice progressing to kernicterus in type I disease (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Crigler Najjar syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes type II is less likely to cause neonatal jaundice (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Crigler Najjar syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman has recurrent mild jaundice with conjugated bilirubin in her urine. Her liver biopsy shows coarse dark pigment within hepatocyte lysosomes. Which hepatocyte transporter is most likely defective?","choices":{"A":"Sinusoidal OATP1B1","B":"Basolateral NTCP","C":"Canalicular MRP2","D":"Canalicular BSEP","E":"Hepatocyte ATP7B"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Benign conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with dark lysosomal pigment and a grossly black liver is Dubin Johnson syndrome, a loss of function of the canalicular MRP2 transporter. OATP1B1 loss causes Rotor syndrome with a normal looking liver, BSEP exports bile salts, NTCP takes up bile salts, and ATP7B defects cause Wilson disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Dubin Johnson syndrome, lines 3284–3302).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists over 50 percent direct bilirubin with a mild course in Dubin Johnson syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Dubin Johnson syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes dark granular pigment in hepatic lysosomes on biopsy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Dubin Johnson syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy notices mild yellow eyes after fasting and exam stress. His indirect bilirubin is 2.4 mg/dL (reference 0.1–1.0 mg/dL total) and other tests are normal. Which genetic change most likely explains this?","choices":{"A":"Coding region frameshift","B":"Complete UGT1A1 deletion","C":"MRP2 nonsense mutation","D":"TATA box promoter mutation","E":"Biallelic OATP1B3 deletion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gilbert syndrome causes mild, fluctuating unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia under 3 mg/dL with stress or fasting, and is most often due to a TATA box promoter mutation that lowers UGT1A1 expression. Complete loss fits Crigler Najjar type I, and MRP2 or OATP changes raise conjugated bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Gilbert syndrome, lines 3275–3283).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes some Gilbert syndrome variants are exceptions to autosomal recessive inheritance (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias). COMLEX: the lecture gives Gilbert syndrome a normal life expectancy (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman has jaundice, itching, tea colored urine, and clay colored stools from a blocked common bile duct. What best explains the color of her stools?","choices":{"A":"Raised fecal urobilinogen","B":"Excess biliverdin in gut","C":"Colonic bile salt deficiency","D":"Bacterial overgrowth in colon","E":"Absent intestinal stercobilin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"With large duct obstruction, conjugated bilirubin fails to reach the gut, so no stercobilin forms and stools turn pale or acholic, while water soluble conjugated bilirubin darkens the urine. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, clinical features, lines 3313–3318).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists jaundice, icterus, pale stools, dark urine, and pruritus in choledocholithiasis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, choledocholithiasis). COMLEX: the lecture explains that pruritus comes from systemic retention of bile salts (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman with a stone in her common bile duct develops epigastric pain radiating to the back and a lipase five times normal. Which complication is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Acute cholecystitis","B":"Acute pancreatitis","C":"Perforated duodenal ulcer","D":"Hepatic abscess","E":"Ascending cholangitis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Impacted ductal stones can cause secondary acute pancreatitis, shown here by back radiating epigastric pain and a high lipase. Cholangitis, abscess, cholecystitis, and ulcer perforation do not explain the lipase. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, choledocholithiasis, lines 3320–3324).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture lists pressure necrosis and biliary colic from impacted ductal stones (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes choledocholithiasis as common bile duct obstruction by a gallstone with severe RUQ or epigastric pain (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, choledocholithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with known gallstones has fever, chills, right upper quadrant pain, and jaundice. Which named sign cluster does she show?","choices":{"A":"Beck triad","B":"Virchow triad","C":"Charcot triad","D":"Whipple triad","E":"Reynolds pentad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fever with chills, right upper quadrant pain, and jaundice form the Charcot triad of ascending cholangitis, usually from enteric coliforms such as E coli, Enterococcus, and Klebsiella. Reynolds pentad adds altered mental status and shock, which she lacks. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, ascending cholangitis, lines 3325–3331); First Aid 2025 GI acute cholangitis (fa_gastro_cache 2267–2268).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture names E coli, Enterococcus, and Klebsiella as the usual organisms of ascending cholangitis (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture describes cholangitis as infection of a stagnant, obstructed biliary tree (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with autoimmune thyroiditis has fatigue, intense itching, and a high ALP. Her antimitochondrial antibody is positive. Which finding is most likely on liver biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Florid duct lesion","B":"Onion skin fibrosis","C":"Interface hepatitis","D":"Bile duct beading","E":"Ground glass cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PBC is T cell mediated destruction of small and medium intrahepatic bile ducts, with the florid duct lesion of granulomatous, lymphoplasmacytic duct destruction. Onion skin fibrosis and beading fit PSC, interface hepatitis fits autoimmune hepatitis (lecture SDL 15, line 3036), and ground glass cells are not a PBC finding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, morphology and duct lesions, lines 3345–3351).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised cholesterol and triglycerides with markedly raised ALP in PBC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp links PBC to autoimmune conditions such as Hashimoto thyroiditis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with ulcerative colitis has a raised ALP on routine labs, and MRCP shows strictures alternating with dilations of his bile ducts. Which antibody is most likely positive?","choices":{"A":"Positive AMA","B":"Positive pANCA","C":"Positive ASMA","D":"Positive anti LKM1","E":"Positive anti dsDNA"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PSC, linked to ulcerative colitis and showing a beaded biliary tree, is pANCA positive in about 65 percent, while AMA is characteristically negative. Anti smooth muscle and anti LKM1 fit autoimmune hepatitis, and anti dsDNA fits lupus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, characteristic serum antibodies, lines 3358–3363).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes concentric periductal onion skin fibrosis on PSC histopathology (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary sclerosing cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that ERCP shows similar findings to MRCP and allows stent placement (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary sclerosing cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with PBC for many years now has high serum cholesterol. Which skin finding is she most likely to develop?","choices":{"A":"Pretibial myxedema","B":"Malar rash","C":"Xanthelasmas","D":"Erythema nodosum","E":"Heliotrope rash"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PBC progresses over years to hypercholesterolemia, xanthelasmas, jaundice, steatorrhea, osteomalacia, and biliary cirrhosis. Pretibial myxedema fits Graves disease, the malar rash fits lupus, erythema nodosum fits sarcoidosis or IBD, and the heliotrope rash fits dermatomyositis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, clinical presentations and risks, lines 3364–3367).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture lists osteomalacia and steatorrhea late in PBC (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture describes PSC as often found by a raised ALP during ulcerative colitis screening (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with obesity, hypertension, and type 2 diabetes has biopsy proven steatohepatitis without alcohol use. Beyond liver failure and cancer, which outcome is his disease additionally tied to?","choices":{"A":"Cardiovascular death","B":"Pulmonary embolism death","C":"Renal failure death","D":"Hemorrhagic stroke death","E":"Sepsis death"},"correct":"A","explanation":"MASLD and MASH progress like alcohol associated disease to fibrosis, cirrhosis, and HCC, and are additionally associated with high cardiovascular mortality from the underlying metabolic syndrome. The lecture ties no other extrahepatic cause of death to MASLD. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, complications, lines 3172–3175).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp names the inflammatory stage of MASLD metabolic dysfunction associated steatohepatitis, or MASH (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised GGT, bilirubin, and ferritin in MASH (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with diabetes, bronze skin, and cirrhosis has a liver biopsy showing golden yellow granules in periportal hepatocytes. Which stain best highlights this pigment?","choices":{"A":"PAS with diastase","B":"Rhodanine copper","C":"Congo red","D":"Oil red O","E":"Prussian blue"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hemosiderin in hereditary hemochromatosis starts in periportal hepatocytes and is highlighted intensely by Prussian blue iron stain, with no inflammation. PAS with diastase marks AAT globules, copper stains fit Wilson disease, Congo red marks amyloid, and oil red O marks fat. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, hemochromatosis morphology, lines 3197–3203).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists MCP 2 and 3 arthropathy and pseudogout in hereditary hemochromatosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists amenorrhea, hypogonadism, and loss of libido from iron deposition (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":3,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl has lifelong unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia near 12 mg/dL (reference 0.1–1.0 mg/dL total) and residual UGT1A1 activity under 10 percent of normal. Which drug most likely lowers her bilirubin?","choices":{"A":"Penicillamine","B":"Cholestyramine","C":"Phenobarbital","D":"Phlebotomy","E":"Ursodeoxycholic acid"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Crigler Najjar type II keeps residual UGT1A1 activity, and its jaundice responds to phenobarbital, which induces the residual enzyme. Penicillamine treats Wilson disease, phlebotomy treats hemochromatosis, and Bootcamp lists cholestyramine and ursodeoxycholic acid as PBC treatment (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis). Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Crigler Najjar type II, lines 3267–3274).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes conjugated bilirubin enters the bile canaliculi through MRP2 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias). COMLEX: the lecture gives type II a milder course with low kernicterus risk (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl has had three episodes of jaundice with right upper quadrant pain over the past year. Examination shows scleral icterus and a palpable right upper quadrant mass. Laboratory studies show a total bilirubin of 4.6 mg/dL (reference range: 0.1–1.0 mg/dL) and direct bilirubin of 3.5 mg/dL (reference range: 0.0–0.3 mg/dL). Ultrasound shows a normal gallbladder and a large fusiform cystic dilation of the common bile duct. Which condition is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Biliary atresia","B":"Choledochal cyst","C":"Cystic fibrosis","D":"Pancreatic pseudocyst","E":"Crigler Najjar syndrome"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The lecture lists extrahepatic biliary atresia, choledochal cysts, and cystic fibrosis as the pediatric causes of large bile duct obstruction. A choledochal cyst is a congenital dilation of the common bile duct that usually presents before age 10 with jaundice, recurrent abdominal pain, and sometimes a mass, and a fusiform dilation of the duct with a normal gallbladder fits it. Biliary atresia presents in the first weeks of life with an absent or irregular gallbladder and progresses rather than coming and going. Cystic fibrosis obstructs ducts with thick secretions and does not form a cystic dilation of the common bile duct. A pancreatic pseudocyst follows pancreatitis and lies beside the pancreas rather than forming the duct wall. Crigler Najjar syndrome raises unconjugated bilirubin without duct dilation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, pediatric etiologies and clinical features, lines 3306–3318); Robbins Ch. 18 (choledochal cyst); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (biliary atresia ultrasound).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists biliary tract obstruction among the causes of conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (First Aid 2025 GI, conjugated hyperbilirubinemia). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that ultrasound in biliary atresia shows an absent or irregularly shaped gallbladder (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary atresia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has fatigue, itching, a high ALP, and a positive antimitochondrial antibody. Which associated autoimmune condition is most common in her disease?","choices":{"A":"Sjögren syndrome","B":"Ulcerative colitis","C":"Crohn disease","D":"Ankylosing spondylitis","E":"Psoriatic arthritis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PBC is linked to Sjögren syndrome in about 70 percent, along with autoimmune thyroiditis, scleroderma, rheumatoid arthritis, Raynaud phenomenon, and celiac disease. Ulcerative colitis goes with PSC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, associated conditions, lines 3336–3341).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture notes that ANAs are present in 40 to 50 percent of PBC (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture states PBC spares large intrahepatic and extrahepatic ducts (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old nonsmoking man has panacinar emphysema and cirrhosis. Both parents carry one abnormal copy of his mutated gene and have intermediate serum levels of its protein. Which term best describes how his alleles are expressed in serum protein levels?","choices":{"A":"Autosomal dominant","B":"X linked dominant","C":"X linked recessive","D":"Autosomal codominant","E":"Maternal mitochondrial"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AAT deficiency is an autosomal codominant SERPINA1 disorder, so each allele contributes protein and heterozygotes have intermediate levels. Robbins calls the disease autosomal recessive because clinical disease needs two Z alleles, but describes allele expression as codominant. Low circulating AAT lets elastase injure the lung, and retained PiZ protein injures the liver. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, AAT deficiency, lines 3237–3246).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp links SERPINA1 mutation to raised elastase activity with lung tissue destruction (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, AAT deficiency). COMLEX: the lecture states PAS positive globules start in periportal hepatocytes (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":17,"title":"SDL 17 — Pathology - Circulatory, Pregnancy-Related, and Neoplastic Liver Diseases","questions":[{"id":"sdl17_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, Budd Chiari syndrome is caused by thrombosis or obstruction of which veins?","choices":{"A":"One minor hepatic vein","B":"A single left hepatic vein","C":"The main portal vein","D":"Two or more major veins","E":"Scattered sublobular veins"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Budd Chiari syndrome is hepatic venous outflow obstruction from thrombosis or obstruction of two or more major hepatic veins. Portal vein thrombosis is a prehepatic problem, and a single small vein does not produce the syndrome. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, Budd Chiari syndrome, lines 3376–3383).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes Budd Chiari syndrome causes congestive liver disease with hepatomegaly, ascites, varices, and abdominal pain but no JVD (First Aid 2025 GI Budd-Chiari syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists Budd Chiari syndrome and cardiac causes as posthepatic causes of portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which disorder is a primary cause of hepatic vein thrombosis?","choices":{"A":"Hemophilia A","B":"Polycythemia vera","C":"Von Willebrand disease","D":"Immune thrombocytopenia","E":"Vitamin K deficiency"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Primary causes of Budd Chiari syndrome include myeloproliferative neoplasms such as polycythemia vera, inherited thrombophilias, antiphospholipid syndrome, PNH, HCC invading hepatic veins, pregnancy, and oral contraceptives. The other choices are bleeding disorders, not thrombotic ones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, Budd Chiari syndrome, lines 3376–3383).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links Budd Chiari syndrome to hypercoagulable states, the postpartum state, and HCC (First Aid 2025 GI Budd-Chiari syndrome). COMLEX: the lecture notes a swollen red purple liver with a tense capsule in Budd Chiari syndrome (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the pericellular centrilobular fibrosis of long standing right heart failure called?","choices":{"A":"Cardiac sclerosis","B":"Bridging necrosis","C":"Hepatoportal sclerosis","D":"Chicken wire fibrosis","E":"Onion skin fibrosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Long standing right heart failure leads to pericellular centrilobular fibrosis termed cardiac sclerosis. Bridging necrosis links lobules in severe hepatitis, hepatoportal sclerosis is a portal venous lesion, chicken wire fibrosis is the perisinusoidal pattern of steatohepatitis, and onion skin fibrosis surrounds ducts in PSC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, passive congestion, lines 3389–3400).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists congestive hepatopathy and nutmeg liver with centrilobular necrosis as posthepatic findings (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists nonselective beta blockers such as carvedilol, nadolol, and propranolol to prevent variceal bleeding (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, eclampsia is preeclampsia plus hyperreflexia and which other feature?","choices":{"A":"Jaundice","B":"Seizures","C":"Hypoglycemia","D":"Hematuria","E":"Hypothermia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Preeclampsia is maternal hypertension, proteinuria, edema, and coagulation defects, and eclampsia is preeclampsia plus hyperreflexia and seizures. Hypoglycemia is a feature of acute fatty liver of pregnancy. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, preeclampsia and eclampsia, lines 3405–3411).","boardPrep":"Step 1: preeclampsia can present as HELLP syndrome, with hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: the lecture explains preeclamptic liver injury by endothelial dysfunction, microvascular thrombosis, and vasospasm (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the characteristic histology of acute fatty liver of pregnancy?","choices":{"A":"Periportal fibrin deposits","B":"Macrovesicular fat with ballooning","C":"Centrilobular hemorrhagic necrosis","D":"Portal lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate","E":"Diffuse microvesicular steatosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"AFLP shows diffuse microvesicular steatosis, with hepatocytes packed with tiny fat droplets around a central nucleus that is not displaced. Periportal fibrin deposits fit preeclampsia, and ballooning with macrovesicular fat fits steatohepatitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, acute fatty liver of pregnancy, lines 3428–3431).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture gives AFLP an incidence of about 1 in 16,000 pregnancies (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: severe AFLP shows lobular disarray, hepatocyte dropout, and cholestasis (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which fetal enzyme defect underlies a subset of acute fatty liver of pregnancy?","choices":{"A":"LCHAD deficiency","B":"MCAD deficiency","C":"G6PD deficiency","D":"HGPRT deficiency","E":"CPT2 deficiency"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In a subset of AFLP cases, homozygous fetal long chain 3 hydroxyacyl CoA dehydrogenase deficiency lets toxic fatty acid intermediates cross into the mother and overwhelm maternal mitochondrial beta oxidation. The other enzyme defects are not the described cause. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, acute fatty liver of pregnancy, lines 3421–3427).","boardPrep":"Step 1: AFLP requires urgent termination of pregnancy (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: AFLP presents late in pregnancy with bleeding, nausea, vomiting, jaundice, hypoglycemia, and encephalopathy (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which immunostaining pattern is pathognomonic of focal nodular hyperplasia?","choices":{"A":"Nuclear beta catenin","B":"Loss of LFABP staining","C":"Map like GS staining","D":"CRP and SAA staining","E":"Membranous CD31 staining"},"correct":"C","explanation":"FNH shows a pathognomonic map like pattern of glutamine synthetase, or GS, staining. Nuclear beta catenin, LFABP loss, and CRP or SAA overexpression mark hepatocellular adenoma subtypes, and CD31 marks endothelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, focal nodular hyperplasia, lines 3444–3450).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid calls FNH the second most common benign liver tumor, mostly in women aged 35 to 50 years (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes FNH becomes isodense to the surrounding liver in the portal venous phase after arterial hyperenhancement (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — risk factors of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which exposure is most strongly associated with hepatocellular adenoma?","choices":{"A":"Vinyl chloride exposure","B":"Chronic hepatitis B","C":"Aflatoxin ingestion","D":"Liver fluke infection","E":"Oral contraceptive use"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hepatocellular adenoma is a benign epithelial neoplasm strongly associated with oral contraceptives, anabolic steroids, and metabolic syndrome or obesity. Vinyl chloride fits angiosarcoma, HBV and aflatoxin fit HCC, and liver flukes fit cholangiocarcinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, hepatocellular adenoma, lines 3453–3456).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes a hepatic adenoma may regress spontaneously or rupture with abdominal pain and shock (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp ties adenoma rupture to a rich arterial supply and lack of stroma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — risk factors of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which hepatocellular adenoma subtype carries a high risk of transformation to HCC?","choices":{"A":"HNF1A inactivated","B":"Inflammatory subtype","C":"Sonic hedgehog subtype","D":"Beta catenin activated","E":"Fibrolamellar subtype"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Beta catenin activated adenomas carry activating CTNNB1 exon 3 mutations, are common in males and anabolic steroid users, and have a high risk of HCC. HNF1A inactivated adenomas have minimal risk, inflammatory adenomas mild risk, and sonic hedgehog adenomas a high bleeding risk. Fibrolamellar is an HCC variant. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, adenoma subtypes, lines 3457–3469).","boardPrep":"Step 1: surgical resection is indicated for adenomas in males, beta catenin mutants, or tumors 5 cm or larger (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: sonic hedgehog adenomas carry GLI1 fusions and a high risk of intraperitoneal hemorrhage (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — risk factors of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which precursor lesions give rise to HCC in cirrhotic livers?","choices":{"A":"Mallory bodies","B":"Dysplastic nodules","C":"Councilman bodies","D":"Hyperplastic polyps","E":"Florid duct lesions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"HCC arises from precursor lesions such as small cell change, large cell change, and dysplastic nodules in cirrhotic livers. Mallory and Councilman bodies are injury findings, florid duct lesions fit PBC, and hyperplastic polyps are colonic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, morphology and precursor lesions, lines 3482–3485).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid calls HCC the most common primary malignant liver tumor in adults and notes hematogenous spread (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists tumor seeding during liver biopsy as an HCC complication (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which gene fusion drives fibrolamellar HCC?","choices":{"A":"TMPRSS2 ERG","B":"KIAA1549 BRAF","C":"DNAJB1 PRKACA","D":"ETV6 NTRK3","E":"RUNX1 RUNX1T1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The fibrolamellar variant occurs in young adults without cirrhosis, is driven by a DNAJB1 PRKACA fusion, and shows oncocytic cells in dense lamellar collagen. The other fusions drive prostate cancer, pilocytic astrocytoma, infantile fibrosarcoma, and acute myeloid leukemia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, fibrolamellar variant, lines 3487–3489).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HCC has a strong propensity for vascular invasion into the portal and hepatic veins (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: HCC grossly may be green from cholestasis or yellow from fat (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which serum tumor marker is raised in advanced HCC?","choices":{"A":"Carcinoembryonic antigen","B":"Cancer antigen 125","C":"Prostate specific antigen","D":"Chromogranin A","E":"Alpha fetoprotein"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Serum alpha fetoprotein is raised in advanced HCC, although it is unhelpful for early screening and negative in fibrolamellar HCC. CEA, CA 125, PSA, and chromogranin A mark other tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, tumor markers, lines 3495–3497).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that HCC recurrence is monitored with serum AFP (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists weight loss, hepatomegaly, and abdominal pain in HCC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which exposure is historically associated with hepatic angiosarcoma?","choices":{"A":"Aflatoxin B1","B":"Liver flukes","C":"Oral contraceptives","D":"Vinyl chloride","E":"Asbestos fibers"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hepatic angiosarcoma is historically associated with occupational vinyl chloride monomer, arsenic, and Thorotrast. Aflatoxin fits HCC, liver flukes fit cholangiocarcinoma, and oral contraceptives fit adenoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, hepatic angiosarcoma, lines 3527–3529).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes hepatic angiosarcoma is often metastatic at diagnosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists PECAM 1, or CD31, positivity in angiosarcoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — associated conditions of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which immunostains are positive in intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma?","choices":{"A":"CK7 and CK19","B":"HepPar1 and AFP","C":"CD31 and CD34","D":"S100 and HMB45","E":"Desmin and actin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma is positive for mucin and CK7 and CK19, and negative for AFP and HepPar1. CD31 and CD34 mark angiosarcoma, S100 and HMB45 mark melanoma, and desmin and actin mark muscle. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, lines 3519–3520).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised CA 19 9 and CEA in cholangiocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholangiocarcinoma). COMLEX: First Aid places the most common cholangiocarcinoma at the convergence of the right and left hepatic ducts (First Aid 2025 GI cholangiocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — associated conditions of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, from which cells does hepatic angiosarcoma arise?","choices":{"A":"Intrahepatic biliary epithelium","B":"Perisinusoidal stellate cells","C":"Sinusoidal endothelial cells","D":"Polygonal hepatocytes","E":"Resident Kupffer macrophages"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hepatic angiosarcoma is a rare, aggressive mesenchymal tumor of sinusoidal endothelial cells that forms hemorrhagic red purple masses. Biliary epithelium gives cholangiocarcinoma, and hepatocytes give HCC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, hepatic angiosarcoma, lines 3521–3526).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiosarcoma cells line and dissect along preexisting sinusoidal spaces (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: angiosarcoma stains for CD34, ERG, and Factor VIII related antigen (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — associated conditions of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with antiphospholipid syndrome has abdominal pain, tender hepatomegaly, and new ascites. Doppler shows thrombi in three major hepatic veins. Which liver finding is most likely on biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Periportal fibrin deposits","B":"Portal egg granulomas","C":"Microvesicular steatosis","D":"Periductal onion skin","E":"Pericentral congestion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Budd Chiari syndrome causes marked pericentral or centrilobular sinusoidal congestion with hepatocellular necrosis, and chronic cases develop pericentral sinusoidal fibrosis. Periportal fibrin fits preeclampsia, egg granulomas fit schistosomiasis, microvesicular fat fits AFLP, and onion skin fibrosis fits PSC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, Budd Chiari morphology, lines 3384–3388).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes Budd Chiari syndrome can produce a mottled nutmeg liver (First Aid 2025 GI Budd-Chiari syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp links posthepatic obstruction to raised sinusoidal hydrostatic pressure (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with long standing right sided heart failure dies, and his liver is examined at autopsy. Which gross appearance is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Chocolate brown liver","B":"Diffusely green liver","C":"Nutmeg mottled liver","D":"Diffusely yellow liver","E":"Jigsaw nodular liver"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Passive congestion from right heart failure gives a nutmeg liver, a mottled red tan pattern of centrilobular congestion alternating with lighter periportal tissue. Chocolate brown fits hemochromatosis, green fits cholestasis, yellow fits steatosis, and jigsaw nodules fit biliary cirrhosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, passive congestion, lines 3389–3398).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in passive congestion, raised central venous pressure is transmitted back through the IVC and hepatic veins (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: centrilobular sinusoids are dilated and filled with red cells (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with severe cardiac decompensation has centrilobular hepatocyte necrosis on biopsy. Which hepatocytes are most likely to remain intact?","choices":{"A":"Zones 1 and 2","B":"Central zone 3","C":"Zones 2 and 3","D":"Zones 1 and 3","E":"Midzonal zone 2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In passive congestion, centrilobular zone 3 hepatocytes undergo ischemic atrophy and necrosis while periportal zone 1 and 2 hepatocytes remain intact. Zone 3 is the necrotic zone, so any choice that includes zone 3 is wrong. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, passive congestion, lines 3395–3398).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists paracentesis with a raised serum ascites albumin gradient in portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists TIPS as a portal hypertension treatment (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman at 36 weeks of gestation has blood pressure of 162/110 mm Hg, proteinuria, edema, and right upper quadrant pain. Her AST is 310 U/L (reference 12–38 U/L). Which liver finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Diffuse microvesicular fat","B":"Pericentral congestion","C":"Portal plasma cell infiltrate","D":"Periportal fibrin deposits","E":"Bridging fibrous septa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In preeclampsia, periportal sinusoids contain fibrin deposits with hemorrhage into the space of Disse, leading to periportal coagulative necrosis. Microvesicular fat fits AFLP, pericentral congestion fits venous outflow problems, plasma cells fit autoimmune hepatitis, and septa fit chronic fibrosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, preeclampsia morphology, lines 3412–3414).","boardPrep":"Step 1: patients with preeclamptic liver injury have raised transaminases and mild jaundice (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: severe coagulopathy marks advanced disease that needs prompt delivery (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman at 37 weeks of gestation with eclampsia suddenly develops severe right upper quadrant pain and hypotension. Which complication is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic vein thrombosis","B":"Ruptured liver hematoma","C":"Acute cholecystitis","D":"Perforated peptic ulcer","E":"Ruptured ectopic pregnancy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In eclampsia, high pressure bleeding forms intrahepatic hematomas that can dissect beneath Glisson capsule into a subcapsular hematoma with a risk of catastrophic rupture. An ectopic pregnancy is not possible at 37 weeks of an intrauterine pregnancy, and in this setting of eclampsia with sudden RUQ pain and hypotension the other choices fit less well than hematoma rupture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, preeclampsia morphology, lines 3414–3417).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture defines HELLP as hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: preeclampsia combines maternal hypertension, proteinuria, edema, and coagulation defects (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman at 35 weeks of gestation has nausea, vomiting, jaundice, and confusion. Her glucose is 45 mg/dL (reference 70–100 mg/dL), and viral serologies are negative. Which mechanism best explains her liver disease?","choices":{"A":"Maternal autoimmune hepatitis","B":"Acute hepatitis A infection","C":"Hepatic microvascular thrombosis","D":"Canalicular bile salt retention","E":"Overwhelmed beta oxidation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acute fatty liver of pregnancy presents in the third trimester with incipient liver failure, hypoglycemia, and encephalopathy. In a subset, fetal LCHAD deficiency sends toxic fatty acid intermediates into the mother, overwhelming maternal mitochondrial beta oxidation. Microvascular thrombosis is the preeclampsia mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, acute fatty liver of pregnancy, lines 3421–3435).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in AFLP, tiny fat droplets surround a central nucleus without displacing it (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: AFLP is a rare, life threatening metabolic emergency of the third trimester (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has a 4 cm liver mass found incidentally on imaging. It is unencapsulated with a central stellate scar and radiating fibrous septa. Which approach is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Urgent surgical resection","B":"Conservative management","C":"Hepatic artery embolization","D":"Systemic chemotherapy","E":"Liver transplantation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A circumscribed unencapsulated nodule with a central stellate scar is focal nodular hyperplasia, a benign hyperplastic lesion with no risk of malignant transformation or spontaneous rupture, so it is usually managed conservatively. Resection, embolization, chemotherapy, and transplant are reserved for lesions such as adenomas 5 cm or larger or malignancy. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, focal nodular hyperplasia, lines 3440–3452).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FNH septa contain thick walled dystrophic arteries with a ductular reaction but no interlobular bile ducts (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: First Aid notes FNH is usually asymptomatic and found incidentally (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — organization of the classic hepatic lobule and hepatic blood flow","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman taking oral contraceptives has a liver adenoma. Its biopsy shows marked steatosis and loss of LFABP expression. Which molecular change is most likely?","choices":{"A":"CTNNB1 exon 3 activation","B":"IL6ST activation","C":"HNF1A inactivation","D":"GLI1 gene fusion","E":"DNAJB1 PRKACA fusion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"HNF1A inactivated adenomas show loss of LFABP expression, marked steatosis, and minimal malignant risk. CTNNB1 mutations define beta catenin adenomas, gp130 activation defines inflammatory adenomas, GLI1 fusions define sonic hedgehog adenomas, and DNAJB1 PRKACA drives fibrolamellar HCC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, adenoma subtypes, lines 3457–3469).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inflammatory adenomas overexpress CRP and SAA and are associated with obesity (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: adenomas are sheets of normal appearing hepatocytes with arterial supply but no portal tracts or bile ducts (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — risk factors of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman who takes oral contraceptives has sudden right upper quadrant pain and hypotension. CT shows hemoperitoneum from a bleeding liver mass. Which lesion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocellular adenoma","B":"Focal nodular hyperplasia","C":"Simple hepatic cyst","D":"Fibrolamellar carcinoma","E":"Amebic liver abscess"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hepatocellular adenoma is linked to oral contraceptives and is prone to spontaneous intraperitoneal hemorrhage or rupture, a surgical emergency. FNH carries no rupture risk, fibrolamellar HCC is malignant without this exposure, a simple cyst does not bleed in this way, and an abscess would bring fever. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, hepatocellular adenoma, lines 3453–3474).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes hepatocellular adenoma carries a raised risk of malignant transformation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses). COMLEX: transformation to HCC is most likely with the beta catenin subtype or a size of 5 cm or more (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — organization of the classic hepatic lobule and hepatic blood flow","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with chronic hepatitis B and cirrhosis has weight loss and a liver mass invading the portal vein. Biopsy shows polygonal cells in thick trabeculae with bile plugs. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Focal nodular hyperplasia","B":"Cavernous hemangioma","C":"Cholangiocarcinoma","D":"Colorectal metastasis","E":"Hepatocellular carcinoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HCC cells resemble hepatocytes growing in thickened trabeculae or pseudoacinar structures with bile plugs, and HCC invades the portal and hepatic veins. Cholangiocarcinoma does not produce bile, FNH and hemangioma are benign, and metastases, though the most common malignant liver tumors, are rarely solitary and would not fit HBV cirrhosis with bile plugs as well. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, morphology, lines 3478–3490).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes aflatoxin B1 forming DNA adducts that cause a TP53 G to T transversion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes metastases are the most common malignant liver tumors overall (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man without liver disease has a large liver mass. His serum tumor markers are unremarkable, and biopsy shows large oncocytic cells in dense lamellar collagen bundles. Which tumor is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocellular adenoma","B":"Fibrolamellar carcinoma","C":"Focal nodular hyperplasia","D":"Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma","E":"Hepatic angiosarcoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fibrolamellar HCC arises in young adults without cirrhosis, is AFP negative, and shows oncocytic cells in dense lamellar collagen. FNH has a central stellate scar rather than lamellar collagen around oncocytic cells, adenoma lacks stroma, cholangiocarcinoma forms glands in desmoplastic stroma, and angiosarcoma is vascular. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, fibrolamellar variant, lines 3487–3497).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes contrast CT or MRI confirms HCC after ultrasound screening (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes HCC on ultrasound as a heterogeneous, hypervascular lesion with irregular borders (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old farmer from rural West Africa without cirrhosis develops HCC. He ate mold contaminated stored peanuts for decades. Which molecular change most likely links this exposure to his tumor?","choices":{"A":"KRAS activating mutation","B":"APC truncating mutation","C":"BRAF V600E mutation","D":"TP53 point mutation","E":"MYCN amplification"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The dietary mycotoxin from Aspergillus on stored crops forms DNA adducts that cause a TP53 inactivating G to T transversion, lowering p53 function. Robbins notes aflatoxin acts synergistically with HBV. KRAS, APC, BRAF, and MYCN changes are not the described route. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, risk factors, lines 3478–3481); Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists HBV with or without cirrhosis, and all other causes of cirrhosis, as HCC associations (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: HCC risk factors include cirrhosis from hemochromatosis and AAT deficiency (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with primary sclerosing cholangitis and ulcerative colitis has a firm gray white sclerotic liver mass. Which finding best separates it from HCC?","choices":{"A":"Mucin positive glands","B":"Bile plugs in tumor","C":"AFP positive cells","D":"HepPar1 positive cells","E":"CD31 positive channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, linked to PSC, forms malignant glands in desmoplastic stroma that are mucin positive and do not make bile. Bile plugs, AFP, and HepPar1 favor HCC, and CD31 marks angiosarcoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, lines 3510–3520).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes cholangiocarcinoma usually presents late with fatigue, weight loss, abdominal pain, and jaundice (First Aid 2025 GI cholangiocarcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists the Courvoisier sign in cholangiocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholangiocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — associated conditions of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man who worked for decades at a PVC plastics plant has an ill defined hemorrhagic red purple liver mass. Which immunostains are most likely positive?","choices":{"A":"Glypican 3 and AFP","B":"CK7 and CK19","C":"CD31 and ERG","D":"Chromogranin and CD56","E":"CD117 and DOG1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vinyl chloride exposure and a hemorrhagic red purple mass point to angiosarcoma, which is positive for the endothelial markers CD31, CD34, ERG, and Factor VIII related antigen. Glypican 3 and AFP fit HCC, CK7 and CK19 fit cholangiocarcinoma, and the others fit neuroendocrine and stromal tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, hepatic angiosarcoma, lines 3521–3531).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links hepatic angiosarcoma to arsenic and vinyl chloride (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists thorium dioxide exposure for angiosarcoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with chronic hepatitis C has a liver mass. Biopsy shows polygonal tumor cells in trabeculae with bile production. Which stain is most likely positive?","choices":{"A":"Mucin positive","B":"ERG positive","C":"Chromogranin positive","D":"HepPar1 positive","E":"Desmin positive"},"correct":"D","explanation":"HCC is positive for AFP, HepPar1, and glypican 3 and negative for mucin. Mucin fits cholangiocarcinoma, ERG fits angiosarcoma, and chromogranin and desmin fit neuroendocrine and muscle tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, HCC diagnostic markers, lines 3502–3509).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists cellular atypia, pleomorphic hyperchromatic nuclei, and a trabecular pattern in HCC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma). COMLEX: the lecture ties HCC to chronic HBV, HCV, cirrhosis, alcohol, MASLD, and aflatoxin (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has had slowly developing hepatic vein obstruction from a hypercoagulable state for several years. Her liver biopsy is reviewed for chronic changes. Which finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Pericentral fibrosis","B":"Periportal fibrin deposits","C":"Concentric periductal fibrosis","D":"Bridging portal fibrosis","E":"Diffuse microvesicular fat"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In chronic or slowly developing Budd Chiari syndrome, pericentral sinusoidal fibrosis develops and the major hepatic veins show organizing thrombi. Periportal fibrin fits preeclampsia, periductal fibrosis fits PSC, bridging portal fibrosis links portal tracts in chronic hepatitis rather than centering on veins, and microvesicular fat fits AFLP. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, Budd Chiari morphology, lines 3384–3388).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists portal vein and splenic vein thrombosis as prehepatic causes of portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: First Aid notes portal vein thrombosis causes no hepatomegaly unless liver disease preexists (First Aid 2025 GI Budd-Chiari syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman at 34 weeks of gestation with preeclampsia has rising transaminases, and her INR climbs to 2.1 (reference 0.8–1.2). Which step is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Plasmapheresis","B":"Liver transplant","C":"Oral corticosteroids","D":"Prompt delivery","E":"Heparin infusion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In preeclamptic liver injury, severe coagulopathy indicates advanced disease requiring prompt delivery or termination of pregnancy. Plasmapheresis, steroids, and transplant do not remove the placental cause, and heparin would worsen bleeding with an INR of 2.1. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, preeclampsia clinical presentation, lines 3418–3420).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in eclampsia, intrahepatic hematomas can dissect beneath Glisson capsule (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: periportal hemorrhage into the space of Disse leads to periportal coagulative necrosis in preeclampsia (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — clinical features of acute liver failure","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has an incidental liver nodule with a central stellate scar and radiating fibrous septa. What underlies the formation of this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Portal vein thrombosis","B":"Clonal CTNNB1 mutation","C":"Chronic HBV infection","D":"Dietary aflatoxin exposure","E":"Aberrant local arteries"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Focal nodular hyperplasia is a nonneoplastic hyperplastic reaction of hepatocytes to local vascular malformations or altered arterial flow. Portal vein thrombosis is a prehepatic inflow obstruction, CTNNB1 mutations fit adenoma, and HBV and aflatoxin fit HCC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, focal nodular hyperplasia, lines 3440–3443).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes FNH as a hyperplastic reaction of hepatocytes to an aberrant dystrophic artery (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes radiating fibrous bands from the central scar containing abnormal vessels (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — organization of the classic hepatic lobule and hepatic blood flow","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with cirrhosis has a liver mass that enhances brightly in the early contrast phase and washes out in the delayed phase on triphasic CT. What explains this enhancement pattern?","choices":{"A":"Portal venous supply","B":"Venous outflow block","C":"Arterial tumor supply","D":"Intratumoral fat content","E":"Central fibrous scar"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Arterial phase hyperenhancement with venous or delayed washout is the hallmark of HCC and reflects a predominant arterial blood supply from tumor neovascularization. The tumor is fed by arteries rather than the portal vein, so contrast arrives early and leaves early, and a central scar fits FNH. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, characteristic image finding, lines 3491–3494).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes multiphase imaging with early arterial hyperenhancement and rapid washout in portal and delayed phases (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma). COMLEX: HCC grossly may be a unifocal mass, multifocal nodules, or a diffusely infiltrative tumor (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with recurrent intrahepatic duct stones has weight loss and dull right upper quadrant pain. CT shows a solitary firm mass in the left hepatic lobe with dilated ducts upstream of it. Needle biopsy shows malignant glands embedded in abundant desmoplastic stroma. Which additional histologic finding is most likely in this tumor?","choices":{"A":"Bile production by tumor cells","B":"Prominent perineural invasion","C":"Tumor cells dissecting sinusoids","D":"Polygonal cells in trabeculae","E":"Ovarian type stroma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hepatolithiasis is a risk factor for intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, a firm, gray-white, sclerotic adenocarcinoma of biliary epithelium with malignant glands in abundant desmoplastic stroma that shows prominent perineural and lymphovascular invasion. Bile production by tumor cells is a feature of hepatocellular carcinoma; cholangiocarcinoma does not produce bile. Malignant endothelial cells dissecting along sinusoids are the pattern of hepatic angiosarcoma. Polygonal cells in trabeculae are the growth pattern of hepatocellular carcinoma. Ovarian-type stroma defines mucinous cystic neoplasms (mostly pancreatic, rarely hepatobiliary), which are cystic lesions rather than an infiltrating desmoplastic adenocarcinoma; Robbins lists hepatobiliary mucinous cystic neoplasms only as a less common precursor of cholangiocarcinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, HCC, and angiosarcoma morphology, lines 3501–3528); First Aid 2025 GI (cholangiocarcinoma histology); Robbins Ch. 18 (mucinous cystic neoplasms as less common precursors, lines 4511–4512).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes cholangiocarcinoma histology as infiltrating neoplastic glands with desmoplastic stroma (First Aid 2025 GI, cholangiocarcinoma). COMLEX: First Aid notes that cholangiocarcinoma usually presents late with fatigue, weight loss, abdominal pain, and jaundice (First Aid 2025 GI, cholangiocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — underlying causes and vectors of bacterial and parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man reviews his medical records with his physician, which list several past liver diagnoses. Which of these places him at risk for hepatocellular carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Hemochromatosis","B":"Gilbert syndrome","C":"Rotor syndrome","D":"Cavernous hemangioma","E":"Focal nodular hyperplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"HCC risk factors include chronic HBV and HCV, cirrhosis of any cause including hereditary hemochromatosis and AAT deficiency, and aflatoxin. Gilbert and Rotor syndromes are benign, FNH has no malignant potential, and First Aid describes hemangioma as a benign venous malformation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, risk factors, lines 3478–3481).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links HCC to Wilson disease and autoimmune disease among the causes of cirrhosis (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp calls HCC the most common primary liver malignancy in adults (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — inherited metabolic liver diseases and their clinical features","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":18,"title":"SDL 18 — Pathology of the Gallbladder","questions":[{"id":"sdl18_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which genetic variants explain a cholesterol gallstone prevalence above 75 percent in Pima, Hopi, and Navajo groups?","choices":{"A":"ABCG8 variants","B":"HFE variants","C":"PKD1 variants","D":"CFTR variants","E":"ATP7B variants"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Native American groups such as the Pima, Hopi, and Navajo exceed 75 percent prevalence of cholesterol gallstones because of ABCG8 transporter variants. HFE and ATP7B variants cause hemochromatosis and Wilson disease (lecture SDL 16), CFTR variants cause cystic fibrosis (lecture SDL 19), and PKD1 variants cause polycystic kidney disease (Robbins Ch 20, library/text/Robbins Patho/Ch.Robbins20.pdf.txt lines 4457–4462). Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, ethnicity and genetics, lines 3544–3547).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists the 8 Fs of gallstones, including fair, feeds (TPN), fasting, and fibrates (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes cholesterol stones are the majority of gallstones and are generally radiolucent (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is cholesterolosis, with foam cells filling the lamina propria, also called?","choices":{"A":"Porcelain gallbladder","B":"Strawberry gallbladder","C":"Hydrops of gallbladder","D":"Courvoisier gallbladder","E":"Phrygian cap gallbladder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cholesterol stones are associated with cholesterolosis, or strawberry gallbladder, in which the lamina propria contains abundant cholesterol laden foam cells. Porcelain gallbladder is wall calcification, and hydrops is a dilated gallbladder of clear mucoid fluid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, cholesterol gallstone morphology, lines 3551–3557).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pure cholesterol stones are pale yellow with a radiating crystalline palisade on cross section (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: fibrate therapy and multiparity are conditions associated with cholesterol stones (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where do brown pigment gallstones form?","choices":{"A":"Sterile gallbladder bile","B":"Main pancreatic duct lumen","C":"Hepatocyte bile canaliculi","D":"Gallbladder mucosal crypts","E":"Infected large bile ducts"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brown pigment stones form in infected large bile ducts and are soft, laminated, and greasy. Black pigment stones form in sterile gallbladder bile. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, black versus brown stones, lines 3567–3577).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp links brown pigment stones to Clonorchis sinensis and E coli infection of the biliary system (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis). COMLEX: First Aid lists black pigment stones as radiopaque and brown pigment stones as radiolucent (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what keeps cholesterol in colloidal solution in bile?","choices":{"A":"Albumin and globulins","B":"Mucin and calcium","C":"Bilirubin and mucin","D":"Bile salts and lecithin","E":"Bicarbonate and chloride"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Water insoluble cholesterol must be incorporated into mixed micelles with bile salts and phospholipids such as lecithin to stay in colloidal solution. Mucin and calcium instead help stones grow. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, solubilization requirement, lines 3583–3585).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists high biliary cholesterol, low bile acids or lecithin, and biliary stasis as general causes of gallstones (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis). COMLEX: supersaturation occurs when hepatocytes hypersecrete cholesterol or bile salt and lecithin secretion drops (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how much of a cholesterol gallstone is crystalline cholesterol monohydrate?","choices":{"A":"Less than 10 percent","B":"Close to 40 percent","C":"Close to 20 percent","D":"Close to 30 percent","E":"More than 50 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cholesterol stones are more than 50 percent crystalline cholesterol monohydrate, with variable calcium salts and a mucin glycoprotein scaffold. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, composition, lines 3596–3597).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid states cholesterol stones make up about 80 percent of stones (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes cholesterol stones as mixed, with more cholesterol than calcium (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of conjugated bilirubin in bile undergoes deconjugation?","choices":{"A":"About 25 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 1 percent","D":"About 10 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Although most bilirubin in bile is conjugated, about 1 percent undergoes spontaneous or enzymatic deconjugation. Chronic hypersecretion of conjugated bilirubin therefore yields high absolute levels of free unconjugated bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, deconjugation, lines 3604–3607).","boardPrep":"Step 1: unconjugated bilirubin binds free calcium in sterile bile to form insoluble calcium bilirubinate (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp states black pigment stones consist mainly of calcium bilirubinate (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what share of acute cholecystitis cases are calculous?","choices":{"A":"About 50 percent","B":"About 10 percent","C":"About 30 percent","D":"About 90 percent","E":"About 60 percent"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute calculous cholecystitis accounts for about 90 percent of cases and follows obstruction of the gallbladder neck or cystic duct by an impacted stone. Acalculous disease accounts for about 10 percent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute calculous cholecystitis, lines 3618–3621).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid calls cholecystitis the most common complication of gallstones (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists IV antibiotics and laparoscopic cholecystectomy for acute cholecystitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, into which toxic product do mucosal phospholipases convert luminal lecithin in acute calculous cholecystitis?","choices":{"A":"Sphingomyelin","B":"Lysolecithin","C":"Phosphatidylserine","D":"Cholesteryl ester","E":"Ceramide"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Mucosal phospholipases hydrolyze luminal lecithin into toxic lysolecithin, which breaks down the protective mucus and exposes the epithelium to bile salt detergent action. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute calculous cholecystitis, lines 3621–3623).","boardPrep":"Step 1: distention compresses mucosal blood flow and causes ischemia with prostaglandin mediated inflammation (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: E coli and Enterococcus may superinfect the inflamed gallbladder (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the primary driver of acute acalculous cholecystitis?","choices":{"A":"Impacted gallstone","B":"Clonorchis infestation","C":"Cystic artery ischemia","D":"Sphincter of Oddi spasm","E":"Biliary atresia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Acalculous cholecystitis is driven mainly by ischemia of the cystic artery, an end artery without collaterals, compounded by stasis, viscous bile, and sludge. An impacted stone defines calculous disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute acalculous cholecystitis, lines 3637–3642).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes acalculous cholecystitis is seen in critically ill patients and can follow CMV infection (First Aid 2025 GI cholecystitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp attributes acalculous cholecystitis to reduced perfusion, biliary stasis, and infection (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what are Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses in chronic cholecystitis?","choices":{"A":"Intramural calcification","B":"Foam cell aggregates","C":"Subserosal hemorrhages","D":"Dilated lymphatics","E":"Mucosal outpouchings"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses are deep mucosal outpouchings that herniate through the muscularis externa and often trap bile or microcrystals. Intramural calcification is porcelain gallbladder, and foam cells fit cholesterolosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, morphology and variants, lines 3664–3666).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic cholecystitis shows lymphocytes, plasma cells, and macrophages with variable mural fibrosis (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: gallstones are present in more than 90 percent of chronic cholecystitis (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — etiologies, clinical features, and complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what defines porcelain gallbladder?","choices":{"A":"Dystrophic calcification","B":"Foamy macrophage infiltrate","C":"Dense hyaline fibrosis","D":"Clear mucoid distention","E":"Transmural wall necrosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Porcelain gallbladder is a rare form of chronic cholecystitis with extensive dystrophic intramural calcification of the wall. Foamy macrophages fit xanthogranulomatous disease, hyaline fibrosis fits hyalinizing cholecystitis, and mucoid distention is hydrops. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, porcelain gallbladder, lines 3667–3668).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes porcelain gallbladder is usually found incidentally on imaging (First Aid 2025 GI porcelain gallbladder). COMLEX: First Aid generally recommends prophylactic cholecystectomy because of cancer risk (First Aid 2025 GI porcelain gallbladder).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — etiologies, clinical features, and complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where does gallstone ileus usually obstruct the bowel?","choices":{"A":"Ligament of Treitz","B":"Sigmoid colon","C":"Splenic flexure","D":"Ileocecal valve","E":"Rectosigmoid junction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A large stone eroding through a cholecystenteric fistula can cause gallstone ileus, with obstruction at the ileocecal valve. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, complications, lines 3684–3686).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes the Rigler triad of pneumobilia, small bowel obstruction, and an ectopic gallstone (First Aid 2025 GI gallstone ileus). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists large gallstones, acute cholecystitis, and malignancy as fistula risk factors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallstone ileus).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — etiologies, clinical features, and complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, in about what share of gallbladder carcinoma cases are gallstones present?","choices":{"A":"About 50 percent","B":"About 25 percent","C":"About 95 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 10 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gallstones are present in about 95 percent of gallbladder carcinomas and are the single most important risk factor. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, risk factors, lines 3691–3693).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised CA 19 9 and CEA in gallbladder adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallbladder adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists the Courvoisier sign in gallbladder adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallbladder adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which lesion is a precursor of gallbladder carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Hyperplastic polyp","B":"Flat dysplasia","C":"Adenomyomatosis","D":"Cholesterol polyp","E":"Rokitansky sinus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gallbladder carcinoma progresses through flat dysplasia or in situ lesions, intracholecystic papillary tubular neoplasms, and intestinal metaplasia. The other choices are not listed precursors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, precursor lesions, lines 3704–3705).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic mucosal irritation drives persistent epithelial injury and regeneration in gallbladder carcinoma (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: gain of function mutations in EGF receptor family members, including HER2, occur in gallbladder carcinoma (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the 5 year survival of gallbladder carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Under 10 percent","B":"Above 30 percent","C":"Above 50 percent","D":"Above 70 percent","E":"Above 90 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gallbladder carcinoma has an extremely poor prognosis, with most tumors found at an advanced, unresectable stage and a 5 year survival under 10 percent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, prognosis, lines 3721–3723).","boardPrep":"Step 1: preoperative diagnosis of gallbladder carcinoma occurs in under 20 percent of cases (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: the tumor appears as an exophytic papillary mass or diffuse firm thickening of the fundic wall (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with obesity, three prior pregnancies, and oral contraceptive use has gallstones on ultrasound that do not show on a plain radiograph. What are her stones most likely made of?","choices":{"A":"Calcium bilirubinate polymer","B":"Calcium carbonate salts","C":"Calcium oxalate crystals","D":"Cholesterol monohydrate","E":"Uric acid crystals"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Female sex hormones, multiparity, oral contraceptives, and obesity raise cholesterol secretion into bile, and cholesterol stones are typically radiolucent, with only 10 to 20 percent opaque. Calcium bilirubinate stones are black pigment stones, about half or more of which are radiopaque. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, cholesterol gallstones, lines 3535–3557).","boardPrep":"Step 1: estrogen raises hepatic LDL receptor expression and HMG CoA reductase activity (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: biliary cholesterol saturation increases progressively with age (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy with hereditary spherocytosis has small, friable, dark, spiculated stones in his gallbladder. Which stone type is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Brown pigment stones","B":"Black pigment stones","C":"Cholesterol stones","D":"Calcium carbonate stones","E":"Calcium oxalate stones"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic hemolytic anemias such as hereditary spherocytosis and sickle cell anemia cause black pigment stones, which are small, multiple, friable, and dark with molded or spiculated contours. Brown stones form in infected ducts. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, pigment gallstones, lines 3558–3573).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mechanical prosthetic heart valves are also associated with pigment stones (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists chronic hemolysis and liver disease as risk factors for black pigment stones (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man from rural Southeast Asia with Clonorchis infection has soft, laminated, greasy stones in his large bile ducts. What are these stones mainly composed of?","choices":{"A":"Pure cholesterol monohydrate","B":"Calcium phosphate and mucin gel","C":"Calcium oxalate and uric acid","D":"Cystine and xanthine crystals","E":"Calcium soaps and bilirubinate"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brown pigment stones form in infected large ducts and are made of calcium salts of unconjugated bilirubin, cholesterol, and calcium soaps of palmitate and stearate. They are characteristically radiolucent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, brown pigment stones, lines 3574–3577).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp states brown pigment stones usually form outside the gallbladder (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis). COMLEX: pigment stones are more common in rural populations and non Western countries (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — underlying causes and vectors of bacterial and parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman with sickle cell anemia forms black gallstones. Her hepatic bilirubin conjugation is normal. Why does free unconjugated bilirubin rise in her bile?","choices":{"A":"Absent hepatic conjugation","B":"Bacterial infection of the bile","C":"Lower biliary calcium levels","D":"Deconjugation of excess output","E":"Cholesterol supersaturated bile"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In chronic hemolysis the liver secretes excessive conjugated bilirubin into bile. About 1 percent of it deconjugates, so chronic hypersecretion yields high absolute levels of free unconjugated bilirubin that binds calcium. Infection drives brown stones, and supersaturation drives cholesterol stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, pathogenesis of black pigment stones, lines 3598–3610).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links pigment stones to Crohn disease, chronic hemolysis, alcoholic cirrhosis, and TPN (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: black stones contain calcium bilirubinate, carbonate, phosphate, mucin, and little cholesterol (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman loses 30 kg in the 6 months after bariatric surgery and develops gallstones. Which change most directly lets cholesterol crystals accumulate into stones?","choices":{"A":"Biliary bacterial infection","B":"Excess bilirubin secretion","C":"Gallbladder hypomotility","D":"Low cholesterol secretion","E":"Increased lecithin output"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Rapid weight loss and gallbladder stasis are major risk factors. Supersaturated cholesterol precipitates as monohydrate crystals, and gallbladder hypomotility or stasis lets the crystals accumulate. More lecithin and less cholesterol would protect against stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1 and 2, risk factors and nucleation and stasis, lines 3547, 3589–3592).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists fasting or rapid weight loss among the 8 Fs of gallstones (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists biliary stasis among the general causes of gallstones (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with obesity has cholesterol supersaturated bile containing crystals. What lets the nucleated crystals grow into macroscopic stones?","choices":{"A":"Mucin glycoprotein gel","B":"Calcium phosphate shell","C":"Fibrin clot matrix","D":"Collagen fiber scaffold","E":"Bacterial biofilm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Epithelial hypersecretion of mucin glycoproteins forms a gel matrix that traps nucleated crystals and allows progressive accretion into macroscopic stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, mucus secretion and accretion, lines 3593–3595).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol is water insoluble and needs mixed micelles with bile salts and lecithin (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists malabsorption syndromes among the classic risk factors for cholesterol stones (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has 8 hours of right upper quadrant pain, fever, and inspiratory arrest on palpation. Ultrasound shows a stone in the gallbladder neck. Which histologic change is most likely early on?","choices":{"A":"Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses","B":"Edema and neutrophils","C":"Dense hyaline fibrosis","D":"Foamy xanthoma cells","E":"Dystrophic wall calcium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Early acute calculous cholecystitis shows edema, congestion, mucosal erosions, and early neutrophilic infiltration. Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses, hyaline fibrosis, xanthoma cells, and calcification are features of chronic disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3 and 4, acute calculous morphology and chronic variants, lines 3629–3636, 3664–3674).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes the Murphy sign as inspiratory arrest on RUQ palpation, with pain that may radiate to the right shoulder (First Aid 2025 GI cholecystitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists gallbladder wall thickening, edema, and pericholecystic fluid on ultrasound (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man in the ICU after severe burns develops fever and right upper quadrant tenderness. Ultrasound shows a distended gallbladder with sludge and no stones. Which outcome is more frequent in his condition than in the stone related form?","choices":{"A":"Porcelain wall calcification","B":"Common duct stone impaction","C":"Gallbladder adenocarcinoma","D":"Cholecystenteric fistula ileus","E":"Gangrene and perforation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acalculous cholecystitis in critically ill patients is recognized late, so gangrene and perforation occur at a significantly higher frequency than in calculous disease. The other outcomes need stones or long standing chronic disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute acalculous cholecystitis, lines 3643–3650).","boardPrep":"Step 1: risk factors for acalculous disease include sepsis with hypotension, trauma, burns, cardiac surgery, diabetes, and vasculitis (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised CRP and leukocytosis in acute cholecystitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with diabetes has acute gallbladder inflammation, and CT shows gas within the gallbladder wall. Which form of the disease is this?","choices":{"A":"Emphysematous cholecystitis","B":"Gangrenous cholecystitis","C":"Gallbladder empyema","D":"Xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis","E":"Hyalinizing cholecystitis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Severe acute cholecystitis can show gas forming bacterial infection, called emphysematous cholecystitis. Gangrenous disease is transmural necrosis, empyema is a gallbladder full of pus, and xanthogranulomatous and hyalinizing forms are chronic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute calculous morphology, lines 3629–3636).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes cholecystitis pain usually lasts more than 4 hours with localized guarding (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp states a HIDA scan is positive when the gallbladder is not seen on delayed or morphine augmented imaging (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman with long standing gallstones has a porcelain gallbladder on imaging. Which malignancy of that organ is she at greatest risk of?","choices":{"A":"Angiosarcoma","B":"MALT lymphoma","C":"Carcinoid tumor","D":"Hepatoblastoma","E":"Adenocarcinoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Chronic cholecystitis carries a markedly increased risk of gallbladder adenocarcinoma, particularly with porcelain gallbladder, hyalinizing cholecystitis, or long standing stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, complications, lines 3677–3680).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp traces chronic cholecystitis with porcelain gallbladder to gallbladder adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis). COMLEX: First Aid notes the cancer risk is mostly adenocarcinoma (First Aid 2025 GI porcelain gallbladder).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with recurrent cholecystitis has vomiting and abdominal distention. Radiographs show air in the biliary tree, dilated small bowel, and an ectopic calcified stone in the bowel. Which mechanism explains her presentation?","choices":{"A":"Emphysematous infection","B":"Choledochal cyst rupture","C":"Cholecystenteric fistula","D":"Duodenal ulcer perforation","E":"Mesenteric ischemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A large stone can erode from the gallbladder into the duodenum or colon through a cholecystenteric fistula, letting air and bacteria into the biliary tree and causing gallstone ileus. Emphysematous infection is a gas forming infection of the gallbladder itself, not a stone in the bowel. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, biliary enteric fistula, lines 3684–3686).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes gallstone ileus raises the risk of acute cholangitis and often needs surgery (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallstone ileus). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes episodic small bowel obstruction with intermittent diffuse pain in gallstone ileus (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallstone ileus).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with chronic cholecystitis has a shrunken, nodular, markedly thickened gallbladder wall filled with lipid laden foamy macrophages. What process most likely produced these cells?","choices":{"A":"Dystrophic wall calcification","B":"Cystic artery thrombosis","C":"Clostridial gas infection","D":"Ruptured Rokitansky sinuses","E":"Hemolytic bile pigment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis forms when ruptured Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses spill bile into the wall, drawing abundant foamy, lipid laden xanthoma cells. Calcification makes porcelain gallbladder, and gas infection is emphysematous disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis, lines 3671–3674).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyalinizing cholecystitis is dense, uniform fibrous replacement of the wall with little epithelium (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: hydrops is a chronically obstructed, dilated gallbladder of clear mucoid fluid (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with long standing gallstones has right upper quadrant pain, weight loss, and a firm thickened fundic wall. She then becomes jaundiced. What does her jaundice most likely indicate?","choices":{"A":"Early localized disease","B":"Porta hepatis invasion","C":"Benign stone passage","D":"Paraneoplastic hemolysis","E":"Drug induced cholestasis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In gallbladder carcinoma, jaundice indicates direct invasion or metastasis into the porta hepatis or common bile duct, an advanced stage. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, jaundice and advanced stage, lines 3716–3717).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder carcinoma has a female to male ratio above 2 to 1 and high incidence in Native American, Hispanic, Chilean, Bolivian, and Northern Indian groups (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists PSC and Clonorchis infection as gallbladder adenocarcinoma risk factors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallbladder adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman has a resected gallbladder carcinoma with marked aneuploidy. Which tumor suppressor change is found in about half of these tumors?","choices":{"A":"TP53 loss of function","B":"APC loss of function","C":"VHL loss of function","D":"SMAD4 loss of function","E":"BRCA1 loss of function"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loss of function TP53 mutations occur in about 50 percent of gallbladder carcinomas and cause marked aneuploidy, alongside gain of function changes in EGF receptor family members and RAS signaling. APC, VHL, SMAD4, and BRCA1 are not among the drivers the lecture lists for this tumor. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, molecular driver alterations, lines 3706–3710).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic S typhi carriage and parasitic infection are chronic inflammatory risk factors (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: intracholecystic papillary tubular neoplasms are precursor lesions (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with symptoms like chronic cholecystitis is found to have gallbladder carcinoma with direct liver invasion. What most explains the poor outlook of this cancer?","choices":{"A":"Chemotherapy induced toxicity","B":"Frequent bone metastasis","C":"Advanced stage at diagnosis","D":"Hormone driven growth","E":"Early brain metastasis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Symptoms mimic gallstones or chronic cholecystitis, so most tumors present at an advanced, unresectable stage with liver invasion, peritoneal spread, or nodal metastasis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, clinical presentation and prognosis, lines 3711–3723).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes gallbladder adenocarcinoma is initially asymptomatic before an RUQ mass and pain appear (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallbladder adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: right upper quadrant pain, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and weight loss are the presenting symptoms (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has cholesterol gallstones on ultrasound, but her plain abdominal radiograph looks normal. About what share of cholesterol stones contain enough calcium to be radiopaque?","choices":{"A":"10 to 20 percent","B":"50 to 75 percent","C":"90 to 95 percent","D":"1 to 2 percent","E":"30 to 40 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cholesterol stones are typically radiolucent, and only 10 to 20 percent contain enough calcium carbonate to be radiopaque. About 50 to 75 percent of black pigment stones are radiopaque. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, cholesterol and black stone morphology, lines 3551–3573).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists Crohn disease, advanced age, estrogen therapy, and rapid weight loss among cholesterol stone associations (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists progesterone excess and malabsorption among classic cholesterol stone risk factors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old man with Crohn disease had his terminal ileum resected and now has black gallstones. How does his ileal disease promote these stones?","choices":{"A":"Excess cholesterol absorption","B":"Biliary bacterial growth","C":"Reduced bilirubin output","D":"Lost bile salt reabsorption","E":"Increased lecithin output"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In ileal disease, impaired bile salt reabsorption leads the liver to secrete excess conjugated bilirubin into bile, and the deconjugated fraction precipitates with calcium as black stones. Bacterial growth drives brown stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, hemolysis and ileal disease, lines 3601–3603).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ileal resection or bypass is an associated condition of pigment stones (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: First Aid lists Crohn disease among pigment stone associations (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with acute calculous cholecystitis develops spiking fevers from bacterial superinfection of her gallbladder. Which organism is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Streptococcus pyogenes","B":"Escherichia coli","C":"Staphylococcus aureus","D":"Candida albicans","E":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bacterial superinfection of acute calculous cholecystitis is usually from enteric organisms such as E coli and Enterococcus. The other organisms are not the described causes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute calculous cholecystitis, lines 3624–3626).","boardPrep":"Step 1: if the gallbladder is filled with pure pus, the condition is termed empyema (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists fever, malaise, and fatigue in acute cholecystitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman has a palpable, nontender, distended gallbladder from a long standing obstruction. At surgery it is filled with clear mucoid fluid. Which condition is this?","choices":{"A":"Hydrops of gallbladder","B":"Gallbladder empyema","C":"Porcelain gallbladder","D":"Strawberry gallbladder","E":"Emphysematous gallbladder"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hydrops is a chronically obstructed, dilated gallbladder filled with clear mucoid fluid. Empyema is filled with pus, porcelain gallbladder is calcified, strawberry gallbladder is cholesterolosis, and emphysematous disease has gas. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, hydrops of the gallbladder, lines 3675–3676).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic cholecystitis can be complicated by pericholecystic abscess or rupture with peritonitis (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: chronic cholecystitis can lead to ascending cholangitis and sepsis (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman from Bolivia has a gallbladder carcinoma resected after 20 years of gallstones. Her oncologist orders tumor profiling to look for a targetable driver. Which alteration is most likely to be found?","choices":{"A":"APC tumor suppressor loss","B":"VHL tumor suppressor loss","C":"EGFR family activation","D":"BCR ABL kinase fusion","E":"JAK2 V617F activation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The lecture lists gain of function mutations in EGF receptor family members, including HER2, and in downstream RAS signaling, alongside TP53 loss, as the molecular drivers of gallbladder carcinoma. APC loss is the classic colorectal driver, VHL loss drives clear cell renal carcinoma, BCR ABL defines CML, and JAK2 V617F drives myeloproliferative neoplasms; none is among the drivers the lecture lists. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, molecular driver alterations, lines 3706–3710).","boardPrep":"Step 1: preoperative diagnosis of gallbladder carcinoma occurs in less than 20 percent of cases (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes gallbladder adenocarcinoma arises from glandular tissue of the gallbladder (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallbladder adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with acute cholecystitis worsens, and at surgery his gallbladder lumen is filled entirely with pus. What is this complication called?","choices":{"A":"Gallbladder hydrops","B":"Porcelain gallbladder","C":"Gangrenous cholecystitis","D":"Biliary sludge","E":"Gallbladder empyema"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When the lumen of an acutely inflamed gallbladder fills with pure pus, it is termed gallbladder empyema. Gangrenous disease is transmural necrosis, hydrops holds clear mucoid fluid, and porcelain gallbladder is calcified. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute calculous morphology, lines 3629–3632).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the inflamed gallbladder is tense and red to green black from subserosal hemorrhage (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes a sonographic Murphy sign in acute cholecystitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":19,"title":"SDL 19 — Pathology of the Pancreas and Pancreatic Neuroendocrine Tumors","questions":[{"id":"sdl19_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the incidence of pancreas divisum, the most common congenital pancreatic anomaly?","choices":{"A":"20 to 30 percent","B":"40 to 50 percent","C":"3 to 10 percent","D":"0.1 to 0.5 percent","E":"60 to 70 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pancreas divisum is the most common congenital pancreatic anomaly, with an incidence of 3 to 10 percent, and about 95 percent of affected people have no symptoms. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, pancreas divisum, lines 3726–3738).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes the ventral and dorsal parts fail to fuse at 7 weeks in pancreas divisum (First Aid 2025 GI pancreas embryology). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists a raised risk of pancreatitis and abdominal pain in pancreas divisum (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreas divisum).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, through which route does the bulk of the gland drain in pancreas divisum?","choices":{"A":"Major papilla via Wirsung","B":"Ampulla via common bile duct","C":"Minor papilla via Wirsung","D":"Major papilla via Santorini","E":"Minor papilla via Santorini"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In pancreas divisum the dorsal and ventral duct systems fail to fuse, so the bulk of the gland, from the dorsal primordium, drains through the small minor papilla via the duct of Santorini. The duct of Wirsung drains only part of the head through the major papilla. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, pancreas divisum, lines 3726–3734).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 5 percent of people with pancreas divisum develop recurrent acute or chronic pancreatitis (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: the fetal duct systems normally fuse between the 5th and 8th gestational weeks (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, in about what share of postmortem examinations is ectopic pancreatic tissue found?","choices":{"A":"About 0.1 percent","B":"About 2 percent","C":"About 15 percent","D":"About 30 percent","E":"About 50 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ectopic pancreas, aberrantly situated pancreatic tissue derived from gut endoderm, is present in about 2 percent of postmortem examinations, usually as an incidental subepithelial rest. About 15, 30, and 50 percent overstate how often it is found, and 0.1 percent understates it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, ectopic pancreas, lines 3749–3758); Robbins Ch. 19 (ectopic pancreas in about 2 percent of careful routine postmortem examinations).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins notes ectopic rests may cause pain from localized inflammation or incite mucosal bleeding (Robbins Ch. 19). COMLEX: ectopic pancreatic nests are usually incidental subepithelial findings (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which event initiates acute pancreatitis?","choices":{"A":"Islet beta cell destruction","B":"Stellate cell collagen deposition","C":"IgG4 plasma cell infiltration","D":"Trypsin activation in gland","E":"Biliary epithelial apoptosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute pancreatitis begins with inappropriate intrapancreatic activation of trypsinogen to trypsin, which activates other zymogens and drives autodigestion and fat necrosis. Stellate cell collagen deposition drives chronic pancreatitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, pathogenesis, lines 3778–3782).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists didanosine, stavudine, furosemide, hydrochlorothiazide, glucocorticoids, and GLP 1 agonists among drug causes of acute pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute pancreatitis). COMLEX: First Aid gives the I GET SMASHED mnemonic for causes of acute pancreatitis (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which serum marker is the most sensitive and specific for acute pancreatitis?","choices":{"A":"Serum lipase","B":"Serum amylase","C":"Serum ALT","D":"Serum calcium","E":"Serum CRP"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Serum lipase is the most sensitive and specific marker. It rises within 4 to 12 hours and stays raised for 8 to 14 days, whereas amylase rises quickly but returns to baseline within 3 to 5 days. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, serum markers, lines 3804–3808).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid diagnoses acute pancreatitis by 2 of 3 criteria, including enzymes at 3 times the upper limit of normal (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised ESR, CRP, and LDH in pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which cells deposit collagen in chronic pancreatitis after activation by TGF beta and PDGF?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic islet alpha cells","B":"Centroacinar duct cells","C":"Pancreatic stellate cells","D":"Resident tissue mast cells","E":"Ductal epithelial cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Repeated acinar injury stimulates local TGF beta and PDGF, which activate pancreatic stellate cells, the periacinar myofibroblasts, to deposit collagen and cause progressive fibrosis and acinar loss. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, pathogenesis, lines 3782–3785).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists dystrophic calcifications and parenchymal atrophy in chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists low fecal elastase 1 in chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what makes up the wall of a pancreatic pseudocyst?","choices":{"A":"Fibrous granulation tissue","B":"Glycogen rich cuboidal cells","C":"Tall mucinous columnar cells","D":"Dense ovarian type stroma","E":"Papillary ductal epithelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A pseudocyst lacks a true epithelial lining. Its wall is fibrous tissue, granulation tissue, and necrotic debris with saponified fat. Glycogen rich cuboidal cells line serous cystadenomas, and mucinous epithelium with ovarian stroma lines MCNs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, pseudocysts, lines 3826–3829).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pseudocysts account for about 75 percent of all pancreatic cysts (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes a pseudocyst as a fibrous capsule surrounding pancreatic fluid (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic pseudocyst).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which gene and chromosome are tied to serous cystic neoplasms?","choices":{"A":"KRAS on 12p","B":"VHL on 3p","C":"GNAS on 20q","D":"RNF43 on 17q","E":"SMAD4 on 18q"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Serous cystadenomas are strongly associated with loss of function of the VHL tumor suppressor on chromosome 3p. KRAS and GNAS mark mucinous lesions, and SMAD4 marks invasive progression. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, serous cystic neoplasms, lines 3841–3845).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serous cystadenomas occur twice as often in females, peak in the 6th to 7th decades, and favor the tail (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: serous cystadenomas carry virtually no risk of malignant transformation (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which feature is pathognomonic of a mucinous cystic neoplasm?","choices":{"A":"Glycogen rich lining","B":"Main duct papillae","C":"Granulation tissue wall","D":"Honeycomb microcysts","E":"Ovarian type stroma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MCNs are lined by tall mucin producing columnar epithelium backed by a pathognomonic dense ovarian type stroma that expresses estrogen and progesterone receptors and inhibin. Main duct papillae fit IPMN, and honeycomb microcysts with glycogen rich cells fit serous cystadenoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, mucinous cystic neoplasms, lines 3849–3853).","boardPrep":"Step 1: MCNs are driven by KRAS mutations in about 50 percent and RNF43 loss in about 33 percent (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: up to one third of MCNs harbor an invasive adenocarcinoma (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinomas arise in the head?","choices":{"A":"About 15 percent","B":"About 60 percent","C":"About 5 percent","D":"About 30 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"About 60 percent of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinomas arise in the head, about 15 percent in the body, and about 5 percent in the tail. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, location dependent presentation, lines 3878–3884).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes pancreatic adenocarcinoma is often metastatic at presentation, with average survival of about 1 year (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: First Aid names tobacco smoking the strongest risk factor (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — clinical features of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which alteration is the earliest driver in low grade PanIN 1 lesions?","choices":{"A":"TP53 inactivation","B":"SMAD4 inactivation","C":"CDKN2A inactivation","D":"KRAS activation","E":"BRCA2 inactivation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Activating KRAS point mutations on 12p occur in more than 90 percent of cases and are the earliest driver in PanIN 1. CDKN2A loss appears at PanIN 2, TP53 and SMAD4 loss at PanIN 3, and BRCA2 is a germline predisposition gene. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, key mutations, lines 3894–3907).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CDKN2A is inactivated in about 30 percent by mutation and up to 90 percent with promoter hypermethylation (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: First Aid names a KRAS activating mutation as the most common genomic abnormality (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what causes the Trousseau sign in pancreatic cancer?","choices":{"A":"Tumor procoagulant mucins","B":"Platelet antibody consumption","C":"Antithrombin gene mutation","D":"Factor V Leiden variant","E":"Estrogen driven clotting"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Trousseau sign, migratory thrombophlebitis or recurrent venous thrombosis, is caused by tumor derived procoagulant mucins. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, systemic presentations, lines 3885–3888).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes migratory thrombophlebitis as redness and tenderness on palpation of the extremities (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp links a hypercoagulable state to superficial thrombophlebitis in pancreatic adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, from which protein is the amyloid in insulinomas derived?","choices":{"A":"Proinsulin","B":"Calcitonin","C":"Light chains","D":"Transthyretin","E":"Islet amylin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Insulinomas show monotonous cords of well differentiated beta cells with characteristic amyloid derived from amylin. Calcitonin amyloid fits medullary thyroid carcinoma, light chains fit AL amyloid, and transthyretin fits senile amyloid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, insulinoma morphology, lines 3911–3915).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 90 percent of insulinomas are benign, solitary, encapsulated nodules under 2 cm (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp states insulinomas arise from beta cells with autonomous insulin production (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, insulinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of gastrinomas occur with MEN1 syndrome?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 25 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 95 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"About 25 percent of gastrinomas occur with MEN1, often multifocal and duodenal, whereas sporadic gastrinomas are usually single. About 10 percent of insulinomas are MEN1 related. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, gastrinomas, lines 3926–3932).","boardPrep":"Step 1: more than 50 percent of gastrinomas are locally invasive or metastatic at diagnosis (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes autonomous gastrin release driving parietal cell acid secretion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastrinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which skin rash is characteristic of a glucagonoma?","choices":{"A":"Painful erythema nodosum","B":"Violaceous livedo reticularis","C":"Velvety acanthosis nigricans","D":"Necrolytic migratory erythema","E":"Itchy dermatitis herpetiformis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Glucagonoma causes mild diabetes, necrolytic migratory erythema, anemia, glossitis, and deep vein thrombosis. The other rashes fit inflammatory bowel disease, vasculopathy, insulin resistance, and celiac disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, glucagonomas, lines 3939–3947).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucagonomas are most common in perimenopausal or postmenopausal women (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists weight loss and thrombosis in glucagonoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, glucagonoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man who does not drink alcohol has had four episodes of acute pancreatitis since age 17. He takes no medications, ultrasound shows no gallstones, and his serum calcium and triglycerides are normal. His younger sister has also had pancreatitis, and neither parent has. MRCP shows that his dorsal and ventral pancreatic ducts never joined. Genetic testing is most likely to show a variant in which gene?","choices":{"A":"ATP7B","B":"UGT1A1","C":"ABCB11","D":"CFTR","E":"HNF1A"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Most people with pancreas divisum have no symptoms, and only about 5 percent develop pancreatitis from inadequate drainage through the narrow minor papilla, particularly when a genetic defect that increases susceptibility to pancreatitis is also present. Recurrent pancreatitis from adolescence with an affected sibling and unaffected parents suggests a recessive susceptibility gene, and CFTR is one of the genes Robbins names in hereditary pancreatitis, along with PRSS1 and SPINK1. ATP7B mutations cause Wilson disease with hepatic and neurologic copper overload. ABCB11 encodes the canalicular bile salt export pump, and its defects cause familial intrahepatic cholestasis rather than pancreatitis. HNF1A variants cause a monogenic form of diabetes and hepatocellular adenomas, not recurrent pancreatitis. UGT1A1 variants cause Gilbert or Crigler Najjar syndrome with unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, pancreas divisum and predisposing genetic defects, lines 3726–3738); Robbins Ch. 19 (pancreas divisum; PRSS1, SPINK1, and CFTR in hereditary pancreatitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that pancreas divisum is a common anomaly, mostly asymptomatic, that may cause chronic abdominal pain or pancreatitis (First Aid 2025 GI, pancreas and spleen embryology). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists an increased risk of pancreatitis and abdominal pain with pancreas divisum (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — cystic fibrosis as an etiology of pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman has had 8 months of postprandial epigastric fullness and intermittent vomiting. She does not use NSAIDs, and upper endoscopy shows no ulcer or mass. CT shows a band of tissue with the same density and enhancement as the pancreatic head wrapped around the duodenum, with a dilated stomach and duodenal bulb proximal to it. Which process best explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Hypertrophy of pyloric muscle","B":"Peptic scarring of the pylorus","C":"Extrinsic duodenal constriction","D":"Intraluminal duodenal mucosal web","E":"Failure of gastric smooth muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A ring of pancreatic tissue around the duodenum is an annular pancreas, formed when a split ventral bud migrates around the duodenal C loop. About one third of cases are incidental, but in symptomatic patients the ring constricts the duodenum from outside and causes stenosis or obstruction, presenting in neonates or in adults with persistent vomiting and distention. Pyloric muscle hypertrophy is an infant lesion at the pylorus and would not dilate the duodenal bulb. Peptic scarring of the pylorus requires ulcer disease, and endoscopy showed none. An intraluminal duodenal web is a recanalization defect inside the lumen, not an encircling band of pancreas. Gastric smooth muscle failure would leave the duodenal bulb undilated and would show no encircling tissue. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, annular pancreas, lines 3739–3748); Robbins Ch. 19 (annular pancreas); First Aid 2025 GI (annular pancreas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that annular pancreas may cause duodenal narrowing and vomiting and is associated with Down syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI, pancreas and spleen embryology). COMLEX: an annular pancreas can first cause symptoms in adulthood (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has epigastric pain and occult blood in his stool. Endoscopy shows a small submucosal gastric nodule, and biopsy shows normal appearing acini, ducts, and islets. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Gastric carcinoid","B":"Gastric lipoma","C":"Gastric leiomyoma","D":"Gastric lymphoma","E":"Ectopic pancreas"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ectopic pancreas forms subepithelial rests of normal acini, ducts, and sometimes islets, most often in the stomach and duodenum. Inflammation can cause epigastric pain, and ulceration can cause occult bleeding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, ectopic pancreas, lines 3749–3759).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ectopic pancreas is found in about 2 percent of postmortem examinations (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: ectopic pancreas in a Meckel diverticulum can cause peptic injury (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with heavy alcohol use has epigastric pain radiating to his back, and CT shows peripancreatic fat necrosis. His serum calcium is 7.2 mg/dL (reference 8.4–10.2 mg/dL). Which mechanism explains his low calcium?","choices":{"A":"Fat saponification","B":"Renal calcium wasting","C":"PTH gland failure","D":"Calcitonin surge","E":"Bone mineral uptake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In acute pancreatitis, released lipases break down fat, and the fatty acids bind calcium to form calcium soaps, so fat saponification causes hypocalcemia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, morphology and complications, lines 3794–3797, 3812–3816).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid attributes hypocalcemia in acute pancreatitis to precipitation of calcium soaps (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes acute pancreatitis pain radiates to the back and improves with leaning forward (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman is admitted with acute pancreatitis. Which admission finding counts toward her Ranson score?","choices":{"A":"AST 150 U/L","B":"Age 45 years","C":"Glucose 120 mg/dL","D":"LDH 200 U/L","E":"LDH 400 U/L"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ranson admission criteria include age over 55, WBC over 16,000 per microliter, glucose over 200 mg/dL, AST over 250 U/L, and LDH over 350 U/L. Only an LDH of 400 U/L meets a threshold here. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, Ranson criteria concept, lines 3786–3792).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ranson criteria at 48 hours include a hematocrit fall over 10 percent and a BUN rise over 5 mg/dL (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: the 48 hour criteria include calcium under 8 mg/dL and PaO2 under 60 mm Hg (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with long standing alcohol use has steatorrhea, weight loss, and pancreatic calcifications on CT. During a painful attack his pancreatic enzymes are barely raised. Which explains this?","choices":{"A":"Rapid renal enzyme loss","B":"Enzymes bound by albumin","C":"Lab assay interference","D":"Acinar cell dropout","E":"Islet cell destruction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In chronic pancreatitis, amylase and lipase may be normal or only mildly raised during attacks because extensive acinar dropout leaves too little functional tissue to release enzymes. Islets are preserved until late and do not make these enzymes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, serum markers, lines 3809–3811).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists fat soluble vitamin A, D, E, and K deficiency in chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: First Aid notes pancreatic insufficiency typically appears below 10 percent function (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — clinical use of pancrelipase in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man with chronic alcoholic pancreatitis had an acute attack 6 weeks ago and now has a walled off peripancreatic fluid collection with debris. Which complication is this lesion prone to?","choices":{"A":"Malignant transformation","B":"Islet amyloid deposits","C":"Abscess formation","D":"GNAS driven carcinoma","E":"Tuberculous granulomas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A pseudocyst arises after acute pancreatitis, especially on chronic alcoholic disease, when walled off fat necrosis liquefies. Its complications are secondary infection forming an abscess, erosion into vessels or viscera, and rupture. It is not a neoplasm, so malignant change and GNAS carcinoma do not apply, islet amyloid belongs to type 2 diabetes, and tuberculous granulomas are not among its listed complications; its infectious risk is abscess. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, pseudocysts, lines 3830–3836).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes pseudocysts raise the risk of extrinsic compression of local structures (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic pseudocyst). COMLEX: First Aid describes a pseudocyst as lined by granulation tissue, not epithelium (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman has a slow growing, painless, 6 cm multiloculated cyst in her pancreatic tail filled with thick, tenacious mucin. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Endoscopic drainage","B":"Percutaneous drainage","C":"Antibiotic therapy","D":"Early resection","E":"Somatostatin analog"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A mucin filled multiloculated tail cyst in a woman is a mucinous cystic neoplasm, a precancerous lesion in which up to one third harbor invasive adenocarcinoma, so early surgical resection is mandatory. Drainage suits pseudocysts, not neoplasms. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, mucinous cystic neoplasms, lines 3849–3861).","boardPrep":"Step 1: About 95 percent of MCNs arise in women (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: TP53 and SMAD4 mutations mark the transition of MCN to invasive cancer (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has a cystic lesion of the pancreatic head that arises within a dilated main duct and contains mucin producing papillae without ovarian stroma. Which mutation best distinguishes this lesion from other pancreatic cysts?","choices":{"A":"VHL inactivation","B":"GNAS activation","C":"BRAF activation","D":"HNF1A inactivation","E":"CTNNB1 activation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An IPMN arises in the larger ducts, favors the head in men, and lacks ovarian stroma. Its oncogenic GNAS mutations, in about two thirds, are unique among pancreatic cysts. VHL marks serous cystadenoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, IPMNs, lines 3862–3873).","boardPrep":"Step 1: up to 20 percent of IPMNs are multifocal (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: IPMNs also carry KRAS mutations in about 80 percent and RNF43 mutations in about 50 percent (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man has mild, progressive painless jaundice, dark urine, clay colored stools, and a palpable nontender gallbladder. Where is his tumor most likely located?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic head","B":"Pancreatic body","C":"Pancreatic tail","D":"Gallbladder fundus","E":"Proximal jejunum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Tumors of the pancreatic head obstruct the distal common bile duct and cause obstructive jaundice with the Courvoisier sign, a palpable nontender gallbladder. Body and tail tumors present late with pain and weight loss. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, location dependent presentation, lines 3877–3884).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes a double duct sign on imaging in pancreatic adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists endoscopic ultrasound with tissue sampling for diagnosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — clinical features of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman developed diabetes a year ago and now has back pain, weight loss, and recurrent superficial vein clots. CT shows a mass in her pancreatic body. Which serum marker is most useful to track her response to treatment?","choices":{"A":"Cancer antigen 125 level","B":"Carbohydrate antigen 19 9","C":"Prostate specific antigen","D":"Neuron specific enolase","E":"Serum alpha fetoprotein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CA 19 9 and CEA are often raised in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma. They lack the sensitivity and specificity for screening but are useful to track response to treatment and detect recurrence. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, tumor markers, lines 3889–3893).","boardPrep":"Step 1: new onset diabetes appears in up to 50 percent of patients up to 2 years before diagnosis (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists metastasis to the liver as a complication (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — etiologies, clinical features, and complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma, and several relatives have had melanoma and atypical moles. Which germline gene is most likely mutated in his family?","choices":{"A":"BRCA2 gene","B":"PALB2 gene","C":"PRSS1 gene","D":"SERPINA1 gene","E":"CDKN2A gene"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Germline CDKN2A mutation causes FAMMM syndrome, with familial atypical moles, melanoma, and pancreatic cancer. BRCA2, PALB2, and PRSS1 are other pancreatic cancer genes without this melanoma pattern, and SERPINA1 causes AAT deficiency. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, germline predisposition, lines 3905–3907).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 10 percent of patients carry germline mutations in cancer genes (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists HNPCC, neurofibromatosis type 1, and hereditary breast and ovarian cancer syndrome as inherited risks (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman has episodes of confusion during fasting that resolve when she eats. During an episode her glucose is 40 mg/dL (reference 70–100 mg/dL), with high insulin and C peptide. Which histology is most likely in her tumor?","choices":{"A":"Ovarian type stroma","B":"Cords with amyloid","C":"Glycogen rich clear cells","D":"Signet ring cells","E":"Desmoplastic glands"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fasting hypoglycemia relieved by food with high insulin and C peptide is the Whipple triad of insulinoma, which shows monotonous cords of well differentiated beta cells with amylin derived amyloid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, insulinomas, lines 3911–3925).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp defines the Whipple triad as hypoglycemic symptoms, hypoglycemia during the episode, and relief with glucose (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, insulinoma). COMLEX: congenital hyperinsulinism occurs in infants of diabetic mothers and Beckwith Wiedemann syndrome (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with MEN1 has severe, refractory ulcers in his duodenum and jejunum and chronic diarrhea. Where are his gastrinomas most likely found?","choices":{"A":"Single pancreatic tail mass","B":"Single pancreatic head mass","C":"Multiple duodenal tumors","D":"Gastric antral polyps","E":"Primary hepatic tumor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MEN1 associated gastrinomas are frequently multifocal and located in the duodenum, whereas sporadic gastrinomas are typically single. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, gastrinomas, lines 3926–3938).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea in Zollinger Ellison syndrome comes from acid inactivation of pancreatic enzymes (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists refractory peptic ulcers, diarrhea, steatorrhea, and weight loss in gastrinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastrinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion by gastrin","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has profuse watery diarrhea of 4 liters a day, potassium of 2.6 mEq/L (reference 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and achlorhydria. CT shows a large mass in his pancreatic tail. Which mechanism explains his diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Excess VIP secretion","B":"Excess gastrin secretion","C":"Excess serotonin secretion","D":"Excess glucagon secretion","E":"Excess calcitonin secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A VIPoma, typically a large solitary tail tumor, causes WDHA or Verner Morrison syndrome: watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria. Gastrin excess raises acid, the opposite of achlorhydria. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, VIPomas, lines 3948–3957).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes VIP driving bicarbonate and chloride secretion by intestinal epithelial cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, VIPoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists weight loss in VIPoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, VIPoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":3,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy whose mother had polyhydramnios vomits with every feed. Imaging shows a ring of pancreatic tissue around the second part of his duodenum. Which condition is classically associated with his anomaly?","choices":{"A":"Trisomy 18","B":"Turner syndrome","C":"Trisomy 13","D":"Trisomy 21","E":"Klinefelter syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Annular pancreas forms a ring of pancreatic tissue around the second part of the duodenum and is associated with trisomy 21, or Down syndrome. Sources: Bootcamp Gastroenterology, annular pancreas (merged_with_ocr line 146); First Aid 2025 GI pancreas embryology (fa_gastro_cache line 191); ring anatomy from Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, annular pancreas, lines 3739–3748).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes annular pancreas may narrow the duodenum and cause vomiting (First Aid 2025 GI pancreas embryology). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes a distended upper and scaphoid lower abdomen in annular pancreas (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, annular pancreas).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with Crohn disease develops acute epigastric pain radiating to his back a few weeks after starting a new maintenance drug. He does not drink and has no gallstones. Which drug most likely caused his pancreatitis?","choices":{"A":"Azathioprine","B":"Amoxicillin","C":"Metformin","D":"Acetaminophen","E":"Levothyroxine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The lecture lists azathioprine, statins, and GLP 1 agonists among drug causes of acute pancreatitis, beside gallstones, alcohol, hypertriglyceridemia, hypercalcemia, trauma, ERCP, infections, and genetic mutations. Amoxicillin, metformin, acetaminophen, and levothyroxine are not among the drug causes it lists. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, etiology, lines 3763–3769).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mumps and coxsackievirus are infectious causes of acute pancreatitis (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists post ERCP injury among traumatic or iatrogenic causes (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has an incidental cystic mass in her pancreatic tail. Resection shows small cysts lined by glycogen rich cuboidal cells and filled with clear, thin fluid. Which gross appearance fits this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Thick mucin cysts","B":"Honeycomb microcysts","C":"Main duct dilation","D":"Necrotic debris cavity","E":"Solid papillary mass"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Serous cystadenomas have a microcystic, honeycomb appearance with 1 to 3 mm cysts lined by glycogen rich cuboidal cells and filled with clear, thin, straw colored fluid. Thick mucin fits MCN, main duct dilation fits IPMN, and a debris cavity fits a pseudocyst. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, serous cystic neoplasms, lines 3837–3848).","boardPrep":"Step 1: surgical resection of a serous cystadenoma is curative if it causes symptoms from mass effect (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: IPMNs lack the dense ovarian stroma of MCNs (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has a resected pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma. Molecular testing shows the tumor has lost TGF beta signaling. Which gene is most likely inactivated?","choices":{"A":"KRAS","B":"TP53","C":"CDKN2A","D":"BRCA2","E":"SMAD4"},"correct":"E","explanation":"SMAD4 on 18q is inactivated in 55 percent of pancreatic adenocarcinomas at the PanIN 3 stage and abolishes TGF beta signaling. KRAS is activated rather than lost, TP53 loss impairs the DNA damage response, and CDKN2A loss disrupts the cell cycle. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, key mutations, lines 3894–3904).","boardPrep":"Step 1: TP53 on 17p is inactivated in 70 to 75 percent of cases (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: First Aid lists chronic pancreatitis, especially beyond 20 years, as a risk factor (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old postmenopausal woman has mild diabetes, glossitis, anemia, a deep vein thrombosis, and a painful, migrating, blistering red rash. Which cells most likely gave rise to her tumor?","choices":{"A":"Islet beta cells","B":"Islet delta cells","C":"Islet alpha cells","D":"Duodenal G cells","E":"Islet PP cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mild diabetes, a migrating blistering rash, anemia, glossitis, and DVT in a postmenopausal woman fit a glucagonoma, an alpha cell tumor with extremely high plasma glucagon. Beta cells form insulinomas and G cells form gastrinomas. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, glucagonomas, lines 3939–3947).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucagonomas show monotonous cells with salt and pepper chromatin, and a high proportion are malignant (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes glucagonomas as arising from alpha cells with autonomous glucagon release (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, glucagonoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 56-year-old man with decades of chronic pancreatitis has already developed steatorrhea, and now his fasting glucose begins to rise. Which complication is he developing?","choices":{"A":"Secondary diabetes","B":"Fat soluble vitamin loss","C":"Chronic steatorrhea","D":"Pseudocyst formation","E":"Protein azotorrhea"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chronic pancreatitis destroys exocrine tissue first, while the islets are preserved until late, so secondary diabetes from endocrine failure develops after exocrine insufficiency. Vitamin loss, steatorrhea, and azotorrhea are exocrine, and pseudocysts are structural. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, morphology and complications, lines 3798–3801, 3817–3822).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic pancreatitis raises the risk of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma, up to 40 percent lifetime in PRSS1 hereditary pancreatitis (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: autoimmune type 1 pancreatitis shows storiform fibrosis, obliterative phlebitis, and IgG4 positive plasma cells (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — clinical use of pancrelipase in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":20,"title":"SDL 20 — Pharmacology of bile acid agents and pancreatic enzyme supplements","questions":[{"id":"sdl20_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, below what level of normal enzyme secretion does exocrine pancreatic insufficiency occur?","choices":{"A":"Below 10 percent","B":"Below 50 percent","C":"Below 30 percent","D":"Below 75 percent","E":"Below 90 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency occurs when pancreatic enzyme secretion falls below 10 percent of normal. It is most often caused by cystic fibrosis, chronic pancreatitis, or surgical resection. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, etiology and pathophysiology of EPI, lines 3960–3963).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes pancreatic insufficiency typically appears at less than 10 percent pancreatic function (First Aid 2025 GI chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists decreased fecal elastase 1 among chronic pancreatitis diagnostics (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the source of the enzyme extracts used in pancrelipase?","choices":{"A":"Recombinant yeast","B":"Porcine pancreas","C":"Bovine stomach","D":"Fungal fermentation","E":"Human donor plasma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Replacement therapy uses animal derived porcine pancreatic extracts containing lipase, amylase, and proteases. Pancrelipase gives about 12 times the lipolytic activity of older pancreatin extracts. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, therapeutic rationale, lines 3967–3972).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancrelipase gives more than 4 times the proteolytic activity per weight of pancreatin (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists steatorrhea and fat soluble vitamin deficiency in chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, when should pancrelipase be taken?","choices":{"A":"Once daily at bedtime","B":"Before breakfast on waking","C":"With each meal and snack","D":"Twice daily between meals","E":"Weekly with a fatty meal"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pancrelipase is given orally with every meal and snack, with the dose individualized by body weight, severity of insufficiency, and dietary fat intake. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, clinical uses and dosing, lines 3984–3989).","boardPrep":"Step 1: therapy starts at 60,000 to 90,000 USP units of lipase per meal (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists cystic fibrosis among causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which receptor does obeticholic acid activate?","choices":{"A":"Farnesoid X receptor","B":"Pregnane X receptor","C":"Liver X receptor alpha","D":"PPAR gamma receptor","E":"Vitamin D receptor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Obeticholic acid is a synthetic chenodeoxycholate derivative and a potent agonist of the nuclear farnesoid X receptor, or FXR, which downregulates hepatic bile acid synthesis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, obeticholic acid mechanism, lines 4029–4035).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obeticholic acid is given at 5 to 10 mg per day for PBC (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: First Aid lists ursodiol as the treatment of primary biliary cholangitis (First Aid 2025 GI primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and regulation of bile acid synthesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about how much of the circulating bile acid pool does long term ursodiol become?","choices":{"A":"Less than 5 percent","B":"About 10 percent","C":"80 to 90 percent","D":"Nearly 100 percent","E":"30 to 50 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ursodiol is normally less than 5 percent of the bile salt pool, but daily long term dosing expands it to 30 to 50 percent of the circulating bile acid pool. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, ursodiol pharmacokinetics, lines 4001–4007).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ursodiol is conjugated with glycine or taurine and has a serum half life of about 100 hours (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists ursodeoxycholic acid in PBC management (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and regulation of bile acid synthesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which adverse effect of obeticholic acid can lead to stopping treatment?","choices":{"A":"Severe hepatotoxicity","B":"Uric acid kidney stones","C":"Oropharyngeal mucositis","D":"Dose dependent pruritus","E":"Fibrosing colonopathy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Obeticholic acid causes severe, dose dependent pruritus in up to 25 percent of patients, leading to stopping in up to 10 percent. Uric acid stones, mucositis, and colonopathy are pancrelipase effects, and hepatotoxicity is the problem the lecture ties to the older agent chenodeoxycholate. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, obeticholic acid adverse effects, lines 4043–4045; ursodiol adverse effects, lines 4027–4028).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obeticholic acid has been studied in MASLD, with improved liver fibrosis (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists cholestyramine with ursodeoxycholic acid in PBC management (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and regulation of bile acid synthesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with alcohol related chronic pancreatitis takes non enteric coated pancrelipase tablets with meals, but his greasy stools persist. Which drug should be added to improve his response?","choices":{"A":"Cholestyramine resin","B":"Loperamide with meals","C":"Metronidazole course","D":"Ondansetron before meals","E":"Proton pump inhibitor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gastric acid rapidly and permanently inactivates pancrelipase, so non enteric coated tablets such as Viokace must be given with acid suppression, a PPI or H2 blocker. Cholestyramine binds bile acids, loperamide only slows transit, metronidazole is a nitroimidazole with potent activity against anaerobes, and ondansetron is a 5 HT3 antagonist antiemetic; none protects the enzymes from acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, pharmacodynamics and formulations, lines 3973–3983); Katzung Ch 50, metronidazole, lines 22–27; Katzung Ch 62, serotonin 5 HT3 antagonists, lines 1802–1806.","boardPrep":"Step 1: enteric coated capsules dissolve in alkaline duodenal pH, so they need no routine acid suppression (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists chronic alcohol use and tobacco use as causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis has taken high dose pancrelipase for years. She now has crampy abdominal pain and distension, and imaging shows a long stricture of her ascending colon. Which complication best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Meconium plug ileus","B":"Ileocolic intussusception","C":"Sigmoid colon volvulus","D":"Fibrosing colonopathy","E":"Toxic colonic dilation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"High doses with high lipase activity were linked to fibrosing colonopathy, with colonic strictures, in patients with cystic fibrosis. Meconium ileus affects newborns, volvulus and intussusception cause acute obstruction without a long stricture, and toxic dilation follows severe colitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, adverse effects and precautions, lines 3990–3996).","boardPrep":"Step 1: excessive pancrelipase doses can cause diarrhea and abdominal pain (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: First Aid lists cystic fibrosis among genetic risk factors for chronic pancreatitis (First Aid 2025 GI chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate rich secretion, including the role of CFTR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man on high dose pancrelipase after a Whipple resection develops flank pain and hematuria. CT shows a renal stone that was not seen on plain film. Which property of his therapy best explains this?","choices":{"A":"High purine content","B":"High oxalate content","C":"High calcium content","D":"High cystine content","E":"High phosphate load"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancreatic extracts contain high purine levels, so high doses can cause hyperuricosuria and renal uric acid stones, which are radiolucent, unlike radiopaque calcium stones such as calcium oxalate. Phosphate is not a constituent the lecture flags in the extracts, and cystine stones come from the inborn error cystinuria. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, adverse effects and precautions, lines 3990–3996); Robbins Ch 20, library/text/Robbins Patho/Ch.Robbins20.pdf.txt, lines 4907–4908 (cystinuria) and 4953 (radiolucent uric acid stones).","boardPrep":"Step 1: capsules must be swallowed whole, since chewing them can cause oropharyngeal mucositis (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: First Aid notes chronic pancreatitis can cause diabetes mellitus from pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid 2025 GI chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman with symptomatic gallstones refuses surgery. Ultrasound shows several 4 mm stones, and plain film shows no calcified stones. Which mechanism explains how the drug chosen for her will dissolve them?","choices":{"A":"Binding bile acids in the gut","B":"Lower biliary cholesterol secretion","C":"Activating hepatic FXR signaling","D":"Raising bilirubin conjugation rate","E":"Stimulating gallbladder contraction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ursodiol dissolves small, radiolucent, noncalcified cholesterol stones by suppressing hepatic cholesterol secretion and lowering the cholesterol content of bile, with dissolution in up to 50 percent over 12 to 24 months. Sequestrants bind bile acids, and FXR agonism is obeticholic acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, ursodiol mechanism and uses, lines 4008–4018).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ursodiol also prevents gallstones in obese patients during rapid weight loss (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists cholesterol supersaturation of bile among causes of cholelithiasis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — epidemiological risk factors, associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with antimitochondrial antibodies has had a high alkaline phosphatase for a year despite full dose ursodiol. Which drug should be added?","choices":{"A":"Cholestyramine","B":"Chenodeoxycholate","C":"Obeticholic acid","D":"Oral prednisone","E":"Oral pancrelipase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Obeticholic acid is approved for PBC at 5 to 10 mg per day with ursodiol when the response to ursodiol alone is inadequate, or alone if ursodiol is not tolerated. Chenodeoxycholate is hepatotoxic, and pancrelipase treats exocrine insufficiency. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, obeticholic acid clinical uses, lines 4037–4042; ursodiol adverse effects, lines 4027–4028).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ursodiol at 13 to 15 mg/kg/day is first line for early PBC and delays transplant (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists liver transplant in PBC management (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and regulation of bile acid synthesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with primary biliary cholangitis stopped his first-line bile acid therapy after 3 months because he could not tolerate it. His serum alkaline phosphatase is 410 U/L (reference range: 25–100 U/L) and total bilirubin is 1.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.1–1.0 mg/dL). He starts once-daily oral monotherapy with a synthetic derivative of chenodeoxycholic acid. Which hepatic effect of this drug most directly protects his hepatocytes and cholangiocytes?","choices":{"A":"Reduced bile acid synthesis","B":"Reduced biliary cholesterol secretion","C":"Binding of bile acids in the gut","D":"Blocked ileal bile acid uptake","E":"Inhibited hepatic cholesterol synthesis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A synthetic chenodeoxycholate derivative given as monotherapy for primary biliary cholangitis when the first-line agent is not tolerated is obeticholic acid. It is a potent agonist of the nuclear farnesoid X receptor, and FXR activation downregulates hepatic bile acid synthesis; the lower intracellular concentration of toxic endogenous bile acids protects hepatocytes and cholangiocytes, and the drug also modulates inflammation and reduces fibrosis. Reduced biliary cholesterol secretion is the action of ursodiol, which lowers the cholesterol content of bile and dissolves small cholesterol stones. Binding bile acids in the gut lumen describes the nonabsorbed bile acid sequestrants, which do not act on the hepatocyte nucleus. Blocking ileal bile acid uptake interrupts enterohepatic recirculation in the gut rather than acting through a hepatic nuclear receptor. Inhibiting hepatic cholesterol synthesis is the action of statins, not of a bile acid agent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, obeticholic acid mechanism and clinical uses, lines 4029–4046; ursodiol mechanism, lines 4008–4013); First Aid 2025 GI (bile, rate-limiting step of bile acid synthesis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase catalyzes the rate-limiting step of bile acid synthesis (First Aid 2025 GI, bile). COMLEX: First Aid gives ursodiol as the treatment of primary biliary cholangitis, the setting for add-on or substitute bile acid agents (First Aid 2025 GI, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy with cystic fibrosis takes enteric coated pancrelipase capsules, but he has been chewing them because they are hard to swallow. He now has painful sores in his mouth. Which adverse effect is he showing?","choices":{"A":"Herpetic gingivostomatitis","B":"Oral candidiasis","C":"Aphthous Crohn ulcers","D":"Oropharyngeal mucositis","E":"Vitamin B12 glossitis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pancrelipase capsules must be swallowed whole and not chewed, because contact with active enzymes can cause oropharyngeal mucositis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, adverse effects and precautions, lines 3990–3993).","boardPrep":"Step 1: high doses with high lipase activity were linked to fibrosing colonopathy in cystic fibrosis (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists PRSS1 and SPINK1 mutations among causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with a BMI of 44 is starting a very low calorie diet for rapid weight loss before bariatric surgery. Her physician wants to lower her risk of forming gallstones during this period. Which drug fits?","choices":{"A":"Cholestyramine","B":"Obeticholic acid","C":"Orlistat","D":"Gemfibrozil","E":"Ursodiol"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ursodiol prevents gallstone formation in obese patients undergoing rapid weight loss by lowering the cholesterol content of bile. Cholestyramine binds bile acids, fibrates raise gallstone risk, orlistat is a GI lipase inhibitor that reduces lipid absorption for weight loss rather than a stone preventive, and obeticholic acid is a second line PBC drug. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, ursodiol clinical uses, lines 4008–4020); Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis; Katzung Ch 16, anti obesity drugs, lines 1781–1785.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ursodiol is first line for early PBC at 13 to 15 mg/kg/day (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists obesity, female sex, fibrates, and rapid weight loss as gallstone risk factors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — epidemiological risk factors, associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with cystic fibrosis takes delayed release pancrelipase capsules with meals and no acid suppression. Her steatorrhea is well controlled. Which feature of her capsules explains why no PPI is needed?","choices":{"A":"Coat dissolves at duodenal pH","B":"Acid stable form of lipase","C":"Gastric lipase is activated","D":"Bile salts shield the enzymes","E":"Jejunal uptake of enzymes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteric coated, delayed release capsules such as Creon have acid resistant coatings that protect the enzymes from gastric acid and dissolve in the alkaline duodenum, so routine acid suppression is not needed. The enzymes themselves are inactivated by acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, pharmacodynamics and formulations, lines 3973–3983).","boardPrep":"Step 1: non enteric coated tablets such as Viokace need a PPI or H2 blocker (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: First Aid notes pancreatic insufficiency causes steatorrhea and fat soluble vitamin deficiency (First Aid 2025 GI chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate rich secretion, including the role of CFTR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with primary biliary cholangitis starts ursodiol. Which mechanism best explains how it protects her hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Binds bile acids in the gut lumen","B":"Blocks hepatic bile acid uptake","C":"Displaces hydrophobic bile acids","D":"Acts as a potent FXR agonist","E":"Raises cholesterol secretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ursodiol stabilizes canalicular membranes and protects hepatocytes by replacing toxic hydrophobic bile acids with nontoxic hydrophilic ursodiol, with cytoprotective and anti inflammatory effects. It lowers rather than raises biliary cholesterol secretion, it does not bind gut bile acids or block their uptake, and potent FXR agonism is the action of obeticholic acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, ursodiol mechanism, lines 4008–4014).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obeticholic acid is added for PBC with an inadequate response to ursodiol (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes PBC as T cell mediated destruction of small and medium intrahepatic ducts (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q05","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has steatorrhea and weight loss after a total pancreatectomy. Which starting lipase dose per meal, in USP units, is standard?","choices":{"A":"60,000 to 90,000 units","B":"9,000 to 12,000 units","C":"5,000 to 8,000 units","D":"120,000 to 180,000 units","E":"2,000 to 4,000 units"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancrelipase therapy starts at 60,000 to 90,000 USP units, or 20,000 to 30,000 IU, of lipase per meal, which reduces steatorrhea to insignificant levels in most patients. Excess doses cause diarrhea and pain. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, clinical uses and dosing, lines 3984–3992).","boardPrep":"Step 1: surgical pancreatic resection is a common cause of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists a positive Sudan III stain among chronic pancreatitis diagnostics (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with heart failure has symptomatic small radiolucent gallstones and is not a surgical candidate. His physician chooses ursodiol over the older agent chenodeoxycholate. Which advantage supports this choice?","choices":{"A":"Faster dissolution","B":"Lacks hepatotoxicity","C":"Clears calcified stones","D":"Once weekly dosing","E":"Clears pigment stones"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ursodiol is remarkably safe and virtually free of serious toxicity, and unlike chenodeoxycholate it is not hepatotoxic. It works only on small, noncalcified cholesterol stones and takes 12 to 24 months. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, ursodiol clinical uses and adverse effects, lines 4015–4028).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile salt induced diarrhea is uncommon with ursodiol (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes cholesterol stones are generally radiolucent on X ray (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones","isHighYield":true}]}]},{"examNumber":2,"sdls":[{"sdlNumber":21,"title":"SDL 21 — Embryology of the Small Intestine","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":22,"title":"SDL 22 — Embryology of the Large Intestine","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":23,"title":"SDL 23 — Histology of the Small Intestine","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":24,"title":"SDL 24 — Histology of the Large Intestine","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":25,"title":"SDL 25 — Physiology - Small & Large Intestinal Motility","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":26,"title":"SDL 26 — Physiology - Digestion & Absorption of the Small Intestine Part 1 (Carbs, Proteins)","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":27,"title":"SDL 27 — Physiology - Digestion & Absorption of the Small Intestine Part 2 (Lipids, Vitamins)","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":28,"title":"SDL 28 — Physiology - Intestinal Fluid & Electrolyte Transport","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":29,"title":"SDL 29 — Pathology - Small Intestine & Colon: Obstructions, Ischemia, and Malabsorption","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":30,"title":"SDL 30 — Review","questions":[]}]},{"examNumber":3,"sdls":[{"sdlNumber":31,"title":"SDL 31 — Pathology - Small Intestine & Colon: Infectious Enterocolitis","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":32,"title":"SDL 32 — Pathology - Small Intestine & Colon: IBS, IBD, Diverticular Disease, and Polyps","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":33,"title":"SDL 33 — Pathology - Small Intestine & Colon: Malignant Lesions, Anal Canal, and Appendix","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":34,"title":"SDL 34 — Nutritional Diseases","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":35,"title":"SDL 35 — Intestinal Helminths","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":36,"title":"SDL 36 — Pharmacology of Antihelminths","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":37,"title":"SDL 37 — Pharmacology of GI motility, IBS, and short-bowel syndrome","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":38,"title":"SDL 38 — Pharmacology of laxatives, antidiarrheals, and IBD","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":39,"title":"SDL 39 — Pharmacology - chemo for colorectal cancer (CRC)","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":40,"title":"SDL 40 — Review","questions":[]}]}]};